const DATA=[{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"I","loc":"London. A Room in the Palace.","id":0},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the King Henry, Westmoreland, Sir Walter Blunt, and others.","id":1},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["So shaken as we are, so wan with care,","Find we a time for frighted peace to pant,","And breathe short-winded accents of new broils","To be commenced in strands afar remote.","No more the thirsty entrance of this soil","Shall daub her lips with her own children's blood;","No more shall trenching war channel her fields,","Nor bruise her flowerets with the armed hoofs","Of hostile paces: those opposed eyes,","Which, like the meteors of a troubled heaven,","All of one nature, of one substance bred,","Did lately meet in the intestine shock","And furious close of civil butchery,","Shall now, in mutual well-beseeming ranks,","March all one way, and be no more opposed","Against acquaintance, kindred, and allies:","The edge of war, like an ill-sheathed knife,","No more shall cut his master. Therefore, friends,","As far as to the sepulchre of Christ—","Whose soldier now, under whose blessed cross","We are impressed and engaged to fight—","Forthwith a power of English shall we levy,","To chase these pagans in those holy fields","Over whose acres walk'd those blessed feet","Which fourteen hundred years ago were nail'd","For our advantage on the bitter cross.","But this our purpose now is twelvemonth old,","And bootless 'tis to tell you we will go:","Therefore we meet not now.—Then let me hear","Of you, my gentle cousin Westmoreland,","What yesternight our Council did decree","In forwarding this dear expedience."],"id":2,"m":"Shaken and pale with worry as we are, let us find a moment for frightened peace to catch her breath, and speak, still panting, of new wars to be started in distant lands. No longer will this thirsty earth wet her lips with the blood of her own children. No longer will war carve trenches through her fields or crush her flowers under the hooves of enemy horses. Those eyes that faced each other in hostility, which, like meteors in a stormy sky, all of one nature and one birth, recently clashed in the shock and savage close of civil war, will now march together in orderly ranks, all one way, no longer set against friends, relatives and allies. The edge of war, like a knife in a badly made sheath, will no longer cut its own master. So, friends, we shall at once raise an English army and march as far as the tomb of Christ, whose soldier we now are, sworn to fight under his blessed cross, to chase the pagans out of those holy fields where his blessed feet walked, the feet that were nailed to the bitter cross for our sake fourteen hundred years ago. But this plan of ours is already a year old, and there's no point telling you that we will go; that's not why we're meeting now. Instead let me hear from you, my noble cousin Westmoreland, what the Council decided last night about speeding up this urgent expedition."},{"t":"sp","who":"WESTMORELAND","lines":["My liege, this haste was hot in question,","And many limits of the charge set down","But yesternight; when, all athwart, there came","A post from Wales loaden with heavy news;","Whose worst was, that the noble Mortimer,","Leading the men of Herefordshire to fight","Against th' irregular and wild Glendower,","Was by the rude hands of that Welshman taken;","A thousand of his people butchered,","Upon whose dead corpse' there was such misuse,","Such beastly, shameless transformation,","By those Welshwomen done, as may not be","Without much shame re-told or spoken of."],"id":3,"m":"My lord, this business was being hotly debated, and many of the duties were assigned only last night, when suddenly a messenger arrived from Wales loaded with bad news. The worst of it was that the noble Mortimer, leading the men of Herefordshire against the lawless and wild Glendower, was captured by that rough Welshman. A thousand of his men were slaughtered, and their dead bodies were so savagely and shamelessly mutilated by the Welshwomen that it can't be described or even mentioned without great shame."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["It seems, then, that the tidings of this broil","Brake off our business for the Holy Land."],"id":4,"m":"It seems, then, that the news of this fighting interrupted our plans for the Holy Land."},{"t":"sp","who":"WESTMORELAND","lines":["This, match'd with other, did, my gracious lord;","For more uneven and unwelcome news","Came from the North, and thus it did import:","On Holy-rood day the gallant Hotspur there,","Young Harry Percy, and brave Archibald,","That ever-valiant and approved Scot,","At Holmedon met;","Where they did spend a sad and bloody hour,","As by discharge of their artillery,","And shape of likelihood, the news was told;","For he that brought them, in the very heat","And pride of their contention did take horse,","Uncertain of the issue any way."],"id":5,"m":"This, together with other news, did, my lord. More troubling and unwelcome news came from the North: on Holy Cross Day the brave Hotspur, young Harry Percy, met the bold Archibald, that ever-brave and tested Scot, at Holmedon. They spent a grim and bloody hour there, as the messenger guessed from the sound of their guns and the way things looked. He rode off in the very heat of the battle, so he didn't know how it ended."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Here is a dear and true-industrious friend,","Sir Walter Blunt, new lighted from his horse,","Stain'd with the variation of each soil","Betwixt that Holmedon and this seat of ours;","And he hath brought us smooth and welcome news.","The Earl of Douglas is discomfited:","Ten thousand bold Scots, two-and-twenty knights,","Balk'd in their own blood, did Sir Walter see","On Holmedon's plains: of prisoners, Hotspur took","Mordake the Earl of Fife and eldest son","To beaten Douglas; and the Earls of Athol,","Of Murray, Angus, and Menteith.","And is not this an honourable spoil,","A gallant prize? ha, cousin, is it not?"],"id":6,"m":"Here is a dear and truly hard-working friend, Sir Walter Blunt, just off his horse and spattered with every kind of soil between Holmedon and here. He has brought us good and welcome news. The Earl of Douglas is defeated. Sir Walter saw ten thousand bold Scots and twenty-two knights lying heaped in their own blood on the plains of Holmedon. Hotspur took as prisoners Mordake, Earl of Fife and eldest son of the beaten Douglas, and the Earls of Athol, Murray, Angus and Menteith. Isn't that an honourable haul, a splendid prize? Well, cousin, isn't it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WESTMORELAND","lines":["Faith, 'tis a conquest for a prince to boast of."],"id":7,"m":"Indeed, it's a victory a prince could boast of."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Yea, there thou makest me sad, and makest me sin","In envy that my Lord Northumberland","Should be the father to so blest a son,—","A son who is the theme of honour's tongue;","Amongst a grove, the very straightest plant;","Who is sweet Fortune's minion and her pride:","Whilst I, by looking on the praise of him,","See riot and dishonour stain the brow","Of my young Harry. O, that it could be proved","That some night-tripping fairy had exchanged","In cradle-clothes our children where they lay,","And call'd mine Percy, his Plantagenet!","Then would I have his Harry, and he mine:","But let him from my thoughts. What think you, coz,","Of this young Percy's pride? the prisoners,","Which he in this adventure hath surprised,","To his own use he keeps; and sends me word,","I shall have none but Mordake Earl of Fife."],"id":8,"m":"Yes, and that's what makes me sad, and makes me sin by envying Lord Northumberland for being the father of such a blessed son, a son whom honour itself talks about, the straightest tree in the forest, Fortune's darling and pride. Meanwhile, when I look at the praise he earns, I see wild living and disgrace stain the face of my young Harry. Oh, if only it could be proved that some fairy roaming at night had swapped our children in their cradles, and named mine Percy and his Plantagenet! Then I would have his Harry, and he would have mine. But let me put my son out of my mind. What do you think, cousin, of young Percy's arrogance? He keeps for himself the prisoners he captured in this battle, and sends me word that I'll get none except Mordake, Earl of Fife."},{"t":"sp","who":"WESTMORELAND","lines":["This is his uncle's teaching, this is Worcester,","Malevolent to you in all aspects;","Which makes him prune himself, and bristle up","The crest of youth against your dignity."],"id":9,"m":"That's his uncle Worcester's teaching. Worcester is hostile to you in every way, and he makes the young man preen himself and raise his crest against your royal authority."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["But I have sent for him to answer this;","And for this cause awhile we must neglect","Our holy purpose to Jerusalem.","Cousin, on Wednesday next our Council we","Will hold at Windsor; so inform the lords:","But come yourself with speed to us again;","For more is to be said and to be done","Than out of anger can be uttered."],"id":10,"m":"But I've sent for him to answer for this, and because of it we must set aside our holy expedition to Jerusalem for a while. Cousin, we will hold our Council at Windsor next Wednesday; tell the lords. But come back to me yourself quickly, because there is more to say and do than I can express while I am angry."},{"t":"sp","who":"WESTMORELAND","lines":["I will, my liege."],"id":11,"m":"I will, my lord."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":12},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"II","loc":"London. An apartment of the Prince's.","id":13},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Prince Henry and Falstaff.","id":14},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Now, Hal, what time of day is it, lad?"],"id":15,"m":"Now, Hal, what time of day is it, lad?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Thou art so fat-witted, with drinking of old sack, and","unbuttoning thee after supper, and sleeping upon benches","after noon, that thou hast forgotten to demand that truly which","thou wouldst truly know. What a devil hast thou to do with the","time of the day? unless hours were cups of sack, and minutes","capons, and the blessed Sun himself a fair hot wench in","flame-coloured taffeta, I see no reason why thou shouldst be","so superfluous to demand the time of the day."],"id":16,"m":"You're so thick-headed from drinking old sherry, unbuttoning yourself after supper and sleeping on benches in the afternoon that you've forgotten how to ask properly for what you really want to know. What the devil do you care about the time of day? Unless hours were cups of sherry, minutes were roast chickens, and the blessed sun itself a pretty, hot girl in flame-coloured silk, I see no reason why you'd bother asking the time."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Indeed, you come near me now, Hal; for we that take purses go","by the Moon and the seven stars, and not by Phoebus,—he, that","wandering knight so fair. And I pr'ythee, sweet wag, when thou","art king,—as, God save thy Grace—Majesty I should say, for grace","thou wilt have none,—"],"id":17,"m":"Indeed, you've got me there, Hal. We who steal purses go by the moon and the seven stars, not by the sun, that wandering knight so fair. And please, sweet joker, when you are king, as God save your Grace (your Majesty, I should say, because grace you'll have none),"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["What, none?"],"id":18,"m":"What, none?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["No, by my troth; not so much as will serve to be prologue","to an egg and butter."],"id":19,"m":"No, I swear, not even enough to say a grace before a snack of egg and butter."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Well, how then? come, roundly, roundly."],"id":20,"m":"Well, what then? Come on, out with it."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Marry, then, sweet wag, when thou art king, let not us that","are squires of the night's body be called thieves of the day's","beauty: let us be Diana's foresters, gentlemen of the shade,","minions of the Moon; and let men say we be men of good","government, being governed, as the sea is, by our noble and","chaste mistress the Moon, under whose countenance we steal."],"id":21,"m":"Well then, sweet joker, when you are king, don't let us who are the night's bodyguards be called thieves who steal the day's beauty. Let us be called Diana's foresters, gentlemen of the shade, darlings of the moon. Let people say we are well-governed men, because, like the sea, we are governed by our noble and chaste mistress the moon, under whose face we steal."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Thou say'st well, and it holds well too; for the fortune of","us that are the Moon's men doth ebb and flow like the sea,","being governed, as the sea is, by the Moon. As, for proof, now: A","purse of gold most resolutely snatch'd on Monday night, and most","dissolutely spent on Tuesday morning; got with swearing Lay by,","and spent with crying Bring in; now ill as low an ebb as the foot","of the ladder, and by-and-by in as high a flow as the ridge of the","gallows."],"id":22,"m":"You put it well, and it fits too, because the fortune of us moon's men ebbs and flows like the sea, since like the sea it is governed by the moon. For example: a purse of gold boldly snatched on Monday night and wildly spent on Tuesday morning; got by shouting \"Hand it over!\" and spent by shouting \"Bring more drink!\"; one moment at a low ebb at the foot of the ladder, and soon at high tide on top of the gallows."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["By the Lord, thou say'st true, lad. And is not my hostess of the","tavern a most sweet wench?"],"id":23,"m":"By God, you're right, lad. And isn't the landlady of the tavern a very sweet woman?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["As the honey of Hybla, my old lad of the castle. And is not a","buff jerkin a most sweet robe of durance?"],"id":24,"m":"As sweet as Sicilian honey, my old rogue of the castle. And isn't a leather jerkin a lovely thing to last you a long time?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["How now, how now, mad wag! what, in thy quips and thy","quiddities? what a plague have I to do with a buff jerkin?"],"id":25,"m":"What's this, you mad joker? What, more of your jokes and quibbles? What the plague have I got to do with a leather jerkin?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Why, what a pox have I to do with my hostess of the tavern?"],"id":26,"m":"Well, what the pox have I got to do with the landlady of the tavern?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Well, thou hast call'd her to a reckoning many a time and oft."],"id":27,"m":"Well, you've asked her for the bill many times."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Did I ever call for thee to pay thy part?"],"id":28,"m":"Did I ever call on you to pay your share?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["No; I'll give thee thy due, thou hast paid all there."],"id":29,"m":"No, I'll give you your due, you've paid for everything there."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Yea, and elsewhere, so far as my coin would stretch;","and where it would not, I have used my credit."],"id":30,"m":"Yes, and elsewhere too, as far as my money would stretch, and where it wouldn't, I've used my credit."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Yea, and so used it, that, were it not here apparent that","thou art heir-apparent—But I pr'ythee, sweet wag, shall there be","gallows standing in England when thou art king? and","resolution thus fobb'd as it is with the rusty curb of old father","antic the law? Do not thou, when thou art king, hang a thief."],"id":31,"m":"Yes, and used it so well that, if it weren't obvious that you're the heir to the throne... But please, sweet joker, will there still be gallows standing in England when you're king, and will courage still be cheated by the rusty bridle of that old clown, the law? When you are king, don't hang a thief."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["No; thou shalt."],"id":32,"m":"No, you will."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Shall I? O rare! By the Lord, I'll be a brave judge."],"id":33,"m":"Will I? Oh, wonderful! By God, I'll be a fine judge."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Thou judgest false already: I mean, thou shalt have the","hanging of the thieves, and so become a rare hangman."],"id":34,"m":"You're judging wrongly already. I mean you'll have the job of hanging the thieves, and so become an excellent hangman."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Well, Hal, well; and in some sort it jumps with my humour;","as well as waiting in the Court, I can tell you."],"id":35,"m":"Well, Hal, well, in a way that suits my temperament, as well as hanging around at Court, I can tell you."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["For obtaining of suits?"],"id":36,"m":"Hoping for favours?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Yea, for obtaining of suits, whereof the hangman hath no","lean wardrobe. 'Sblood, I am as melancholy as a gib-cat or a","lugg'd bear."],"id":37,"m":"Yes, for the dead men's suits of clothes, of which the hangman has a big wardrobe. By God's blood, I'm as gloomy as a tomcat or a bear on a chain."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Or an old lion, or a lover's lute."],"id":38,"m":"Or an old lion, or a lover's lute."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Yea, or the drone of a Lincolnshire bagpipe."],"id":39,"m":"Yes, or the drone of a Lincolnshire bagpipe."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["What say'st thou to a hare, or the melancholy of Moor-ditch?"],"id":40,"m":"What do you say to a hare, or the gloom of the Moorditch sewer?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Thou hast the most unsavoury similes, and art, indeed, the","most comparative, rascalliest, sweet young prince,—But, Hal, I","pr'ythee trouble me no more with vanity. I would to God thou and","I knew where a commodity of good names were to be bought. An old","lord of the Council rated me the other day in the street about you,","sir,—but I mark'd him not; and yet he talk'd very wisely,—but I","regarded him not; and yet he talk'd wisely, and in the street too."],"id":41,"m":"You have the most unpleasant comparisons, and you're the most comparison-making, rascally, sweet young prince. But, Hal, please don't bother me any more with worldly nonsense. I wish to God you and I knew where we could buy a supply of good reputations. An old lord of the Council told me off in the street the other day about you, sir, but I paid him no attention; and yet he talked very wisely, but I ignored him; and yet he talked wisely, and in the street too."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Thou didst well; for wisdom cries out in the streets, and no man","regards it."],"id":42,"m":"You did well, because wisdom cries out in the streets and no one listens to it."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["O, thou hast damnable iteration, and art, indeed, able to corrupt","a saint.","Thou hast done much harm upon me, Hal; God forgive thee for it!","Before I knew thee, Hal, I knew nothing; and now am I, if a man","should speak truly, little better than one of the wicked. I must","give over this life, and I will give it over; by the Lord, an I do","not, I am a villain: I'll be damn'd for never a king's son in","Christendom."],"id":43,"m":"Oh, you have a damnable way of quoting scripture, and you could corrupt a saint. You've done me a lot of harm, Hal, God forgive you for it! Before I knew you, Hal, I knew nothing of evil, and now, to tell the truth, I'm little better than one of the wicked. I must give up this life, and I will give it up. By God, if I don't, I'm a villain. I won't be damned for any king's son in Christendom."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Where shall we take a purse to-morrow, Jack?"],"id":44,"m":"Where shall we steal a purse tomorrow, Jack?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Zounds, where thou wilt, lad; I'll make one: an I do not, call","me villain, and baffle me."],"id":45,"m":"By God's wounds, wherever you like, lad, I'll be one of the gang. If I'm not, call me a villain and disgrace me."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["I see a good amendment of life in thee,—from praying to","purse-taking."],"id":46,"m":"I see a fine reform in your life, from praying to purse-snatching."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Why, Hal, 'tis my vocation, Hal; 'tis no sin for a man to labour","in his vocation."],"id":47,"m":"Why, Hal, it's my calling, Hal. It's no sin for a man to work at his calling."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Pointz.","id":48},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","cont":1,"lines":["—Pointz!—Now shall we know if Gadshill have set a match. O, if","men were to be saved by merit, what hole in Hell were hot enough","for him? This is the most omnipotent villain that ever cried","Stand! to a true man."],"id":49,"m":"Poins! Now we'll find out if Gadshill has set up a robbery. Oh, if men were saved by what they deserved, what hole in hell would be hot enough for him? This is the most almighty villain that ever shouted \"Stand!\" to an honest man."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Good morrow, Ned."],"id":50,"m":"Good morning, Ned."},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["Good morrow, sweet Hal.—What says Monsieur Remorse? what","says Sir John Sack-and-sugar? Jack, how agrees the Devil and","thee about thy soul, that thou soldest him on Good-Friday last","for a cup of Madeira and a cold capon's leg?"],"id":51,"m":"Good morning, sweet Hal. What does Monsieur Remorse say? What does Sir John Sherry-and-Sugar say? Jack, how are you and the Devil getting on over your soul, which you sold him last Good Friday for a cup of Madeira and a cold chicken leg?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Sir John stands to his word,—the Devil shall have his bargain;","for he was never yet a breaker of proverbs,—he will give the","Devil his due."],"id":52,"m":"Sir John keeps his word: the Devil will have his bargain. He has never broken a proverb, so he will give the Devil his due."},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["Then art thou damn'd for keeping thy word with the Devil."],"id":53,"m":"Then you're damned for keeping your word to the Devil."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Else he had been damn'd for cozening the Devil."],"id":54,"m":"Otherwise he'd be damned for cheating the Devil."},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["But, my lads, my lads, to-morrow morning, by four o'clock,","early at Gads-hill! there are pilgrims gong to Canterbury","with rich offerings, and traders riding to London with fat","purses: I have visards for you all; you have horses for","yourselves: Gadshill lies to-night in Rochester: I have bespoke","supper to-morrow night in Eastcheap: we may do it as secure as","sleep. If you will go, I will stuff your purses full of crowns;","if you will not, tarry at home and be hang'd."],"id":55,"m":"But, lads, lads, tomorrow morning, by four o'clock, early, at Gad's Hill! There are pilgrims going to Canterbury with rich offerings and traders riding to London with fat purses. I have masks for you all; you have your own horses. Gadshill is staying in Rochester tonight. I have ordered supper for tomorrow night in Eastcheap. We can do it as safely as sleeping. If you come, I'll stuff your purses full of crowns; if you won't, stay home and be hanged."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Hear ye, Yedward; if I tarry at home and go not, I'll hang you","for going."],"id":56,"m":"Listen, Edward, if I stay home and don't go, I'll have you hanged for going."},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["You will, chops?"],"id":57,"m":"Will you, fat-face?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Hal, wilt thou make one?"],"id":58,"m":"Hal, will you be one of us?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Who, I rob? I a thief? not I, by my faith."],"id":59,"m":"Who, me rob? Me a thief? Not I, I swear."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["There's neither honesty, manhood, nor good fellowship in thee,","nor thou camest not of the blood royal, if thou darest not stand","for ten shillings."],"id":60,"m":"There's no honesty, courage or friendship in you, and you're not of royal blood, if you don't dare to stand up for ten shillings."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Well, then, once in my days I'll be a madcap."],"id":61,"m":"Well then, once in my life I'll be a wild one."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Why, that's well said."],"id":62,"m":"Well, that's well said."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Well, come what will, I'll tarry at home."],"id":63,"m":"Well, come what may, I'll stay at home."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["By the Lord, I'll be a traitor, then, when thou art king."],"id":64,"m":"By God, then I'll be a traitor when you're king."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["I care not."],"id":65,"m":"I don't care."},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["Sir John, I pr'ythee, leave the Prince and me alone: I will","lay him down such reasons for this adventure, that he shall go."],"id":66,"m":"Sir John, please leave the Prince and me alone. I'll give him such reasons for this adventure that he'll come."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Well, God give thee the spirit of persuasion, and him the ears","of profiting, that what thou speakest may move, and what he","hears may be believed, that the true Prince may, for recreation-","sake, prove a false thief; for the poor abuses of the time want","countenance. Farewell; you shall find me in Eastcheap."],"id":67,"m":"Well, may God give you the gift of persuasion and him ears that profit from it, so that what you say may move him and what he hears may be believed, so that the true Prince may, for fun's sake, turn out to be a false thief, since the petty crimes of these times need someone important to back them. Farewell; you'll find me in Eastcheap."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Farewell, thou latter Spring! farewell, All-hallown Summer!"],"id":68,"m":"Farewell, you late spring! Farewell, you Indian summer!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Falstaff.","id":69},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["Now, my good sweet honey-lord, ride with us to-morrow: I","have a jest to execute that I cannot manage alone. Falstaff,","Bardolph, Peto, and Gadshill, shall rob those men that we have","already waylaid: yourself and I will not be there; and when they","have the booty, if you and I do not rob them, cut this head off","from my shoulders."],"id":70,"m":"Now, my good sweet honey-lord, ride with us tomorrow. I have a joke to play that I can't manage alone. Falstaff, Bardolph, Peto and Gadshill will rob those men we've already set up as targets. You and I won't be there, and once they have the loot, if you and I don't rob them, cut this head off my shoulders."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["But how shall we part with them in setting forth?"],"id":71,"m":"But how shall we get away from them when we set out?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["Why, we will set forth before or after them, and appoint them","a place of meeting, wherein it is at our pleasure to fail; and","then will they adventure upon the exploit themselves; which they","shall have no sooner achieved but we'll set upon them."],"id":72,"m":"Why, we'll set out before or after them and agree a meeting place, where we can choose not to turn up. Then they'll go ahead with the robbery by themselves, and as soon as they've done it, we'll attack them."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Ay, but 'tis like that they will know us by our horses, by our","habits, and by every other appointment, to be ourselves."],"id":73,"m":"Yes, but they're likely to recognise us by our horses, our clothes and everything else we have."},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["Tut! our horses they shall not see,—I'll tie them in the wood;","our visards we will change, after we leave them; and, sirrah, I","have cases of buckram for the nonce, to immask our noted","outward garments."],"id":74,"m":"Nonsense! They won't see our horses, I'll tie them up in the wood. We'll change our masks after we leave them, and, my friend, I have overalls of coarse cloth ready for the purpose to cover our well-known clothes."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["But I doubt they will be too hard for us."],"id":75,"m":"But I'm afraid they'll be too strong for us."},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["Well, for two of them, I know them to be as true-bred","cowards as ever turn'd back; and for the third, if he fight","longer than he sees reason, I'll forswear arms. The virtue of","this jest will be, the incomprehensible lies that this same fat","rogue will tell us when we meet at supper: how thirty, at least,","he fought with; what wards, what blows, what extremities he","endured; and in the reproof of this lies the jest."],"id":76,"m":"Well, two of them I know to be as thorough-bred cowards as ever ran away, and as for the third, if he fights longer than he thinks sensible, I'll give up fighting for good. The beauty of this joke will be the huge lies this fat rogue will tell us when we meet at supper: how he fought at least thirty men, what defensive moves, what blows, what dangers he went through. And the joke lies in exposing those lies."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Well, I'll go with thee: provide us all things necessary and","meet me to-night in Eastcheap; there I'll sup. Farewell."],"id":77,"m":"Well, I'll go with you. Get everything we need and meet me tonight in Eastcheap; I'll have supper there. Farewell."},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["Farewell, my lord."],"id":78,"m":"Farewell, my lord."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":79},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["I know you all, and will awhile uphold","The unyok'd humour of your idleness:","Yet herein will I imitate the Sun,","Who doth permit the base contagious clouds","To smother-up his beauty from the world,","That, when he please again to be himself,","Being wanted, he may be more wonder'd at,","By breaking through the foul and ugly mists","Of vapours that did seem to strangle him.","If all the year were playing holidays,","To sport would be as tedious as to work;","But, when they seldom come, they wish'd-for come,","And nothing pleaseth but rare accidents.","So, when this loose behaviour I throw off,","And pay the debt I never promised,","By how much better than my word I am,","By so much shall I falsify men's hopes;","And, like bright metal on a sullen ground,","My reformation, glittering o'er my fault,","Shall show more goodly and attract more eyes","Than that which hath no foil to set it off.","I'll so offend, to make offence a skill;","Redeeming time, when men think least I will."],"id":80,"m":"I know you all, and for a while I'll go along with your unrestrained, idle ways. But in this I'll copy the sun, which lets the low, polluting clouds hide its beauty from the world, so that when it chooses to be itself again, being missed, it may be wondered at all the more for breaking through the foul and ugly mists that seemed to strangle it. If every day of the year were a holiday, playing would be as boring as working; but because holidays come rarely, they are longed for, and nothing pleases like the unusual. So, when I throw off this loose behaviour and pay the debt I never promised to pay, the better I turn out than people expected, the more I will prove their expectations wrong. And like bright metal against a dull background, my reformation, shining over my faults, will look finer and attract more attention than goodness that has nothing to set it off. I'll do wrong in such a way that I make wrongdoing into a clever tactic, making up for lost time when people least expect me to."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":81},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"III","loc":"London. A room in the palace.","id":82},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter King Henry, Northumberland, Worcester, Hotspur, Sir Walter Blunt, and others.","id":83},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["My blood hath been too cold and temperate,","Unapt to stir at these indignities,","And you have found me; for, accordingly,","You tread upon my patience: but be sure","I will from henceforth rather be myself,","Mighty and to be fear'd, than my condition,","Which hath been smooth as oil, soft as young down,","And therefore lost that title of respect","Which the proud soul ne'er pays but to the proud."],"id":84,"m":"My temper has been too cold and mild, slow to react to these insults, and you have found that out; and so you walk all over my patience. But be sure that from now on I'll be what I truly am, a mighty king to be feared, rather than follow my natural disposition, which has been as smooth as oil and soft as a chick's down, and so has lost the respect that proud men only ever pay to the proud."},{"t":"sp","who":"WORCESTER","lines":["Our House, my sovereign liege, little deserves","The scourge of greatness to be used on it;","And that same greatness too which our own hands","Have holp to make so portly."],"id":85,"m":"Our family, my sovereign lord, hardly deserves to have the whip of royal power used on it, and especially not that same power which our own hands helped to make so great."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["My good lord,—"],"id":86,"m":"My good lord,"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Worcester, get thee gone; for I do see","Danger and disobedience in thine eye:","O, sir, your presence is too bold and peremptory,","And majesty might never yet endure","The moody frontier of a servant brow.","You have good leave to leave us: when we need","Your use and counsel, we shall send for you."],"id":87,"m":"Worcester, go away, because I see danger and disobedience in your eyes. Oh, sir, your manner is too bold and overbearing, and royalty has never put up with a servant's sullen, frowning face. You have my permission to leave us; when we need your help and advice, we'll send for you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Worcester.","id":88},{"t":"sd","text":"To Northumberland.","id":89},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","cont":1,"lines":["You were about to speak."],"id":90,"m":"You were about to speak."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["Yea, my good lord.","Those prisoners in your Highness' name demanded,","Which Harry Percy here at Holmedon took,","Were, as he says, not with such strength denied","As is deliver'd to your Majesty:","Either envy, therefore, or misprision","Is guilty of this fault, and not my son."],"id":91,"m":"Yes, my good lord. Those prisoners demanded in your Highness's name, which Harry Percy here took at Holmedon, were not, he says, refused as firmly as you were told. So either malice or a misunderstanding is to blame for this offence, not my son."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["My liege, I did deny no prisoners.","But, I remember, when the fight was done,","When I was dry with rage and extreme toil,","Breathless and faint, leaning upon my sword,","Came there a certain lord, neat, trimly dress'd,","Fresh as a bridegroom; and his chin new reap'd","Show'd like a stubble-land at harvest-home:","He was perfumed like a milliner;","And 'twixt his finger and his thumb he held","A pouncet-box, which ever and anon","He gave his nose, and took't away again;","Who therewith angry, when it next came there,","Took it in snuff: and still he smiled and talk'd;","And, as the soldiers bore dead bodies by,","He call'd them untaught knaves, unmannerly,","To bring a slovenly unhandsome corse","Betwixt the wind and his nobility.","With many holiday and lady terms","He question'd me; amongst the rest, demanded","My prisoners in your Majesty's behalf.","I then, all smarting with my wounds being cold,","Out of my grief and my impatience","To be so pester'd with a popinjay,","Answer'd neglectingly, I know not what,—","He should, or he should not; for't made me mad","To see him shine so brisk, and smell so sweet,","And talk so like a waiting-gentlewoman","Of guns and drums and wounds,—God save the mark!—","And telling me the sovereign'st thing on Earth","Was parmaceti for an inward bruise;","And that it was great pity, so it was,","This villainous salt-petre should be digg'd","Out of the bowels of the harmless earth,","Which many a good tall fellow had destroy'd","So cowardly; and, but for these vile guns,","He would himself have been a soldier.","This bald unjointed chat of his, my lord,","I answered indirectly, as I said;","And I beseech you, let not his report","Come current for an accusation","Betwixt my love and your high Majesty."],"id":92,"m":"My lord, I refused no prisoners. But I remember that when the fighting was over, when I was parched with rage and exhaustion, breathless and faint and leaning on my sword, a certain lord came along, neat and smartly dressed, as fresh as a bridegroom, his chin newly shaved so that it looked like a field of stubble after the harvest. He was perfumed like a dealer in fancy goods, and between his finger and thumb he held a little perfume box, which every so often he lifted to his nose and took away again, and the nose, offended, sniffed it up angrily when it came back. And all the time he smiled and chattered. When the soldiers carried dead bodies past, he called them ignorant, ill-mannered rascals for bringing a dirty, ugly corpse between the wind and his noble self. He questioned me in fancy, ladylike language, and among other things demanded my prisoners on your Majesty's behalf. Then I, smarting all over as my wounds cooled, in my pain and irritation at being pestered by this parrot, answered carelessly, I don't know what: he should have them, or he shouldn't. It made me mad to see him shine so smartly and smell so sweet, and talk like a lady's maid about guns and drums and wounds (God forgive the comparison!), telling me that the best thing on earth for an internal bruise was whale ointment, and that it was a great pity, so it was, that this wicked gunpowder should be dug out of the harmless earth, when it had killed many a fine brave fellow in such a cowardly way; and that, if it weren't for these awful guns, he'd have been a soldier himself. I answered his feeble, disjointed chatter offhandedly, my lord, as I said. And I beg you, don't let his report be accepted as an accusation that comes between my loyalty and your Majesty."},{"t":"sp","who":"BLUNT","lines":["The circumstance consider'd, good my lord,","Whatever Harry Percy then had said","To such a person, and in such a place,","At such a time, with all the rest re-told,","May reasonably die, and never rise","To do him wrong, or any way impeach","What then he said, so he unsay it now."],"id":93,"m":"Considering the circumstances, my good lord, whatever Harry Percy said to such a person, in such a place, at such a time, along with everything else reported, can reasonably be allowed to die and never rise again to harm him or to be used against him, as long as he takes it back now."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Why, yet he doth deny his prisoners,","But with proviso and exception,","That we at our own charge shall ransom straight","His brother-in-law, the foolish Mortimer;","Who, on my soul, hath wilfully betray'd","The lives of those that he did lead to fight","Against that great magician, damn'd Glendower,","Whose daughter, as we hear, the Earl of March","Hath lately married. Shall our coffers, then,","Be emptied to redeem a traitor home?","Shall we buy treason? and indent with fears","When they have lost and forfeited themselves?","No, on the barren mountains let him starve;","For I shall never hold that man my friend","Whose tongue shall ask me for one penny cost","To ransom home revolted Mortimer."],"id":94,"m":"Why, he still refuses to hand over his prisoners, except on the condition that we pay the ransom at our own expense for his brother-in-law, that foolish Mortimer, who, I swear, deliberately threw away the lives of the men he led against that great magician, the damned Glendower, whose daughter, we hear, the Earl of March has recently married. Should our treasury be emptied to bring a traitor home? Should we pay for treason, and strike bargains with cowards who have lost themselves and their rights? No, let him starve on the barren mountains, because I will never consider any man my friend who asks me to spend one penny to ransom the rebel Mortimer."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["Revolted Mortimer!","He never did fall off, my sovereign liege,","But by the chance of war: to prove that true","Needs no more but one tongue for all those wounds,","Those mouthed wounds, which valiantly he took,","When on the gentle Severn's sedgy bank,","In single opposition, hand to hand,","He did confound the best part of an hour","In changing hardiment with great Glendower.","Three times they breathed, and three times did they drink,","Upon agreement, of swift Severn's flood;","Who then, affrighted with their bloody looks,","Ran fearfully among the trembling reeds,","And hid his crisp head in the hollow bank","Blood-stained with these valiant combatants.","Never did base and rotten policy","Colour her working with such deadly wounds;","Nor never could the noble Mortimer","Receive so many, and all willingly:","Then let not him be slander'd with revolt."],"id":95,"m":"Rebel Mortimer! He never turned against you, my sovereign lord, except by the fortune of war. To prove that, you need only one tongue for all those wounds, those gaping mouths of wounds, which he bravely received when, on the reedy bank of the gentle river Severn, in single combat, hand to hand, he spent the best part of an hour trading blows with the great Glendower. Three times they paused for breath, and three times, by agreement, they drank from the swift Severn, which, frightened by their bloody looks, ran fearfully among the trembling reeds and hid its curly-waved head in the hollow bank, stained with the blood of these brave fighters. Never did low and rotten scheming disguise itself with such deadly wounds, nor could the noble Mortimer have taken so many, and all willingly, if he were a traitor. So don't let him be slandered as a rebel."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Thou dost belie him, Percy, thou dost belie him;","He never did encounter with Glendower:","I tell thee,","He durst as well have met the Devil alone","As Owen Glendower for an enemy.","Art not ashamed? But, sirrah, henceforth","Let me not hear you speak of Mortimer:","Send me your prisoners with the speediest means,","Or you shall hear in such a kind from me","As will displease you.—My Lord Northumberland,","We license your departure with your son.—","Send us your prisoners, or you'll hear of it."],"id":96,"m":"You're lying about him, Percy, you're lying about him. He never fought Glendower. I tell you, he would as soon have dared to face the Devil alone as have Owen Glendower for an enemy. Aren't you ashamed? But, sir, from now on don't let me hear you talk about Mortimer. Send me your prisoners as fast as possible, or you'll hear from me in a way you won't like. My Lord Northumberland, you have permission to leave, with your son. Send us your prisoners, or you'll hear about it."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt King Henry, Blunt, and train.","id":97},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["An if the Devil come and roar for them,","I will not send them: I will after straight,","And tell him so; for I will else my heart,","Although it be with hazard of my head."],"id":98,"m":"Even if the Devil comes and roars for them, I won't send them. I'll go after him at once and tell him so, because I must relieve my feelings, even if it costs me my head."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["What, drunk with choler? stay, and pause awhile:","Here comes your uncle."],"id":99,"m":"What, drunk with anger? Wait, stop for a moment. Here comes your uncle."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Worcester.","id":100},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["Speak of Mortimer!","Zounds, I will speak of him; and let my soul","Want mercy, if I do not join with him:","Yea, on his part I'll empty all these veins,","And shed my dear blood drop by drop i' the dust,","But I will lift the down-trod Mortimer","As high i' the air as this unthankful King,","As this ingrate and canker'd Bolingbroke."],"id":101,"m":"Speak of Mortimer! By God's wounds, I will speak of him, and may my soul be denied mercy if I don't join forces with him. Yes, for his sake I'll empty all these veins and spill my precious blood drop by drop in the dust, but I'll raise the trampled Mortimer as high as this ungrateful King, this ungrateful and poisonous Bolingbroke."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["[To Worcester] Brother, the King hath made your nephew mad."],"id":102,"m":"Brother, the King has driven your nephew mad."},{"t":"sp","who":"WORCESTER","lines":["Who struck this heat up after I was gone?"],"id":103,"m":"Who stirred up this fury after I left?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["He will, forsooth, have all my prisoners;","And when I urged the ransom once again","Of my wife's brother, then his cheek look'd pale,","And on my face he turn'd an eye of death,","Trembling even at the name of Mortimer."],"id":104,"m":"He wants, if you please, all my prisoners. And when I pressed again for the ransom of my wife's brother, his cheek turned pale and he gave me a deadly look, trembling at the very name of Mortimer."},{"t":"sp","who":"WORCESTER","lines":["I cannot blame him: was not he proclaim'd","By Richard that dead is the next of blood?"],"id":105,"m":"I can't blame him. Wasn't Mortimer proclaimed next in line to the throne by Richard, who is now dead?"},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["He was; I heard the proclamation:","And then it was when the unhappy King—","Whose wrongs in us God pardon!—did set forth","Upon his Irish expedition;","From whence he intercepted did return","To be deposed, and shortly murdered."],"id":106,"m":"He was; I heard the proclamation. It was when the unlucky King (God pardon us for the wrongs we did him!) set off on his Irish expedition, from which he was called back to be deposed and soon afterwards murdered."},{"t":"sp","who":"WORCESTER","lines":["And for whose death we in the world's wide mouth","Live scandalized and foully spoken of."],"id":107,"m":"And because of his death the whole world gossips about us, and we are disgraced and spoken of with contempt."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["But, soft! I pray you; did King Richard then","Proclaim my brother Edmund Mortimer","Heir to the crown?"],"id":108,"m":"But wait, please. Did King Richard really proclaim my brother-in-law Edmund Mortimer heir to the crown?"},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["He did; myself did hear it."],"id":109,"m":"He did; I heard it myself."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["Nay, then I cannot blame his cousin King,","That wish'd him on the barren mountains starve.","But shall it be, that you, that set the crown","Upon the head of this forgetful man,","And for his sake wear the detested blot","Of murderous subornation,—shall it be,","That you a world of curses undergo,","Being the agents, or base second means,","The cords, the ladder, or the hangman rather?—","O, pardon me, that I descend so low,","To show the line and the predicament","Wherein you range under this subtle King;—","Shall it, for shame, be spoken in these days,","Or fill up chronicles in time to come,","That men of your nobility and power","Did gage them both in an unjust behalf,—","As both of you, God pardon it! have done,—","To put down Richard, that sweet lovely rose,","And plant this thorn, this canker, Bolingbroke?","And shall it, in more shame, be further spoken,","That you are fool'd, discarded, and shook off","By him for whom these shames ye underwent?","No! yet time serves, wherein you may redeem","Your banish'd honours, and restore yourselves","Into the good thoughts of the world again;","Revenge the jeering and disdain'd contempt","Of this proud King, who studies day and night","To answer all the debt he owes to you","Even with the bloody payment of your deaths:","Therefore, I say,—"],"id":110,"m":"Well then, I can't blame his cousin the King for wishing him to starve on the barren mountains. But is it to be that you, who put the crown on the head of this ungrateful man and for his sake bear the hateful stain of being accessories to murder, is it to be that you suffer a world of curses as his agents, his low instruments, the ropes, the ladder, or rather the hangman? Oh, forgive me for sinking so low to show the rank and position you hold under this cunning King. Shall it be said in these times, to your shame, or written in history books to come, that men of your nobility and power pledged both to an unjust cause, as you both did, God forgive you, to put down Richard, that sweet lovely rose, and plant this thorn, this canker, Bolingbroke? And shall it be said, to your even greater shame, that you are fooled, thrown away and shaken off by the very man for whom you suffered these disgraces? No! There is still time for you to win back your banished honour and restore yourselves to the world's good opinion. Take revenge on the mocking, scornful contempt of this proud King, who thinks day and night about how to repay all he owes you with the bloody payment of your deaths. So, I say,"},{"t":"sp","who":"WORCESTER","lines":["Peace, cousin, say no more:","And now I will unclasp a secret book,","And to your quick-conceiving discontent","I'll read you matter deep and dangerous;","As full of peril and adventurous spirit","As to o'er-walk a current roaring loud","On the unsteadfast footing of a spear."],"id":111,"m":"Quiet, nephew, say no more. Now I'll open a secret book, and to your quick and angry mind I'll read out matters deep and dangerous, as full of danger and daring as walking across a roaring torrent on the shaky footing of a spear."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["If we fall in, good night, or sink or swim!","Send danger from the east unto the west,","So honour cross it from the north to south,","And let them grapple. O, the blood more stirs","To rouse a lion than to start a hare!"],"id":112,"m":"If we fall in, goodnight, whether we sink or swim! Let danger run from east to west, as long as honour crosses it from north to south, and let them wrestle. Oh, the blood is stirred more by rousing a lion than by startling a hare!"},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["Imagination of some great exploit","Drives him beyond the bounds of patience."],"id":113,"m":"The thought of some great adventure is driving him beyond all patience."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["By Heaven, methinks it were an easy leap,","To pluck bright honour from the pale-faced Moon;","Or dive into the bottom of the deep,","Where fathom-line could never touch the ground,","And pluck up drowned honour by the locks;","So he that doth redeem her thence might wear","Without corrival all her dignities:","But out upon this half-faced fellowship!"],"id":114,"m":"By heaven, it seems to me it would be an easy leap to pluck bright honour from the pale-faced moon, or to dive to the bottom of the sea, where no measuring line could ever reach the bed, and pull drowned honour up by her hair, so that the man who rescued her could wear all her glories without a rival. But away with this sharing of glory!"},{"t":"sp","who":"WORCESTER","lines":["He apprehends a world of figures here,","But not the form of what he should attend.—","Good cousin, give me audience for a while."],"id":115,"m":"He imagines a world of fancies here, but not the real business he should be attending to. Good nephew, listen to me for a while."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["I cry you mercy."],"id":116,"m":"I beg your pardon."},{"t":"sp","who":"WORCESTER","lines":["Those same noble Scots","That are your prisoners,—"],"id":117,"m":"Those noble Scots who are your prisoners,"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["I'll keep them all;","By God, he shall not have a Scot of them;","No, if a Scot would save his soul, he shall not:","I'll keep them, by this hand."],"id":118,"m":"I'll keep them all. By God, he won't have a single Scot of them. No, not if a Scot could save his soul by it, he won't. I'll keep them, by this hand."},{"t":"sp","who":"WORCESTER","lines":["You start away,","And lend no ear unto my purposes.","Those prisoners you shall keep;—"],"id":119,"m":"You fly off and don't listen to what I'm proposing. You will keep those prisoners;"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["Nay, I will; that's flat.","He said he would not ransom Mortimer;","Forbade my tongue to speak of Mortimer;","But I will find him when he lies asleep,","And in his ear I'll holla Mortimer!","Nay,","I'll have a starling shall be taught to speak","Nothing but Mortimer, and give it him,","To keep his anger still in motion."],"id":120,"m":"Yes, I will, and that's final. He said he wouldn't ransom Mortimer, forbade me even to speak of Mortimer, but I'll find him when he's asleep and shout \"Mortimer!\" in his ear. No, I'll get a starling taught to say nothing but \"Mortimer\" and give it to him, to keep his anger always on the boil."},{"t":"sp","who":"WORCESTER","lines":["Hear you, cousin; a word."],"id":121,"m":"Listen, nephew, a word."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["All studies here I solemnly defy,","Save how to gall and pinch this Bolingbroke:","And that same sword-and-buckler Prince of Wales,","But that I think his father loves him not,","And would be glad he met with some mischance,","I'd have him poison'd with a pot of ale."],"id":122,"m":"I solemnly reject every pursuit here except how to annoy and torment this Bolingbroke. And that brawling Prince of Wales, if I didn't think his father doesn't love him and would be glad if some accident befell him, I'd have him poisoned with a pot of ale."},{"t":"sp","who":"WORCESTER","lines":["Farewell, kinsman: I will talk to you","When you are better temper'd to attend."],"id":123,"m":"Goodbye, nephew. I'll talk to you when you're in a better mood to listen."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["Why, what a wasp-stung and impatient fool","Art thou, to break into this woman's mood,","Tying thine ear to no tongue but thine own!"],"id":124,"m":"Why, what an impatient fool you are, like a man stung by a wasp, to fly into this womanish temper, listening to no tongue but your own!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["Why, look you, I am whipp'd and scourged with rods,","Nettled, and stung with pismires, when I hear","Of this vile politician, Bolingbroke.","In Richard's time,—what do you call the place?—","A plague upon't!—it is in Gloucestershire;—","'Twas where the madcap Duke his uncle kept,","His uncle York;—where I first bow'd my knee","Unto this king of smiles, this Bolingbroke;—","When you and he came back from Ravenspurg."],"id":125,"m":"Well, look, I feel whipped and beaten with rods, stung by nettles and bitten by ants, when I hear about this vile schemer Bolingbroke. In Richard's time, what do you call the place? Plague take it! It's in Gloucestershire. It was where that mad Duke his uncle lived, his uncle York, where I first bowed my knee to this king of smiles, this Bolingbroke, when you and he came back from Ravenspurgh."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["At Berkeley-castle."],"id":126,"m":"At Berkeley Castle."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["You say true:—","Why, what a candy deal of courtesy","This fawning greyhound then did proffer me!","Look, when his infant fortune came to age,","And, Gentle Harry Percy, and kind cousin,—","O, the Devil take such cozeners!—God forgive me!—","Good uncle, tell your tale; for I have done."],"id":127,"m":"You're right. Why, what a sugary load of courtesy this fawning greyhound offered me then! \"Look, when my infant fortune comes of age,\" and \"Gentle Harry Percy,\" and \"Kind cousin.\" Oh, the Devil take such cheats! God forgive me! Good uncle, tell your story, I've finished."},{"t":"sp","who":"WORCESTER","lines":["Nay, if you have not, to't again;","We'll stay your leisure."],"id":128,"m":"No, if you haven't, go on again. We'll wait until you're ready."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["I have done, i'faith."],"id":129,"m":"I've finished, really."},{"t":"sp","who":"WORCESTER","lines":["Then once more to your Scottish prisoners.","Deliver them up without their ransom straight,","And make the Douglas' son your only mean","For powers in Scotland; which, for divers reasons","Which I shall send you written, be assured,","Will easily be granted.—","[To Northumberland] You, my lord,","Your son in Scotland being thus employ'd,","Shall secretly into the bosom creep","Of that same noble prelate, well beloved,","Th' Archbishop."],"id":130,"m":"Then back to your Scottish prisoners. Hand them over at once without ransom, and use Douglas's son as your only go-between for raising forces in Scotland, which, for various reasons I'll send you in writing, I assure you will easily be granted. You, my lord, while your son is busy in Scotland like this, will secretly worm your way into the confidence of that noble and well-loved churchman, the Archbishop."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["Of York, is't not?"],"id":131,"m":"Of York, isn't it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WORCESTER","lines":["True; who bears hard","His brother's death at Bristol, the Lord Scroop.","I speak not this in estimation,","As what I think might be, but what I know","Is ruminated, plotted, and set down,","And only stays but to behold the face","Of that occasion that shall bring it on."],"id":132,"m":"Yes, who resents his brother Lord Scroop's death at Bristol. I don't say this as a guess about what might happen, but as something I know has been considered, planned and written down, and only waits to see the right moment to bring it about."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["I smell't: upon my life, it will do well."],"id":133,"m":"I can smell it. On my life, it will work well."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["Before the game's a-foot, thou still lett'st slip."],"id":134,"m":"You always let the hounds loose before the hunt has started."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["Why, it cannot choose but be a noble plot:—","And then the power of Scotland and of York","To join with Mortimer, ha?"],"id":135,"m":"Why, it can't help being a noble plot. And then the forces of Scotland and of York join with Mortimer, eh?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WORCESTER","lines":["And so they shall."],"id":136,"m":"And so they will."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["In faith, it is exceedingly well aim'd."],"id":137,"m":"Indeed, it's extremely well aimed."},{"t":"sp","who":"WORCESTER","lines":["And 'tis no little reason bids us speed,","To save our heads by raising of a head;","For, bear ourselves as even as we can,","The King will always think him in our debt,","And think we think ourselves unsatisfied,","Till he hath found a time to pay us home:","And see already how he doth begin","To make us strangers to his looks of love."],"id":138,"m":"And there's good reason for us to hurry, to save our heads by raising an army. However carefully we behave, the King will always think he's in our debt, and think that we think ourselves unrewarded, until he finds a chance to pay us off for good. And look how he's already beginning to withhold his friendly looks from us."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["He does, he does: we'll be revenged on him."],"id":139,"m":"He is, he is. We'll have revenge on him."},{"t":"sp","who":"WORCESTER","lines":["Cousin, farewell: no further go in this","Than I by letters shall direct your course.","When time is ripe,—which will be suddenly,—","I'll steal to Glendower and Lord Mortimer;","Where you and Douglas, and our powers at once,","As I will fashion it, shall happily meet,","To bear our fortunes in our own strong arms,","Which now we hold at much uncertainty."],"id":140,"m":"Nephew, goodbye. Go no further in this than I direct you in my letters. When the time is ripe, which will be soon, I'll slip away to Glendower and Lord Mortimer, where you and Douglas and all our forces will meet together, as I'll arrange it, to hold our fortunes in our own strong arms, which at present are very uncertain."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["Farewell, good brother: we shall thrive, I trust."],"id":141,"m":"Goodbye, good brother. We will succeed, I trust."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["Uncle, adieu: O, let the hours be short,","Till fields and blows and groans applaud our sport!"],"id":142,"m":"Uncle, goodbye. Oh, let the hours be short until battlefields, blows and groans applaud our game!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":143},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"I","loc":"Rochester. An Inn-Yard.","id":144},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Carrier with a lantern in his hand.","id":145},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CARRIER","lines":["Heigh-ho! an't be not four by the day, I'll be hang'd:","Charles' wain is over the new chimney, and yet our horse' not","pack'd.—What, ostler!"],"id":146,"m":"Heigh-ho! If it isn't four in the morning, I'll be hanged. The Plough constellation is over the new chimney, and our horse still isn't loaded. Hey, stable-boy!"},{"t":"sp","who":"OSTLER","lines":["[within] Anon, anon."],"id":147,"m":"Coming, coming."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CARRIER","lines":["I pr'ythee, Tom, beat Cut's saddle, put a few flocks in the","point; the poor jade is wrung in the withers out of all cess."],"id":148,"m":"Please, Tom, soften Cut's saddle and put a bit of padding under the pommel. The poor nag's shoulders are rubbed raw beyond all measure."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter another Carrier.","id":149},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND CARRIER","lines":["Peas and beans are as dank here as a dog, and that is the","next way to give poor jades the bots; this house is turned","upside down since Robin ostler died."],"id":150,"m":"The peas and beans here are as damp as a dog, and that's the quickest way to give poor horses worms. This inn has gone to pieces since Robin the stable-boy died."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CARRIER","lines":["Poor fellow! never joyed since the price of oats rose; it was","the death of him."],"id":151,"m":"Poor man! He was never happy after the price of oats went up; it was the death of him."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND CARRIER","lines":["I think this be the most villainous house in all London road","for fleas: I am stung like a tench."],"id":152,"m":"I think this is the worst inn on the whole London road for fleas. I've been bitten so much I'm spotted like a tench."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CARRIER","lines":["Like a tench! by the Mass, there is ne'er a king in Christendom","could be better bit than I have been since the first cock.—What,","ostler! come away and be hang'd; come away."],"id":153,"m":"Like a tench! By the Mass, no king in Christendom could have been better bitten than I've been since the first cock crowed. Hey, stable-boy! Come on, and be hanged, come on."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND CARRIER","lines":["I have a gammon of bacon and two razes of ginger, to be","delivered as far as Charing-cross."],"id":154,"m":"I have a leg of bacon and two roots of ginger to be delivered all the way to Charing Cross."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CARRIER","lines":["'Odsbody! the turkeys in my pannier are quite starved.—What,","ostler! A plague on thee! hast thou never an eye in thy head?","canst not hear? An 'twere not as good a deed as drink to break","the pate of thee, I am a very villain. Come, and be hang'd:","hast no faith in thee?"],"id":155,"m":"God's body! The turkeys in my basket are half starved. Hey, stable-boy! Plague on you! Haven't you got eyes in your head? Can't you hear? If cracking your skull wouldn't be as good a deed as having a drink, I'm a real villain. Come on, and be hanged! Have you no honesty in you?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Gadshill.","id":156},{"t":"sp","who":"GADSHILL","lines":["Good morrow, carriers. What's o'clock?"],"id":157,"m":"Good morning, carriers. What time is it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CARRIER","lines":["I think it be two o'clock."],"id":158,"m":"I think it's two o'clock."},{"t":"sp","who":"GADSHILL","lines":["I pr'ythee, lend me thy lantern, to see my gelding in the","stable."],"id":159,"m":"Please lend me your lantern so I can look at my horse in the stable."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CARRIER","lines":["Nay, soft, I pray ye; I know a trick worth two of that, i'faith."],"id":160,"m":"No, wait a minute. I know a trick worth two of that, I swear."},{"t":"sp","who":"GADSHILL","lines":["I pr'ythee, lend me thine."],"id":161,"m":"Then please lend me yours."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND CARRIER","lines":["Ay, when? canst tell? Lend me thy lantern, quoth a? marry, I'll","see thee hang'd first."],"id":162,"m":"Yes, when? Can you say? \"Lend me your lantern,\" he says! By God, I'll see you hanged first."},{"t":"sp","who":"GADSHILL","lines":["Sirrah carrier, what time do you mean to come to London?"],"id":163,"m":"Fellow carrier, what time do you mean to reach London?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND CARRIER","lines":["Time enough to go to bed with a candle, I warrant thee.—","Come, neighbour Muggs, we'll call up the gentlemen: they will","along with company, for they have great charge."],"id":164,"m":"In good time to go to bed by candlelight, I promise you. Come on, neighbour Muggs, we'll wake the gentlemen. They want to travel in company, because they're carrying a lot of valuables."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Carriers.","id":165},{"t":"sp","who":"GADSHILL","lines":["What, ho! chamberlain!"],"id":166,"m":"Hey there! Chamberlain!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CHAMBERLAIN","lines":["[Within] At hand, quoth pick-purse."],"id":167,"m":"\"Right here,\" said the pickpocket."},{"t":"sp","who":"GADSHILL","lines":["That's even as fair as—at hand, quoth the chamberlain; for","thou variest no more from picking of purses than giving","direction doth from labouring; thou lay'st the plot how."],"id":168,"m":"That's as good as \"Right here,\" said the chamberlain, because you're no different from a pickpocket, just as giving orders is no different from doing the work: you plan how it's done."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Chamberlain.","id":169},{"t":"sp","who":"CHAMBERLAIN","lines":["Good morrow, Master Gadshill. It holds current that I told","you yesternight: there's a franklin in the wild of Kent hath","brought three hundred marks with him in gold: I heard him","tell it to one of his company last night at supper; a kind of","auditor; one that hath abundance of charge too, God knows what.","They are up already, and call for eggs and butter; they will away","presently."],"id":170,"m":"Good morning, Master Gadshill. What I told you last night still holds. There's a rich farmer from the Weald of Kent who has brought three hundred marks in gold with him. I heard him tell one of his companions at supper last night, a sort of accountant, who's carrying plenty of valuables too, God knows what. They're up already and calling for eggs and butter; they'll be off soon."},{"t":"sp","who":"GADSHILL","lines":["Sirrah, if they meet not with Saint Nicholas' clerks, I'll give","thee this neck."],"id":171,"m":"Friend, if they don't meet up with highwaymen, you can have my neck."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHAMBERLAIN","lines":["No, I'll none of it: I pr'ythee, keep that for the hangman; for","I know thou worshippest Saint Nicholas as truly as a man of","falsehood may."],"id":172,"m":"No, I don't want it. Please save it for the hangman, because I know you worship Saint Nicholas, the patron of thieves, as faithfully as any dishonest man can."},{"t":"sp","who":"GADSHILL","lines":["What talkest thou to me of the hangman? if I hang, I'll make","a fat pair of gallows; for, if I hang, old Sir John hangs with","me, and thou know'st he is no starveling. Tut! there are other","Trojans that thou dreamest not of, the which, for sport-sake,","are content to do the profession some grace; that would, if","matters should be look'd into, for their own credit-sake, make","all whole. I am joined with no foot land-rakers, no long-staff","sixpenny strikers, none of these mad mustachio purple-hued","malt-worms; but with nobility and tranquillity, burgomasters and","great oneyers; such as can hold in, such as will strike sooner","than speak, and speak sooner than drink, and drink sooner than","pray: and yet, zwounds, I lie; for they pray continually to their","saint, the Commonwealth; or, rather, not pray to her, but prey on","her, for they ride up and down on her, and make her their boots."],"id":173,"m":"Why are you talking to me about the hangman? If I hang, I'll make a fine fat pair of gallows, because if I hang, old Sir John hangs with me, and you know he's no skinny wretch. Nonsense! There are other fine fellows you don't dream of, who for fun are happy to lend our trade some dignity, and who, if things were looked into, would put everything right to protect their own reputations. I don't keep company with footpads, with petty muggers armed with long staffs, or with those wild, moustached, purple-faced beer drinkers, but with nobles and quiet gentlemen, mayors and great ones, men who can keep their mouths shut, who will strike before they speak, and speak before they drink, and drink before they pray. And yet, by God's wounds, I'm lying, because they pray constantly to their saint, the Commonwealth; or rather, they don't pray to her but prey on her, because they ride up and down on her and use her as their boots."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHAMBERLAIN","lines":["What, the Commonwealth their boots? will she hold out water","in foul way?"],"id":174,"m":"What, they use the Commonwealth as their boots? Will she keep the water out on a muddy road?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GADSHILL","lines":["She will, she will; justice hath liquor'd her. We steal as in a","castle, cock-sure; we have the receipt of fernseed,—we walk","invisible."],"id":175,"m":"She will, she will. Justice has greased her well. We steal as safely as if we were in a castle. We have the recipe for fern-seed: we walk invisible."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHAMBERLAIN","lines":["Nay, by my faith, I think you are more beholding to the night","than to fern-seed for your walking invisible."],"id":176,"m":"No, I swear, I think you owe more to the darkness than to fern-seed for your walking invisible."},{"t":"sp","who":"GADSHILL","lines":["Give me thy hand: thou shalt have a share in our purchase, as","I am a true man."],"id":177,"m":"Give me your hand. You'll have a share of our loot, as I'm an honest man."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHAMBERLAIN","lines":["Nay, rather let me have it, as you are a false thief."],"id":178,"m":"No, I'd rather have it as you're a dishonest thief."},{"t":"sp","who":"GADSHILL","lines":["Go to; homo is a common name to all men. Bid the ostler","bring my gelding out of the stable. Farewell, you muddy knave."],"id":179,"m":"Get away! \"Man\" is a name common to everyone. Tell the stable-boy to bring my horse out of the stable. Goodbye, you dirty rascal."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":180},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"II","loc":"The Road by Gads-hill.","id":181},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Prince Henry and Pointz; Bardolph and Peto at some distance.","id":182},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["Come, shelter, shelter: I have remov'd Falstaff's horse,","and he frets like a gumm'd velvet."],"id":183,"m":"Come on, hide, hide! I've moved Falstaff's horse, and he's fretting like cheap velvet stiffened with gum."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Stand close."],"id":184,"m":"Keep out of sight."},{"t":"sd","text":"They retire.","id":185},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Falstaff.","id":186},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Pointz! Pointz, and be hang'd! Pointz!"],"id":187,"m":"Poins! Poins, and be hanged! Poins!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["[Coming forward] Peace, ye fat-kidney'd rascal! what a brawling dost thou keep!"],"id":188,"m":"Quiet, you fat-guts rascal! What a racket you're making!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Where's Pointz, Hal?"],"id":189,"m":"Where's Poins, Hal?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["He is walk'd up to the top of the hill: I'll go seek him."],"id":190,"m":"He's walked up to the top of the hill. I'll go and look for him."},{"t":"sd","text":"Retires.","id":191},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["I am accursed to rob in that thief's company: the rascal hath","removed my horse, and tied him I know not where. If I travel but","four foot by the squire further a-foot, I shall break my wind.","Well, I doubt not but to die a fair death for all this, if I 'scape","hanging for killing that rogue. I have forsworn his company hourly","any time this two-and-twenty year, and yet I am bewitch'd with the","rogue's company. If the rascal have not given me medicines to make","me love him, I'll be hang'd; it could not be else: I have drunk","medicines.—","Pointz!—Hal!—a plague upon you both!—Bardolph!—Peto!—I'll","starve, ere I'll rob a foot further. An 'twere not as good a deed as","drink, to turn true man, and to leave these rogues, I am the veriest","varlet that ever chewed with a tooth. Eight yards of uneven ground","is threescore and ten miles a-foot with me; and the stony-hearted","villains know it well enough: a plague upon't, when thieves cannot","be true one to another!","[They whistle] Whew!—A plague upon you all! Give me","my horse, you rogues; give me my horse, and be hang'd!"],"id":192,"m":"I'm cursed to be robbing in that thief's company. The rascal has moved my horse and tied him up I don't know where. If I walk even four more feet by the measuring rule, I'll run out of breath. Well, I'm sure I'll die a respectable death after all this, if I escape hanging for killing that rogue. I've sworn off his company every hour for the last twenty-two years, and yet I'm bewitched by the rascal. If he hasn't given me love potions to make me love him, I'll be hanged. It can't be anything else: I've drunk potions. Poins! Hal! A plague on both of you! Bardolph! Peto! I'll starve before I rob a foot further. If turning honest and leaving these rogues isn't as good a deed as drinking, I'm the worst scoundrel that ever chewed with a tooth. Eight yards of rough ground is seventy miles on foot to me, and the stony-hearted villains know it well enough. A plague on it, when thieves can't be honest with one another! Whew! A plague on you all! Give me my horse, you rogues, give me my horse, and be hanged!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["[Coming forward] Peace! lie down; lay thine ear close to the","ground, and list if thou canst hear the tread of travellers."],"id":193,"m":"Quiet! Lie down, put your ear close to the ground and listen whether you can hear travellers coming."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Have you any levers to lift me up again, being down? 'Sblood, I'll","not bear mine own flesh so far a-foot again for all the coin in thy","father's exchequer. What a plague mean ye to colt me thus?"],"id":194,"m":"Have you got any levers to lift me up again once I'm down? By God's blood, I won't carry my own flesh so far on foot again for all the money in your father's treasury. Why the plague are you tricking me like this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Thou liest; thou art not colted, thou art uncolted."],"id":195,"m":"You're lying; you're not tricked, you're un-horsed."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["I pr'ythee, good Prince Hal, help me to my horse, good king's","son."],"id":196,"m":"Please, good Prince Hal, help me to my horse, good king's son."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Out, ye rogue! shall I be your ostler?"],"id":197,"m":"Get away, you rogue! Am I supposed to be your stable-boy?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Go, hang thyself in thine own heir-apparent garters! If I be","ta'en, I'll peach for this. An I have not ballads made on you","all, and sung to filthy tunes, let a cup of sack be my poison.","When a jest is so forward, and a-foot too, I hate it."],"id":198,"m":"Go and hang yourself with your own heir-apparent garters! If I'm caught, I'll turn informer about this. If I don't have ballads written about you all and sung to dirty tunes, let a cup of sherry be my poison. When a joke goes this far, and on foot too, I hate it."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Gadshill.","id":199},{"t":"sp","who":"GADSHILL","lines":["Stand!"],"id":200,"m":"Stand!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["So I do, against my will."],"id":201,"m":"I'm doing that already, against my will."},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["O, 'tis our setter: I know his voice."],"id":202,"m":"Oh, it's our scout; I know his voice."},{"t":"sd","text":"Comes forward with Bardolph and Peto.","id":203},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["What news?"],"id":204,"m":"What's the news?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GADSHILL","lines":["Case ye, case ye; on with your visards: there's money of","the King's coming down the hill; 'tis going to the King's","exchequer."],"id":205,"m":"Mask up, mask up, put your masks on. There's money of the King's coming down the hill; it's on its way to the King's treasury."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["You lie, ye rogue; 'tis going to the King's tavern."],"id":206,"m":"You're lying, you rogue; it's on its way to the King's tavern."},{"t":"sp","who":"GADSHILL","lines":["There's enough to make us all."],"id":207,"m":"There's enough to make all our fortunes."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["To be hang'd."],"id":208,"m":"To get us all hanged."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Sirs, you four shall front them in the narrow lane; Ned","Pointz and I will walk lower; if they 'scape from your","encounter, then they light on us."],"id":209,"m":"Gentlemen, you four will confront them in the narrow lane. Ned Poins and I will wait further down. If they escape from you, they'll run into us."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETO","lines":["How many be there of them?"],"id":210,"m":"How many of them are there?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GADSHILL","lines":["Some eight or ten."],"id":211,"m":"About eight or ten."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Zwounds, will they not rob us?"],"id":212,"m":"By God's wounds, won't they rob us?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["What, a coward, Sir John Paunch?"],"id":213,"m":"What, are you a coward, Sir John Paunch?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Indeed, I am not John of Gaunt, your grandfather; but yet","no coward, Hal."],"id":214,"m":"Well, I'm not John of Gaunt, your grandfather, but I'm still no coward, Hal."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Well, we leave that to the proof."],"id":215,"m":"Well, we'll leave that to be proved."},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["Sirrah Jack, thy horse stands behind the hedge: when thou","need'st him, there thou shalt find him. Farewell, and stand fast."],"id":216,"m":"Jack, my friend, your horse is behind the hedge. When you need him, you'll find him there. Goodbye, and stand firm."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Now cannot I strike him, if I should be hang'd."],"id":217,"m":"Now I can't hit him, even if I were to be hanged for not doing it."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["[aside to POINTZ] Ned, where are our disguises?"],"id":218,"m":"Ned, where are our disguises?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["[aside to PRINCE HENRY] Here, hard by: stand close."],"id":219,"m":"Here, close by. Keep out of sight."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Prince and Pointz.","id":220},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Now, my masters, happy man be his dole, say I: every man","to his business."],"id":221,"m":"Now, gentlemen, good luck to every man, I say. Everyone to his job."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Travellers.","id":222},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST TRAVELLER","lines":["Come, neighbour:","The boy shall lead our horses down the hill;","We'll walk a-foot awhile and ease our legs."],"id":223,"m":"Come, neighbour. The boy can lead our horses down the hill; we'll walk for a while and stretch our legs."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIEVES","lines":["Stand!"],"id":224,"m":"Stand!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND TRAVELLER","lines":["Jesu bless us!"],"id":225,"m":"Jesus bless us!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Strike; down with them; cut the villains' throats. Ah,","whoreson caterpillars! bacon-fed knaves! they hate us youth:","down with them; fleece them."],"id":226,"m":"Strike! Down with them! Cut the villains' throats! Ah, you son-of-a-whore caterpillars, bacon-fed rascals! They hate us young folk. Down with them, fleece them!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST TRAVELLER","lines":["O, we're undone, both we and ours for ever!"],"id":227,"m":"Oh, we're ruined, both we and our families, for ever!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Hang ye, gorbellied knaves, are ye undone? No, ye fat chuffs;","I would your store were here! On, bacons on! What, ye knaves!","young men must live. You are grand-jurors, are ye? we'll jure","ye, i'faith."],"id":228,"m":"Hang you, you pot-bellied rascals, are you ruined? No, you fat misers, I wish all your wealth were here! Move, you pigs, move! What, you rascals, young men have to live. You're grand jurors, are you? We'll jury you, I swear."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Falstaff, Gadshill, and the rest, driving the Travellers out.","id":229},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Prince Henry and Pointz, in buckram suits.","id":230},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["The thieves have bound the true men. Now, could thou and I rob","the thieves, and go merrily to London, it would be argument for a","week, laughter for a month, and a good jest for ever."],"id":231,"m":"The thieves have tied up the honest men. Now, if you and I could rob the thieves and ride merrily to London, it would be something to talk about for a week, to laugh about for a month, and a good joke for ever."},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["Stand close: I hear them coming."],"id":232,"m":"Keep out of sight; I hear them coming."},{"t":"sd","text":"They retire.","id":233},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Falstaff, Gadshill, Bardolph, and Peto.","id":234},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Come, my masters, let us share, and then to horse before day.","An the Prince and Pointz be not two arrant cowards, there's no","equity stirring: there's no more valour in that Pointz than in a","wild duck."],"id":235,"m":"Come on, gentlemen, let's share it out, and then to horse before daylight. If the Prince and Poins aren't two out-and-out cowards, there's no justice in the world. There's no more courage in that Poins than in a wild duck."},{"t":"sd","text":"As they are sharing, the Prince and Poins set upon them.","id":236},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Your money!"],"id":237,"m":"Your money!"},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["Villains!"],"id":238,"m":"Villains!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Falstaff, after a blow or two, and the others run away, leaving the booty behind them.","id":239},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Got with much ease. Now merrily to horse:","The thieves are scatter'd, and possess'd with fear","So strongly that they dare not meet each other;","Each takes his fellow for an officer.","Away, good Ned. Fat Falstaff sweats to death,","And lards the lean earth as he walks along:","Were't not for laughing, I should pity him."],"id":240,"m":"That was easily done. Now merrily to horse. The thieves are scattered and so gripped by fear that they don't dare meet up; each one thinks his friend is a constable. Come on, good Ned. Fat Falstaff is sweating himself to death and basting the lean earth with fat as he walks along. If I weren't laughing, I'd pity him."},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["How the rogue roar'd!"],"id":241,"m":"How the rogue roared!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":242},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"III","loc":"Warkworth. A Room in the Castle.","id":243},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Hotspur, reading a letter.","id":244},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["—But, for mine own part, my lord, I could be well contented to","be there, in respect of the love I bear your House.—He could be","contented; why is he not, then? In respect of the love he bears","our House!—he shows in this, he loves his own barn better than he","loves our house. Let me see some more. The purpose you undertake","is dangerous;—Why, that's certain: 'tis dangerous to take a cold,","to sleep, to drink; but I tell you, my lord fool, out of this nettle,","danger, we pluck this flower, safety. The purpose you undertake is","dangerous; the friends you have named uncertain; the time itself","unsorted; and your whole plot too light for the counterpoise of so","great an opposition.—","Say you so, say you so? I say unto you again, you are a shallow,","cowardly hind, and you lie. What a lack-brain is this! By the Lord,","our plot is a good plot as ever was laid; our friends true and","constant: a good plot, good friends, and full of expectation; an","excellent plot, very good friends. What a frosty-spirited rogue is","this! Why, my Lord of York commends the plot and the general course","of the action. Zwounds! an I were now by this rascal, I could brain","him with his lady's fan. Is there not my father, my uncle, and","myself? Lord Edmund Mortimer, my Lord of York, and Owen Glendower?","is there not, besides, the Douglas? have I not all their letters to","meet me in arms by the ninth of the next month? and are they not","some of them set forward already? What a pagan rascal is this! an","infidel! Ha! you shall see now, in very sincerity of fear and cold","heart, will he to the King, and lay open all our proceedings. O, I","could divide myself, and go to buffets, for moving such a dish of","skimm'd milk with so honourable an action!","Hang him! let him tell the King: we are prepared. I will set","forward to-night.—"],"id":245,"m":"\"But for my own part, my lord, I would be glad to be there, because of the love I bear your family.\" He would be glad to; then why isn't he? \"Because of the love he bears our family!\" He shows by this that he loves his own barn better than our house. Let me see some more. \"The plan you are undertaking is dangerous.\" Well, that's certain: it's dangerous to catch a cold, to sleep, to drink. But I tell you, my lord fool, out of this nettle, danger, we pick this flower, safety. \"The plan you are undertaking is dangerous, the friends you have named unreliable, the timing itself badly chosen, and your whole plot too weak to balance against such a great opposition.\" Is that what you say, is that what you say? I say to you again, you're a shallow, cowardly peasant, and you're lying. What a brainless fool this is! By God, our plot is as good a plot as was ever laid, our friends true and loyal: a good plot, good friends, and full of promise; an excellent plot, very good friends. What a cold-blooded rogue this is! Why, my lord of York approves of the plot and the whole course of action. By God's wounds, if I were next to this rascal now, I could brain him with his lady's fan. Aren't there my father, my uncle and myself? Lord Edmund Mortimer, my lord of York and Owen Glendower? And besides them, isn't there Douglas? Don't I have letters from all of them promising to meet me in arms by the ninth of next month? And haven't some of them set out already? What a godless rascal this is, an unbeliever! Ha! Now you'll see him, in pure fear and cowardice, go to the King and reveal everything we're doing. Oh, I could split myself in two and start punching myself, for trying to interest such a bowl of skimmed milk in such an honourable enterprise! Hang him! Let him tell the King, we're ready. I'll set out tonight."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Lady Percy.","id":246},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","cont":1,"lines":["How now, Kate! I must leave you within these two hours."],"id":247,"m":"What's the matter, Kate? I must leave you within these two hours."},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY PERCY","lines":["O, my good lord, why are you thus alone?","For what offence have I this fortnight been","A banish'd woman from my Harry's bed?","Tell me, sweet lord, what is't that takes from thee","Thy stomach, pleasure, and thy golden sleep?","Why dost thou bend thine eyes upon the earth,","And start so often when thou sitt'st alone?","Why hast thou lost the fresh blood in thy cheeks;","And given my treasures and my rights of thee","To thick-eyed musing and curst melancholy?","In thy faint slumbers I by thee have watch'd,","And heard thee murmur tales of iron wars;","Speak terms of manage to thy bounding steed;","Cry Courage! to the field! And thou hast talk'd","Of sallies and retires, of trenches, tents,","Of palisadoes, frontiers, parapets,","Of basilisks, of cannon, culverin,","Of prisoners ransomed, and of soldiers slain,","And all the 'currents of a heady fight.","Thy spirit within thee hath been so at war,","And thus hath so bestirr'd thee in thy sleep,","That beads of sweat have stood upon thy brow,","Like bubbles in a late-disturbed stream;","And in thy face strange motions have appear'd,","Such as we see when men restrain their breath","On some great sudden hest. O, what portents are these?","Some heavy business hath my lord in hand,","And I must know it, else he loves me not."],"id":248,"m":"Oh, my good lord, why are you always alone like this? What have I done to be banished from my Harry's bed for this fortnight? Tell me, sweet lord, what is it that takes away your appetite, your pleasure and your precious sleep? Why do you keep your eyes fixed on the ground, and start so often when you sit alone? Why have you lost the fresh colour in your cheeks, and given away my treasures and my rights in you to dull-eyed brooding and bad-tempered gloom? As you slept fitfully, I've watched over you and heard you mutter stories of iron wars, give riding commands to your leaping horse, and shout \"Courage! To the battlefield!\" And you've talked of attacks and retreats, of trenches and tents, of fences of stakes, forts and parapets, of heavy guns, cannon and light cannon, of prisoners ransomed and soldiers killed, and all the events of a furious battle. Your spirit inside you has been so much at war, and has so stirred you up in your sleep, that beads of sweat have stood on your forehead like bubbles in a stream that has just been disturbed, and strange expressions have crossed your face, like those we see when men hold their breath at some great and sudden command. Oh, what do these signs mean? My lord has some serious business in hand, and I must know it, or else he doesn't love me."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["What, ho!"],"id":249,"m":"Hey there!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Servant.","id":250},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","cont":1,"lines":["Is Gilliams with the packet gone?"],"id":251,"m":"Has Gilliams gone with the letters?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["He is, my lord, an hour ago."],"id":252,"m":"He has, my lord, an hour ago."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["Hath Butler brought those horses from the sheriff?"],"id":253,"m":"Has Butler brought those horses from the sheriff?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["One horse, my lord, he brought even now."],"id":254,"m":"He brought one horse just now, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["What horse? a roan, a crop-ear, is it not?"],"id":255,"m":"What horse? A roan with clipped ears, isn't it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["It is, my lord."],"id":256,"m":"It is, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["That roan shall be my throne.","Well, I will back him straight: O esperance!—","Bid Butler lead him forth into the park."],"id":257,"m":"That roan will be my throne. Well, I'll ride him at once. Oh, hope! Tell Butler to lead him out into the park."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Servant.","id":258},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY PERCY","lines":["But hear you, my lord."],"id":259,"m":"But listen, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["What say'st thou, my lady?"],"id":260,"m":"What do you say, my lady?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY PERCY","lines":["What is it carries you away?"],"id":261,"m":"What is it that's carrying you away?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["Why, my horse, my love, my horse."],"id":262,"m":"Why, my horse, my love, my horse."},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY PERCY","lines":["Out, you mad-headed ape!","A weasel hath not such a deal of spleen","As you are toss'd with. In faith,","I'll know your business, Harry, that I will.","I fear my brother Mortimer doth stir","About his title, and hath sent for you","To line his enterprise: but if you go,—"],"id":263,"m":"Get away, you mad-headed monkey! A weasel isn't as hot-tempered as you are. I swear I'll know your business, Harry, I will. I'm afraid my brother Mortimer is stirring up trouble about his claim to the throne and has sent for you to back his enterprise. But if you go,"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["So far a-foot, I shall be weary, love."],"id":264,"m":"So far on foot, I'll get tired, love."},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY PERCY","lines":["Come, come, you paraquito, answer me","Directly to this question that I ask:","In faith, I'll break thy little finger, Harry,","An if thou wilt not tell me true."],"id":265,"m":"Come on, come on, you parrot, give me a straight answer to my question. I swear I'll break your little finger, Harry, if you won't tell me the truth."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["Away,","Away, you trifler! Love? I love thee not,","I care not for thee, Kate: this is no world","To play with mammets and to tilt with lips:","We must have bloody noses and crack'd crowns,","And pass them current too.—Gods me, my horse!—","What say'st thou, Kate? what wouldst thou have with me?"],"id":266,"m":"Go away, go away, you time-waster! Love? I don't love you, I don't care about you, Kate. This is no time to play with dolls and joust with lips. We must have bloody noses and broken heads, and pass them around like coins too. God save me, my horse! What do you say, Kate? What do you want with me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY PERCY","lines":["Do you not love me? do you not indeed?","Well, do not, then; for, since you love me not,","I will not love myself. Do you not love me?","Nay, tell me if you speak in jest or no."],"id":267,"m":"Don't you love me? Don't you really? Well then, don't, because if you don't love me, I won't love myself. Don't you love me? No, tell me whether you're joking or not."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["Come, wilt thou see me ride?","And when I am o' horseback, I will swear","I love thee infinitely. But hark you, Kate;","I must not have you henceforth question me","Whither I go, nor reason whereabout:","Whither I must, I must; and, to conclude,","This evening must I leave you, gentle Kate.","I know you wise; but yet no further wise","Than Harry Percy's wife; constant you are;","But yet a woman: and, for secrecy,","No lady closer; for I well believe","Thou wilt not utter what thou dost not know;","And so far will I trust thee, gentle Kate."],"id":268,"m":"Come, will you watch me ride? And when I'm on horseback, I'll swear I love you infinitely. But listen, Kate, from now on you mustn't question me about where I'm going or why. Where I must go, I must go. And, to sum up, I must leave you this evening, gentle Kate. I know you're wise, but no wiser than Harry Percy's wife should be. You're faithful, but still a woman. And as for keeping secrets, no lady keeps them better, because I'm quite sure you won't reveal what you don't know. That's how far I'll trust you, gentle Kate."},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY PERCY","lines":["How! so far?"],"id":269,"m":"What? That far?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["Not an inch further. But hark you, Kate:","Whither I go, thither shall you go too;","To-day will I set forth, to-morrow you.","Will this content you, Kate?"],"id":270,"m":"Not an inch further. But listen, Kate: where I go, you'll go too. I'll set out today, you tomorrow. Will this satisfy you, Kate?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY PERCY","lines":["It must of force."],"id":271,"m":"It has to."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":272},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"IV","loc":"Eastcheap. A Room in the Boar's-Head Tavern.","id":273},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Prince Henry.","id":274},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Ned, pr'ythee, come out of that fat room, and lend me thy","hand to laugh a little."],"id":275,"m":"Ned, please come out of that stuffy room and help me laugh a little."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Pointz.","id":276},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["Where hast been, Hal?"],"id":277,"m":"Where have you been, Hal?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["With three or four loggerheads amongst three or fourscore","hogsheads. I have sounded the very base-string of humility.","Sirrah, I am sworn brother to a leash of drawers; and can call","them all by their Christian names, as, Tom, Dick, and Francis.","They take it already upon their salvation, that though I be but","Prince of Wales, yet I am the king of courtesy; and tell me flatly","I am no proud Jack, like Falstaff, but a corinthian, a lad of mettle,","a good boy,—by the Lord, so they call me;—and, when I am King","of England, I shall command all the good lads in Eastcheap. They","call drinking deep, dying scarlet; and, when you breathe in your","watering, they cry hem! and bid you play it off. To conclude, I am","so good a proficient in one quarter of an hour, that I can drink with","any tinker in his own language during my life. I tell thee, Ned, thou","hast lost much honour, that thou wert not with me in this action. But,","sweet Ned,—to sweeten which name of Ned, I give thee this pennyworth","of sugar, clapp'd even now into my hand by an under-skinker; one that","never spake other English in his life than Eight shillings and sixpence,","and You are welcome; with this shrill addition, Anon, anon, sir! Score","a pint of bastard in the Half-moon,—or so. But, Ned, to drive away","the time till Falstaff come, I pr'ythee, do thou stand in some by-room,","while I question my puny drawer to what end he gave me the sugar;","and do thou never leave calling Francis! that his tale to me may be","nothing but Anon. Step aside, and I'll show thee a precedent."],"id":278,"m":"With three or four blockheads among sixty or eighty barrels of wine. I've plucked the lowest string of humility. My friend, I'm sworn brother to a trio of barmen and can call them all by their first names, Tom, Dick and Francis. They already swear on their salvation that though I'm only Prince of Wales, I'm the king of courtesy, and tell me straight out that I'm no stuck-up fellow like Falstaff, but a good-time lad, a lad of spirit, a good boy (by God, that's what they call me), and that when I'm King of England I'll have all the good lads in Eastcheap at my command. They call heavy drinking \"dyeing scarlet\", and when you pause for breath while drinking, they cry \"Hem!\" and tell you to knock it back. In short, I learned so much in a quarter of an hour that I can drink with any tinker in his own slang for the rest of my life. I tell you, Ned, you lost a lot of honour by not being with me in this campaign. But, sweet Ned, to sweeten the name of Ned I give you this pennyworth of sugar, which a junior barman pushed into my hand just now, one who has never spoken any other English in his life than \"Eight shillings and sixpence,\" and \"You are welcome,\" with the shrill addition, \"Coming, coming, sir! Put a pint of sweet wine on the Half-moon room's bill,\" or something like that. But, Ned, to pass the time till Falstaff comes, please stand in some side room while I ask my little barman why he gave me the sugar, and you keep calling \"Francis!\" so that all he can say to me is \"Coming.\" Step aside, and I'll show you an example."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Pointz.","id":279},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["[Within] Francis!"],"id":280,"m":"Francis!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Thou art perfect."],"id":281,"m":"You're perfect."},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["[Within] Francis!"],"id":282,"m":"Francis!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Francis.","id":283},{"t":"sp","who":"FRANCIS","lines":["Anon, anon, sir.—Look down into the Pomegranate, Ralph."],"id":284,"m":"Coming, coming, sir. Look in on the Pomegranate room, Ralph."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Come hither, Francis."],"id":285,"m":"Come here, Francis."},{"t":"sp","who":"FRANCIS","lines":["My lord?"],"id":286,"m":"My lord?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["How long hast thou to serve, Francis?"],"id":287,"m":"How long have you got left of your apprenticeship, Francis?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FRANCIS","lines":["Forsooth, five years, and as much as to—"],"id":288,"m":"Truly, five years, and as much as to..."},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["[within] Francis!"],"id":289,"m":"Francis!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FRANCIS","lines":["Anon, anon, sir."],"id":290,"m":"Coming, coming, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Five year! by'r Lady, a long lease for the clinking of","pewter. But, Francis, darest thou be so valiant as to play","the coward with thy indenture and show it a fair pair of heels","and run from it?"],"id":291,"m":"Five years! By Our Lady, that's a long contract for clinking pewter pots. But, Francis, would you dare to be brave enough to play the coward with your contract, show it a clean pair of heels and run away from it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FRANCIS","lines":["O Lord, sir, I'll be sworn upon all the books in England,","I could find in my heart—"],"id":292,"m":"Oh Lord, sir, I'll swear on every Bible in England, I could find it in my heart..."},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["[within] Francis!"],"id":293,"m":"Francis!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FRANCIS","lines":["Anon, anon, sir."],"id":294,"m":"Coming, coming, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["How old art thou, Francis?"],"id":295,"m":"How old are you, Francis?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FRANCIS","lines":["Let me see,—about Michaelmas next I shall be—"],"id":296,"m":"Let me see, about next Michaelmas I'll be..."},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["[within] Francis!"],"id":297,"m":"Francis!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FRANCIS","lines":["Anon, sir.—Pray you, stay a little, my lord."],"id":298,"m":"Coming, sir. Please wait a moment, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Nay, but hark you, Francis: for the sugar thou gavest","me, 'twas a pennyworth, was't not?"],"id":299,"m":"No, but listen, Francis. That sugar you gave me, it was a pennyworth, wasn't it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FRANCIS","lines":["O Lord, sir, I would it had been two!"],"id":300,"m":"Oh Lord, sir, I wish it had been two!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["I will give thee for it a thousand pound: ask me when","thou wilt, and thou shalt have it."],"id":301,"m":"I'll give you a thousand pounds for it. Ask me whenever you like, and you'll have it."},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["[within] Francis!"],"id":302,"m":"Francis!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FRANCIS","lines":["Anon, anon."],"id":303,"m":"Coming, coming."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Anon, Francis? No, Francis; but to-morrow, Francis; or,","Francis, a Thursday; or, indeed, Francis, when thou wilt. But,","Francis,—"],"id":304,"m":"Coming, Francis? No, Francis, but tomorrow, Francis; or Thursday, Francis; or, really, Francis, whenever you like. But, Francis,"},{"t":"sp","who":"FRANCIS","lines":["My lord?"],"id":305,"m":"My lord?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["—wilt thou rob this leathern-jerkin, crystal-button,","nott-pated, agate-ring, puke-stocking, caddis-garter,","smooth-tongue, Spanish-pouch,—"],"id":306,"m":"will you rob this leather-jacketed, crystal-buttoned, crop-haired, agate-ringed, dark-stockinged, cheap-gartered, smooth-talking, Spanish-pursed fellow?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FRANCIS","lines":["O Lord, sir, who do you mean?"],"id":307,"m":"Oh Lord, sir, who do you mean?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Why, then, your brown bastard is your only drink; for,","look you, Francis, your white canvas doublet will sully: in","Barbary, sir, it cannot come to so much."],"id":308,"m":"Why then, cheap brown wine is your only drink, because, look, Francis, your white canvas jacket will get dirty. In Barbary, sir, it can't come to so much."},{"t":"sp","who":"FRANCIS","lines":["What, sir?"],"id":309,"m":"What, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["[within] Francis!"],"id":310,"m":"Francis!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Away, you rogue! dost thou not hear them call?"],"id":311,"m":"Go on, you rogue! Can't you hear them calling?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Here they both call him; Francis stands amazed, not knowing which way to go.","id":312},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Vintner.","id":313},{"t":"sp","who":"VINTNER","lines":["What, stand'st thou still, and hear'st such a calling? Look","to the guests within. [Exit Francis]—My lord, old Sir John,","with half-a-dozen more, are at the door: shall I let them in?"],"id":314,"m":"What, are you standing still when you hear such calling? Look after the guests inside. My lord, old Sir John and half a dozen others are at the door. Shall I let them in?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Let them alone awhile, and then open the door."],"id":315,"m":"Let them wait a while, then open the door."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Vintner.","id":316},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","cont":1,"lines":["Pointz!"],"id":317,"m":"Poins!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Pointz.","id":318},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["Anon, anon, sir."],"id":319,"m":"Coming, coming, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Sirrah, Falstaff and the rest of the thieves are at the","door: shall we be merry?"],"id":320,"m":"My friend, Falstaff and the rest of the thieves are at the door. Shall we have some fun?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["As merry as crickets, my lad. But hark ye; what cunning","match have you made with this jest of the drawer? Come,","what's the issue?"],"id":321,"m":"As merry as crickets, my lad. But listen, what clever game were you playing with this joke on the barman? Come on, what's the point of it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["I am now of all humours that have showed themselves humours","since the old days of goodman Adam to the pupil age of this","present twelve o'clock at midnight.—What's o'clock, Francis?"],"id":322,"m":"I'm in the mood for every mood that has existed since the old days of good old Adam up to the young hour of this present midnight. What's the time, Francis?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FRANCIS","lines":["[Within] Anon, anon, sir."],"id":323,"m":"Coming, coming, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["That ever this fellow should have fewer words than a parrot, and","yet the son of a woman! His industry is up-stairs and down-stairs;","his eloquence the parcel of a reckoning. I am not yet of Percy's","mind, the Hotspur of the North; he that kills me some six or seven","dozen of Scots at a breakfast, washes his hands, and says to his wife,","Fie upon this quiet life! I want work. O my sweet Harry, says she,","how many hast thou kill'd to-day? Give my roan horse a drench,","says he; and answers, Some fourteen, an hour after,—a trifle, a","trifle.","I pr'ythee, call in Falstaff: I'll play Percy, and that damn'd","brawn shall play Dame Mortimer his wife. Rivo! says the drunkard.","Call in ribs, call in tallow."],"id":324,"m":"To think that this fellow should know fewer words than a parrot, and yet be born of a woman! His work is running upstairs and downstairs, his eloquence is reading out a bill. I'm not yet in the same mood as Percy, the Hotspur of the North, who kills six or seven dozen Scots before breakfast, washes his hands and says to his wife, \"Damn this quiet life! I want work.\" \"Oh my sweet Harry,\" she says, \"how many have you killed today?\" \"Give my roan horse a dose of medicine,\" he says, and answers, an hour later, \"About fourteen. A trifle, a trifle.\" Please call in Falstaff. I'll play Percy, and that damned pig will play Lady Mortimer his wife. \"Cheers!\" says the drunkard. Call in the ribs, call in the lard."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Falstaff, Gadshill, Bardolph, and Peto; followed by Francis with wine.","id":325},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["Welcome, Jack: where hast thou been?"],"id":326,"m":"Welcome, Jack. Where have you been?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["A plague of all cowards, I say, and a vengeance too! marry, and","amen!—","Give me a cup of sack, boy.—Ere I lead this life long, I'll sew","nether-stocks, and mend them and foot them too. A plague of all","cowards!—","Give me a cup of sack, rogue.—Is there no virtue extant?"],"id":327,"m":"A plague on all cowards, I say, and a curse on them too! Indeed, and amen! Give me a cup of sherry, boy. Before I lead this life much longer, I'll take up sewing stockings, mending them and putting new feet on them too. A plague on all cowards! Give me a cup of sherry, you rogue. Is there no courage left in the world?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Drinks.","id":328},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Didst thou never see Titan kiss a dish of butter? pitiful-hearted","butter, that melted at the sweet tale of the Sun! if thou didst,","then behold that compound."],"id":329,"m":"Did you ever see the sun kiss a dish of butter? Soft-hearted butter, which melted at the sun's sweet talk! If you did, look at that mixture there."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["You rogue, here's lime in this sack too: there is nothing but roguery","to be found in villainous man: yet a coward is worse than a cup of","sack with lime in it, a villanous coward.—Go thy ways, old Jack: die","when thou wilt, if manhood, good manhood, be not forgot upon the face","of the Earth, then am I a shotten herring. There live not three good","men unhang'd in England; and one of them is fat, and grows old: God","help the while! a bad world, I say.","I would I were a weaver; I could sing psalms or any thing. A plague of","all cowards! I say still."],"id":330,"m":"You rogue, there's lime in this sherry too. There's nothing but roguery to be found in villainous mankind. And yet a coward is worse than a cup of sherry with lime in it, a villainous coward. Go your way, old Jack, die when you like: if courage, real courage, isn't forgotten on the face of the earth, then I'm a spawned-out herring. There aren't three good men left unhanged in England, and one of them is fat and growing old. God help us! It's a bad world, I say. I wish I were a weaver; I could sing psalms or anything. A plague on all cowards, I still say."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["How now, wool-sack? what mutter you?"],"id":331,"m":"What's up, you sack of wool? What are you muttering?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["A king's son! If I do not beat thee out of thy kingdom with a dagger","of lath, and drive all thy subjects afore thee like a flock of","wild-geese, I'll never wear hair on my face more. You Prince of Wales!"],"id":332,"m":"A king's son! If I don't beat you out of your kingdom with a wooden dagger and drive all your subjects in front of you like a flock of wild geese, I'll never grow hair on my face again. You, Prince of Wales!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Why, you whoreson round man, what's the matter?"],"id":333,"m":"Why, you son-of-a-whore round man, what's the matter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Are not you a coward? answer me to that:—and Pointz there?"],"id":334,"m":"Aren't you a coward? Answer me that. And Poins there?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["Zwounds, ye fat paunch, an ye call me coward, by the Lord, I'll","stab thee."],"id":335,"m":"By God's wounds, you fat belly, if you call me a coward, by God, I'll stab you."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["I call thee coward! I'll see thee damn'd ere I call thee coward:","but I would give a thousand pound, I could run as fast as thou canst.","You are straight enough in the shoulders; you care not who sees your","back: call you that backing of your friends? A plague upon such","backing! give me them that will face me.—Give me a cup of sack:","I am a rogue, if I drunk to-day."],"id":336,"m":"I call you a coward! I'll see you damned before I call you a coward. But I'd give a thousand pounds to be able to run as fast as you can. You've got nice straight shoulders, you don't care who sees your back. Do you call that backing up your friends? A plague on such backing! Give me men who will face me. Give me a cup of sherry. I'm a rogue if I've had a drink today."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["O villain! thy lips are scarce wiped since thou drunk'st last."],"id":337,"m":"Oh, you villain! Your lips are hardly wiped since your last drink."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["All is one for that. A plague of all cowards! still say I."],"id":338,"m":"It's all the same. A plague on all cowards, I still say."},{"t":"sd","text":"Drinks.","id":339},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["What's the matter?"],"id":340,"m":"What's the matter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["What's the matter? there be four of us here have ta'en a thousand","pound this day morning."],"id":341,"m":"What's the matter? There are four of us here who took a thousand pounds this morning."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Where is it, Jack? where is it?"],"id":342,"m":"Where is it, Jack? Where is it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Where is it! taken from us it is: a hundred upon poor four of us!"],"id":343,"m":"Where is it! It's been taken from us. A hundred men attacked just the four of us!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["What, a hundred, man?"],"id":344,"m":"What, a hundred, man?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["I am a rogue, if I were not at half-sword with a dozen of them two","hours together. I have 'scaped by miracle. I am eight times thrust","through the doublet, four through the hose; my buckler cut through","and through; my sword hack'd like a hand-saw,—ecce signum! I never","dealt better since I was a man: all would not do. A plague of all","cowards! Let them speak: if they speak more or less than truth,","they are villains and the sons of darkness."],"id":345,"m":"I'm a rogue if I wasn't at close quarters with a dozen of them for two hours. I escaped by a miracle. I've been stabbed eight times through my jacket and four through my breeches, my shield is cut right through, my sword is hacked like a handsaw, look, here's the proof! I never fought better since I became a man, and it was no use. A plague on all cowards! Let them tell you. If they say more or less than the truth, they're villains and sons of darkness."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Speak, sirs; how was it?"],"id":346,"m":"Tell us, gentlemen, how did it happen?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GADSHILL","lines":["We four set upon some dozen,—"],"id":347,"m":"We four attacked about a dozen,"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Sixteen at least, my lord."],"id":348,"m":"Sixteen at least, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"GADSHILL","lines":["—and bound them."],"id":349,"m":"and tied them up."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETO","lines":["No, no; they were not bound."],"id":350,"m":"No, no, they weren't tied up."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["You rogue, they were bound, every man of them; or I am a Jew","else, an Ebrew Jew."],"id":351,"m":"You rogue, they were tied up, every one of them, or else I'm a Jew, a Hebrew Jew."},{"t":"sp","who":"GADSHILL","lines":["As we were sharing, some six or seven fresh men sea upon us,—"],"id":352,"m":"As we were sharing out the loot, six or seven fresh men attacked us,"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["And unbound the rest, and then come in the other."],"id":353,"m":"And untied the rest, and then the others joined in."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["What, fought you with them all?"],"id":354,"m":"What, did you fight with all of them?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["All? I know not what you call all; but if I fought not with fifty","of them, I am a bunch of radish: if there were not two or three","and fifty upon poor old Jack, then am I no two-legged creature."],"id":355,"m":"All? I don't know what you call all, but if I didn't fight fifty of them, I'm a bunch of radishes. If there weren't fifty-two or fifty-three against poor old Jack, then I'm no two-legged creature."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Pray God you have not murdered some of them."],"id":356,"m":"I pray to God you haven't murdered some of them."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Nay, that's past praying for: I have pepper'd two of them; two I","am sure I have paid, two rogues in buckram suits. I tell thee what,","Hal, if I tell thee a lie, spit in my face, call me horse.","Thou knowest my old ward: here I lay, and thus I bore my point.","Four rogues in buckram let drive at me,—"],"id":357,"m":"No, it's too late to pray for that. I've peppered two of them. Two I've certainly finished off, two rogues in canvas suits. I'll tell you what, Hal, if I'm telling you a lie, spit in my face and call me a horse. You know my old fighting stance: here I stood, and this is how I held my sword. Four rogues in canvas came at me,"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["What, four? thou saidst but two even now."],"id":358,"m":"What, four? You said only two just now."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Four, Hal; I told thee four."],"id":359,"m":"Four, Hal; I told you four."},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["Ay, ay, he said four."],"id":360,"m":"Yes, yes, he said four."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["These four came all a-front, and mainly thrust at me. I made me no more","ado but took all their seven points in my target, thus."],"id":361,"m":"These four came at me all from the front and thrust at me with all their might. I didn't make a fuss, I just took all their seven sword-points on my shield, like this."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Seven? why, there were but four even now."],"id":362,"m":"Seven? Why, there were only four just now."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["In buckram?"],"id":363,"m":"In canvas?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["Ay, four, in buckram suits."],"id":364,"m":"Yes, four, in canvas suits."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Seven, by these hilts, or I am a villain else."],"id":365,"m":"Seven, by the hilt of this sword, or else I'm a villain."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["[aside to Pointz] Pr'ythee let him alone; we shall have more","anon."],"id":366,"m":"Please leave him alone; we'll have more in a minute."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Dost thou hear me, Hal?"],"id":367,"m":"Are you listening to me, Hal?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Ay, and mark thee too, Jack."],"id":368,"m":"Yes, and taking note of you too, Jack."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Do so, for it is worth the listening to. These nine in buckram","that I told thee of,—"],"id":369,"m":"Do, because it's worth listening to. These nine in canvas that I told you about,"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["So, two more already."],"id":370,"m":"So, two more already."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["—their points being broken,—"],"id":371,"m":"their sword-points being broken,"},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["Down fell their hose."],"id":372,"m":"Their trousers fell down."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["—began to give me ground: but I followed me close, came in foot","and hand; and with a thought seven of the eleven I paid."],"id":373,"m":"began to give way. But I followed close, came in with foot and hand, and in a flash I finished off seven of the eleven."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["O monstrous! eleven buckram men grown out of two!"],"id":374,"m":"Oh, monstrous! Eleven canvas men grown out of two!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["But, as the Devil would have it, three misbegotten knaves in Kendal","Green came at my back and let drive at me; for it was so dark, Hal,","that thou couldst not see thy hand."],"id":375,"m":"But, as the Devil would have it, three misbegotten rascals in Kendal green came up behind me and attacked me, because it was so dark, Hal, that you couldn't see your own hand."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["These lies are like the father that begets them, gross as a mountain,","open, palpable. Why, thou nott-pated fool, thou whoreson, obscene","greasy tallow-keech,—"],"id":376,"m":"These lies are like the father who produces them, as huge as a mountain, open and obvious. Why, you thick-headed fool, you son of a whore, you obscene, greasy lump of lard,"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["What, art thou mad? art thou mad? is not the truth the truth?"],"id":377,"m":"What, are you mad? Are you mad? Isn't the truth the truth?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Why, how couldst thou know these men in Kendal green, when it was","so dark thou couldst not see thy hand? come, tell us your reason:","what sayest thou to this?"],"id":378,"m":"Why, how could you tell these men were in Kendal green when it was so dark you couldn't see your hand? Come on, give us your reason. What do you say to this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["Come, your reason, Jack, your reason."],"id":379,"m":"Come on, your reason, Jack, your reason."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["What, upon compulsion? No; were I at the strappado, or all the racks","in the world, I would not tell you on compulsion. Give you a reason on","compulsion! if reasons were as plentiful as blackberries, I would","give no man a reason upon compulsion, I."],"id":380,"m":"What, under force? No. If I were being tortured on the strappado or all the racks in the world, I wouldn't tell you under force. Give you a reason under force! If reasons were as common as blackberries, I wouldn't give any man a reason under force, not I."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["I'll be no longer guilty of this sin; this sanguine coward, this","bed-presser, this horse-back-breaker, this huge hill of flesh,—"],"id":381,"m":"I won't go on being guilty of this sin. This red-faced coward, this bed-breaker, this horse's-back-breaker, this huge hill of flesh,"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Away, you starveling, you eel-skin, you dried neat's-tongue, you","stock-fish,—","O, for breath to utter what is like thee!—you tailor's-yard, you","sheath, you bow-case, you vile standing tuck,—"],"id":382,"m":"Get away, you starveling, you eel-skin, you dried ox-tongue, you dried cod! Oh, give me breath to say what you're like! You tailor's measuring stick, you sword sheath, you bow case, you vile upright rapier,"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Well, breathe awhile, and then to it again: and, when thou hast","tired thyself in base comparisons, hear me speak but this:—"],"id":383,"m":"Well, take a breath, and then start again. And when you've worn yourself out with low comparisons, just listen to me say this."},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["Mark, Jack."],"id":384,"m":"Listen, Jack."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["—We two saw you four set on four; you bound them, and were masters of","their wealth.—Mark now, how a plain tale shall put you down.—","Then did we two set on you four; and, with a word, outfaced you from","your prize, and have it; yea, and can show it you here in the house:","and, Falstaff, you carried yourself away as nimbly, with as quick","dexterity, and roared for mercy, and still ran and roar'd, as ever I","heard bull-calf. What a slave art thou, to hack thy sword as thou","hast done, and then say it was in fight!","What trick, what device, what starting-hole canst thou now find","out to hide thee from this open and apparent shame?"],"id":385,"m":"We two saw you four attack four men. You tied them up and took their money. Now see how a plain story will defeat you. Then we two attacked you four, and with one word we scared you away from your loot and have it, yes, and can show it to you here in the inn. And, Falstaff, you carried your guts away as nimbly and quickly, roaring for mercy and still running and roaring, as any bull-calf I ever heard. What a wretch you are, to hack your sword the way you did and then say it was done in the fight! What trick, what device, what bolt-hole can you find now to hide you from this open and obvious shame?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["Come, let's hear, Jack; what trick hast thou now?"],"id":386,"m":"Come on, let's hear it, Jack. What trick have you got now?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["By the Lord, I knew ye as well as he that made ye. Why, hear ye,","my masters:","Was it for me to kill the heir-apparent? should I turn upon the","true Prince? why, thou knowest I am as valiant as Hercules: but","beware instinct; the lion will not touch the true Prince.","Instinct is a great matter; I was now a coward on instinct.","I shall think the better of myself and thee during my life; I for a","valiant lion, and thou for a true prince. But, by the Lord, lads,","I am glad you have the money.—","[To Hostess within] Hostess, clap-to the doors: watch","to-night, pray to-morrow.—Gallants, lads, boys, hearts of gold,","all the titles of good fellowship come to you!","What, shall we be merry? shall we have a play extempore?"],"id":387,"m":"By God, I knew you as well as the one who made you. Why, listen, gentlemen. Was it for me to kill the heir to the throne? Should I turn on the true Prince? Why, you know I'm as brave as Hercules, but beware of instinct: the lion won't touch the true prince. Instinct is a great thing; I was a coward on instinct. I'll think better of myself and of you for the rest of my life: of myself as a brave lion, and of you as a true prince. But, by God, lads, I'm glad you have the money. Landlady, shut the doors! Stay up tonight, pray tomorrow. Gallants, lads, boys, hearts of gold, may every title of good fellowship be yours! What, shall we have some fun? Shall we have an improvised play?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Content; and the argument shall be thy running away."],"id":388,"m":"Agreed, and the plot will be your running away."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Ah, no more of that, Hal, an thou lovest me!"],"id":389,"m":"Ah, no more of that, Hal, if you love me!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the Hostess.","id":390},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["O Jesu, my lord the Prince,—"],"id":391,"m":"Oh Jesus, my lord the Prince,"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["How now, my lady the hostess! What say'st thou to me?"],"id":392,"m":"Well, my lady the landlady! What do you have to say to me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["Marry, my lord, there is a nobleman of the Court at door would","speak with you: he says he comes from your father."],"id":393,"m":"Well, my lord, there's a nobleman from the Court at the door who wants to speak to you. He says he comes from your father."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Give him as much as will make him a royal man, and send him back","again to my mother."],"id":394,"m":"Give him enough to make him a royal man, and send him back to my mother."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["What manner of man is he?"],"id":395,"m":"What sort of man is he?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["An old man."],"id":396,"m":"An old man."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["What doth gravity out of his bed at midnight? Shall I give him","his answer?"],"id":397,"m":"What's an old greybeard doing out of bed at midnight? Shall I give him his answer?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Pr'ythee, do, Jack."],"id":398,"m":"Please do, Jack."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Faith, and I'll send him packing."],"id":399,"m":"Indeed, I'll send him packing."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":400},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Now, sirs:—by'r Lady, you fought fair;—so did you, Peto;—so did you,","Bardolph: you are lions, too, you ran away upon instinct, you will not","touch the true Prince; no,—fie!"],"id":401,"m":"Now, gentlemen, by Our Lady, you fought well. So did you, Peto, and so did you, Bardolph. You're lions too, you ran away on instinct, you won't touch the true prince. No, shame on you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["Faith, I ran when I saw others run."],"id":402,"m":"Honestly, I ran when I saw others running."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Tell me now in earnest, how came Falstaff's sword so hack'd?"],"id":403,"m":"Now tell me seriously, how did Falstaff's sword get so hacked?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PETO","lines":["Why, he hack'd it with his dagger; and said he would swear truth out of","England, but he would make you believe it was done in fight; and","persuaded us to do the like."],"id":404,"m":"Why, he hacked it with his dagger, and said he would swear truth right out of England but he'd make you believe it was done in the fight, and he persuaded us to do the same."},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["Yea, and to tickle our noses with spear-grass to make them bleed;","and then to beslubber our garments with it, and swear it was the","blood of true men. I did that I did not this seven year before;","I blush'd to hear his monstrous devices."],"id":405,"m":"Yes, and to tickle our noses with spear-grass to make them bleed, and then smear our clothes with it and swear it was the blood of honest men. I did something I hadn't done for seven years: I blushed to hear his outrageous schemes."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["O villain, thou stolest a cup of sack eighteen years ago, and wert","taken with the manner, and ever since thou hast blush'd extempore.","Thou hadst fire and sword on thy side, and yet thou rann'st away:","what instinct hadst thou for it?"],"id":406,"m":"Oh, you villain, you stole a cup of sherry eighteen years ago and were caught red-handed, and you've been blushing on the spot ever since. You had fire and sword on your side, and still you ran away. What instinct did you have for that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["My lord, do you see these meteors? do you behold these","exhalations?"],"id":407,"m":"My lord, do you see these meteors? Do you see these fiery vapours?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["I do."],"id":408,"m":"I do."},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["What think you they portend?"],"id":409,"m":"What do you think they mean?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Hot livers and cold purses."],"id":410,"m":"Hot livers and empty purses."},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["Choler, my lord, if rightly taken."],"id":411,"m":"Bad temper, my lord, if rightly understood."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["No, if rightly taken, halter.—Here comes lean Jack, here comes","bare-bone.—"],"id":412,"m":"No, if rightly taken, the hangman's rope. Here comes lean Jack, here comes the bag of bones."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Falstaff.","id":413},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","cont":1,"lines":["How now, my sweet creature of bombast! How long is't ago, Jack,","since thou saw'st thine own knee?"],"id":414,"m":"Well, my sweet creature stuffed with padding! How long is it, Jack, since you last saw your own knee?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["My own knee! when I was about thy years, Hal, I was not an eagle's","talon in the waist; I could have crept into any alderman's thumb-ring:","a plague of sighing and grief! it blows a man up like a bladder.","There's villanous news abroad: here was Sir John Bracy from your","father; you must to the Court in the morning.","That same mad fellow of the North, Percy; and he of Wales, that gave","Amaimon the bastinado, and swore the Devil his true liegeman upon the","cross of a Welsh hook,—what a plague call you him?"],"id":415,"m":"My own knee! When I was about your age, Hal, I wasn't as thick as an eagle's claw round the waist; I could have crept through an alderman's thumb ring. A plague on sighing and grief! It blows a man up like a balloon. There's terrible news about. Sir John Bracy was here from your father; you must go to Court in the morning. That same madman from the North, Percy, and the Welshman who beat the demon Amaimon and swore the Devil in as his loyal subject on the cross of a Welsh pike, what the plague do you call him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["O, Glendower."],"id":416,"m":"Oh, Glendower."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Owen, Owen,—the same; and his son-in-law Mortimer; and old","Northumberland; and that sprightly Scot of Scots, Douglas, that","runs o' horseback up a hill perpendicular,—"],"id":417,"m":"Owen, Owen, that's the one; and his son-in-law Mortimer, and old Northumberland, and that lively Scot of Scots, Douglas, who can ride his horse straight up a vertical hill,"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["He that rides at high speed and with his pistol kills a sparrow","flying."],"id":418,"m":"The one who rides at full gallop and kills a flying sparrow with his pistol."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["You have hit it."],"id":419,"m":"You've hit it."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["So did he never the sparrow."],"id":420,"m":"He never hit the sparrow, though."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Well, that rascal hath good metal in him; he will not run."],"id":421,"m":"Well, that rascal has good spirit in him; he won't run away."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Why, what a rascal art thou, then, to praise him so for running!"],"id":422,"m":"Why, what a rascal you are, then, to praise him so much for running!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["O' horseback, ye cuckoo! but a-foot he will not budge a foot."],"id":423,"m":"On horseback, you cuckoo! But on foot he won't budge an inch."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Yes, Jack, upon instinct."],"id":424,"m":"Yes, Jack, on instinct."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["I grant ye, upon instinct. Well, he is there too, and one Mordake,","and a thousand blue-caps more:","Worcester is stolen away to-night; thy father's beard is turn'd","white with the news: you may buy land now as cheap as stinking","mackerel.","But, tell me, Hal, art not thou horrible afeard? thou being","heir-apparent, could the world pick thee out three such enemies again","as that fiend Douglas, that spirit Percy, and that devil Glendower?","art thou not horribly afraid? doth not thy blood thrill at it?"],"id":425,"m":"I grant you, on instinct. Well, he's there too, and one Mordake, and a thousand more blue-bonneted Scots. Worcester has slipped away tonight. Your father's beard has turned white at the news. You can buy land now as cheap as rotten mackerel. But tell me, Hal, aren't you horribly afraid? You, the heir to the throne, could the world find you three such enemies again as that fiend Douglas, that spirit Percy and that devil Glendower? Aren't you horribly afraid? Doesn't your blood run cold at it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Not a whit, i'faith; I lack some of thy instinct."],"id":426,"m":"Not a bit, honestly. I lack some of your instinct."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Well, thou wilt be horribly chid to-morrow when thou comest to","thy father. If thou love life, practise an answer."],"id":427,"m":"Well, you'll get a terrible telling-off tomorrow when you go to your father. If you value your life, rehearse an answer."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Do thou stand for my father and examine me upon the particulars","of my life."],"id":428,"m":"You stand in for my father and question me about the details of my life."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Shall I? content: this chair shall be my state, this dagger my","sceptre, and this cushion my crown."],"id":429,"m":"Shall I? All right. This chair will be my throne, this dagger my sceptre and this cushion my crown."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Thy state is taken for a joint-stool, thy golden sceptre for a","leaden dagger, and thy precious rich crown for a pitiful bald crown."],"id":430,"m":"Your throne looks like a stool, your golden sceptre like a lead dagger, and your precious rich crown like a pitiful bald head."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Well, an the fire of grace be not quite out of thee, now shalt","thou be moved.—","Give me a cup of sack, to make my eyes look red, that it may be","thought I have wept; for I must speak in passion, and I will do it","in King Cambyses' vein."],"id":431,"m":"Well, if the fire of grace isn't completely out in you, now you'll be moved. Give me a cup of sherry to make my eyes look red, so it'll be thought I've been crying, because I must speak with emotion, and I'll do it in the style of King Cambyses in the old play."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Well, here is my leg."],"id":432,"m":"Well, here's my bow."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["And here is my speech.—Stand aside, nobility."],"id":433,"m":"And here's my speech. Stand aside, nobles."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["O Jesu, this is excellent sport, i faith!"],"id":434,"m":"Oh Jesus, this is great fun, honestly!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Weep not, sweet Queen; for trickling tears are vain."],"id":435,"m":"Weep not, sweet Queen, for trickling tears are vain."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["O, the Father, how he holds his countenance!"],"id":436,"m":"Oh, Father, how straight he keeps his face!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["For God's sake, lords, convey my tristful Queen;","For tears do stop the floodgates of her eyes."],"id":437,"m":"For God's sake, lords, lead away my sorrowful Queen, for tears are blocking the floodgates of her eyes."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["O Jesu, he doth it as like one of these harlotry players as ever","I see!"],"id":438,"m":"Oh Jesus, he does it just like one of those rascally actors I've seen!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Peace, good pint-pot; peace, good tickle-brain.—Harry, I do not","only marvel where thou spendest thy time, but also how thou art","accompanied: for though the camomile, the more it is trodden on,","the faster it grows, yet youth, the more it is wasted, the sooner","it wears. That thou art my son, I have partly thy mother's word,","partly my own opinion; but chiefly a villainous trick of thine eye,","and a foolish hanging of thy nether lip, that doth warrant me. If,","then, thou be son to me, here lies the point: Why, being son to me,","art thou so pointed at?","Shall the blessed Sun of heaven prove a micher, and eat blackberries?","a question not to be ask'd. Shall the son of England prove a thief,","and take purses? a question to be ask'd.","There is a thing, Harry, which thou hast often heard of, and it is","known to many in our land by the name of pitch: this pitch, as","ancient writers do report, doth defile; so doth the company thou","keepest: for, Harry, now I do not speak to thee in drink, but in","tears; not in pleasure, but in passion; not in words only,","but in woes also. And yet there is a virtuous man whom I have","often noted in thy company, but I know not his name."],"id":439,"m":"Quiet, good pint-pot; quiet, good brain-tickler. Harry, I wonder not only where you spend your time but also what company you keep. For though camomile grows faster the more it is trodden on, youth wears out sooner the more it is wasted. That you are my son, I have partly your mother's word and partly my own opinion, but mainly a nasty habit of your eye and a silly drooping of your lower lip that convinces me. So if you are my son, here's the point: why, being my son, are you pointed at so much? Should the blessed sun of heaven play truant and eat blackberries? That's a question not to be asked. Should the son of England turn out a thief and steal purses? That's a question to be asked. There is something, Harry, which you have often heard of, known to many in our land by the name of pitch. This pitch, as ancient writers tell us, makes you dirty; so does the company you keep. For, Harry, I'm not speaking to you now in drink but in tears, not in pleasure but in sorrow, not in words only but in griefs too. And yet there is a virtuous man whom I have often seen in your company, but I don't know his name."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["What manner of man, an it like your Majesty?"],"id":440,"m":"What sort of man, if it please your Majesty?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["A goodly portly man, i'faith, and a corpulent; of a cheerful look,","a pleasing eye, and a most noble carriage; and, as I think, his age","some fifty, or, by'r Lady, inclining to threescore; and now I","remember me, his name is Falstaff: if that man should be lewdly given,","he deceiveth me; for, Harry, I see virtue in his looks.","If, then, the tree may be known by the fruit, as the fruit by the tree,","then, peremptorily I speak it, there is virtue in that Falstaff: him","keep with, the rest banish. And tell me now, thou naughty varlet, tell","me where hast thou been this month?"],"id":441,"m":"A fine, stately man, honestly, and a plump one, with a cheerful look, a pleasing eye and a most noble bearing. I think his age is about fifty, or, by Our Lady, nearer sixty. And now I remember, his name is Falstaff. If that man is wicked, he deceives me, because, Harry, I see virtue in his looks. If, then, the tree can be known by its fruit, as the fruit by the tree, then I say firmly there is virtue in that Falstaff. Keep company with him, banish the rest. And now tell me, you wicked rascal, tell me where you've been this month."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Dost thou speak like a king? Do thou stand for me, and I'll play","my father."],"id":442,"m":"Do you call that speaking like a king? You stand in for me, and I'll play my father."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Depose me! if thou dost it half so gravely, so majestically, both","in word and matter, hang me up by the heels for a rabbit-sucker or a","poulter's hare."],"id":443,"m":"Depose me! If you do it half as seriously and majestically, in both words and content, hang me up by the heels like a baby rabbit or a hare in a poultry shop."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Well, here I am set."],"id":444,"m":"Well, here I am, seated."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["And here I stand.—Judge, my masters."],"id":445,"m":"And here I stand. Judge us, gentlemen."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Now, Harry, whence come you?"],"id":446,"m":"Now, Harry, where have you come from?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["My noble lord, from Eastcheap."],"id":447,"m":"My noble lord, from Eastcheap."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["The complaints I hear of thee are grievous."],"id":448,"m":"The complaints I hear about you are serious."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["'Sblood, my lord, they are false.—Nay, I'll tickle ye for a","young prince, i'faith."],"id":449,"m":"By God's blood, my lord, they're false. No, I'll entertain you as a young prince, I promise."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Swearest thou, ungracious boy? henceforth ne'er look on me. Thou art","violently carried away from grace: there is a devil haunts thee, in","the likeness of an old fat man,—a tun of man is thy companion. Why","dost thou converse with that trunk of humours, that bolting-hutch of","beastliness, that swollen parcel of dropsies, that huge bombard of","sack, that roasted Manningtree ox with the pudding in his belly, that","reverend Vice, that grey Iniquity, that father ruffian, that vanity","in years? Wherein is he good, but to taste sack and drink it? wherein","neat and cleanly, but to carve a capon and eat it? wherein cunning, but","in craft? wherein crafty, but in villany? wherein villainous, but in","all things? wherein worthy, but in nothing?"],"id":450,"m":"Are you swearing, you graceless boy? From now on never look at me. You've been dragged violently away from grace. A devil haunts you in the form of a fat old man; a barrel of a man is your companion. Why do you keep company with that trunk full of diseases, that sieve-bin of beastliness, that swollen bundle of dropsy, that huge leather jug of sherry, that roasted Manningtree ox with the stuffing in its belly, that respected Vice, that grey-haired Iniquity, that old ruffian, that aged emptiness? What is he good for except tasting sherry and drinking it? What is he neat and clean at except carving a chicken and eating it? What is he clever at except cunning? Cunning at what except villainy? Villainous in what? In everything. Worthy in what? In nothing."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["I would your Grace would take me with you: whom means your Grace?"],"id":451,"m":"I wish your Grace would let me follow you. Whom does your Grace mean?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["That villainous abominable misleader of youth, Falstaff, that old","white-bearded Satan."],"id":452,"m":"That villainous, abominable misleader of youth, Falstaff, that old white-bearded Satan."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["My lord, the man I know."],"id":453,"m":"My lord, I know the man."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["I know thou dost."],"id":454,"m":"I know you do."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["But to say I know more harm in him than in myself, were to say more","than I know. That he is old,—(the more the pity,—his white hairs do","witness it. If sack and sugar be a fault, God help the wicked! if to","be old and merry be a sin, then many an old host that I know is damn'd:","if to be fat be to be hated, then Pharaoh's lean kine are to be loved.","No, my good lord: banish Peto, banish Bardolph, banish Pointz; but,","for sweet Jack Falstaff, kind Jack Falstaff, true Jack Falstaff,","valiant Jack Falstaff, and therefore more valiant, being, as he is, old","Jack Falstaff, banish not him thy Harry's company, banish not him thy","Harry's company: banish plump Jack, and banish all the world."],"id":455,"m":"But to say I know more harm in him than in myself would be to say more than I know. That he's old, more's the pity, his white hairs prove. But if sherry and sugar are a crime, God help the wicked! If being old and merry is a sin, then many an old innkeeper I know is damned. If being fat means being hated, then Pharaoh's thin cows in the Bible are to be loved. No, my good lord: banish Peto, banish Bardolph, banish Poins. But sweet Jack Falstaff, kind Jack Falstaff, loyal Jack Falstaff, brave Jack Falstaff, and all the braver for being old Jack Falstaff, don't banish him from your Harry's company, don't banish him from your Harry's company. Banish plump Jack, and you banish all the world."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["I do, I will."],"id":456,"m":"I do, I will."},{"t":"sd","text":"A knocking heard.","id":457},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Hostess, Francis, and Bardolph.","id":458},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Bardolph, running.","id":459},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["O, my lord, my lord! the sheriff with a most monstrous watch is","at the door."],"id":460,"m":"Oh, my lord, my lord! The sheriff is at the door with an enormous body of watchmen."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Out, ye rogue!—Play out the play: I have much to say in the","behalf of that Falstaff."],"id":461,"m":"Get out, you rogue! Finish the play. I have a lot to say on behalf of that Falstaff."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter the Hostess, hastily.","id":462},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["O Jesu, my lord, my lord,—"],"id":463,"m":"Oh Jesus, my lord, my lord,"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Heigh, heigh! the Devil rides upon a fiddlestick: what's the matter?"],"id":464,"m":"Hey, hey! The Devil's riding a fiddlestick. What's the matter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["The sheriff and all the watch are at the door: they are come to","search the house. Shall I let them in?"],"id":465,"m":"The sheriff and the whole watch are at the door. They've come to search the house. Shall I let them in?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Dost thou hear, Hal? never call a true piece of gold a counterfeit:","thou art essentially mad without seeming so."],"id":466,"m":"Do you hear, Hal? Never call a genuine gold coin a fake. You're really mad without seeming so."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["And thou a natural coward, without instinct."],"id":467,"m":"And you're a born coward, without instinct."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["I deny your major: if you will deny the sheriff, so; if not, let him","enter: if I become not a cart as well as another man, a plague on my","bringing up! I hope I shall as soon be strangled with a halter as","another."],"id":468,"m":"I deny your premise. If you'll refuse to let the sheriff in, fine; if not, let him come in. If I don't look as good in the hangman's cart as any other man, a plague on my upbringing! I hope I'll be strangled with a rope as quickly as anyone else."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Go, hide thee behind the arras:—the rest walk, up above. Now,","my masters, for a true face and good conscience."],"id":469,"m":"Go and hide behind the tapestry. The rest of you, go upstairs. Now, gentlemen, for an honest face and a clear conscience."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Both which I have had; but their date is out, and therefore I'll","hide me."],"id":470,"m":"I've had both of those, but they've expired, so I'll hide."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Call in the sheriff.—"],"id":471,"m":"Call in the sheriff."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt all but the Prince and Pointz.","id":472},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Sheriff and Carrier.","id":473},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","cont":1,"lines":["Now, master sheriff, what's your will with me?"],"id":474,"m":"Now, Master Sheriff, what do you want with me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SHERIFF","lines":["First, pardon me, my lord. A hue-and-cry","Hath followed certain men unto this house."],"id":475,"m":"First, pardon me, my lord. A public pursuit has followed certain men to this house."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["What men?"],"id":476,"m":"What men?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SHERIFF","lines":["One of them is well known, my gracious lord,—","A gross fat man."],"id":477,"m":"One of them is well known, my gracious lord, a huge fat man."},{"t":"sp","who":"CARRIER","lines":["As fat as butter."],"id":478,"m":"As fat as butter."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["The man, I do assure you, is not here;","For I myself at this time have employ'd him.","And, sheriff, I will engage my word to thee,","That I will, by to-morrow dinner-time,","Send him to answer thee, or any man,","For any thing he shall be charged withal:","And so, let me entreat you leave the house."],"id":479,"m":"The man, I assure you, is not here, because I've sent him on an errand myself. And, sheriff, I give you my word that by lunchtime tomorrow I'll send him to answer to you, or to any man, for anything he's accused of. So please leave the house."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHERIFF","lines":["I will, my lord. There are two gentlemen","Have in this robbery lost three hundred marks."],"id":480,"m":"I will, my lord. Two gentlemen have lost three hundred marks in this robbery."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["It may be so: if he have robb'd these men,","He shall be answerable; and so, farewell."],"id":481,"m":"That may be so. If he robbed these men, he'll answer for it. And so, goodbye."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHERIFF","lines":["Good night, my noble lord."],"id":482,"m":"Good night, my noble lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["I think it is good morrow, is it not?"],"id":483,"m":"I think it's good morning, isn't it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SHERIFF","lines":["Indeed, my lord, I think't be two o'clock."],"id":484,"m":"Yes, my lord, I think it's two o'clock."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Sheriff and Carrier.","id":485},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["This oily rascal is known as well as Paul's. Go, call him forth."],"id":486,"m":"This greasy rascal is as well known as Saint Paul's Cathedral. Go and call him out."},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["Falstaff!—fast asleep behind the arras, and snorting like a","horse."],"id":487,"m":"Falstaff! He's fast asleep behind the tapestry, snorting like a horse."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Hark, how hard he fetches breath. Search his pockets."],"id":488,"m":"Listen to how hard he's breathing. Search his pockets."},{"t":"sd","text":"Pointz searches.","id":489},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","cont":1,"lines":["What hast thou found?"],"id":490,"m":"What have you found?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["Nothing but papers, my lord."],"id":491,"m":"Nothing but papers, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Let's see what they be: read them."],"id":492,"m":"Let's see what they are. Read them."},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["[Reads] Item, A capon, 2s. 2d.","Item, Sauce, 4d.","Item, Sack two gallons, 5s. 8d.","Item, Anchovies and sack after supper, 2s. 6d.","Item, Bread, ob."],"id":493,"m":"Item, a chicken, two shillings and twopence. Item, sauce, fourpence. Item, sherry, two gallons, five shillings and eightpence. Item, anchovies and sherry after supper, two shillings and sixpence. Item, bread, a halfpenny."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["O monstrous! but one half-pennyworth of bread to this intolerable","deal of sack! What there is else, keep close; we'll read it at more","advantage: there let him sleep till day.","I'll to the Court in the morning. We must all to the wars, and thy","place shall be honourable. I'll procure this fat rogue a charge of","foot; and I know his death will be a march of twelve-score. The money","shall be paid back again with advantage. Be with me betimes in the","morning; and so, good morrow, Pointz."],"id":494,"m":"Oh, monstrous! Only a halfpenny's worth of bread to this unbelievable amount of sherry! Keep the rest of the papers safe; we'll read them at a better time. Let him sleep there till daylight. I'll go to Court in the morning. We must all go to the wars, and you'll have an honourable position. I'll get this fat rogue command of a company of foot soldiers; I know a march of two hundred and forty yards will kill him. The money will be paid back with interest. Come to me early in the morning. And so, good morning, Poins."},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["Good morrow, good my lord."],"id":495,"m":"Good morning, my good lord."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":496},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"I","loc":"Bangor. A Room in the Archdeacon's House.","id":497},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Hotspur, Worcester, Mortimer, and Glendower.","id":498},{"t":"sp","who":"MORTIMER","lines":["These promises are fair, the parties sure,","And our induction full of prosperous hope."],"id":499,"m":"These promises are good, our allies reliable, and our beginning full of hope of success."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["Lord Mortimer,—and cousin Glendower,—Will you sit down?—","And uncle Worcester,—A plague upon it! I have forgot the map."],"id":500,"m":"Lord Mortimer, and cousin Glendower, will you sit down? And uncle Worcester... A plague on it! I've forgotten the map."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLENDOWER","lines":["No, here it is.","Sit, cousin Percy; sit, good cousin Hotspur;","For by that name as oft as Lancaster","Doth speak of you, his cheek looks pale, and with","A rising sigh he wisheth you in Heaven."],"id":501,"m":"No, here it is. Sit, cousin Percy, sit, good cousin Hotspur, because whenever King Henry calls you by that name, his cheek turns pale, and with a rising sigh he wishes you were in heaven."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["And you in Hell, as oft as he hears Owen Glendower spoke of."],"id":502,"m":"And you in hell, whenever he hears Owen Glendower mentioned."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLENDOWER","lines":["I cannot blame him: at my nativity","The front of heaven was full of fiery shapes,","Of burning cressets; ay, and at my birth","The frame and huge foundation of the Earth","Shaked like a coward."],"id":503,"m":"I can't blame him. When I was born, the face of the sky was full of fiery shapes, of blazing torches; yes, and at my birth the frame and huge foundation of the earth shook like a coward."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["Why, so it would have done at the same season, if your mother's","cat had but kitten'd, though yourself had never been born."],"id":504,"m":"Why, it would have done that at the same time if your mother's cat had just had kittens, even if you'd never been born at all."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLENDOWER","lines":["I say the Earth did shake when I was born."],"id":505,"m":"I tell you the earth shook when I was born."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["And I say the Earth was not of my mind, if you suppose as","fearing you it shook."],"id":506,"m":"And I tell you the earth didn't agree with me, if you think it shook out of fear of you."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLENDOWER","lines":["The Heavens were all on fire, the Earth did tremble."],"id":507,"m":"The heavens were all on fire, the earth trembled."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["O, then th' Earth shook to see the Heavens on fire,","And not in fear of your nativity.","Diseased Nature oftentimes breaks forth","In strange eruptions; oft the teeming Earth","Is with a kind of colic pinch'd and vex'd","By the imprisoning of unruly wind","Within her womb; which, for enlargement striving,","Shakes the old beldam Earth, and topples down","Steeples and moss-grown towers. At your birth,","Our grandam Earth, having this distemperature,","In passion shook."],"id":508,"m":"Oh, then the earth shook to see the heavens on fire, not out of fear of your birth. Sick nature often breaks out in strange eruptions. Often the pregnant earth is griped and troubled with a kind of colic, caused by unruly wind trapped inside her womb, which, struggling to get out, shakes the old grandmother earth and topples steeples and moss-covered towers. At your birth our grandmother earth, suffering from this disorder, shook in a fit."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLENDOWER","lines":["Cousin, of many men","I do not bear these crossings. Give me leave","To tell you once again, that at my birth","The front of heaven was full of fiery shapes;","The goats ran from the mountains, and the herds","Were strangely clamorous to the frighted fields.","These signs have mark'd me extraordinary;","And all the courses of my life do show","I am not in the roll of common men.","Where is he living,—clipp'd in with the sea","That chides the banks of England, Scotland, Wales,—","Which calls me pupil, or hath read to me?","And bring him out that is but woman's son","Can trace me in the tedious ways of art,","And hold me pace in deep experiments."],"id":509,"m":"Cousin, there are not many men from whom I'll take such contradiction. Allow me to tell you once more that at my birth the face of the sky was full of fiery shapes, the goats ran down from the mountains and the herds made a strange noise in the frightened fields. These signs have marked me out as extraordinary, and the whole course of my life shows that I am not on the list of ordinary men. Where is the man living, anywhere within the sea that beats against the shores of England, Scotland and Wales, who can call me his pupil or say he has taught me? And show me any son of a woman who can follow me along the hard paths of magic and keep up with me in deep experiments."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["I think there is no man speaks better Welsh.—I'll to dinner."],"id":510,"m":"I don't think anyone speaks better Welsh. I'm going to dinner."},{"t":"sp","who":"MORTIMER","lines":["Peace, cousin Percy; you will make him mad."],"id":511,"m":"Quiet, cousin Percy; you'll drive him mad."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLENDOWER","lines":["I can call spirits from the vasty deep."],"id":512,"m":"I can call up spirits from the vast deep."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["Why, so can I, or so can any man;","But will they come when you do call for them?"],"id":513,"m":"Why, so can I, or so can anyone; but will they come when you call them?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GLENDOWER","lines":["Why, I can teach you, cousin, to command the Devil."],"id":514,"m":"Why, I can teach you, cousin, to command the Devil."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["And I can teach thee, coz, to shame the Devil","By telling truth: tell truth, and shame the Devil.","If thou have power to raise him, bring him hither,","And I'll be sworn I've power to shame him hence.","O, while you live, tell truth, and shame the Devil!"],"id":515,"m":"And I can teach you, cousin, to shame the Devil by telling the truth: tell the truth and shame the Devil. If you have the power to raise him, bring him here, and I swear I have the power to shame him away. Oh, as long as you live, tell the truth and shame the Devil!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MORTIMER","lines":["Come, come, no more of this unprofitable chat."],"id":516,"m":"Come on, come on, no more of this pointless talk."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLENDOWER","lines":["Three times hath Henry Bolingbroke made head","Against my power; thrice from the banks of Wye","And sandy-bottom'd Severn have I sent","Him bootless home and weather-beaten back."],"id":517,"m":"Three times Henry Bolingbroke has led an army against my forces; three times I have sent him back home from the banks of the Wye and the sandy-bottomed Severn, empty-handed and battered by the weather."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["Home without boots, and in foul weather too!","How 'scaped he agues, in the Devil's name!"],"id":518,"m":"Home without boots, and in bad weather too! How in the Devil's name did he escape catching a fever?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GLENDOWER","lines":["Come, here's the map: shall we divide our right","According to our threefold order ta'en?"],"id":519,"m":"Come, here's the map. Shall we divide up what we're entitled to, according to our three-way agreement?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MORTIMER","lines":["Th' archdeacon hath divided it","Into three limits very equally.","England, from Trent and Severn hitherto,","By south and east is to my part assign'd:","All westward, Wales beyond the Severn shore,","And all the fertile land within that bound,","To Owen Glendower:—and, dear coz, to you","The remnant northward, lying off from Trent.","And our indentures tripartite are drawn;","Which being sealed interchangeably,—","A business that this night may execute,—","To-morrow, cousin Percy, you, and I,","And my good Lord of Worcester, will set forth","To meet your father and the Scottish power,","As is appointed us, at Shrewsbury.","My father Glendower is not ready yet,","Nor shall we need his help these fourteen days:—","[To Glendower] Within that space you may have drawn together","Your tenants, friends, and neighbouring gentlemen."],"id":520,"m":"The Archdeacon has divided it very equally into three parts. England, from the Trent and the Severn to here, to the south and east, is assigned to me. Everything westward, Wales beyond the Severn shore and all the fertile land within that boundary, goes to Owen Glendower. And to you, dear cousin, the rest to the north, lying beyond the Trent. Our three-way contracts are drawn up, and once they are sealed by each of us, which can be done tonight, tomorrow, cousin Percy, you, I and my good lord of Worcester will set out to meet your father and the Scottish forces at Shrewsbury, as arranged. My father-in-law Glendower isn't ready yet, and we won't need his help for a fortnight. In that time you can have gathered your tenants, friends and the gentlemen of your neighbourhood."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLENDOWER","lines":["A shorter time shall send me to you, lords:","And in my conduct shall your ladies come;","From whom you now must steal, and take no leave,","For there will be a world of water shed","Upon the parting of your wives and you."],"id":521,"m":"I'll come to you sooner than that, lords, and I'll escort your ladies. You must slip away from them now without saying goodbye, because there'll be an ocean of tears shed when you and your wives part."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["Methinks my moiety, north from Burton here,","In quantity equals not one of yours.","See how this river comes me cranking in,","And cuts me from the best of all my land","A huge half-moon, a monstrous cantle out.","I'll have the current in this place damn'd up;","And here the smug and silver Trent shall run","In a new channel, fair and evenly:","It shall not wind with such a deep indent,","To rob me of so rich a bottom here."],"id":522,"m":"It seems to me that my share, north from Burton here, isn't as big as either of yours. Look how this river comes winding in on me and cuts a huge half-moon, a monstrous chunk, out of the best of my land. I'll have the river dammed up at this point, and the smooth silver Trent will run here in a new channel, neatly and straight. It won't wind with such a deep curve to rob me of such a rich piece of valley here."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLENDOWER","lines":["Not wind? it shall, it must; you see it doth."],"id":523,"m":"Not wind? It will, it must; you can see it does."},{"t":"sp","who":"MORTIMER","lines":["Yea, but","Mark how he bears his course, and runs me up","With like advantage on the other side;","Gelding th' opposed continent as much","As on the other side it takes from you."],"id":524,"m":"Yes, but look how it runs on and cuts back into my land with the same advantage on the other side, taking away as much from the land opposite as it takes from you on this side."},{"t":"sp","who":"WORCESTER","lines":["Yea, but a little charge will trench him here,","And on this north side win this cape of land;","And then he runneth straight and evenly."],"id":525,"m":"Yes, but a little cost will dig him a new channel here and win this point of land on the north side, and then it will run straight and even."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["I'll have it so: a little charge will do it."],"id":526,"m":"I'll have it that way. A little cost will do it."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLENDOWER","lines":["I will not have it alter'd."],"id":527,"m":"I won't have it changed."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["Will not you?"],"id":528,"m":"Won't you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GLENDOWER","lines":["No, nor you shall not."],"id":529,"m":"No, and you won't either."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["Who shall say me nay?"],"id":530,"m":"Who will tell me no?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GLENDOWER","lines":["Why, that will I."],"id":531,"m":"Why, I will."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["Let me not understand you, then; speak it in Welsh."],"id":532,"m":"Then make sure I don't understand you: say it in Welsh."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLENDOWER","lines":["I can speak English, lord, as well as you;","For I was train'd up in the English Court;","Where, being but young, I framed to the harp","Many an English ditty lovely well,","And gave the tongue a helpful ornament,","A virtue that was never seen in you."],"id":533,"m":"I can speak English, my lord, as well as you, because I was brought up at the English court, where, while still young, I set many English songs very beautifully to the harp and gave the language a helpful decoration, an accomplishment you've never shown."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["Marry, and I am glad of it with all my heart:","I had rather be a kitten, and cry mew,","Than one of these same metre ballet-mongers;","I had rather hear a brazen canstick turn'd,","Or a dry wheel grate on the axletree;","And that would set my teeth nothing on edge,","Nothing so much as mincing poetry:","'Tis like the forced gait of a shuffling nag."],"id":534,"m":"Well, I'm glad of that with all my heart. I'd rather be a kitten and cry \"miaow\" than one of these verse ballad-sellers. I'd rather hear a brass candlestick being turned on a lathe, or a dry wheel grating on its axle; that wouldn't set my teeth on edge at all, nothing like as much as affected poetry. It's like the forced pace of a hobbling horse."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLENDOWER","lines":["Come, you shall have Trent turn'd."],"id":535,"m":"Come on, you shall have the Trent diverted."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["I do not care: I'll give thrice so much land","To any well-deserving friend;","But in the way of bargain, mark ye me,","I'll cavil on the ninth part of a hair.","Are the indentures drawn? shall we be gone?"],"id":536,"m":"I don't care. I'll give three times as much land to any friend who deserves it; but when it comes to a bargain, mind you, I'll argue over the ninth part of a hair. Are the contracts drawn up? Shall we go?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GLENDOWER","lines":["The Moon shines fair; you may away by night:","I'll in and haste the writer, and withal","Break with your wives of your departure hence:","I am afraid my daughter will run mad,","So much she doteth on her Mortimer."],"id":537,"m":"The moon is shining brightly; you can leave tonight. I'll go in and hurry up the clerk, and at the same time break the news of your departure to your wives. I'm afraid my daughter will go mad, she loves her Mortimer so much."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":538},{"t":"sp","who":"MORTIMER","lines":["Fie, cousin Percy! how you cross my father!"],"id":539,"m":"Shame on you, cousin Percy! How you provoke my father-in-law!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["I cannot choose: sometimes he angers me","With telling me of the moldwarp and the ant,","Of the dreamer Merlin and his prophecies,","And of a dragon and a finless fish,","A clip-wing'd griffin and a moulten raven,","A couching lion and a ramping cat,","And such a deal of skimble-skamble stuff","As puts me from my faith. I tell you what,","He held me last night at the least nine hours","In reckoning up the several devils' names","That were his lacqueys: I cried hum, and well,","But mark'd him not a word. O, he's as tedious","As a tired horse, a railing wife;","Worse than a smoky house: I had rather live","With cheese and garlic in a windmill, far,","Than feed on cates and have him talk to me","In any summer-house in Christendom."],"id":540,"m":"I can't help it. Sometimes he makes me angry by telling me about the mole and the ant, about the dreamer Merlin and his prophecies, and about a dragon and a fish without fins, a griffin with clipped wings and a raven that's lost its feathers, a lion lying down and a cat rearing up, and such a load of rambling nonsense that it tries my patience. I'll tell you what: last night he kept me for at least nine hours listing the names of the various devils that were his servants. I said \"hmm\" and \"very good\", but didn't take in a word. Oh, he's as tiresome as a tired horse or a nagging wife, worse than a smoky house. I'd much rather live on cheese and garlic in a windmill far away than eat delicacies and have him talk to me in any fine country house in Christendom."},{"t":"sp","who":"MORTIMER","lines":["In faith, he is a worthy gentleman;","Exceedingly well-read, and profited","In strange concealments; valiant as a lion,","And wondrous affable, and as bountiful","As mines of India. Shall I tell you, cousin?","He holds your temper in a high respect,","And curbs himself even of his natural scope","When you do cross his humour; faith, he does:","I warrant you, that man is not alive","Might so have tempted him as you have done,","Without the taste of danger and reproof:","But do not use it oft, let me entreat you."],"id":541,"m":"Honestly, he's a worthy gentleman, extremely well read and skilled in secret arts, as brave as a lion and wonderfully friendly, and as generous as the mines of India. Shall I tell you something, cousin? He has a great respect for your temperament, and he holds himself back from his natural freedom when you cross him; he really does. I promise you, there's no man alive who could have provoked him as you have without feeling danger and rebuke. But please don't do it often, I beg you."},{"t":"sp","who":"WORCESTER","lines":["In faith, my lord, you are too wilful-blunt;","And since your coming hither have done enough","To put him quite beside his patience.","You must needs learn, lord, to amend this fault:","Though sometimes it show greatness, courage, blood—","And that's the dearest grace it renders you,—","Yet oftentimes it doth present harsh rage,","Defect of manners, want of government,","Pride, haughtiness, opinion, and disdain;","The least of which haunting a nobleman","Loseth men's hearts, and leaves behind a stain","Upon the beauty of all parts besides,","Beguiling them of commendation."],"id":542,"m":"Honestly, my lord, you're too stubborn and blunt, and since you came here you've done enough to drive him completely out of patience. You must learn to correct this fault, my lord. Although it sometimes shows greatness, courage and spirit, and that's the best credit it gives you, yet it often shows harsh anger, lack of manners and self-control, pride, arrogance, self-importance and scorn. Even the least of these, when it clings to a nobleman, loses him people's affection and leaves a stain on all his other good qualities, cheating them of praise."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["Well, I am school'd: good manners be your speed!","Here come our wives, and let us take our leave."],"id":543,"m":"Well, I've been taught my lesson. May good manners bring you success! Here come our wives; let's say goodbye."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Glendower, with Lady Mortimer and Lady Percy.","id":544},{"t":"sp","who":"MORTIMER","lines":["This is the deadly spite that angers me,","My wife can speak no English, I no Welsh."],"id":545,"m":"This is the cruel thing that annoys me: my wife can speak no English, and I no Welsh."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLENDOWER","lines":["My daughter weeps: she will not part with you;","She'll be a soldier too, she'll to the wars."],"id":546,"m":"My daughter's weeping; she won't part from you. She wants to be a soldier too and go to the wars."},{"t":"sp","who":"MORTIMER","lines":["Good father, tell her that she and my aunt Percy","Shall follow in your conduct speedily."],"id":547,"m":"Good father, tell her that she and my aunt Percy will follow soon under your escort."},{"t":"sd","text":"Glendower speaks to Lady Mortimer in Welsh, and she answers him in the same.","id":548},{"t":"sp","who":"GLENDOWER","lines":["She's desperate here; a peevish self-will'd harlotry,","One that no persuasion can do good upon."],"id":549,"m":"She's desperate here; a stubborn, self-willed hussy that no persuasion can do any good with."},{"t":"sd","text":"Lady Mortimer speaks to Mortimer in Welsh.","id":550},{"t":"sp","who":"MORTIMER","lines":["I understand thy looks: that pretty Welsh","Which thou pour'st down from these swelling heavens","I am too perfect in; and, but for shame,","In such a parley should I answer thee."],"id":551,"m":"I understand your looks. That pretty Welsh that you pour down from these brimming eyes, I understand only too well; and if I weren't ashamed, I'd answer you in the same language of tears."},{"t":"sd","text":"Lady Mortimer speaks to him again in Welsh.","id":552},{"t":"sp","who":"MORTIMER","cont":1,"lines":["I understand thy kisses, and thou mine,","And that's a feeling disputation:","But I will never be a truant, love,","Till I have learn'd thy language; for thy tongue","Makes Welsh as sweet as ditties highly penn'd,","Sung by a fair queen in a Summer's bower,","With ravishing division, to her lute."],"id":553,"m":"I understand your kisses and you understand mine, and that's a conversation of feeling. But I'll never stop studying, love, until I've learned your language, because your tongue makes Welsh as sweet as finely written songs sung beautifully to her lute by a lovely queen in a summer bower."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLENDOWER","lines":["Nay, if you melt, then will she run mad."],"id":554,"m":"No, if you melt like that, she'll go mad."},{"t":"sd","text":"Lady Mortimer speaks to Mortimer again in Welsh.","id":555},{"t":"sp","who":"MORTIMER","lines":["O, I am ignorance itself in this!"],"id":556,"m":"Oh, I'm ignorance itself in this!"},{"t":"sp","who":"GLENDOWER","lines":["She bids you on the wanton rushes lay you down,","And rest your gentle head upon her lap,","And she will sing the song that pleaseth you,","And on your eyelids crown the god of sleep,","Charming your blood with pleasing heaviness;","Making such difference betwixt wake and sleep,","As is the difference betwixt day and night,","The hour before the heavenly-harness'd team","Begins his golden progress in the East."],"id":557,"m":"She asks you to lie down on the soft rushes and rest your gentle head in her lap, and she'll sing the song you like and crown your eyelids with the god of sleep, enchanting your blood with a pleasant drowsiness, making the state between waking and sleeping like the twilight between day and night in the hour before the sun's heavenly team of horses begins its golden journey in the east."},{"t":"sp","who":"MORTIMER","lines":["With all my heart I'll sit and hear her sing:","By that time will our book, I think, be drawn."],"id":558,"m":"With all my heart I'll sit and listen to her sing. By then, I think, our contract will be drawn up."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLENDOWER","lines":["Do so:","An those musicians that shall play to you","Hang in the air a thousand leagues from hence,","And straight they shall be here: sit, and attend."],"id":559,"m":"Do. And the musicians who will play for you are hanging in the air a thousand leagues from here, yet they'll be here at once. Sit down and listen."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["Come, Kate, thou art perfect in lying down: come, quick,","quick, that I may lay my head in thy lap."],"id":560,"m":"Come, Kate, you're perfect at lying down. Come on, quick, quick, so I can lay my head in your lap."},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY PERCY","lines":["Go, ye giddy goose."],"id":561,"m":"Go away, you silly goose."},{"t":"sd","text":"The music plays.","id":562},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["Now I perceive the Devil understands Welsh;","And 'tis no marvel he's so humorous.","By'r Lady, he's a good musician."],"id":563,"m":"Now I see the Devil understands Welsh, and it's no wonder he's so moody. By Our Lady, he's a good musician."},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY PERCY","lines":["Then should you be nothing but musical; for you are","altogether governed by humours. Lie still, ye thief, and hear","the lady sing in Welsh."],"id":564,"m":"Then you should be nothing but musical, because you're completely ruled by your moods. Lie still, you thief, and listen to the lady sing in Welsh."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["I had rather hear Lady, my brach, howl in Irish."],"id":565,"m":"I'd rather hear my hound Lady howl in Irish."},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY PERCY","lines":["Wouldst thou have thy head broken?"],"id":566,"m":"Do you want your head broken?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["No."],"id":567,"m":"No."},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY PERCY","lines":["Then be still."],"id":568,"m":"Then be quiet."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["Neither; 'tis a woman's fault."],"id":569,"m":"I won't do that either; it's a woman's fault."},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY PERCY","lines":["Now God help thee!"],"id":570,"m":"Now God help you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["Peace! she sings."],"id":571,"m":"Quiet! She's singing."},{"t":"sd","text":"A Welsh song by Lady Mortimer.","id":572},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","cont":1,"lines":["Come, Kate, I'll have your song too."],"id":573,"m":"Come, Kate, I'll have your song too."},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY PERCY","lines":["Not mine, in good sooth."],"id":574,"m":"Not mine, truly."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["Not yours, in good sooth! 'Heart! you swear like a","comfit-maker's wife. Not mine, in good sooth; and, As true","as I live; and, As God shall mend me; and, As sure as day;","And givest such sarcenet surety for thy oaths,","As if thou ne'er walk'dst further than Finsbury.","Swear me, Kate, like a lady as thou art,","A good mouth-filling oath; and leave in sooth,","And such protest of pepper-gingerbread,","To velvet-guards and Sunday-citizens. Come, sing."],"id":575,"m":"\"Not mine, truly\"! By God's heart, you swear like a sweet-maker's wife. \"Not mine, truly,\" and \"As true as I live,\" and \"As God shall save me,\" and \"As sure as day.\" You give such flimsy silk guarantees for your oaths, as if you'd never walked further than Finsbury Fields. Swear like the lady you are, Kate, a good mouth-filling oath, and leave \"truly\" and such gingerbread protests to people in velvet-trimmed clothes and Sunday-best citizens. Come on, sing."},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY PERCY","lines":["I will not sing."],"id":576,"m":"I won't sing."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["'Tis the next way to turn tailor, or be redbreast-teacher.","An the indentures be drawn, I'll away within these two hours;","and so, come in when ye will."],"id":577,"m":"Singing is the quickest way to become a tailor or a teacher of robins. If the contracts are drawn up, I'll leave within two hours; and so come in when you like."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":578},{"t":"sp","who":"GLENDOWER","lines":["Come, come, Lord Mortimer; you are as slow","As hot Lord Percy is on fire to go.","By this our book's drawn; we'll but seal, and then","To horse immediately."],"id":579,"m":"Come, come, Lord Mortimer, you're as slow as hot-tempered Lord Percy is on fire to go. By now our contract's drawn up; we'll just seal it, and then to horse at once."},{"t":"sp","who":"MORTIMER","lines":["With all my heart."],"id":580,"m":"With all my heart."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":581},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"II","loc":"London. A Room in the Palace.","id":582},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter King Henry, Prince Henry, and Lords.","id":583},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Lords, give us leave; the Prince of Wales and I","Must have some private conference: but be near at hand,","For we shall presently have need of you."],"id":584,"m":"Lords, leave us; the Prince of Wales and I must have a private conversation. But stay close by, because we'll need you shortly."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Lords.","id":585},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","cont":1,"lines":["I know not whether God will have it so,","For some displeasing service I have done,","That, in His secret doom, out of my blood","He'll breed revengement and a scourge for me;","But thou dost, in thy passages of life,","Make me believe that thou art only mark'd","For the hot vengeance and the rod of Heaven","To punish my mistreadings. Tell me else,","Could such inordinate and low desires,","Such poor, such base, such lewd, such mean attempts,","Such barren pleasures, rude society,","As thou art match'd withal and grafted to,","Accompany the greatness of thy blood,","And hold their level with thy princely heart?"],"id":586,"m":"I don't know whether God wills it, for some offence I have committed, that in his secret judgement he will breed revenge and a punishment for me out of my own blood. But by the way you live your life you make me believe that you are marked out only as the burning vengeance and rod of heaven to punish my wrongdoings. Otherwise, tell me, how could such unruly and low desires, such poor, base, coarse and mean activities, such empty pleasures and rough company as you are joined and bound to, go along with the greatness of your blood and stand level with your princely heart?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["So please your Majesty, I would I could","Quit all offences with as clear excuse","As well as I am doubtless I can purge","Myself of many I am charged withal:","Yet such extenuation let me beg,","As, in reproof of many tales devised","By smiling pick-thanks and base news-mongers,—","Which oft the ear of greatness needs must hear,—","I may, for some things true, wherein my youth","Hath faulty wander'd and irregular,","Find pardon on my true submission."],"id":587,"m":"If it please your Majesty, I wish I could clear myself of all offences as plainly as I am sure I can clear myself of many I am accused of. Yet let me beg this much allowance: that, once I have disproved many stories invented by smiling flatterers and low gossips, which great men must often listen to, I may be pardoned, on my sincere submission, for the things that are true, in which my youth has wandered into faults and disorder."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["God pardon thee! Yet let me wonder, Harry,","At thy affections, which do hold a wing","Quite from the flight of all thy ancestors.","Thy place in Council thou hast rudely lost,","Which by thy younger brother is supplied;","And art almost an alien to the hearts","Of all the Court and princes of my blood:","The hope and expectation of thy time","Is ruin'd; and the soul of every man","Prophetically does forethink thy fall.","Had I so lavish of my presence been,","So common-hackney'd in the eyes of men,","So stale and cheap to vulgar company,","Opinion, that did help me to the crown,","Had still kept loyal to possession,","And left me in reputeless banishment,","A fellow of no mark nor likelihood.","By being seldom seen, I could not stir","But, like a comet, I was wonder'd at;","That men would tell their children, This is he;","Others would say, Where, which is Bolingbroke?","And then I stole all courtesy from Heaven,","And dress'd myself in such humility,","That I did pluck allegiance from men's hearts,","Loud shouts and salutations from their mouths,","Even in the presence of the crowned King.","Thus did I keep my person fresh and new;","My presence, like a robe pontifical,","Ne'er seen but wonder'd at: and so my state,","Seldom but sumptuous, showed like a feast,","And won by rareness such solemnity.","The skipping King, he ambled up and down","With shallow jesters and rash bavin wits,","Soon kindled and soon burnt; carded his state,","Mingled his royalty, with capering fools;","Had his great name profaned with their scorns;","And gave his countenance, against his name,","To laugh at gibing boys, and stand the push","Of every beardless vain comparative;","Grew a companion to the common streets,","Enfeoff'd himself to popularity;","That, being daily swallow'd by men's eyes,","They surfeited with honey, and began","To loathe the taste of sweetness, whereof a little","More than a little is by much too much.","So, when he had occasion to be seen,","He was but as the cuckoo is in June,","Heard, not regarded; seen, but with such eyes","As, sick and blunted with community,","Afford no extraordinary gaze,","Such as is bent on sun-like majesty","When it shines seldom in admiring eyes;","But rather drowsed, and hung their eyelids down,","Slept in his face, and render'd such aspect","As cloudy men use to their adversaries,","Being with his presence glutted, gorged, and full.","And in that very line, Harry, stand'st thou;","For thou hast lost thy princely privilege","With vile participation: not an eye","But is a-weary of thy common sight,","Save mine, which hath desired to see thee more;","Which now doth that I would not have it do,","Make blind itself with foolish tenderness."],"id":588,"m":"God pardon you! But let me wonder, Harry, at your tastes, which fly in a completely different direction from all your ancestors. You have rudely lost your place in the Council, which your younger brother has filled, and you are almost a stranger to the hearts of the whole Court and the princes of my blood. The hope and expectation of your future is ruined, and every man's soul is predicting your downfall. If I had been so free with my presence, so worn out in men's eyes like a hired horse, so stale and cheap in common company, public opinion, which helped me to the crown, would have stayed loyal to the king in possession and left me in disgraced exile, a fellow of no importance or prospects. By being seldom seen, I couldn't move without being wondered at like a comet, so that men would tell their children, \"That's him,\" and others would say, \"Where? Which one is Bolingbroke?\" And then I stole every grace from heaven and dressed myself in such humility that I plucked loyalty from men's hearts and loud cheers and greetings from their mouths, even in the presence of the crowned King. In this way I kept myself fresh and new; my presence, like a bishop's robe, was never seen without being wondered at. And so my public appearances, rare but splendid, were like a feast and gained great dignity by being rare. The frivolous King Richard ambled up and down with shallow jesters and quick, brushwood wits, soon lit and soon burnt out. He watered down his majesty and mixed his royalty with capering fools, let his great name be soiled by their mockery, and, against the dignity of his title, lent his presence to laughing at jeering boys and putting up with the challenges of every beardless, empty mocker. He became a companion of the common streets and gave himself away to popularity, so that, being swallowed daily by men's eyes, they were sick of honey and began to hate the taste of sweetness, of which a little more than a little is far too much. So when he had to appear in public, he was like the cuckoo in June, heard but not noticed; seen, but with eyes that, tired and dulled by familiarity, gave him no special attention, such as is given to sun-like majesty when it shines rarely on admiring eyes. Instead they were drowsy, let their eyelids droop, slept in his face, and gave him the sort of look that sullen men give their enemies, being glutted, gorged and full of his presence. And you are in exactly that position, Harry, because you've lost your princely privilege by mixing with low company. There isn't an eye that isn't tired of the common sight of you, except mine, which has wanted to see more of you, and which now does what I don't want it to do: blinds itself with foolish, loving tears."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["I shall hereafter, my thrice-gracious lord,","Be more myself."],"id":589,"m":"From now on, my most gracious lord, I'll be more truly myself."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["For all the world,","As thou art to this hour, was Richard then","When I from France set foot at Ravenspurg;","And even as I was then is Percy now.","Now, by my sceptre, and my soul to boot,","He hath more worthy interest to the state","Than thou, the shadow of succession;","For, of no right, nor colour like to right,","He doth fill fields with harness in the realm,","Turns head against the lion's armed jaws;","And, being no more in debt to years than thou,","Leads ancient lords and reverend bishops on","To bloody battles and to bruising arms.","What never-dying honour hath he got","Against renowned Douglas! whose high deeds,","Whose hot incursions, and great name in arms,","Holds from all soldiers chief majority","And military title capital","Through all the kingdoms that acknowledge Christ:","Thrice hath this Hotspur, Mars in swathing-clothes,","This infant warrior, in his enterprises","Discomfited great Douglas; ta'en him once,","Enlarged him, and made a friend of him,","To fill the mouth of deep defiance up,","And shake the peace and safety of our throne.","And what say you to this? Percy, Northumberland,","Th' Archbishop's Grace of York, Douglas, and Mortimer","Capitulate against us, and are up.","But wherefore do I tell these news to thee?","Why, Harry, do I tell thee of my foes,","Which art my near'st and dearest enemy?","Thou that art like enough,—through vassal fear,","Base inclination, and the start of spleen,—","To fight against me under Percy's pay,","To dog his heels, and curtsy at his frowns,","To show how much thou art degenerate."],"id":590,"m":"For all the world, what you are at this moment, Richard was then, when I landed from France at Ravenspurgh; and just as I was then, Percy is now. Now, by my sceptre and my soul as well, he has a worthier claim to the state than you, the mere shadow of an heir. With no right, or anything that looks like right, he fills the fields of the kingdom with armour, turns to face the armed jaws of the lion, and, though he's no older than you, leads old lords and reverend bishops into bloody battles and bruising combat. What everlasting honour he has won against the famous Douglas, whose great deeds, fierce raids and great name in arms give him the highest rank and military title among all soldiers in every Christian kingdom! Three times this Hotspur, Mars in nappies, this infant warrior, has defeated great Douglas in his campaigns. He captured him once, set him free and made a friend of him, to swell the cry of deep defiance and shake the peace and security of our throne. And what do you say to this? Percy, Northumberland, his Grace the Archbishop of York, Douglas and Mortimer have made a pact against us and are in arms. But why do I tell you this news? Why, Harry, do I tell you about my enemies, when you are my closest and dearest enemy? You're quite likely, out of cowardly fear, low tendencies and a fit of bad temper, to fight against me in Percy's pay, to follow at his heels and bow when he frowns, to show how degenerate you are."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Do not think so; you shall not find it so:","And God forgive them that so much have sway'd","Your Majesty's good thoughts away from me!","I will redeem all this on Percy's head,","And, in the closing of some glorious day,","Be bold to tell you that I am your son;","When I will wear a garment all of blood,","And stain my favour in a bloody mask,","Which, wash'd away, shall scour my shame with it:","And that shall be the day, whene'er it lights,","That this same child of honour and renown,","This gallant Hotspur, this all-praised knight,","And your unthought-of Harry, chance to meet.","For every honour sitting on his helm,","Would they were multitudes, and on my head","My shames redoubled! for the time will come,","That I shall make this northern youth exchange","His glorious deeds for my indignities.","Percy is but my factor, good my lord,","T' engross up glorious deeds on my behalf;","And I will call him to so strict account,","That he shall render every glory up,","Yea, even the slightest worship of his time,","Or I will tear the reckoning from his heart.","This, in the name of God, I promise here:","The which if I perform, and do survive,","I do beseech your Majesty, may salve","The long-grown wounds of my intemperance:","If not, the end of life cancels all bands;","And I will die a hundred thousand deaths","Ere break the smallest parcel of this vow."],"id":591,"m":"Don't think so; you won't find it so. And God forgive those who have turned your Majesty's good opinion so far away from me! I will make up for all this on Percy's head, and at the end of some glorious day I'll be bold enough to tell you that I am your son, when I'll wear a garment all of blood and stain my face with a bloody mask, which, when it is washed away, will scour my shame away with it. And that will be the day, whenever it comes, when this same child of honour and fame, this gallant Hotspur, this much-praised knight, and your disregarded Harry happen to meet. I wish every honour that sits on his helmet were multiplied many times, and my shames on my head doubled! Because the time will come when I'll make this northern youth exchange his glorious deeds for my disgraces. Percy is only my agent, my good lord, buying up glorious deeds on my behalf; and I'll call him to such a strict account that he'll hand over every glory, yes, even the smallest honour he has ever won, or I'll tear the account from his heart. This, in the name of God, I promise here. If I carry it out and survive, I beg your Majesty to let it heal the long-standing wounds of my wild behaviour. If not, the end of life cancels all debts, and I'll die a hundred thousand deaths before I break the smallest part of this vow."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["A hundred thousand rebels die in this.","Thou shalt have charge and sovereign trust herein.—"],"id":592,"m":"A hundred thousand rebels die by this promise. You shall have a command and the highest trust in this war."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Sir Walter Blunt.","id":593},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","cont":1,"lines":["How now, good Blunt! thy looks are full of speed."],"id":594,"m":"How now, good Blunt! You look full of haste."},{"t":"sp","who":"BLUNT","lines":["So is the business that I come to speak of.","Lord Mortimer of Scotland hath sent word","That Douglas and the English rebels met","Th' eleventh of this month at Shrewsbury:","A mighty and a fearful head they are,","If promises be kept on every hand,","As ever offer'd foul play in a State."],"id":595,"m":"So is the business I've come to tell you about. Lord Mortimer of Scotland has sent word that Douglas and the English rebels met at Shrewsbury on the eleventh of this month. If promises are kept on every side, they are as mighty and fearful an army as ever threatened rebellion in a state."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["The Earl of Westmoreland set forth to-day;","With him my son, Lord John of Lancaster;","For this advertisement is five days old.","On Wednesday next you, Harry, shall set forward;","On Thursday we ourselves will march:","Our meeting is Bridgenorth: and, Harry, you","Shall march through Glostershire; by which account,","Our business valued, some twelve days hence","Our general forces at Bridgenorth shall meet.","Our hands are full of business: let's away;","Advantage feeds him fat, while men delay."],"id":596,"m":"The Earl of Westmoreland set out today, and with him my son, Lord John of Lancaster, because this news is five days old. Next Wednesday, Harry, you'll set out; on Thursday we will march ourselves. Our meeting place is Bridgnorth. And, Harry, you will march through Gloucestershire; so, by my reckoning of what needs to be done, our whole army will meet at Bridgnorth in about twelve days. We have our hands full of business. Let's go. Opportunity feeds the enemy fat while men delay."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":597},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"III","loc":"Eastcheap. A Room in the Boar's-Head Tavern.","id":598},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Falstaff and Bardolph.","id":599},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Bardolph, am I not fallen away vilely since this last action? do I","not bate? do I not dwindle? Why, my skin hangs about me like an","old lady's loose gown; I am withered like an old apple-John.","Well, I'll repent, and that suddenly, while I am in some liking; I","shall be out of heart shortly, and then I shall have no strength to","repent.","An I have not forgotten what the inside of a church is made of, I","am a peppercorn, a brewer's horse: the inside of a church!","Company, villainous company, hath been the spoil of me."],"id":600,"m":"Bardolph, haven't I wasted away dreadfully since this last business? Am I not shrinking? Am I not dwindling? Why, my skin hangs on me like an old lady's loose gown; I'm as wrinkled as an old dried apple. Well, I'll repent, and quickly, while I'm still in good shape. I'll lose heart soon, and then I won't have the strength to repent. If I haven't forgotten what the inside of a church looks like, I'm a peppercorn, a brewer's cart-horse. The inside of a church! Company, villainous company, has been the ruin of me."},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["Sir John, you are so fretful, you cannot live long."],"id":601,"m":"Sir John, you fret so much you can't live long."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Why, there is it: come, sing me a song; make me merry. I was as","virtuously given as a gentleman need to be; virtuous enough; swore","little; diced not above seven times a week; paid money that I borrowed","—three or four times; lived well, and in good compass: and now I live","out of all order, out of all compass."],"id":602,"m":"Well, there it is. Come on, sing me a song, cheer me up. I was as virtuously inclined as a gentleman needs to be: virtuous enough; swore little; played dice no more than seven times a week; paid back money I borrowed three or four times; lived well and within bounds. And now I live in total disorder, beyond all bounds."},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["Why, you are so fat, Sir John, that you must needs be out of all","compass,—out of all reasonable compass, Sir John."],"id":603,"m":"Well, you're so fat, Sir John, that you're bound to be out of all bounds, out of all reasonable bounds, Sir John."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Do thou amend thy face, and I'll amend my life: thou art our admiral,","thou bearest the lantern in the poop,—but 'tis in the nose of thee;","thou art the Knight of the Burning Lamp."],"id":604,"m":"You mend your face, and I'll mend my life. You're our flagship, you carry the lantern at the stern, but in your case it's in your nose. You're the Knight of the Burning Lamp."},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["Why, Sir John, my face does you no harm."],"id":605,"m":"Well, Sir John, my face does you no harm."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["No, I'll be sworn; I make as good use of it as many a man doth of a","death's-head or a memento mori: I never see thy face but I think upon","hell-fire, and Dives that lived in purple; for there he is in his robes,","burning, burning. If thou wert any way given to virtue, I would swear","by thy face; my oath should be, By this fire, that's God's angel: but","thou art altogether given over; and wert indeed, but for the light in","thy face, the son of utter darkness. When thou rann'st up Gad's-hill in","the night to catch my horse, if I did not think thou hadst been an ignis","fatuus or a ball of wildfire, there's no purchase in money. O, thou art","a perpetual triumph, an everlasting bonfire-light! Thou hast saved me a","thousand marks in links and torches, walking with thee in the night","betwixt tavern and tavern: but the sack that thou hast drunk me would","have bought me lights as good cheap at the dearest chandler's in Europe.","I have maintain'd that salamander of yours with fire any time this","two-and-thirty years; God reward me for it!"],"id":606,"m":"No, I swear it doesn't. I make as good use of it as many men do of a skull or a reminder of death. I never see your face without thinking of hell-fire and the rich man in the Bible who lived in purple, because there he is in his robes, burning, burning. If you had any leaning towards virtue, I'd swear by your face; my oath would be \"By this fire, which is God's angel.\" But you're completely lost to sin, and if it weren't for the light in your face, you'd be the son of utter darkness. When you ran up Gad's Hill in the night to catch my horse, if I didn't think you were a will-o'-the-wisp or a ball of fireworks, then money is worth nothing. Oh, you're a permanent torchlight parade, an everlasting bonfire! You've saved me a thousand marks in torches, walking with you at night from tavern to tavern. But the sherry you've drunk at my expense would have bought me lights just as cheaply at the dearest candle-maker's in Europe. I've kept that fire-eating salamander of yours supplied with fire for the last thirty-two years. God reward me for it!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["'Sblood, I would my face were in your stomach!"],"id":607,"m":"By God's blood, I wish my face were in your stomach!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["God-a-mercy! so should I be sure to be heart-burn'd.—"],"id":608,"m":"God have mercy! Then I'd be sure to get heartburn."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the Hostess.","id":609},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","cont":1,"lines":["How now, Dame Partlet the hen! have you enquir'd yet who","pick'd my pocket?"],"id":610,"m":"How now, Dame Partlet the hen! Have you found out yet who picked my pocket?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["Why, Sir John, what do you think, Sir John? do you think I","keep thieves in my house? I have search'd, I have inquired,","so has my husband, man by man, boy by boy, servant by servant:","the tithe of a hair was never lost in my house before."],"id":611,"m":"Why, Sir John, what do you think, Sir John? Do you think I keep thieves in my house? I've searched, I've asked questions, and so has my husband, man by man, boy by boy, servant by servant. Not a tenth of a hair was ever lost in my house before."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Ye lie, hostess: Bardolph was shaved, and lost many a hair; and","I'll be sworn my pocket was pick'd. Go to, you are a woman, go."],"id":612,"m":"You're lying, landlady. Bardolph was shaved and lost many a hair, and I'll swear my pocket was picked. Go on, you're a woman, go."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["Who, I? no; I defy thee: God's light, I was never call'd so in","mine own house before."],"id":613,"m":"Who, me? No, I defy you. By God's light, I was never called that in my own house before."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Go to, I know you well enough."],"id":614,"m":"Go on, I know you well enough."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["No, Sir John; you do not know me, Sir John. I know you, Sir John:","you owe me money, Sir John; and now you pick a quarrel to beguile me","of it: I bought you a dozen of shirts to your back."],"id":615,"m":"No, Sir John, you don't know me, Sir John. I know you, Sir John. You owe me money, Sir John, and now you're picking a quarrel to cheat me out of it. I bought you a dozen shirts for your back."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Dowlas, filthy dowlas: I have given them away to bakers' wives,","and they have made bolters of them."],"id":616,"m":"Coarse cloth, filthy coarse cloth. I've given them away to bakers' wives, and they've made flour sieves out of them."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["Now, as I am a true woman, holland of eight shillings an ell.","You owe money here besides, Sir John, for your diet and by-drinkings,","and money lent you, four-and-twenty pound."],"id":617,"m":"Now, as I'm an honest woman, they were fine linen at eight shillings a yard. You owe money here as well, Sir John, for your meals and drinks between meals, and money lent you, twenty-four pounds."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["He had his part of it; let him pay."],"id":618,"m":"He had his share of it; let him pay."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["He? alas, he is poor; he hath nothing."],"id":619,"m":"Him? Alas, he's poor; he has nothing."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["How! poor? look upon his face; what call you rich? let","them coin his nose, let them coin his cheeks: I'll not pay a","denier. What, will you make a younker of me? shall I not take","mine ease in mine inn, but I shall have my pocket pick'd? I have","lost a seal-ring of my grandfather's worth forty mark."],"id":620,"m":"What! Poor? Look at his face. What do you call rich? Let them make coins out of his nose and his cheeks. I won't pay a penny. What, are you treating me like a greenhorn? Can't I relax in my own inn without having my pocket picked? I've lost a signet ring of my grandfather's worth forty marks."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["O Jesu, I have heard the Prince tell him, I know not how oft,","that that ring was copper!"],"id":621,"m":"Oh Jesus, I've heard the Prince tell him I don't know how often that that ring was copper!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["How! the Prince is a Jack, a sneak-cup: 'sblood, an he were","here, I would cudgel him like a dog, if he would say so.—"],"id":622,"m":"What! The Prince is a rascal, a sneaky drinker. By God's blood, if he were here and said so, I'd beat him like a dog."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Prince Henry and Pointz, marching. Falstaff meets them, playing on his truncheon like a fife.","id":623},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","cont":1,"lines":["How now, lad? is the wind in that door, i'faith? must we all","march?"],"id":624,"m":"Well, lad? Is that the way the wind blows, really? Must we all march?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["Yea, two-and-two, Newgate-fashion."],"id":625,"m":"Yes, two by two, like prisoners going to Newgate."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["My lord, I pray you, hear me."],"id":626,"m":"My lord, please listen to me."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["What say'st thou, Mistress Quickly? How doth thy husband? I love","him well; he is an honest man."],"id":627,"m":"What do you say, Mistress Quickly? How's your husband? I like him very much; he's an honest man."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["Good my lord, hear me."],"id":628,"m":"My good lord, listen to me."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Pr'ythee, let her alone, and list to me."],"id":629,"m":"Please leave her alone and listen to me."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["What say'st thou, Jack?"],"id":630,"m":"What do you say, Jack?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["The other night I fell asleep here behind the arras, and had my","pocket pick'd: this house is turn'd bawdy-house; they pick pockets."],"id":631,"m":"The other night I fell asleep here behind the tapestry and had my pocket picked. This house has turned into a brothel; they pick pockets."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["What didst thou lose, Jack?"],"id":632,"m":"What did you lose, Jack?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Wilt thou believe me, Hal? three or four bonds of forty pound","a-piece and a seal-ring of my grandfather's."],"id":633,"m":"Will you believe me, Hal? Three or four bonds worth forty pounds each, and a signet ring of my grandfather's."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["A trifle, some eight-penny matter."],"id":634,"m":"A trifle, something worth about eightpence."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["So I told him, my lord; and I said I heard your Grace say so;","and, my lord, he speaks most vilely of you, like a foul-mouth'd","man as he is; and said he would cudgel you."],"id":635,"m":"That's what I told him, my lord, and I said I'd heard your Grace say so. And, my lord, he speaks most vilely of you, foul-mouthed man that he is, and said he would beat you."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["What! he did not?"],"id":636,"m":"What! He didn't?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["There's neither faith, truth, nor womanhood in me else."],"id":637,"m":"If he did not, there's no faith, truth or womanhood in me."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["There's no more faith in thee than in a stew'd prune; nor no more","truth in thee than in a drawn fox; and, for woman-hood, Maid Marian","may be the deputy's wife of the ward to thee. Go, you thing, go."],"id":638,"m":"There's no more faith in you than in a stewed prune, no more truth in you than in a hunted fox, and as for womanhood, Maid Marian would be a respectable alderman's wife compared with you. Go, you thing, go."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["Say, what thing? what thing? I am an honest man's wife: and,","setting thy knighthood aside, thou art a knave to call me so."],"id":639,"m":"Tell me, what thing? What thing? I'm an honest man's wife, and, knighthood aside, you're a rascal to call me that."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Setting thy womanhood aside, thou art a beast to say otherwise."],"id":640,"m":"Womanhood aside, you're a beast to say otherwise."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["Say, what beast, thou knave, thou?"],"id":641,"m":"Tell me, what beast, you rascal, you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["What beast! why, an otter."],"id":642,"m":"What beast! Why, an otter."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["An otter, Sir John, why an otter?"],"id":643,"m":"An otter, Sir John? Why an otter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Why, she's neither fish nor flesh; a man knows not where to have","her."],"id":644,"m":"Why, she's neither fish nor flesh; a man doesn't know where to have her."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["Thou art an unjust man in saying so; thou or any man knows where","to have me, thou knave, thou!"],"id":645,"m":"You're an unfair man to say so. You or any man knows where to have me, you rascal, you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Thou say'st true, hostess; and he slanders thee most grossly."],"id":646,"m":"You're right, landlady, and he slanders you most grossly."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["So he doth you, my lord; and said this other day you ought him a","thousand pound."],"id":647,"m":"And he slanders you too, my lord. He said the other day that you owed him a thousand pounds."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Sirrah, do I owe you a thousand pound?"],"id":648,"m":"My man, do I owe you a thousand pounds?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["A thousand pound, Hal! a million: thy love is worth a million;","thou owest me thy love."],"id":649,"m":"A thousand pounds, Hal? A million! Your love is worth a million; you owe me your love."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["Nay, my lord, he call'd you Jack, and said he would cudgel you."],"id":650,"m":"No, my lord, he called you a rascal and said he'd beat you."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Did I, Bardolph?"],"id":651,"m":"Did I, Bardolph?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["Indeed, Sir John, you said so."],"id":652,"m":"You did say so, Sir John."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Yea, if he said my ring was copper."],"id":653,"m":"Yes, if he said my ring was copper."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["I say 'tis copper: darest thou be as good as thy word now?"],"id":654,"m":"I say it's copper. Do you dare to keep your word now?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Why, Hal, thou know'st, as thou art but man, I dare; but as thou","art prince, I fear thee as I fear the roaring of the lion's whelp."],"id":655,"m":"Why, Hal, you know that, as you're just a man, I dare; but as you're a prince, I fear you as I fear the roaring of a lion's cub."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["And why not as the lion?"],"id":656,"m":"And why not as the lion?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["The King himself is to be feared as the lion: dost thou think I'll","fear thee as I fear thy father? nay, an I do, I pray God my girdle","break."],"id":657,"m":"The King himself is the one to be feared like the lion. Do you think I'll fear you as I fear your father? No, if I do, I pray God my belt breaks."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Sirrah, there's no room for faith, truth, nor honesty in this","bosom of thine; it is all fill'd up with midriff.","Charge an honest woman with picking thy pocket! why, thou whoreson,","impudent, emboss'd rascal, if there were anything in thy pocket but","tavern-reckonings, and one poor pennyworth of sugar-candy to make thee","long-winded,—if thy pocket were enrich'd with any other injuries but","these, I am a villain: and yet you will stand to it; you will not","pocket-up wrong. Art thou not ashamed!"],"id":658,"m":"My man, there's no room for faith, truth or honesty in that chest of yours; it's all filled up with guts. Accusing an honest woman of picking your pocket! Why, you son-of-a-whore, shameless, swollen rascal, if there was anything in your pocket except tavern bills and one poor pennyworth of sugar candy to give you breath, if your pocket was enriched with any other losses than these, I'm a villain. And yet you'll stick to your story; you won't pocket an insult. Aren't you ashamed?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Dost thou hear, Hal? thou know'st, in the state of innocency Adam fell;","and what should poor Jack Falstaff do in the days of villainy?","Thou see'st I have more flesh than another man; and therefore more","frailty. You confess, then, you pick'd my pocket?"],"id":659,"m":"Do you hear, Hal? You know that Adam fell in the state of innocence; so what can poor Jack Falstaff be expected to do in these days of wickedness? You can see I have more flesh than other men, and therefore more weakness. So you admit you picked my pocket?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["It appears so by the story."],"id":660,"m":"It looks like it, from the story."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Hostess, I forgive thee: go, make ready breakfast; love thy husband,","look to thy servants, cherish thy guests: thou shalt find me tractable","to any honest reason; thou see'st I am pacified.—Still? Nay, pr'ythee,","be gone."],"id":661,"m":"Landlady, I forgive you. Go and get breakfast ready. Love your husband, look after your servants, take care of your guests. You'll find me ready to listen to any honest argument. You see I'm calmed down. Still here? No, please go."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Hostess.","id":662},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","cont":1,"lines":["Now, Hal, to the news at Court: for the robbery, lad, how is","that answered?"],"id":663,"m":"Now, Hal, the news from Court. About the robbery, lad, how has that been dealt with?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["O, my sweet beef, I must still be good angel to thee: the money","is paid back again."],"id":664,"m":"Oh, my sweet beef, I must always be your good angel: the money's been paid back."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["O, I do not like that paying back; 'tis a double labour."],"id":665,"m":"Oh, I don't like that paying back; it's twice the work."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["I am good friends with my father, and may do any thing."],"id":666,"m":"I'm on good terms with my father and can do anything."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Rob me the exchequer the first thing thou doest, and do it with","unwash'd hands too."],"id":667,"m":"Then the first thing you do, rob the treasury for me, and do it without washing your hands, too."},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["Do, my lord."],"id":668,"m":"Do it, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["I have procured thee, Jack, a charge of Foot."],"id":669,"m":"I've got you a command of foot soldiers, Jack."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["I would it had been of Horse. Where shall I find one that can steal","well? O, for a fine thief, of the age of two-and-twenty or thereabouts!","I am heinously unprovided. Well, God be thanked for these rebels; they","offend none but the virtuous: I laud them, I praise them."],"id":670,"m":"I wish it had been cavalry. Where will I find someone who can steal well? Oh, for a fine thief of about twenty-two! I'm terribly short of supplies. Well, thank God for these rebels; they offend nobody but the virtuous. I applaud them, I praise them."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Bardolph,—"],"id":671,"m":"Bardolph,"},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["My lord?"],"id":672,"m":"My lord?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Go bear this letter to Lord John of Lancaster,","My brother John; this to my Lord of Westmoreland.—"],"id":673,"m":"Go and take this letter to Lord John of Lancaster, my brother John, and this one to my lord of Westmoreland."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Bardolph.","id":674},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","cont":1,"lines":["Go, Pointz, to horse, to horse; for thou and I","Have thirty miles to ride yet ere dinner-time.—"],"id":675,"m":"Go, Poins, to horse, to horse, because you and I have thirty miles to ride before dinner."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Pointz.","id":676},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","cont":1,"lines":["Meet me to-morrow, Jack, i' the Temple-hall","At two o'clock in th' afternoon:","There shalt thou know thy charge; and there receive","Money and order for their furniture.","The land is burning; Percy stands on high;","And either they or we must lower lie."],"id":677,"m":"Meet me tomorrow, Jack, in the Temple hall at two o'clock in the afternoon. There you'll learn what your command is and receive money and orders for equipping your men. The country is on fire; Percy stands high; and either they or we must be brought low."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":678},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Rare words! brave world!—Hostess, my breakfast; come:—","O, I could wish this tavern were my drum!"],"id":679,"m":"Fine words! Brave world! Landlady, my breakfast, come on! Oh, I wish this tavern were my drum!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":680},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"I","loc":"The Rebel Camp near Shrewsbury.","id":681},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Hotspur, Worcester, and Douglas.","id":682},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["Well said, my noble Scot: if speaking truth","In this fine age were not thought flattery,","Such attribution should the Douglas have,","As not a soldier of this season's stamp","Should go so general-current through the world.","By God, I cannot flatter; I defy","The tongues of soothers; but a braver place","In my heart's love hath no man than yourself:","Nay, task me to my word; approve me, lord."],"id":683,"m":"Well said, my noble Scot. If telling the truth weren't taken for flattery in these refined times, Douglas would have such praise that no soldier of this generation would be so widely honoured throughout the world. By God, I can't flatter; I despise the tongues of flatterers. But no man has a higher place in my heart's love than you. No, hold me to my word; test me, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOUGLAS","lines":["Thou art the king of honour:","No man so potent breathes upon the ground","But I will beard him."],"id":684,"m":"You are the king of honour. There's no man alive so powerful that I won't defy him."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["Do so, and 'tis well.—"],"id":685,"m":"Do that, and all is well."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Messenger with letters.","id":686},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","cont":1,"lines":["What letters hast thou there?—I can but thank you."],"id":687,"m":"What letters have you got there? I can only thank you."},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["These letters come from your father."],"id":688,"m":"These letters come from your father."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["Letters from him! why comes he not himself?"],"id":689,"m":"Letters from him! Why doesn't he come himself?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["He cannot come, my lord; he's grievous sick."],"id":690,"m":"He can't come, my lord; he's seriously ill."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["Zwounds! how has he the leisure to be sick","In such a justling time? Who leads his power?","Under whose government come they along?"],"id":691,"m":"By God's wounds! How does he have time to be ill at such a hectic time? Who's leading his army? Under whose command are they coming?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["His letters bears his mind, not I, my lord."],"id":692,"m":"His letters tell you his mind, not I, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"WORCESTER","lines":["I pr'ythee, tell me, doth he keep his bed?"],"id":693,"m":"Please tell me, is he in bed?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["He did, my lord, four days ere I set forth,","And at the time of my departure thence","He was much fear'd by his physicians."],"id":694,"m":"He was, my lord, four days before I set out, and when I left, his doctors were very worried about him."},{"t":"sp","who":"WORCESTER","lines":["I would the state of time had first been whole","Ere he by sickness had been visited:","His health was never better worth than now."],"id":695,"m":"I wish our affairs had been settled before he fell ill. His health was never worth more than now."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["Sick now! droop now! this sickness doth infect","The very life-blood of our enterprise;","'Tis catching hither, even to our camp.","He writes me here, that inward sickness,—","And that his friends by deputation could not","So soon be drawn; nor did he think it meet","To lay so dangerous and dear a trust","On any soul removed, but on his own.","Yet doth he give us bold advertisement,","That with our small conjunction we should on,","To see how fortune is disposed to us;","For, as he writes, there is no quailing now,","Because the King is certainly possess'd","Of all our purposes. What say you to it?"],"id":696,"m":"Sick now! Failing now! This sickness infects the very lifeblood of our enterprise; it's spreading even here to our camp. He writes to me here that he has an internal illness, and that his allies could not be gathered so quickly by a deputy, nor did he think it right to put such a dangerous and precious trust in anyone but himself. Still, he boldly advises that we should go ahead with our small combined forces to see how fortune is inclined towards us, because, as he writes, there's no backing out now, since the King certainly knows all our plans. What do you say to that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WORCESTER","lines":["Your father's sickness is a maim to us."],"id":697,"m":"Your father's illness is a crippling blow to us."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["A perilous gash, a very limb lopp'd off:—","And yet, in faith, 'tis not; his present want","Seems more than we shall find it. Were it good","To set the exact wealth of all our states","All at one cast? to set so rich a main","On the nice hazard of one doubtful hour?","It were not good; for therein should we read","The very bottom and the soul of hope,","The very list, the very utmost bound","Of all our fortunes."],"id":698,"m":"A dangerous gash, a limb cut right off. And yet, honestly, it's not. His absence now seems worse than it will turn out to be. Would it be wise to stake all our resources on one throw of the dice, to bet so much on the risky chance of one uncertain hour? It wouldn't, because then we'd be reaching the very bottom of our hope, the very edge, the utmost limit of all our fortunes."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOUGLAS","lines":["Faith, and so we should;","Where now remains a sweet reversion;","And we may boldly spend upon the hope","Of what is to come in:","A comfort of retirement lives in this."],"id":699,"m":"Indeed, so we would; whereas now we have something good in reserve, and we can boldly spend in the hope of what is still to come. There's the comfort of a place to retreat to in this."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["A rendezvous, a home to fly unto,","If that the Devil and mischance look big","Upon the maidenhead of our affairs."],"id":700,"m":"A meeting place, a home to run to, if the Devil and bad luck threaten the start of our venture."},{"t":"sp","who":"WORCESTER","lines":["But yet I would your father had been here.","The quality and hair of our attempt","Brooks no division: it will be thought","By some, that know not why he is away,","That wisdom, loyalty, and mere dislike","Of our proceedings, kept the earl from hence:","And think how such an apprehension","May turn the tide of fearful faction,","And breed a kind of question in our cause;","For well you know we of the offering side","Must keep aloof from strict arbitrement,","And stop all sight-holes, every loop from whence","The eye of reason may pry in upon us.","This absence of your father's draws a curtain,","That shows the ignorant a kind of fear","Before not dreamt of."],"id":701,"m":"But still I wish your father had been here. The nature of our enterprise can't stand division. Some people who don't know why he's away will think that wisdom, loyalty and simple disapproval of what we're doing kept the Earl away. And think how such a belief could turn the tide among our nervous supporters and raise doubts about our cause. You know well that we who are the attackers must keep clear of close examination and block every peephole and every loophole through which the eye of reason might pry into us. This absence of your father's draws back a curtain that shows the ignorant a cause for fear they never dreamed of before."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["Nay, you strain too far.","I, rather, of his absence make this use:","It lends a lustre and more great opinion,","A larger dare to our great enterprise,","Than if the earl were here; for men must think,","If we, without his help, can make a head","To push against the kingdom, with his help","We shall o'erturn it topsy-turvy down.","Yet all goes well, yet all our joints are whole."],"id":702,"m":"No, you're going too far. I make this use of his absence instead: it gives more glory and a greater reputation, a bigger sense of daring, to our great enterprise than if the Earl were here. Because people will think that if we can raise an army without his help to attack the kingdom, with his help we'll turn it upside down. All is still well; all our limbs are still whole."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOUGLAS","lines":["As heart can think: there is not such a word","Spoke in Scotland as this term of fear."],"id":703,"m":"As well as the heart could wish. There's no such word as \"fear\" spoken in Scotland."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Sir Richard Vernon.","id":704},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["My cousin Vernon! welcome, by my soul."],"id":705,"m":"My cousin Vernon! Welcome, by my soul."},{"t":"sp","who":"VERNON","lines":["Pray God my news be worth a welcome, lord.","The Earl of Westmoreland, seven thousand strong,","Is marching hitherwards; with him Prince John."],"id":706,"m":"I pray to God my news deserves a welcome, my lord. The Earl of Westmoreland is marching this way, seven thousand strong, and Prince John with him."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["No harm: what more?"],"id":707,"m":"No harm in that. What else?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VERNON","lines":["And further, I have learn'd","The King himself in person is set forth,","Or hitherwards intended speedily,","With strong and mighty preparation."],"id":708,"m":"And further, I've learned that the King himself has set out in person, or plans to come this way quickly, with a strong and mighty army."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["He shall be welcome too. Where is his son,","The nimble-footed madcap Prince of Wales,","And his comrades, that daff the world aside,","And bid it pass?"],"id":709,"m":"He'll be welcome too. Where's his son, the nimble-footed madcap Prince of Wales, and his friends, who toss the world aside and tell it to go its way?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VERNON","lines":["All furnish'd, all in arms;","All plumed like estridges that with the wind","Bate it; like eagles having lately bathed;","Glittering in golden coats, like images;","As full of spirit as the month of May","And gorgeous as the Sun at midsummer;","Wanton as youthful goats, wild as young bulls.","I saw young Harry—with his beaver on,","His cuisses on his thighs, gallantly arm'd—","Rise from the ground like feather'd Mercury,","And vault it with such ease into his seat,","As if an angel dropp'd down from the clouds,","To turn and wind a fiery Pegasus,","And witch the world with noble horsemanship."],"id":710,"m":"All equipped, all in armour, all plumed like ostriches flapping their wings in the wind, like eagles that have just bathed, glittering in golden coats like statues of saints, as full of spirit as the month of May and as splendid as the sun at midsummer, as playful as young goats and as wild as young bulls. I saw young Harry, with his visor up and his thigh armour on, splendidly armed, rise from the ground like winged Mercury and vault into the saddle so easily that it was as if an angel had dropped from the clouds to turn and wheel a fiery Pegasus and enchant the world with noble horsemanship."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["No more, no more: worse than the Sun in March,","This praise doth nourish agues. Let them come;","They come like sacrifices in their trim,","And to the fire-eyed maid of smoky war,","All hot and bleeding, will we offer them:","The mailed Mars shall on his altar sit","Up to the ears in blood. I am on fire","To hear this rich reprisal is so nigh,","And yet not ours.—Come, let me taste my horse,","Who is to bear me, like a thunderbolt,","Against the bosom of the Prince of Wales:","Harry and Harry shall, hot horse to horse,","Meet, and ne'er part till one drop down a corse.—","O, that Glendower were come!"],"id":711,"m":"No more, no more! Worse than the sun in March, this praise breeds fevers. Let them come. They come like sacrificial animals in their finery, and we will offer them, all hot and bleeding, to the fiery-eyed goddess of smoky war. Armoured Mars will sit on his altar up to his ears in blood. I'm on fire to hear that this rich prize is so near and not yet ours. Come, let me try out my horse, which is to carry me like a thunderbolt against the chest of the Prince of Wales. Harry and Harry will meet, hot horse against horse, and never part till one drops down a corpse. Oh, if only Glendower had come!"},{"t":"sp","who":"VERNON","lines":["There is more news:","I learn'd in Worcester, as I rode along,","He cannot draw his power this fourteen days."],"id":712,"m":"There's more news. I learned in Worcester, as I rode along, that he can't gather his army for a fortnight."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOUGLAS","lines":["That's the worst tidings that I hear of yet."],"id":713,"m":"That's the worst news I've heard so far."},{"t":"sp","who":"WORCESTER","lines":["Ay, by my faith, that bears a frosty sound."],"id":714,"m":"Yes, by my faith, that has a chilling sound."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["What may the King's whole battle reach unto?"],"id":715,"m":"How big might the King's whole army be?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VERNON","lines":["To thirty thousand."],"id":716,"m":"Thirty thousand."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["Forty let it be:","My father and Glendower being both away,","The powers of us may serve so great a day.","Come, let us take a muster speedily:","Doomsday is near; die all, die merrily."],"id":717,"m":"Let it be forty. With my father and Glendower both away, our forces will be enough for such a great day. Come, let's hold a roll-call quickly. Doomsday is near; if we all die, let's die merrily."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOUGLAS","lines":["Talk not of dying: I am out of fear","Of death or death's hand for this one half-year."],"id":718,"m":"Don't talk about dying. I'm free from the fear of death or death's hand for the next half-year."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":719},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"II","loc":"A public Road near Coventry.","id":720},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Falstaff and Bardolph.","id":721},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Bardolph, get thee before to Coventry; fill me a bottle of","sack: our soldiers shall march through; we'll to Sutton-Co'fil'","to-night."],"id":722,"m":"Bardolph, go on ahead to Coventry and fill me a bottle of sherry. Our soldiers will march through; we'll get to Sutton Coldfield tonight."},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["Will you give me money, captain?"],"id":723,"m":"Will you give me money, captain?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Lay out, lay out."],"id":724,"m":"Pay for it yourself, pay for it yourself."},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["This bottle makes an angel."],"id":725,"m":"This bottle will make an angel, a ten-shilling coin."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["An if it do, take it for thy labour; an if it make twenty,","take them all; I'll answer the coinage. Bid my lieutenant","Peto meet me at the town's end."],"id":726,"m":"And if it does, take it for your trouble; and if it makes twenty, take them all, I'll vouch for the coins. Tell my lieutenant Peto to meet me at the edge of town."},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["I will, captain: farewell."],"id":727,"m":"I will, captain. Goodbye."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":728},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["If I be not ashamed of my soldiers, I am a soused gurnet. I have","misused the King's press damnably. I have got, in exchange of","a hundred and fifty soldiers, three hundred and odd pounds. I","press'd me none but good householders, yeomen's sons; inquired","me out contracted bachelors, such as had been ask'd twice on the","banns; such a commodity of warm slaves as had as lief hear the","Devil as a drum; such as fear the report of a caliver worse than","a struck fowl or a hurt wild-duck. I press'd me none but such","toasts-and-butter, with hearts in their bodies no bigger than","pins'-heads, and they have bought out their services; and now","my whole charge consists of ancients, corporals, lieutenants,","gentlemen of companies, slaves as ragged as Lazarus in the","painted cloth, where the glutton's dogs licked his sores; and","such as, indeed, were never soldiers, but discarded unjust","serving-men, younger sons to younger brothers, revolted tapsters,","and ostlers trade-fallen; the cankers of a calm world and a long","peace; ten times more dishonourable ragged than an old faced","ancient: and such have I, to fill up the rooms of them that have","bought out their services, that you would think that I had a","hundred and fifty tattered Prodigals lately come from","swine-keeping, from eating draff and husks. A mad fellow met me on","the way, and told me I had unloaded all the gibbets, and press'd","the dead bodies.","No eye hath seen such scarecrows. I'll not march through Coventry","with them, that's flat: nay, and the villains march wide betwixt","the legs, as if they had gyves on; for, indeed, I had the most of","them out of prison. There's but a shirt and a half in all my company;","and the half-shirt is two napkins tack'd together and thrown over the","shoulders like a herald's coat without sleeves; and the shirt, to say","the truth, stolen from my host at Saint Alban's, or the red-nose","innkeeper of Daventry.","But that's all one; they'll find linen enough on every hedge."],"id":729,"m":"If I'm not ashamed of my soldiers, I'm a pickled fish. I've abused the King's power to conscript men damnably. In exchange for a hundred and fifty soldiers, I've got three hundred and something pounds. I conscripted nobody but well-off householders and farmers' sons; I sought out engaged bachelors whose wedding banns had been read twice, a whole load of comfortable cowards who would as soon hear the Devil as a drum, men who fear the bang of a musket more than a wounded bird or a hurt wild duck. I conscripted nobody but such milksops, with hearts no bigger than pinheads, and they've all bought themselves out of service. And now my whole company consists of standard-bearers, corporals, lieutenants and gentlemen of the ranks, wretches as ragged as Lazarus in the painted wall-hanging, where the glutton's dogs licked his sores; and men who were never really soldiers at all, but dishonest servants who've been sacked, younger sons of younger brothers, runaway barmen and stable-boys out of work, the cankers of a calm world and a long peace, ten times more dishonourably ragged than an old patched flag. And I've got such men to fill the places of those who bought themselves out that you'd think I had a hundred and fifty tattered Prodigal Sons just back from keeping pigs and eating swill and husks. A madman met me on the way and told me I'd emptied all the gallows and conscripted the dead bodies. No eye has ever seen such scarecrows. I won't march through Coventry with them, that's certain. And the villains march with their legs wide apart, as if they still had shackles on, because I got most of them out of prison. There's only a shirt and a half in my whole company, and the half-shirt is two napkins tacked together and thrown over the shoulders like a herald's sleeveless coat, and the shirt, to tell the truth, was stolen from my landlord at Saint Albans or the red-nosed innkeeper of Daventry. But that doesn't matter; they'll find plenty of linen drying on every hedge."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Prince Henry and Westmoreland.","id":730},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["How now, blown Jack! how now, quilt!"],"id":731,"m":"How now, puffed-up Jack! How now, quilt!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["What, Hal! how now, mad wag! what a devil dost thou in","Warwickshire?—My good Lord of Westmoreland, I cry you mercy:","I thought your honour had already been at Shrewsbury."],"id":732,"m":"What, Hal! How now, mad joker! What the devil are you doing in Warwickshire? My good lord of Westmoreland, I beg your pardon. I thought your honour was already at Shrewsbury."},{"t":"sp","who":"WESTMORELAND","lines":["Faith, Sir John, 'tis more than time that I were there, and you too;","but my powers are there already. The King, I can tell you, looks for","us all: we must away all, to-night."],"id":733,"m":"Honestly, Sir John, it's high time I was there, and you too; but my troops are there already. The King, I can tell you, is expecting us all. We must all move on tonight."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Tut, never fear me: I am as vigilant as a cat to steal cream."],"id":734,"m":"Nonsense, don't worry about me. I'm as alert as a cat stealing cream."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["I think, to steal cream, indeed; for thy theft hath already made thee","butter. But tell me, Jack, whose fellows are these that come after?"],"id":735,"m":"Stealing cream, indeed, I think, because your thieving has already turned you into butter. But tell me, Jack, whose men are these coming along behind?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Mine, Hal, mine."],"id":736,"m":"Mine, Hal, mine."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["I did never see such pitiful rascals."],"id":737,"m":"I've never seen such pitiful wretches."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Tut, tut; good enough to toss; food for powder, food for powder;","they'll fill a pit as well as better: tush, man, mortal men,","mortal men."],"id":738,"m":"Tut, tut, good enough to be tossed on a pike. Food for gunpowder, food for gunpowder. They'll fill a mass grave as well as better men. Nonsense, man, they're mortal men, mortal men."},{"t":"sp","who":"WESTMORELAND","lines":["Ay, but, Sir John, methinks they are exceeding poor and bare,—too","beggarly."],"id":739,"m":"Yes, but, Sir John, I think they're extremely poor and thin, too beggarly."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Faith, for their poverty, I know not where they had that; and,","for their bareness, I am sure they never learn'd that of me."],"id":740,"m":"Well, as for their poverty, I don't know where they got that; and as for their thinness, I'm sure they never learned that from me."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["No, I'll be sworn; unless you call three fingers on the ribs","bare. But, sirrah, make haste: Percy is already in the field."],"id":741,"m":"No, I'll swear to that, unless you call three fingers' thickness of fat on the ribs thin. But hurry up, man. Percy is already in the field."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":742},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["What, is the King encamp'd?"],"id":743,"m":"What, is the King in camp?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WESTMORELAND","lines":["He is, Sir John: I fear we shall stay too long."],"id":744,"m":"He is, Sir John. I'm afraid we'll be too late."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":745},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Well,","To the latter end of a fray and the beginning of a feast","Fits a dull fighter and a keen guest."],"id":746,"m":"Well, the end of a fight and the start of a feast suit a dull fighter and a keen guest."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":747},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"III","loc":"The Rebel Camp near Shrewsbury.","id":748},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Hotspur, Worcester, Douglas, and Vernon.","id":749},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["We'll fight with him to-night."],"id":750,"m":"We'll fight him tonight."},{"t":"sp","who":"WORCESTER","lines":["It may not be."],"id":751,"m":"That's impossible."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOUGLAS","lines":["You give him, then, advantage."],"id":752,"m":"Then you're giving him an advantage."},{"t":"sp","who":"VERNON","lines":["Not a whit."],"id":753,"m":"Not at all."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["Why say you so? looks he not for supply?"],"id":754,"m":"Why do you say that? Isn't he expecting reinforcements?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VERNON","lines":["So do we."],"id":755,"m":"So are we."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["His is certain, ours is doubtful."],"id":756,"m":"His are certain, ours are doubtful."},{"t":"sp","who":"WORCESTER","lines":["Good cousin, be advised; stir not to-night."],"id":757,"m":"Good nephew, take advice; don't move tonight."},{"t":"sp","who":"VERNON","lines":["Do not, my lord."],"id":758,"m":"Don't, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOUGLAS","lines":["You do not counsel well:","You speak it out of fear and cold heart."],"id":759,"m":"You're not giving good advice. You say it out of fear and cowardice."},{"t":"sp","who":"VERNON","lines":["Do me no slander, Douglas: by my life,—","And I dare well maintain it with my life,—","If well-respected honour bid me on,","I hold as little counsel with weak fear","As you, my lord, or any Scot that this day lives:","Let it be seen to-morrow in the battle","Which of us fears."],"id":760,"m":"Don't slander me, Douglas. By my life, and I dare defend it with my life, if honour properly considered calls me on, I take as little notice of weak fear as you do, my lord, or any Scot alive today. Let it be seen in tomorrow's battle which of us is afraid."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOUGLAS","lines":["Yea, or to-night."],"id":761,"m":"Yes, or tonight."},{"t":"sp","who":"VERNON","lines":["Content."],"id":762,"m":"Agreed."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["To-night, say I."],"id":763,"m":"Tonight, I say."},{"t":"sp","who":"VERNON","lines":["Come, come, it may not be. I wonder much,","Being men of such great leading as you are,","That you foresee not what impediments","Drag back our expedition: certain Horse","Of my cousin Vernon's are not yet come up:","Your uncle Worcester's Horse came but to-day;","And now their pride and mettle is asleep,","Their courage with hard labour tame and dull,","That not a horse is half the half himself."],"id":764,"m":"Come, come, it can't be done. I'm amazed that men with such great experience of command as you don't see what obstacles hold back our advance. Some of my cousin Vernon's cavalry haven't arrived yet. Your uncle Worcester's cavalry only came today, and now their pride and spirit are asleep, their courage tamed and dulled by hard work, so that not one horse is a quarter of his usual self."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["So are the horses of the enemy","In general, journey-bated and brought low:","The better part of ours are full of rest."],"id":765,"m":"So are the enemy's horses, generally worn out by the journey and run down. Most of ours are well rested."},{"t":"sp","who":"WORCESTER","lines":["The number of the King exceedeth ours.","For God's sake, cousin, stay till all come in."],"id":766,"m":"The King's army is bigger than ours. For God's sake, nephew, wait till everyone has arrived."},{"t":"sd","text":"The Trumpet sounds a parley.","id":767},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Sir Walter Blunt.","id":768},{"t":"sp","who":"BLUNT","lines":["I come with gracious offers from the King,","If you vouchsafe me hearing and respect."],"id":769,"m":"I come with generous offers from the King, if you will grant me a respectful hearing."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["Welcome, Sir Walter Blunt; and would to God","You were of our determination!","Some of us love you well; and even those some","Envy your great deservings and good name,","Because you are not of our quality,","But stand against us like an enemy."],"id":770,"m":"Welcome, Sir Walter Blunt; and I wish to God you were on our side! Some of us love you well, and even those of us who do resent your great merits and good name because you aren't one of us, but stand against us like an enemy."},{"t":"sp","who":"BLUNT","lines":["And God defend but still I should stand so,","So long as out of limit and true rule","You stand against anointed majesty!","But to my charge: the King hath sent to know","The nature of your griefs; and whereupon","You conjure from the breast of civil peace","Such bold hostility, teaching his duteous land","Audacious cruelty. If that the King","Have any way your good deserts forgot,","Which he confesseth to be manifold,","He bids you name your griefs; and with all speed","You shall have your desires with interest,","And pardon absolute for yourself and these","Herein misled by your suggestion."],"id":771,"m":"And God forbid that I should stand anywhere else, as long as you stand against the anointed King, outside the law and true allegiance! But to my message. The King has sent to find out the nature of your grievances, and why you summon up such bold hostility out of the heart of peace at home, teaching his dutiful country reckless cruelty. If the King has in any way forgotten your services, which he admits are many, he invites you to name your grievances, and you will quickly have what you want with interest, and a full pardon for yourself and those you have misled into this."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["The King is kind; and well we know the King","Knows at what time to promise, when to pay.","My father and my uncle and myself","Did give him that same royalty he wears;","And—when he was not six-and-twenty strong,","Sick in the world's regard, wretched and low,","A poor unminded outlaw sneaking home—","My father gave him welcome to the shore:","And—when he heard him swear and vow to God,","He came but to be Duke of Lancaster,","To sue his livery and beg his peace,","With tears of innocence and terms of zeal—","My father, in kind heart and pity moved,","Swore him assistance, and performed it too.","Now, when the lords and barons of the realm","Perceived Northumberland did lean to him,","The more and less came in with cap and knee;","Met him in boroughs, cities, villages,","Attended him on bridges, stood in lanes,","Laid gifts before him, proffer'd him their oaths,","Give him their heirs as pages, follow'd him","Even at the heels in golden multitudes.","He presently—as greatness knows itself—","Steps me a little higher than his vow","Made to my father, while his blood was poor,","Upon the naked shore at Ravenspurg;","And now, forsooth, takes on him to reform","Some certain edicts and some strait decrees","That lie too heavy on the commonwealth;","Cries out upon abuses, seems to weep","Over his country's wrongs; and, by this face,","This seeming brow of justice, did he win","The hearts of all that he did angle for:","Proceeded further; cut me off the heads","Of all the favourites, that the absent King","In deputation left behind him here","When he was personal in the Irish war."],"id":772,"m":"The King is kind, and we know well that the King knows when to promise and when to pay. My father, my uncle and I gave him the very crown he wears. And when he had fewer than twenty-six followers, out of favour with the world, wretched and low, a poor, ignored outlaw sneaking home, my father welcomed him ashore. And when he heard him swear and vow to God that he had come only to be Duke of Lancaster, to claim his inheritance and beg for peace, with innocent tears and passionate words, my father, moved by kindness and pity, swore to help him, and did so too. Now, when the lords and barons of the kingdom saw that Northumberland supported him, great and small came to him with caps off and knees bent, met him in towns, cities and villages, waited for him on bridges, stood in lanes, laid gifts before him, offered him their oaths, gave him their heirs as pages, and followed at his heels in glittering crowds. Immediately, as greatness becomes aware of itself, he went a step higher than the vow he made to my father, when he was weak, on the bare shore at Ravenspurgh. And now, if you please, he takes it on himself to reform certain laws and harsh decrees that weigh too heavily on the country, cries out against abuses, seems to weep over his country's wrongs; and with this face, this pretended look of justice, he won the hearts of everyone he was fishing for. He went further: cut off the heads of all the favourites whom the absent King had left behind here as his deputies when he was in person at the Irish war."},{"t":"sp","who":"BLUNT","lines":["Tut, I came not to hear this."],"id":773,"m":"Tut, I didn't come to hear this."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["Then to the point:","In short time after, he deposed the King;","Soon after that, deprived him of his life;","And, in the neck of that, task'd the whole State:","To make that worse, suffer'd his kinsman March","(Who is, if every owner were well placed,","Indeed his king) to be engaged in Wales,","There without ransom to lie forfeited;","Disgraced me in my happy victories,","Sought to entrap me by intelligence;","Rated my uncle from the Council-board;","In rage dismiss'd my father from the Court;","Broke oath on oath, committed wrong on wrong;","And, in conclusion, drove us to seek out","This head of safety; and withal to pry","Into his title, the which now we find","Too indirect for long continuance."],"id":774,"m":"Then to the point. Shortly afterwards he deposed the King, soon after that took his life, and straight after that taxed the whole country. To make it worse, he allowed his kinsman March (who, if everyone had his rightful place, is really his king) to be held hostage in Wales, left there without ransom. He belittled me in my lucky victories and tried to trap me with spies; scolded my uncle and drove him from the Council table, angrily dismissed my father from Court, broke oath after oath, committed wrong after wrong, and, in short, drove us to raise this army for our safety, and at the same time to look into his claim to the throne, which we now find too crooked to last long."},{"t":"sp","who":"BLUNT","lines":["Shall I return this answer to the King?"],"id":775,"m":"Shall I take this answer back to the King?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["Not so, Sir Walter: we'll withdraw awhile.","Go to the King; and let there be impawn'd","Some surety for a safe return again,","And in the morning early shall my uncle","Bring him our purposes: and so, farewell."],"id":776,"m":"No, Sir Walter. We'll withdraw for a while. Go to the King, and let some hostage be pledged to guarantee my uncle's safe return, and early in the morning my uncle will bring him our intentions. And so goodbye."},{"t":"sp","who":"BLUNT","lines":["I would you would accept of grace and love."],"id":777,"m":"I wish you would accept his grace and love."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["And may be so we shall."],"id":778,"m":"And maybe we will."},{"t":"sp","who":"BLUNT","lines":["Pray God you do."],"id":779,"m":"I pray to God you do."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":780},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"IV","loc":"York. A Room in the Archbishop's Palace.","id":781},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the Archbishop of York and Sir Michael.","id":782},{"t":"sp","who":"ARCHBISHOP","lines":["Hie, good Sir Michael; bear this sealed brief","With winged haste to the Lord Marshal;","This to my cousin Scroop; and all the rest","To whom they are directed. If you knew","How much they do import, you would make haste."],"id":783,"m":"Hurry, good Sir Michael. Take this sealed letter with all speed to the Lord Marshal, this one to my cousin Scroop, and all the rest to those they are addressed to. If you knew how important they are, you would hurry."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR MICHAEL","lines":["My good lord,","I guess their tenour."],"id":784,"m":"My good lord, I can guess what they say."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARCHBISHOP","lines":["Like enough you do.","To-morrow, good Sir Michael, is a day","Wherein the fortune of ten thousand men","Must bide the touch; for, sir, at Shrewsbury,","As I am truly given to understand,","The King, with mighty and quick-raised power,","Meets with Lord Harry: and, I fear, Sir Michael,","What with the sickness of Northumberland,","Whose power was in the first proportion,","And what with Owen Glendower's absence thence,","Who with them was a rated sinew too,","And comes not in, o'er-rul'd by prophecies,—","I fear the power of Percy is too weak","To wage an instant trial with the King."],"id":785,"m":"You very likely can. Tomorrow, good Sir Michael, is a day when the fortune of ten thousand men must be put to the test. For, sir, at Shrewsbury, as I am reliably told, the King, with a mighty army quickly raised, meets Lord Harry Percy. And I fear, Sir Michael, what with the illness of Northumberland, whose army was the largest, and what with the absence of Owen Glendower, who was also counted on as a main strength and isn't coming, held back by prophecies, I fear Percy's army is too weak for an immediate trial of strength with the King."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR MICHAEL","lines":["Why, my good lord, you need not fear;","There's Douglas and Lord Mortimer."],"id":786,"m":"Why, my good lord, you needn't be afraid. There's Douglas and Lord Mortimer."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARCHBISHOP","lines":["No, Mortimer's not there."],"id":787,"m":"No, Mortimer isn't there."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR MICHAEL","lines":["But there is Mordake, Vernon, Lord Harry Percy,","And there's my Lord of Worcester; and a head","Of gallant warriors, noble gentlemen."],"id":788,"m":"But there's Mordake, Vernon, Lord Harry Percy, and my lord of Worcester, and a force of brave warriors, noble gentlemen."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARCHBISHOP","lines":["And so there is: but yet the King hath drawn","The special head of all the land together;","The Prince of Wales, Lord John of Lancaster,","The noble Westmoreland, and warlike Blunt;","And many more corrivals and dear men","Of estimation and command in arms."],"id":789,"m":"So there is. But the King has gathered the best of the whole country: the Prince of Wales, Lord John of Lancaster, the noble Westmoreland and warlike Blunt, and many more partners and valued men of reputation and command in war."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR MICHAEL","lines":["Doubt not, my lord, they shall be well opposed."],"id":790,"m":"Don't doubt it, my lord, they'll be well matched."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARCHBISHOP","lines":["I hope no less, yet needful 'tis to fear;","And, to prevent the worst, Sir Michael, speed:","For if Lord Percy thrive not, ere the King","Dismiss his power, he means to visit us,","For he hath heard of our confederacy;","And 'tis but wisdom to make strong against him:","Therefore make haste. I must go write again","To other friends; and so, farewell, Sir Michael."],"id":791,"m":"I hope so, yet it's necessary to fear. And to prevent the worst, Sir Michael, hurry. If Lord Percy doesn't succeed, the King means to visit us before he disbands his army, because he has heard of our alliance, and it's only wise to prepare our defences against him. So hurry. I must go and write to other friends. And so goodbye, Sir Michael."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":792},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"I","loc":"The King's Camp near Shrewsbury.","id":793},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter King Henry, Prince Henry, Lancaster, Sir Walter Blunt, and Sir John Falstaff.","id":794},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["How bloodily the Sun begins to peer","Above yon busky hill! the day looks pale","At his distemperature."],"id":795,"m":"How bloodily the sun is beginning to peep over that wooded hill! The day looks pale at its sickly look."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["The southern wind","Doth play the trumpet to his purposes;","And by his hollow whistling in the leaves","Foretells a tempest and a blustering day."],"id":796,"m":"The south wind is acting as trumpeter for its intentions, and by its hollow whistling in the leaves it foretells a storm and a blustery day."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Then with the losers let it sympathize,","For nothing can seem foul to those that win.—"],"id":797,"m":"Then let it share the mood of the losers, because nothing can seem bad to those who win."},{"t":"sd","text":"The trumpet sounds. Enter Worcester and Vernon.","id":798},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","cont":1,"lines":["How, now, my Lord of Worcester! 'tis not well","That you and I should meet upon such terms","As now we meet. You have deceived our trust;","And made us doff our easy robes of peace,","To crush our old limbs in ungentle steel:","This is not well, my lord, this is not well.","What say you to't? will you again unknit","This churlish knot of all-abhorred war,","And move in that obedient orb again","Where you did give a fair and natural light;","And be no more an exhaled meteor,","A prodigy of fear, and a portent","Of broached mischief to the unborn times?"],"id":799,"m":"Well, my lord of Worcester! It isn't right that you and I should meet on such terms as we meet on now. You have betrayed our trust and made us take off our comfortable robes of peace to squeeze our old limbs into harsh steel armour. This isn't right, my lord, this isn't right. What do you say to it? Will you untie this brutal knot of hated war again and move once more in that obedient orbit where you gave out a fair and natural light, and no longer be a meteor sucked up from the earth, a fearful omen, a sign of evil let loose on generations not yet born?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WORCESTER","lines":["Hear me, my liege:","For mine own part, I could be well content","To entertain the lag-end of my life","With quiet hours; for I do protest,","I have not sought the day of this dislike."],"id":800,"m":"Listen to me, my lord. For my own part, I would be glad to spend the last part of my life in quiet hours, because I swear I have not looked for this day of conflict."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["You have not sought it! why, how comes it, then?"],"id":801,"m":"You haven't looked for it! Why, how has it come about, then?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Rebellion lay in his way, and he found it."],"id":802,"m":"Rebellion lay in his path, and he found it."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Peace, chewet, peace!"],"id":803,"m":"Quiet, chatterbox, quiet!"},{"t":"sp","who":"WORCESTER","lines":["It pleased your Majesty to turn your looks","Of favour from myself and all our House;","And yet I must remember you, my lord,","We were the first and dearest of your friends.","For you my staff of office did I break","In Richard's time; and posted day and night","To meet you on the way, and kiss your hand,","When yet you were in place and in account","Nothing so strong and fortunate as I.","It was myself, my brother, and his son,","That brought you home, and boldly did outdare","The dangers of the time. You swore to us,—","And you did swear that oath at Doncaster,—","That you did nothing purpose 'gainst the state;","Nor claim no further than your new-fall'n right,","The seat of Gaunt, dukedom of Lancaster:","To this we swore our aid. But in short space","It rain'd down fortune showering on your head;","And such a flood of greatness fell on you,—","What with our help, what with the absent King,","What with the injuries of a wanton time,","The seeming sufferances that you had borne,","And the contrarious winds that held the King","So long in his unlucky Irish wars","That all in England did repute him dead,—","And, from this swarm of fair advantages,","You took occasion to be quickly woo'd","To gripe the general sway into your hand;","Forgot your oath to us at Doncaster;","And, being fed by us, you used us so","As that ungentle gull, the cuckoo-bird,","Useth the sparrow; did oppress our nest;","Grew by our feeding to so great a bulk,","That even our love durst not come near your sight","For fear of swallowing; but with nimble wing","We were enforced, for safety-sake, to fly","Out of your sight, and raise this present head:","Whereby we stand opposed by such means","As you yourself have forged against yourself,","By unkind usage, dangerous countenance,","And violation of all faith and troth","Sworn to us in your younger enterprise."],"id":804,"m":"Your Majesty chose to turn your favourable looks away from me and our whole family. And yet I must remind you, my lord, that we were the first and dearest of your friends. For your sake I broke my staff of office in Richard's time and rode day and night to meet you on the way and kiss your hand, when you were nowhere near as strong and fortunate in position and reputation as I was. It was I, my brother and his son who brought you home and boldly faced the dangers of the time. You swore to us, and you swore that oath at Doncaster, that you intended nothing against the state and claimed no more than your newly inherited right, the seat of Gaunt, the dukedom of Lancaster. To this we swore our help. But in a short time fortune rained down in showers on your head, and such a flood of greatness fell on you, what with our help, the King's absence, the wrongs of a disorderly time, the injuries you seemed to have suffered, and the contrary winds that kept the King so long in his unlucky Irish wars that everyone in England thought he was dead, that out of this swarm of advantages you let yourself be quickly persuaded to seize total power into your own hands, and forgot your oath to us at Doncaster. And after being fed by us, you treated us as that unkind gull, the cuckoo, treats the sparrow: you crushed our nest and grew so huge on our feeding that even our love didn't dare come near you for fear of being swallowed. So we were forced, for safety's sake, to fly out of your sight on quick wings and raise this army. And so we stand against you by means you yourself have forged against yourself, through unkind treatment, threatening looks, and the breaking of all the faith and promises you swore to us in your earlier enterprise."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["These things, indeed, you have articulate,","Proclaim'd at market-crosses, read in churches,","To face the garment of rebellion","With some fine colour that may please the eye","Of fickle changelings and poor discontents,","Which gape and rub the elbow at the news","Of hurlyburly innovation:","And never yet did insurrection want","Such water-colours to impaint his cause;","Nor moody beggars, starving for a time","Of pellmell havoc and confusion."],"id":805,"m":"These things, indeed, you have listed, proclaimed at market crosses and read out in churches, to trim the garment of rebellion with some fine colour that may please the eyes of fickle turncoats and poor malcontents, who gape and rub their elbows at the news of riotous change. And rebellion has never lacked such watercolours to paint over its cause, nor sullen beggars hungry for a time of chaos and confusion."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["In both our armies there is many a soul","Shall pay full dearly for this encounter,","If once they join in trial. Tell your nephew,","The Prince of Wales doth join with all the world","In praise of Henry Percy: by my hopes,","This present enterprise set off his head,","I do not think a braver gentleman,","More active-valiant or more valiant-young,","More daring or more bold, is now alive","To grace this latter age with noble deeds.","For my part,—I may speak it to my shame,—","I have a truant been to chivalry;","And so I hear he doth account me too:","Yet this before my father's Majesty,—","I am content that he shall take the odds","Of his great name and estimation,","And will, to save the blood on either side,","Try fortune with him in a single fight."],"id":806,"m":"In both our armies there are many souls who will pay very dearly for this encounter, once they meet in battle. Tell your nephew that the Prince of Wales joins with all the world in praising Henry Percy. By my hopes of heaven, if this present rebellion is not counted against him, I don't think there is a braver gentleman alive, more actively brave or more bravely young, more daring or more bold, to grace this modern age with noble deeds. For my part, I say it to my shame, I have been a truant from chivalry, and I hear that's how he regards me too. Yet I say this before my father's Majesty: I am willing to let him have the advantage of his great name and reputation, and, to save blood on both sides, I will try my fortune with him in single combat."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["And, Prince of Wales, so dare we venture thee,","Albeit considerations infinite","Do make against it.—No, good Worcester, no;","We love our people well; even those we love","That are misled upon your cousin's part;","And, will they take the offer of our grace,","Both he, and they, and you, yea, every man","Shall be my friend again, and I'll be his:","So tell your cousin, and then bring me word","What he will do: but, if he will not yield,","Rebuke and dread correction wait on us,","And they shall do their office. So, be gone;","We will not now be troubled with reply:","We offer fair; take it advisedly."],"id":807,"m":"And, Prince of Wales, we would dare to risk you in it, though countless considerations count against it. No, good Worcester, no, we love our people well; we love even those who are misled on your nephew's side. And if they will accept our offer of mercy, he, they and you, yes, every man, shall be my friend again, and I'll be his. So tell your nephew, and then bring me word what he will do. But if he won't give in, rebuke and fearful punishment wait at our command, and they will do their job. So go. We won't be troubled with a reply now. Our offer is fair; consider it wisely."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Worcester with Vernon.","id":808},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["It will not be accepted, on my life:","The Douglas and the Hotspur both together","Are confident against the world in arms."],"id":809,"m":"It won't be accepted, on my life. Douglas and Hotspur together are confident against the whole world in arms."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Hence, therefore, every leader to his charge;","For, on their answer, will we set on them:","And God befriend us, as our cause is just!"],"id":810,"m":"Away, then, every commander to his troops, because once they've answered, we'll attack them. And may God befriend us, since our cause is just!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt the King, Blunt, and Prince John.","id":811},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Hal, if thou see me down in the battle, and bestride me,","so; 'tis a point of friendship."],"id":812,"m":"Hal, if you see me down in the battle, stand over me to protect me. It's a point of friendship."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Nothing but a colossus can do thee that friendship.","Say thy prayers, and farewell."],"id":813,"m":"Only a colossus could do you that kind of friendship. Say your prayers, and goodbye."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["I would it were bedtime, Hal, and all well."],"id":814,"m":"I wish it were bedtime, Hal, and all was well."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Why, thou owest God a death."],"id":815,"m":"Why, you owe God a death."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":816},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["'Tis not due yet; I would be loth to pay Him before His day.","What need I be so forward with him that calls not on me?","Well, 'tis no matter; honour pricks me on. Yea, but how if honour","prick me off when I come on? how then? Can honour set-to a leg?","no: or an arm? no: or take away the grief of a wound? no. Honour","hath no skill in surgery then? no. What is honour? a word. What","is that word, honour? air. A trim reckoning!—Who hath it? he that","died o' Wednesday. Doth he feel it? no. Doth he hear it? no. Is it","insensible, then? yea, to the dead. But will it not live with the","living? no. Why? detraction will not suffer it. Therefore I'll none","of it: honour is a mere scutcheon:—and so ends my catechism."],"id":817,"m":"It isn't due yet; I'd hate to pay him before the day it's due. Why should I be so eager to meet someone who isn't calling on me? Well, it doesn't matter; honour spurs me on. Yes, but what if honour marks me off the list of the living when I come on? What then? Can honour set a broken leg? No. Or an arm? No. Or take away the pain of a wound? No. Honour has no skill in surgery, then? No. What is honour? A word. What is in that word \"honour\"? Air. A fine sum! Who has it? The man who died on Wednesday. Does he feel it? No. Does he hear it? No. So it can't be sensed? Yes, by the dead. But won't it live with the living? No. Why? Slander won't allow it. So I'll have none of it. Honour is just a painted coat of arms at a funeral. And so my catechism ends."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":818},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"II","loc":"The Rebel Camp.","id":819},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Worcester and Vernon.","id":820},{"t":"sp","who":"WORCESTER","lines":["O no, my nephew must not know, Sir Richard,","The liberal-kind offer of the King."],"id":821,"m":"Oh no, my nephew mustn't know about the King's generous and kind offer, Sir Richard."},{"t":"sp","who":"VERNON","lines":["'Twere best he did."],"id":822,"m":"It would be best if he did."},{"t":"sp","who":"WORCESTER","lines":["Then are we all undone.","It is not possible, it cannot be,","The King should keep his word in loving us;","He will suspect us still, and find a time","To punish this offence in other faults:","Suspicion all our lives shall be stuck full of eyes;","For treason is but trusted like the fox,","Who, ne'er so tame, so cherish'd, and lock'd up,","Will have a wild trick of his ancestors.","Look how we can, or sad or merrily,","Interpretation will misquote our looks;","And we shall feed like oxen at a stall,","The better cherish'd, still the nearer death.","My nephew's trespass may be well forgot:","It hath th' excuse of youth and heat of blood,","And an adopted name of privilege,—","A hare-brain'd Hotspur, govern'd by a spleen:","All his offences live upon my head","And on his father's: we did train him on;","And, his corruption being ta'en from us,","We, as the spring of all, shall pay for all.","Therefore, good cousin, let not Harry know,","In any case, the offer of the King."],"id":823,"m":"Then we are all ruined. It isn't possible, it can't be, that the King will keep his word to love us. He'll always suspect us and find a time to punish this offence through other faults. All our lives suspicion will be watching us with a hundred eyes, because treason is trusted only like a fox, which, however tame, well cared for and locked up, will still have some wild trick of its ancestors. However we look, sad or merry, people will misread our looks, and we'll feed like oxen in a stall: the better looked after, the nearer to death. My nephew's offence may well be forgotten. It has the excuse of youth and hot blood, and a nickname that gives him licence, a hare-brained Hotspur, ruled by his temper. All his offences rest on my head and on his father's. We led him on, and since he caught his corruption from us, we, as the source of it all, will pay for all. So, good cousin, don't let Harry know about the King's offer in any circumstances."},{"t":"sp","who":"VERNON","lines":["Deliver what you will, I'll say 'tis so.","Here comes your cousin."],"id":824,"m":"Tell him what you like; I'll say it's so. Here comes your nephew."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Hotspur and Douglas; Officers and Soldiers behind.","id":825},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["My uncle is return'd: deliver up","My Lord of Westmoreland.—Uncle, what news?"],"id":826,"m":"My uncle has returned. Release my lord of Westmoreland. Uncle, what news?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WORCESTER","lines":["The King will bid you battle presently."],"id":827,"m":"The King will offer you battle at once."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOUGLAS","lines":["Defy him by the Lord Of Westmoreland."],"id":828,"m":"Send him a challenge by the lord of Westmoreland."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["Lord Douglas, go you and tell him so."],"id":829,"m":"Lord Douglas, go and tell him so."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOUGLAS","lines":["Marry, I shall, and very willingly."],"id":830,"m":"Indeed I will, and very willingly."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":831},{"t":"sp","who":"WORCESTER","lines":["There is no seeming mercy in the King."],"id":832,"m":"There's no sign of mercy in the King."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["Did you beg any? God forbid!"],"id":833,"m":"Did you beg for any? God forbid!"},{"t":"sp","who":"WORCESTER","lines":["I told him gently of our grievances,","Of his oath-breaking; which he mended thus,","By new-forswearing that he is forsworn:","He calls us rebels, traitors; and will scourge","With haughty arms this hateful name in us."],"id":834,"m":"I told him gently about our grievances and his oath-breaking, which he corrected by swearing falsely all over again that he hadn't broken his oath. He calls us rebels and traitors, and will punish this hateful name in us with proud arms."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Douglas.","id":835},{"t":"sp","who":"DOUGLAS","lines":["Arm, gentlemen; to arms! for I have thrown","A brave defiance in King Henry's teeth,","And Westmoreland, that was engaged, did bear it;","Which cannot choose but bring him quickly on."],"id":836,"m":"To arms, gentlemen, to arms! I've thrown a bold defiance in King Henry's teeth, and Westmoreland, who was our hostage, carried it, which is bound to bring him on quickly."},{"t":"sp","who":"WORCESTER","lines":["The Prince of Wales stepp'd forth before the King,","And, nephew, challenged you to single fight."],"id":837,"m":"The Prince of Wales stepped out in front of the King and, nephew, challenged you to single combat."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["O, would the quarrel lay upon our heads;","And that no man might draw short breath to-day","But I and Harry Monmouth! Tell me, tell me,","How show'd his tasking? seem'd it in contempt?"],"id":838,"m":"Oh, I wish the quarrel rested on our heads alone, and that no man would be out of breath today except me and Harry Monmouth! Tell me, tell me, how did his challenge sound? Did it seem scornful?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VERNON","lines":["No, by my soul: I never in my life","Did hear a challenge urged more modestly,","Unless a brother should a brother dare","To gentle exercise and proof of arms.","He gave you all the duties of a man;","Trimm'd up your praises with a princely tongue;","Spoke your deservings like a chronicle;","Making you ever better than his praise,","By still dispraising praise valued with you;","And, which became him like a prince indeed,","He made a blushing cital of himself;","And chid his truant youth with such a grace,","As if he master'd there a double spirit,","Of teaching and of learning instantly.","There did he pause: but let me tell the world,","If he outlive the envy of this day,","England did never owe so sweet a hope,","So much misconstrued in his wantonness."],"id":839,"m":"No, by my soul. I never in my life heard a challenge made more modestly, unless a brother were challenging a brother to a friendly bout of arms. He gave you every honour due to a man, decorated your praises with a princely tongue, listed your merits like a history book, always making you better than his praise by dismissing praise itself as inadequate compared with you. And, what really suited him like a true prince, he gave a blushing account of himself and scolded his truant youth so gracefully that it was as if he had a double gift, teaching and learning at the same moment. There he stopped. But let me tell the world, if he survives the hostility of this day, England never had so sweet a hope, so much misjudged in his wildness."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["Cousin, I think thou art enamoured","Upon his follies: never did I hear","Of any prince so wild o' liberty.","But be he as he will, yet once ere night","I will embrace him with a soldier's arm,","That he shall shrink under my courtesy.—","Arm, arm with speed: and, fellows, soldiers, friends,","Better consider what you have to do","Than I, that have not well the gift of tongue,","Can lift your blood up with persuasion."],"id":840,"m":"Cousin, I think you've fallen in love with his follies. I never heard of any prince so wildly free. But let him be what he likes, before night I'll embrace him with a soldier's arm so that he'll shrink under my courtesy. To arms, to arms, quickly! And, comrades, soldiers, friends, you'd better think for yourselves about what you have to do, because I don't really have the gift of words to stir up your blood with persuasion."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Messenger.","id":841},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["My lord, here are letters for you."],"id":842,"m":"My lord, here are letters for you."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["I cannot read them now.—","O gentlemen, the time of life is short!","To spend that shortness basely were too long,","If life did ride upon a dial's point,","Still ending at th' arrival of an hour.","An if we live, we live to tread on kings;","If die, brave death, when princes die with us!","Now, for our consciences, the arms are fair,","When the intent of bearing them is just."],"id":843,"m":"I can't read them now. Oh gentlemen, life is short! But to spend that short time basely would be too long, even if life rode on the hand of a clock and ended when the hour came. If we live, we live to trample on kings; if we die, it's a fine death when princes die with us! Now, as for our consciences, the cause is fair when the reason for taking up arms is just."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter another Messenger.","id":844},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["My lord, prepare: the King comes on apace."],"id":845,"m":"My lord, get ready. The King is advancing fast."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["I thank him, that he cuts me from my tale,","For I profess not talking; only this,","Let each man do his best: and here draw I","A sword, whose temper I intend to stain","With the best blood that I can meet withal","In the adventure of this perilous day.","Now, Esperance! Percy! and set on.","Sound all the lofty instruments of war,","And by that music let us all embrace;","For, Heaven to Earth, some of us never shall","A second time do such a courtesy."],"id":846,"m":"I thank him for cutting short my speech, because talking isn't my trade. Only this: let each man do his best. And here I draw a sword whose steel I mean to stain with the best blood I can meet in the adventure of this dangerous day. Now, Esperance! Percy! And charge! Sound all the lofty instruments of war, and to that music let us all embrace, because, heaven to earth, some of us will never do such a kindness a second time."},{"t":"sd","text":"The trumpets sound. They embrace, and exeunt.","id":847},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"III","loc":"Plain between the Camps.","id":848},{"t":"sd","text":"Excursions, and Parties fighting. Alarum to the battle. Then enter Douglas and Sir Walter Blunt, meeting.","id":849},{"t":"sp","who":"BLUNT","lines":["What is thy name, that in the battle thus","Thou crossest me? what honour dost thou seek","Upon my head?"],"id":850,"m":"What's your name, that you come at me like this in the battle? What honour are you looking for from my head?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DOUGLAS","lines":["Know, then, my name is Douglas,","And I do haunt thee in the battle thus","Because some tell me that thou art a king."],"id":851,"m":"Know, then, my name is Douglas, and I hunt you like this in the battle because some people tell me you are a king."},{"t":"sp","who":"BLUNT","lines":["They tell thee true."],"id":852,"m":"They tell you the truth."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOUGLAS","lines":["The Lord of Stafford dear to-day hath bought","Thy likeness; for, instead of thee, King Harry,","This sword hath ended him: so shall it thee,","Unless thou yield thee as my prisoner."],"id":853,"m":"The Lord of Stafford paid dearly today for looking like you, because this sword killed him instead of you, King Harry; and it will kill you, unless you surrender as my prisoner."},{"t":"sp","who":"BLUNT","lines":["I was not born a yielder, thou proud Scot;","And thou shalt find a king that will revenge","Lord Stafford's death."],"id":854,"m":"I wasn't born to surrender, you proud Scot, and you'll find a king who will avenge Lord Stafford's death."},{"t":"sd","text":"They fight, and Blunt is slain. Enter Hotspur.","id":855},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["O Douglas, hadst thou fought at Holmedon thus,","I never had triumphed o'er a Scot."],"id":856,"m":"Oh Douglas, if you had fought like this at Holmedon, I would never have beaten a Scot."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOUGLAS","lines":["All's done, all's won; here breathless lies the King."],"id":857,"m":"It's all over, all won. Here lies the King, dead."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["Where?"],"id":858,"m":"Where?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DOUGLAS","lines":["Here."],"id":859,"m":"Here."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["This, Douglas? no; I know this face full well:","A gallant knight he was, his name was Blunt;","Semblably furnish'd like the King himself."],"id":860,"m":"This, Douglas? No, I know this face very well. He was a brave knight; his name was Blunt, dressed just like the King himself."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOUGLAS","lines":["A fool go with thy soul, where're it goes!","A borrow'd title hast thou bought too dear:","Why didst thou tell me that thou wert a king?"],"id":861,"m":"May the name of fool go with your soul, wherever it goes! You've paid too much for a borrowed title. Why did you tell me you were a king?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["The King hath many marching in his coats."],"id":862,"m":"The King has many men marching in his coats of arms."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOUGLAS","lines":["Now, by my sword, I will kill all his coats;","I'll murder all his wardrobe piece by piece,","Until I meet the King."],"id":863,"m":"Now, by my sword, I'll kill all his coats. I'll murder his whole wardrobe piece by piece until I meet the King."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["Up, and away!","Our soldiers stand full fairly for the day."],"id":864,"m":"Up, and away! Our soldiers are well placed to win the day."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":865},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarums. Enter Falstaff.","id":866},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Though I could 'scape shot-free at London, I fear the shot","here; here's no scoring but upon the pate.—Soft! who are you?","Sir Walter Blunt: there's honour for you! here's no vanity! I am","as hot as molten lead, and as heavy too: God keep lead out of me!","I need no more weight than mine own bowels. I have led my","ragamuffins where they are peppered: there's not three of my","hundred and fifty left alive; and they are for the town's end, to","beg during life. But who comes here?"],"id":867,"m":"Though I could escape paying the bill in London, I'm afraid of the shot here; there's no chalking it up here except on your head. Wait! Who are you? Sir Walter Blunt. There's honour for you! That's no empty show! I'm as hot as molten lead, and as heavy too. God keep lead out of me! I need no more weight than my own guts. I've led my ragamuffins where they've been shot to pieces. There aren't three of my hundred and fifty left alive, and they're fit for nothing but begging at the town gates for the rest of their lives. But who's coming here?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Prince Henry.","id":868},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["What, stand'st thou idle here? lend me thy sword:","Many a nobleman lies stark and stiff","Under the hoofs of vaunting enemies,","Whose deaths are yet unrevenged: I pr'ythee,","Lend me thy sword."],"id":869,"m":"What, are you standing idle here? Lend me your sword. Many a nobleman lies stark and stiff under the hooves of boasting enemies, and their deaths are not yet avenged. Please lend me your sword."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["O Hal, I pr'ythee give me leave to breathe awhile. Turk","Gregory never did such deeds in arms as I have done this","day. I have paid Percy, I have made him sure."],"id":870,"m":"Oh Hal, please let me get my breath for a while. Turk Gregory never did such deeds in battle as I've done today. I've paid Percy back; I've made sure of him."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["He is indeed; and living to kill thee.","I pr'ythee, lend me thy sword."],"id":871,"m":"He's sure indeed, and alive to kill you. Please lend me your sword."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Nay, before God, Hal, if Percy be alive, thou gett'st not","my sword; but take my pistol, if thou wilt."],"id":872,"m":"No, before God, Hal, if Percy's alive, you won't get my sword. But take my pistol, if you like."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Give it me: what, is it in the case?"],"id":873,"m":"Give it to me. What, is it in the holster?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Ay, Hal. 'Tis hot, 'tis hot: there's that will sack a city."],"id":874,"m":"Yes, Hal. It's hot, it's hot. There's something there that will sack a city."},{"t":"sd","text":"The Prince draws out a bottle of sack.","id":875},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["What, is't a time to jest and dally now?"],"id":876,"m":"What, is this a time to joke and fool around?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Throws it at him, and exit.","id":877},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Well, if Percy be alive, I'll pierce him. If he do come in my","way, so; if he do not, if I come in his willingly, let him make","a carbonado of me. I like not such grinning honour as Sir","Walter hath: give me life; which if I can save, so; if not,","honour comes unlooked for, and there's an end."],"id":878,"m":"Well, if Percy's alive, I'll pierce him. If he comes my way, fine; if he doesn't, and I go into his way willingly, let him cut me up for grilling. I don't like the kind of grinning honour Sir Walter has. Give me life. If I can save it, fine; if not, honour comes without my asking for it, and that's the end of it."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":879},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"IV","loc":"Another Part of the Field.","id":880},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarums. Excursions. Enter King Henry, Prince Henry, Lancaster, and Westmoreland.","id":881},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["I pr'ythee,","Harry, withdraw thyself; thou bleedest too much.—","Lord John of Lancaster, go you unto him."],"id":882,"m":"Please, Harry, withdraw; you're bleeding too much. Lord John of Lancaster, go with him."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCE JOHN","lines":["Not I, my lord, unless I did bleed too."],"id":883,"m":"Not I, my lord, unless I were bleeding too."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["I do beseech your Majesty, make up,","Lest your retirement do amaze your friends."],"id":884,"m":"I beg your Majesty, go forward, in case your retreat alarms your friends."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["I will do so.—","My Lord of Westmoreland, lead him to his tent."],"id":885,"m":"I will. My lord of Westmoreland, lead him to his tent."},{"t":"sp","who":"WESTMORELAND","lines":["Come, my lord, I'll lead you to your tent."],"id":886,"m":"Come, my lord, I'll lead you to your tent."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Lead me, my lord? I do not need your help:","And God forbid, a shallow scratch should drive","The Prince of Wales from such a field as this,","Where stain'd nobility lies trodden on,","And rebels' arms triumph in massacres!"],"id":887,"m":"Lead me, my lord? I don't need your help. God forbid that a shallow scratch should drive the Prince of Wales from such a battlefield as this, where noblemen lie trampled in blood and rebels' weapons triumph in slaughter!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCE JOHN","lines":["We breathe too long:—come, cousin Westmoreland,","Our duty this way lies; for God's sake, come."],"id":888,"m":"We're resting too long. Come, cousin Westmoreland, our duty lies this way. For God's sake, come."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Lancaster and Westmoreland.","id":889},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["By Heaven, thou hast deceived me, Lancaster;","I did not think thee lord of such a spirit:","Before, I loved thee as a brother, John;","But now I do respect thee as my soul."],"id":890,"m":"By heaven, you've surprised me, Lancaster; I didn't think you had such spirit. Before, I loved you as a brother, John; but now I respect you as my own soul."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["I saw him hold Lord Percy at the point","With lustier maintenance than I did look for","Of such an ungrown warrior."],"id":891,"m":"I saw him hold Lord Percy off at sword's point with more vigorous resistance than I expected from such a young warrior."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["O, this boy","Lends mettle to us all!"],"id":892,"m":"Oh, this boy gives us all courage!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":893},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarums. Enter Douglas.","id":894},{"t":"sp","who":"DOUGLAS","lines":["Another king! they grow like Hydra's heads:","I am the Douglas, fatal to all those","That wear those colours on them.—What art thou,","That counterfeit'st the person of a king?"],"id":895,"m":"Another king! They grow like the Hydra's heads. I am Douglas, deadly to everyone who wears those colours. Who are you, pretending to be a king?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["The King himself; who, Douglas, grieves at heart","So many of his shadows thou hast met,","And not the very King. I have two boys","Seek Percy and thyself about the field:","But, seeing thou fall'st on me so luckily,","I will assay thee; so, defend thyself."],"id":896,"m":"The King himself, who is sorry at heart, Douglas, that you've met so many of his shadows and not the real King. I have two sons looking for Percy and you around the battlefield; but since you've fallen on me so luckily, I'll take you on. So defend yourself."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOUGLAS","lines":["I fear thou art another counterfeit;","And yet, in faith, thou bear'st thee like a king:","But mine I'm sure thou art, whoe'er thou be,","And thus I win thee."],"id":897,"m":"I'm afraid you're another fake; and yet, honestly, you carry yourself like a king. But whoever you are, I'm sure you're mine, and this is how I'll win you."},{"t":"sd","text":"They fight; the King being in danger, re-enter Prince Henry.","id":898},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Hold up thy head, vile Scot, or thou art like","Never to hold it up again! the spirits","Of valiant Shirley, Stafford, Blunt are in my arms:","It is the Prince of Wales that threatens thee;","Who never promiseth but he means to pay.—"],"id":899,"m":"Hold your head up, you vile Scot, or you're likely never to hold it up again! The spirits of brave Shirley, Stafford and Blunt are in my arms. It's the Prince of Wales who threatens you, and he never promises without meaning to pay."},{"t":"sd","text":"They fight: Douglas flies.","id":900},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","cont":1,"lines":["Cheerly, my lord: how fares your Grace?","Sir Nicholas Gawsey hath for succour sent,","And so hath Clifton: I'll to Clifton straight."],"id":901,"m":"Cheer up, my lord. How is your Grace? Sir Nicholas Gawsey has sent for help, and so has Clifton. I'll go to Clifton at once."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Stay, and breathe awhile:","Thou hast redeem'd thy lost opinion;","And show'd thou makest some tender of my life,","In this fair rescue thou hast brought to me."],"id":902,"m":"Stay and rest for a while. You've won back your lost reputation and shown that you value my life, by this fine rescue you've made."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["O God, they did me too much injury","That ever said I hearken'd for your death!","If it were so, I might have let alone","Th' insulting hand of Douglas over you,","Which would have been as speedy in your end","As all the poisonous potions in the world,","And saved the treacherous labour of your son."],"id":903,"m":"Oh God, those who said I was waiting for your death did me a great wrong! If that were so, I could have left Douglas's insulting hand alone over you, which would have ended you as quickly as all the poisons in the world, and saved your son the treacherous labour."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Make up to Clifton: I'll to Sir Nicholas Gawsey."],"id":904,"m":"Go on to Clifton. I'll go to Sir Nicholas Gawsey."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":905},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Hotspur.","id":906},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["If I mistake not, thou art Harry Monmouth."],"id":907,"m":"If I'm not mistaken, you're Harry Monmouth."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Thou speak'st as if I would deny my name."],"id":908,"m":"You speak as if I would deny my name."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["My name is Harry Percy."],"id":909,"m":"My name is Harry Percy."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Why, then I see","A very valiant rebel of the name.","I am the Prince of Wales; and think not, Percy,","To share with me in glory any more:","Two stars keep not their motion in one sphere;","Nor can one England brook a double reign,","Of Harry Percy and the Prince of Wales."],"id":910,"m":"Why, then I see a very brave rebel of that name. I am the Prince of Wales; and don't think, Percy, that you'll share glory with me any longer. Two stars can't move in one sphere, nor can one England stand a double reign of Harry Percy and the Prince of Wales."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["Nor shall it, Harry; for the hour is come","To end the one of us; and would to God","Thy name in arms were now as great as mine!"],"id":911,"m":"Nor shall it, Harry, because the hour has come to end one of us; and I wish to God your name in war were now as great as mine!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["I'll make it greater ere I part from thee;","And all the budding honours on thy crest","I'll crop, to make a garland for my head."],"id":912,"m":"I'll make it greater before I leave you, and I'll cut off all the budding honours on your helmet to make a garland for my head."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["I can no longer brook thy vanities."],"id":913,"m":"I can't stand your boasting any longer."},{"t":"sd","text":"They fight.","id":914},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Falstaff.","id":915},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Well said, Hal! to it, Hal! Nay, you shall find no boy's","play here, I can tell you."],"id":916,"m":"Well done, Hal! At him, Hal! No, you won't find any child's play here, I can tell you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Douglas; he fights with Falstaff, who falls down as if he were dead, and exit Douglas. Hotspur is wounded, and falls.","id":917},{"t":"sp","who":"HOTSPUR","lines":["O Harry, thou hast robb'd me of my youth!","I better brook the loss of brittle life","Than those proud titles thou hast won of me;","They wound my thoughts worse than thy sword my flesh:","But thoughts the slave of life, and life Time's fool,","And Time, that takes survey of all the world,","Must have a stop. O, I could prophesy,","But that the earthy and cold hand of death","Lies on my tongue: no, Percy, thou art dust,","And food for—"],"id":918,"m":"Oh Harry, you've robbed me of my youth! I can bear the loss of fragile life better than the proud titles you've won from me; they wound my thoughts worse than your sword wounds my flesh. But thoughts are the slaves of life, and life is time's fool, and time, which surveys the whole world, must have an end. Oh, I could prophesy, except that the earthy, cold hand of death lies on my tongue. No, Percy, you are dust, and food for..."},{"t":"sd","text":"Dies.","id":919},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["For worms, brave Percy: fare thee well, great heart!","Ill-weaved ambition, how much art thou shrunk!","When that this body did contain a spirit,","A kingdom for it was too small a bound;","But now two paces of the vilest earth","Is room enough. This earth that bears thee dead","Bears not alive so stout a gentleman.","If thou wert sensible of courtesy,","I should not make so dear a show of zeal:","But let my favours hide thy mangled face;","And, even in thy behalf, I'll thank myself","For doing these fair rites of tenderness.","Adieu, and take thy praise with thee to Heaven!","Thy ignominy sleep with thee in the grave,","But not remember'd in thy epitaph!—"],"id":920,"m":"For worms, brave Percy. Farewell, great heart! Badly woven ambition, how much you have shrunk! When this body held a spirit, a kingdom was too small a boundary for it; but now two paces of the lowest earth is room enough. This earth that carries you dead does not carry so brave a gentleman alive. If you could feel courtesy, I wouldn't show my admiration so openly. But let my plumes cover your mangled face, and on your behalf I'll thank myself for doing these kind last rites. Goodbye, and take your praise with you to heaven! Let your disgrace sleep with you in the grave and not be remembered in your epitaph!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Sees Falstaff on the ground.","id":921},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","cont":1,"lines":["What, old acquaintance? could not all this flesh","Keep in a little life? Poor Jack, farewell!","I could have better spared a better man:","O, I should have a heavy miss of thee,","If I were much in love with vanity!","Death hath not struck so fat a deer to-day,","Though many dearer, in this bloody fray.","Embowell'd will I see thee by-and-by:","Till then in blood by noble Percy lie."],"id":922,"m":"What, old friend? Couldn't all this flesh hold in a little life? Poor Jack, farewell! I could have better spared a better man. Oh, I would miss you badly if I were much in love with foolishness! Death hasn't struck down so fat a deer today, though many more precious ones, in this bloody battle. I'll see you disembowelled for embalming soon; till then lie in blood beside noble Percy."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":923},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["[Rising] Embowell'd! if thou embowel me to-day, I'll give you leave","to powder me and eat me too to-morrow. 'Sblood, 'twas time to","counterfeit, or that hot termagant Scot had paid me scot and lot too.","Counterfeit! I lie; I am no counterfeit: to die, is to be a","counterfeit; for he is but the counterfeit of a man who hath not the","life of a man: but to counterfeit dying, when a man thereby liveth,","is to be no counterfeit, but the true and perfect image of life indeed.","The better part of valour is discretion; in the which better part I","have saved my life.—","Zwounds, I am afraid of this gunpowder Percy, though he be dead: how,","if he should counterfeit too, and rise? by my faith, I am afraid he","would prove the better counterfeit. Therefore I'll make him sure; yea,","and I'll swear I kill'd him. Why may not he rise as well as I?","Nothing confutes me but eyes, and nobody sees me. Therefore,","sirrah, with a new wound in your thigh, come you along with me."],"id":924,"m":"Disembowelled! If you disembowel me today, I'll give you permission to salt me and eat me too tomorrow. By God's blood, it was time to fake it, or that hot-tempered savage Scot would have paid me off in full. Fake! I'm lying; I'm no fake. To die is to be a fake, because a man without a man's life is only a fake of a man. But to fake dying, when a man stays alive by it, is not to be a fake but the true and perfect image of life. The better part of courage is caution, and by that better part I have saved my life. By God's wounds, I'm afraid of this gunpowder Percy, even though he's dead. What if he's faking too, and gets up? By my faith, I'm afraid he'd turn out the better faker. So I'll make sure of him; yes, and I'll swear I killed him. Why couldn't he get up as well as I did? Nothing can disprove me but eyes, and nobody sees me. So, my man, with a new wound in your thigh, come along with me."},{"t":"sd","text":"Takes Hotspur on his back.","id":925},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Prince Henry and Lancaster.","id":926},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Come, brother John; full bravely hast thou flesh'd","Thy maiden sword."],"id":927,"m":"Come, brother John, you've bravely blooded your sword for the first time."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCE JOHN","lines":["But, soft! whom have we here?","Did you not tell me this fat man was dead?"],"id":928,"m":"But wait! Who have we here? Didn't you tell me this fat man was dead?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["I did; I saw him dead, breathless and bleeding","Upon the ground.—","Art thou alive? or is it fantasy","That plays upon our eyesight? I pr'ythee, speak;","We will not trust our eyes without our ears.","Thou art not what thou seem'st."],"id":929,"m":"I did. I saw him dead, not breathing and bleeding on the ground. Are you alive? Or is it a fantasy playing tricks on our eyes? Please speak; we won't trust our eyes without our ears. You aren't what you seem."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["No, that's certain; I am not a double man: but if I be not","Jack Falstaff, then am I a Jack. There is Percy! [Throwing the","body down.] if your father will do me any honour, so; if not, let","him kill the next Percy himself. I look to be either earl or","duke, I can assure you."],"id":930,"m":"No, that's certain; I'm not a ghostly double. But if I'm not Jack Falstaff, then I'm a knave. There is Percy! If your father will do me any honour, fine; if not, let him kill the next Percy himself. I expect to be made either an earl or a duke, I can assure you."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Why, Percy I kill'd myself, and saw thee dead."],"id":931,"m":"Why, I killed Percy myself, and saw you dead."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Didst thou?—Lord, Lord, how this world is given to lying!—","I grant you I was down and out of breath; and so was he: but","we rose both at an instant, and fought a long hour by Shrewsbury","clock. If I may be believed, so; if not, let them that should","reward valour bear the sin upon their own heads. I'll take it upon","my death, I gave him this wound in the thigh: if the man were","alive, and would deny it, zwounds, I would make him eat a piece of","my sword."],"id":932,"m":"Did you? Lord, Lord, how this world is given to lying! I grant you I was down and out of breath, and so was he; but we both got up at the same moment and fought a long hour by Shrewsbury clock. If I'm believed, fine; if not, let those who ought to reward courage carry the sin on their own heads. I'll swear on my deathbed I gave him this wound in the thigh. If the man were alive and denied it, by God's wounds, I'd make him eat a piece of my sword."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCE JOHN","lines":["This is the strangest tale that ever I heard."],"id":933,"m":"This is the strangest story I ever heard."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["This is the strangest fellow, brother John.—","Come, bring your luggage nobly on your back:","For my part, if a lie may do thee grace,","I'll gild it with the happiest terms I have.—"],"id":934,"m":"This is the strangest fellow, brother John. Come, carry your baggage nobly on your back. For my part, if a lie will do you credit, I'll gild it with the finest words I have."},{"t":"sd","text":"A retreat is sounded.","id":935},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","cont":1,"lines":["The trumpet sounds retreat; the day is ours.","Come, brother, let's to th' highest of the field,","To see what friends are living, who are dead."],"id":936,"m":"The trumpet is sounding the retreat; the day is ours. Come, brother, let's go to the highest point of the field to see which of our friends are alive and who are dead."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Prince Henry and Lancaster.","id":937},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["I'll follow, as they say, for reward. He that rewards me, God","reward him! If I do grow great, I'll grow less; for I'll purge,","and leave sack, and live cleanly as a nobleman should do."],"id":938,"m":"I'll follow, as they say, for my reward. Whoever rewards me, God reward him! If I become great, I'll grow smaller, because I'll take medicines, give up sherry and live decently, as a nobleman should."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit, bearing off the body.","id":939},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"V","loc":"Another Part of the Field.","id":940},{"t":"sd","text":"The trumpets sound. Enter King Henry, Prince Henry, Lancaster, Westmoreland, and others, with Worcester and Vernon prisoners.","id":941},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Thus ever did rebellion find rebuke.—","Ill-spirited Worcester! did not we send grace,","Pardon, and terms of love to all of you?","And wouldst thou turn our offers contrary?","Misuse the tenour of thy kinsman's trust?","Three knights upon our party slain to-day,","A noble earl, and many a creature else,","Had been alive this hour,","If, like a Christian, thou hadst truly borne","Betwixt our armies true intelligence."],"id":942,"m":"This is how rebellion always met its punishment. Evil-minded Worcester! Didn't we send mercy, pardon and terms of friendship to all of you? And would you twist our offers into their opposite, and abuse your nephew's trust? Three knights on our side killed today, a noble earl and many others would be alive at this hour if you had, like a Christian, carried honest messages between our armies."},{"t":"sp","who":"WORCESTER","lines":["What I have done my safety urged me to;","And I embrace this fortune patiently,","Since not to be avoided it falls on me."],"id":943,"m":"What I did, my safety drove me to do, and I accept this fate patiently, since it falls on me and can't be avoided."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Bear Worcester to the death, and Vernon too:","Other offenders we will pause upon.—"],"id":944,"m":"Take Worcester to his execution, and Vernon too. We'll think further about the other offenders."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Worcester and Vernon, guarded.","id":945},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","cont":1,"lines":["How goes the field?"],"id":946,"m":"How goes the battle?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["The noble Scot, Lord Douglas, when he saw","The fortune of the day quite turn'd from him,","The noble Percy slain, and all his men","Upon the foot of fear, fled with the rest;","And, falling from a hill, he was so bruised","That the pursuers took him. At my tent","The Douglas is: and I beseech your Grace","I may dispose of him."],"id":947,"m":"The noble Scot, Lord Douglas, when he saw the fortune of the day had completely turned against him, noble Percy killed and all his men running in fear, fled with the rest; and falling from a hill he was so badly bruised that his pursuers caught him. Douglas is in my tent, and I beg your Grace to let me decide what to do with him."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["With all my heart."],"id":948,"m":"With all my heart."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Then, brother John of Lancaster, to you","This honourable bounty shall belong:","Go to the Douglas, and deliver him","Up to his pleasure, ransomless and free:","His valour, shown upon our crests to-day,","Hath taught us how to cherish such high deeds","Even in the bosom of our adversaries."],"id":949,"m":"Then, brother John of Lancaster, this honourable gift shall be yours. Go to Douglas and set him free to go where he pleases, without ransom. His courage, shown on our helmets today, has taught us to value such great deeds even in our enemies."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Then this remains, that we divide our power.—","You, son John, and my cousin Westmoreland,","Towards York shall bend you with your dearest speed,","To meet Northumberland and the prelate Scroop,","Who, as we hear, are busily in arms:","Myself,—and you, son Harry,—will towards Wales,","To fight with Glendower and the Earl of March.","Rebellion in this land shall lose his sway,","Meeting the check of such another day;","And since this business so fair is done,","Let us not leave till all our own be won."],"id":950,"m":"Then all that remains is to divide our army. You, my son John, and my cousin Westmoreland, head towards York with all possible speed to meet Northumberland and the churchman Scroop, who, we hear, are busy raising arms. I, and you, son Harry, will go towards Wales to fight Glendower and the Earl of March. Rebellion in this country will lose its power when it meets the check of another day like this. And since this business has gone so well, let's not stop until all that is ours is won."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":951}];
const STUDY={"chars":[{"name":"Prince Hal","group":"The royal house","desc":"Henry, Prince of Wales, the King's eldest son and heir, called Hal by his tavern friends and Harry Monmouth by his enemies. He spends his time in Eastcheap with Falstaff, Poins and their gang, robbing travellers for sport and staging plays in the tavern, to his father's despair. From his first soliloquy he tells the audience that this is a deliberate act: he will 'imitate the sun' and break out of the clouds when it suits him. Summoned by the King, he vows to redeem himself 'on Percy's head'. At Shrewsbury he rescues his father from Douglas, kills Hotspur in single combat, lets Falstaff take the credit, and frees Douglas without ransom."},{"name":"King Henry IV","group":"The royal house","desc":"Henry Bolingbroke, who deposed Richard II with the Percys' help and now finds his reign troubled by war in Wales, the North and Scotland. He longs for a crusade to the Holy Land that never happens, and he is haunted by guilt and by his son's wild behaviour, which he half believes is God's punishment. He is a shrewd politician who knows the value of being seen rarely. He turns against the Percys over the Holmedon prisoners, offers pardon before Shrewsbury, and is saved in the battle by the son he doubted."},{"name":"Prince John of Lancaster","group":"The royal house","desc":"The King's younger son. He takes Hal's place in the Council, fights bravely at Shrewsbury and earns Hal's respect. Hal gives him the honour of freeing Douglas."},{"name":"Earl of Westmoreland","group":"The King's side","desc":"The King's kinsman and loyal commander. He brings the news from Wales and Holmedon in the first scene, serves as hostage while Worcester parleys, and marches with Prince John."},{"name":"Sir Walter Blunt","group":"The King's side","desc":"A plain, loyal knight. He brings news of Holmedon, carries the King's offer of pardon to the rebels, and at Shrewsbury fights dressed as the King. Douglas kills him thinking he is Henry."},{"name":"Hotspur (Harry Percy)","group":"The Percys","desc":"Young Henry Percy, son of the Earl of Northumberland, famous for his victory over the Scots at Holmedon. Brave, impatient, quick-tempered and obsessed with honour, he refuses to hand over his prisoners, rages at the King, mocks Glendower's magic and poetry, and teases his wife rather than share his plans. He is everything the King wishes his own son were. He insists on fighting at Shrewsbury though his father and Glendower fail him, and he dies at Hal's hand, mourning the loss of his 'proud titles' more than his life."},{"name":"Earl of Worcester","group":"The Percys","desc":"Thomas Percy, Hotspur's uncle and the brains of the rebellion. Dismissed from court by the King, he plans the alliance with Mortimer, Glendower, Douglas and the Archbishop. Convinced that the King will never trust the Percys again, he hides the King's generous offer from Hotspur before the battle. He is captured and sent to his execution."},{"name":"Earl of Northumberland","group":"The Percys","desc":"Henry Percy, Hotspur's father, who helped Bolingbroke to the crown. He joins the plot but falls 'grievous sick' before Shrewsbury and sends only letters, leaving his son to fight without him."},{"name":"Lady Percy (Kate)","group":"The Percys","desc":"Hotspur's wife, Mortimer's sister. Sharp, affectionate and witty, she demands to know why her husband talks of war in his sleep. He teases her, refuses to tell her his plans, and leaves for the war."},{"name":"Edmund Mortimer, Earl of March","group":"The rebels","desc":"Hotspur's brother-in-law, captured by Glendower in Wales and married to his daughter. King Richard named him heir to the throne, which is why the King refuses to ransom him and why the rebels want him. He is gentle and in love with a wife whose Welsh he cannot understand. He does not reach Shrewsbury."},{"name":"Owen Glendower","group":"The rebels","desc":"The Welsh leader, proud of the portents at his birth and his power to 'call spirits from the vasty deep'. Educated at the English court, he is a poet and musician as well as a warrior. Hotspur mocks him to his face. He agrees to the three-way division of England but fails to arrive in time for Shrewsbury."},{"name":"Earl of Douglas","group":"The rebels","desc":"Archibald, the fierce Scottish lord beaten and captured by Hotspur at Holmedon and freed to become his ally. At Shrewsbury he kills Blunt and others dressed as the King and nearly kills Henry himself before Hal drives him off. Captured after the battle, he is freed without ransom for his valour."},{"name":"Sir Richard Vernon","group":"The rebels","desc":"A rebel lord who brings the news of the King's army and gives a glowing description of Hal in armour. He goes with Worcester to the parley and agrees to keep the King's offer secret. He is executed with Worcester."},{"name":"Archbishop of York","group":"The rebels","desc":"Richard Scroop, who joins the plot to avenge his brother's execution. He fears that Hotspur's army is too weak without Northumberland and Glendower and prepares his own defence."},{"name":"Sir John Falstaff","group":"Eastcheap","desc":"A fat, old, witty knight, Hal's companion in the tavern and on the highway. He drinks sherry, robs travellers, lies outrageously and talks his way out of every exposure. He plays the King in the tavern play and pleads 'banish plump Jack, and banish all the world'. Given a company of soldiers, he takes bribes from good men and leads beggars and ex-prisoners to their deaths. He dismisses honour as 'a word', fakes death to escape Douglas, and claims to have killed Hotspur."},{"name":"Poins","group":"Eastcheap","desc":"Ned Poins, Hal's closest friend in the gang. He plans the Gad's Hill joke: he and Hal will rob the robbers and expose Falstaff's lies. He helps Hal tease Francis and searches Falstaff's pockets."},{"name":"Bardolph","group":"Eastcheap","desc":"Falstaff's red-faced, red-nosed follower, the butt of endless jokes about his burning face. He takes part in the robbery and later serves as Falstaff's corporal."},{"name":"Peto and Gadshill","group":"Eastcheap","desc":"Members of the gang. Gadshill is the 'setter' who finds out which travellers are carrying money; Peto confesses to Hal how Falstaff hacked his own sword."},{"name":"Mistress Quickly","group":"Eastcheap","desc":"The Hostess of the Boar's Head tavern in Eastcheap. She is flustered, muddled and kind, laughs at the tavern play, and is owed money by Falstaff, who accuses her of picking his pocket."},{"name":"Francis","group":"Eastcheap","desc":"A young drawer (barman) at the tavern, whose only words seem to be 'Anon, anon, sir'. Hal and Poins tease him until he does not know which way to turn."}],"scenes":{"a1s1":{"sum":"In London King Henry, weary from civil war, plans a crusade to the Holy Land. Westmoreland brings bad news: Mortimer has been captured by the Welsh rebel Glendower and a thousand of his men slaughtered, and Hotspur has fought the Scots at Holmedon. Blunt brings news of Hotspur's great victory over Douglas. The King envies Northumberland his son and wishes his own Harry had been swapped in the cradle. He learns that Hotspur is keeping his prisoners, a stand Westmoreland blames on his uncle Worcester, postpones the crusade, summons Hotspur and calls a Council at Windsor.","key":["The crusade that keeps being postponed shows a king whose guilt and troubles keep him from the holy war he wants.","The King's wish that a fairy had swapped Hal and Hotspur in their cradles sets up the play's central comparison.","Hotspur's refusal to hand over his prisoners is the first crack between the King and the Percys."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does the King plan at the start of the play?","o":["A war against France","A crusade to the Holy Land","A marriage for his son","A visit to Wales"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"Who has captured Mortimer?","o":["Douglas","Hotspur","Glendower","The Archbishop of York"],"a":2,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does the King wish about Hal and Hotspur?","o":["That they would fight each other one day for the crown","That they had been swapped at birth","That they were friends","That they were both dead"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Why does the King postpone his crusade?","o":["Rebellion and unrest at home need him first","The Pope has forbidden any more wars in the East","His army has refused to sail without better pay","The French have closed the sea route to the Holy Land"],"a":0,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"What does the King's envy of Northumberland reveal?","o":["He fears Hal is unfit to be his heir","He secretly plans to make Hotspur his heir","He wants to take Northumberland's lands for himself","He blames Northumberland for Mortimer's capture"],"a":0,"b":4,"p":"inference"}]},"a1s2":{"sum":"In his lodgings Prince Hal jokes with Falstaff about time, the moon, thieves, hanging and the gallows. Poins arrives with a plan to rob pilgrims and traders at Gad's Hill the next morning. Hal at first refuses, but when Falstaff leaves, Poins explains the real joke: he and Hal will let the others do the robbery, then rob them in disguise and enjoy Falstaff's lies at supper. Alone, Hal reveals that he is only pretending: like the sun hidden by clouds, he will shine all the brighter when he throws off his wild ways.","key":["The witty, prose banter between Hal and Falstaff shows real affection but also Hal's coolness.","Poins's trick sets up the Gad's Hill robbery and the comic exposure in 2.4.","Hal's soliloquy 'I know you all' tells the audience his wildness is a calculated plan, which changes how we see every tavern scene."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Poins plan for Gad's Hill?","o":["To rob the King's treasury","To rob the robbers in disguise","To ambush Hotspur","To betray Falstaff to the sheriff"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"What does Hal compare himself to in his soliloquy?","o":["The moon among the stars","A lion in the field","The sun behind clouds","A storm"],"a":2,"b":1,"p":"concept"},{"q":"What is Falstaff's excuse for robbing purses?","o":["It is his vocation","He is poor","Hal ordered him to","The travellers are rebels"],"a":0,"b":2,"p":"question"},{"q":"What is the purpose of Hal's soliloquy?","o":["To reveal that he plans to rob his father's treasury","To confess that he truly loves Falstaff like a father","To show the audience his wildness is a plan","To show that he means to give up the crown to John"],"a":2,"b":4,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"What does Hal assume about how people will judge him?","o":["People will forget his wild days within a week","A reform looks brighter after a bad name","Nobody at court has noticed what he does at all","His father will always prefer him to his brothers"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"assumption"}]},"a1s3":{"sum":"The King warns the Percys that he will now be 'mighty and to be feared', and dismisses Worcester. Hotspur explains that he refused the prisoners to a perfumed courtier, but will hand them over if the King ransoms Mortimer. The King calls Mortimer a traitor and demands the prisoners. Left alone, Hotspur explodes with rage. Learning that Richard named Mortimer heir to the throne, he attacks his father and uncle for helping 'this canker Bolingbroke'. Worcester reveals a plot: release the Scots, ally with Douglas, the Archbishop of York, Mortimer and Glendower, and rise against the King.","key":["Hotspur's account of the 'popinjay' lord shows his contempt for courtly manners and his soldier's pride.","Hotspur's speech about plucking 'bright honour from the pale-faced moon' defines his idea of honour.","The rebellion is born from wounded pride and fear: Worcester knows the King will never feel he has repaid the Percys."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why did Hotspur refuse to hand over his prisoners?","o":["He wanted the ransom money for himself and his family in the north","He was angry at a perfumed lord who demanded them","Douglas begged him not to give them up to the King","The King had insulted his wife"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"Why does the King refuse to ransom Mortimer?","o":["He thinks Mortimer betrayed his men and fears his claim","Mortimer is too expensive","Glendower will not sell him","Mortimer is already dead"],"a":0,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"Who reveals the plan for rebellion?","o":["Hotspur","Northumberland","Worcester","Douglas"],"a":2,"b":4,"p":"inference"},{"q":"Why does the Mortimer question matter so much to the King?","o":["Mortimer has a claim to the crown","Mortimer owes the King a great deal of money","Mortimer once insulted the Queen in public","Mortimer is the King's own favourite nephew"],"a":0,"b":4,"p":"implication"},{"q":"How does Worcester use Hotspur's anger?","o":["He turns it into a plan for rebellion","He calms him down and sends him home to his wife","He reports it to the King to win back his favour","He tells him to apologise to the King at once"],"a":0,"b":4,"p":"information"}]},"a2s1":{"sum":"Before dawn at an inn in Rochester, two carriers complain about fleas, damp fodder and the dead ostler as they load their goods for London. Gadshill tries to borrow a lantern and is refused. The inn's chamberlain, Gadshill's informer, tells him a rich farmer is carrying three hundred marks. Gadshill boasts that his gang includes great men who will protect him.","key":["The carriers' grumbling gives a glimpse of ordinary English life, far from court and war.","The chamberlain is in league with the robbers, showing corruption reaching every level.","Gadshill's joke that his partners 'pray' to the commonwealth by preying on it links the robbery to the politics of the play."],"quiz":[{"q":"Where is this scene set?","o":["The Boar's Head in Eastcheap","An inn-yard in Rochester","Gad's Hill","Warkworth"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does the chamberlain tell Gadshill?","o":["That the sheriff is coming to the inn with his men to search it","That a rich farmer is carrying three hundred marks","That Hal will not come tonight","That the carriers are armed"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Why won't the carriers lend Gadshill a lantern?","o":["They don't trust him","They have none","They are asleep","The ostler has taken it"],"a":0,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"What does Gadshill's boast about 'great men' suggest?","o":["He plans to betray the Prince to the sheriff","He works secretly for the King's spies in the town","He expects the Prince's company to protect him","He has never met anyone of rank in his whole life"],"a":2,"b":4,"p":"inference"},{"q":"Why does Shakespeare open the act with the carriers?","o":["To show ordinary life the robbers will prey on","To introduce the rebels who will fight at Shrewsbury","To explain how the King pays for the war in Wales","To show that Hal is already hiding in the inn-yard"],"a":0,"b":4,"p":"purpose"}]},"a2s2":{"sum":"On the road at Gad's Hill, Poins has hidden Falstaff's horse, and Falstaff puffs and curses on foot. Hal and Poins slip away to put on their disguises. Falstaff, Gadshill, Bardolph and Peto rob the travellers. As they share out the money, Hal and Poins, in buckram, attack them. Falstaff strikes a blow or two and runs away with the rest, leaving the loot behind. Hal laughs that Falstaff 'lards the lean earth' as he runs.","key":["The comedy of fat Falstaff on foot sets up the bigger joke of his lies.","Falstaff calls the travellers 'fat chuffs' and claims 'young men must live', comic hypocrisy from an old fat man.","The robbery is a mirror of the rebellion: thieves robbing thieves, just as Henry took a crown that was not his."],"quiz":[{"q":"What has Poins done to Falstaff's horse?","o":["Sold it","Hidden it","Lamed it","Lent it to Hal"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What happens when Hal and Poins attack?","o":["Falstaff fights bravely and drives them off with his sword","Falstaff runs away, leaving the money","Falstaff recognises them at once by their voices","Bardolph captures Poins"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"implication"},{"q":"What are Hal and Poins disguised in?","o":["Monks' habits","Buckram suits","Soldiers' uniforms","Women's clothes"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Why do Hal and Poins rob the robbers?","o":["To keep the money for their own debts","To enjoy Falstaff's lies about it later","To return the money to the King's men","To catch Falstaff and send him to prison"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"What does Falstaff's complaint about walking show?","o":["He cares more for comfort than for the robbery","He is too honest to take part in the robbery","He is secretly afraid of the King's soldiers","He knows that Hal has planned to trick him"],"a":0,"b":4,"p":"inference"}]},"a2s3":{"sum":"At Warkworth Castle Hotspur reads a letter from a lord who refuses to join the rebellion because the plan is too dangerous. Hotspur rages at the 'frosty-spirited rogue' and resolves to set out that night. His wife Kate asks why he has been sleepless, talking of battles in his dreams, and demands to know his business. Hotspur teases her, refuses to tell her anything, and promises she will follow him the next day.","key":["'Out of this nettle, danger, we pluck this flower, safety': Hotspur's optimism about the plot.","Lady Percy's speech shows the cost of Hotspur's obsession: he has abandoned her bed for war.","Hotspur's playful refusal to trust his wife shows affection but also his belief that war is not for women."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does the letter-writer refuse to join the rebellion?","o":["He is ill","He thinks the plan is too dangerous","He supports Glendower","He has no soldiers"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"What has Lady Percy noticed?","o":["Hotspur talks of battles in his sleep","Hotspur is drinking heavily every night with his men","Hotspur has bought a new horse","Hotspur has written to the King"],"a":0,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Why won't Hotspur tell Kate his plans?","o":["He thinks she will tell the King everything, as her brother serves him","He teases that a woman cannot reveal what she does not know","He does not know them himself yet","Worcester has forbidden it"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"What does the letter-writer's refusal show about the rebellion?","o":["All the lords of England have joined it","The King has already learned all of it","Hotspur has called it off for the winter","Some allies already doubt the plan"],"a":3,"b":4,"p":"inference"},{"q":"What does the scene suggest about Hotspur's marriage?","o":["It is affectionate but he keeps her out of his plans","It is cold, and he plans to leave her for good","It is unhappy, and she is plotting against him","It is new, and he has hardly met her before now"],"a":0,"b":4,"p":"inference"}]},"a2s4":{"sum":"In the Boar's Head tavern Hal boasts of befriending the drawers, then teases Francis with Poins until the boy is confused. Falstaff arrives cursing cowards and tells how he fought off two, then four, seven, nine, eleven men in buckram and three in Kendal green. Hal exposes him, but Falstaff claims he knew Hal all along and would not harm the true prince 'on instinct'. News comes of the rebellion, and Hal must go to court. They rehearse: Falstaff plays the King and praises Falstaff, then Hal plays the King and condemns 'that old white-bearded Satan'. Falstaff begs, 'banish plump Jack, and banish all the world', and Hal replies, 'I do, I will.' The sheriff arrives; Hal hides Falstaff, sends the sheriff away, and promises to repay the money.","key":["Falstaff's multiplying buckram men are the play's great comic set piece, and his escape shows his genius for survival.","The play-within-the-play rehearses Hal's interview with his father and foreshadows his rejection of Falstaff.","'I do, I will' is light in the tavern but sounds like a promise about the future."],"quiz":[{"q":"How many men in buckram does Falstaff finally claim?","o":["Two","Four","Nine","Eleven"],"a":3,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What is Falstaff's excuse when exposed?","o":["He was drunk and could not see who they were in the dark","He knew it was Hal and would not hurt the true prince","His sword broke against a tree","Bardolph ran first"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"question"},{"q":"How does Hal answer Falstaff's 'banish plump Jack'?","o":["'Never'","'I do, I will'","'Thou shalt be my judge'","'Not yet'"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does the growing number of buckram men reveal?","o":["Falstaff really fought a large band of thieves","Falstaff is drunk and cannot count his enemies","Falstaff is trying to protect Bardolph and Peto","Falstaff is inventing the story as he goes"],"a":3,"b":4,"p":"inference"},{"q":"Why does 'I do, I will' matter for the whole play?","o":["It shows Hal knows he must reject Falstaff","It shows Hal plans to banish his own father","It shows Hal will banish the tavern's Hostess","It shows Hal never meant the play-acting at all"],"a":0,"b":4,"p":"implication"}]},"a3s1":{"sum":"At Bangor the rebel leaders meet to divide England. Glendower boasts that the earth shook at his birth and that he can call spirits; Hotspur mocks him. They divide the kingdom on a map: Mortimer the south and east, Glendower Wales and the west, Hotspur the north. Hotspur demands that the river Trent be straightened, quarrels with Glendower, then gives in. Mortimer and Worcester warn Hotspur about his temper. The wives arrive: Lady Mortimer speaks only Welsh and sings to her husband; Hotspur mocks the Welsh song, teases Kate for her mild oaths and asks her to sing; she refuses, and he leaves.","key":["The rebels carve up England before winning it, which shows their weakness and division.","Hotspur's 'tell truth and shame the devil' mockery of Glendower reveals both his honesty and his lack of tact.","The tender Welsh song and the lovers who cannot share a language give the rebellion a private, human cost."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Glendower claim happened at his birth?","o":["The earth shook and the sky was on fire","A great comet appeared over the hills of Wales for a week","His mother died the night he was born","The river Severn flooded the whole valley"],"a":0,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Hotspur want changed on the map?","o":["The border of Scotland","The course of the river Trent","The ownership of London","The Welsh coast"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"What can Mortimer not do?","o":["Ride a horse","Speak Welsh","Read a map","Sing"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does the quarrel over the map show about the rebels?","o":["They are united and sure of quick victory","They have no interest in dividing England","They all trust Glendower's magic completely","Their alliance is fragile and quarrelsome"],"a":3,"b":4,"p":"question"},{"q":"How does Hotspur treat Glendower's claims?","o":["He mocks them as boasting","He believes every word of them","He is frightened into silence","He asks Glendower to teach him"],"a":0,"b":2,"p":"view"}]},"a3s2":{"sum":"Alone with Hal, the King wonders whether God is punishing him through his son. He explains how he won the crown by being seen rarely, like a comet, while Richard cheapened himself by mixing with the crowd, and says Hal is just like Richard and Hotspur just like the young Bolingbroke. He even fears Hal will fight for Percy. Hal swears to redeem himself 'on Percy's head' and make Hotspur exchange his glorious deeds for Hal's shame. He reminds Hal that his younger brother John has taken his place in the Council. The King gives him a command. Blunt reports that the rebels are gathering at Shrewsbury, and the King sets out the march.","key":["The King's speech about being 'seldom seen' explains his political method and his fear of Hal's 'vile participation'.","The King's comparison of Hal to Richard and Hotspur to himself sets up the play's mirrors.","Hal's vow treats honour as a ledger: Percy is 'but my factor', collecting glory that Hal will claim."],"quiz":[{"q":"How did Henry win popularity before he was king?","o":["By giving money to the poor in the streets of London","By being seen rarely, like a comet","By fighting in France","By marrying well"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"information"},{"q":"Who does the King say Hal resembles?","o":["Hotspur, the son of Northumberland","King Richard","Owen Glendower","Sir John Falstaff"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Hal promise?","o":["To leave England","To redeem himself on Percy's head","To give up the crown","To execute Falstaff"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"implication"},{"q":"What does the King's comet image say about power?","o":["A king should be seen among the people every day","A ruler must always win his crown in a battle","A king's power comes from the stars and nothing else","Being seen rarely makes a ruler seem wonderful"],"a":3,"b":2,"p":"concept"},{"q":"How does the King try to stir Hal?","o":["By praising Hotspur and comparing Hal to Richard","By threatening to disinherit him in favour of John","By sending him away to Ireland for a whole year","By promising him the crown if he wins at Shrewsbury"],"a":0,"b":2,"p":"purpose"}]},"a3s3":{"sum":"In the tavern Falstaff complains he is wasting away and jokes about Bardolph's fiery face. He accuses the Hostess of picking his pocket, claiming he lost bonds and his grandfather's seal-ring; she says he owes her for shirts and twenty-four pounds for food, drink and loans, and that Hal has often called the ring copper. Hal arrives and reveals that the 'stolen' pocket contained only tavern bills and a pennyworth of sugar candy. Falstaff forgives the Hostess. Hal tells him the money has been paid back and that he has got Falstaff a command of foot soldiers. Hal sends out letters and leaves for the war.","key":["Falstaff's claim to have lost bonds and a valuable ring is another exposed lie.","Falstaff admits that with 'more flesh than another man' he has 'more frailty': comic self-knowledge.","Hal is now all business: 'The land is burning; Percy stands on high.'"],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Falstaff claim he lost?","o":["His horse and his saddle at the inn","Bonds and his grandfather's seal-ring","His sword and dagger","A letter from the King to his son"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What was really in Falstaff's pocket?","o":["Gold coins and a ring from the King","Tavern bills and sugar candy","Love letters from the Hostess","Nothing at all"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What has Hal got for Falstaff?","o":["A pardon","A command of foot soldiers","A knighthood","A seat on the Council"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does the quarrel with the Hostess show about Falstaff?","o":["He is honest about his debts and pays them","He lies to avoid paying what he owes","He has lost a fortune to a skilled thief","He is too ill to remember what he spends"],"a":1,"b":4,"p":"question"},{"q":"What does Hal's change of tone suggest?","o":["He is tired of Falstaff and goes back to bed","He is turning from tavern jokes to the war","He plans another robbery on the road to York","He wants to stay in the tavern for good"],"a":1,"b":4,"p":"inference"}]},"a4s1":{"sum":"In the rebel camp near Shrewsbury, Hotspur and Douglas praise each other. A messenger brings letters: Northumberland is ill and will not come. Hotspur tries to see this as an advantage, but Worcester fears it will look like disapproval. Vernon reports that the King and Prince John are coming with a large army, and gives a dazzling description of Hal in armour vaulting onto his horse. Hotspur rejects the praise and longs to fight Hal 'hot horse to horse'. Vernon adds that Glendower cannot arrive for fourteen days.","key":["The rebellion begins to fall apart before the battle as Northumberland and Glendower fail to come.","Vernon's description of Hal 'like feather'd Mercury' shows the prince transformed.","Hotspur's 'Doomsday is near; die all, die merrily' shows reckless courage against the odds."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why doesn't Northumberland come?","o":["He has changed sides","He is ill","He is defending the North","He is in Wales"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"How does Vernon describe Hal?","o":["Drunk and unprepared, still in his tavern clothes","Splendid, vaulting onto his horse like an angel","Afraid of battle and pale","Wounded"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"How long before Glendower can arrive?","o":["Two days at most","A week","Fourteen days","A month"],"a":2,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"How does Hotspur respond to Northumberland's absence?","o":["He tries to turn it into an advantage","He decides to call off the battle at once","He blames Worcester and threatens to leave","He sends Douglas to fetch his father home"],"a":0,"b":4,"p":"view"},{"q":"Why does Vernon's praise of Hal unsettle Hotspur?","o":["It challenges his view of Hal as a wastrel","It proves that the King's army is too small","It shows that Vernon is a spy for the King","It means that Hal will not come to the fight"],"a":0,"b":2,"p":"purpose"}]},"a4s2":{"sum":"On the road near Coventry Falstaff confesses that he has abused the King's power to conscript soldiers: he took money from men who could afford to buy themselves out and filled their places with beggars, ex-prisoners and scarecrows. Hal and Westmoreland meet him and are shocked at his men. Falstaff replies that they are 'food for powder'. They hurry on to Shrewsbury.","key":["Falstaff's corruption has real victims: his soldiers are poor men sent to die.","'Food for powder' is comic and chilling, an honest view of what war does to common soldiers.","Falstaff's plan to arrive at 'the latter end of a fray' shows his priorities."],"quiz":[{"q":"How did Falstaff make money from recruiting?","o":["He sold the soldiers' weapons","He took bribes from men who bought their way out","He charged soldiers for food","He robbed the King's treasury"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Falstaff call his soldiers?","o":["Brave heroes of England","Food for powder","Lions","Gentlemen"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"concept"},{"q":"Where is Falstaff going?","o":["Wales, to fight Glendower","Shrewsbury","York, to join John","London, to the King"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Falstaff's recruiting reveal about war?","o":["The poor die while the rich buy their way out","Every man fights for honour and the King","The army is made up of trained knights","Soldiers are always well paid for their work"],"a":0,"b":4,"p":"inference"},{"q":"How does Falstaff justify his ragged men?","o":["They are good enough to be killed","They are the bravest men in England","They will grow strong on the march","They were the only men in Coventry"],"a":0,"b":2,"p":"question"}]},"a4s3":{"sum":"In the rebel camp Hotspur and Douglas want to fight that night; Worcester and Vernon advise waiting for reinforcements. Blunt arrives with an offer: if they state their grievances, the King will satisfy them and pardon them. Hotspur gives a long account of how the Percys helped Bolingbroke land at Ravenspurgh on his promise to claim only his dukedom, and how he then took the crown, killed Richard and turned against them. He promises that Worcester will bring an answer in the morning.","key":["Hotspur's history of Bolingbroke's rise is the rebels' case: Henry broke his oath and took the crown.","The King's offer seems generous, which makes the battle feel avoidable.","The rebels argue among themselves about timing, a sign of weak leadership."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Blunt offer the rebels?","o":["Money","Pardon and satisfaction of their grievances","Mortimer's release","A share of the kingdom"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Where did Bolingbroke land on his return?","o":["Milford Haven","Ravenspurgh","Dover","Bristol"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Who will take the rebels' answer to the King?","o":["Hotspur","Worcester","Douglas","Sir Richard Vernon"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What point of view does Hotspur's history of Bolingbroke give?","o":["The Percys see Henry as an oath-breaker","The Percys see Henry as a rightful king","The Percys think Richard deserved to die","The Percys believe the King loves them still"],"a":0,"b":4,"p":"view"},{"q":"Why do Worcester and Vernon want to wait?","o":["Their forces are not yet all there","They hope the King will die soon","They fear the coming of winter","They want to talk to Glendower"],"a":0,"b":2,"p":"purpose"}]},"a4s4":{"sum":"In York the Archbishop sends Sir Michael with urgent letters to his allies. He fears that without Northumberland and Glendower, Hotspur's army is too weak to beat the King, who has gathered the best of the land. If Percy loses, the King will turn on York next, so the Archbishop prepares his defence.","key":["The short scene shows the wider conspiracy and prepares for its continuation in Part 2.","The Archbishop's fears tell the audience the odds before the battle.","The King's 'corrivals' (Hal, John, Westmoreland, Blunt) are listed as the best of England."],"quiz":[{"q":"Where is this scene set?","o":["London","York","Shrewsbury","Wales"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does the Archbishop fear?","o":["That Hal will die","That Hotspur's army is too weak","That the King will not fight","That Douglas will betray them"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Who carries his letters?","o":["Vernon","Sir Michael","Blunt","Poins"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does the Archbishop's scene add to the story?","o":["It shows Glendower's army already on the march","It shows the King has forgiven all of the rebels","It shows Hal travelling north to rescue his father","It shows the rebellion's danger beyond Shrewsbury"],"a":3,"b":4,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does the Archbishop expect if Hotspur loses?","o":["The King will pardon him at once","The rebellion will end for ever","The King will turn on him next","Glendower will march to York"],"a":2,"b":1,"p":"assumption"}]},"a5s1":{"sum":"At dawn in the King's camp, Worcester and Vernon come to parley. The King reproaches Worcester for disturbing the peace. Worcester recalls how the Percys helped Henry at Doncaster and how he then treated them like a cuckoo treats the sparrow. The King dismisses these 'water-colours'. Hal praises Hotspur and offers to fight him in single combat to save lives. The King offers pardon to all if they submit. Alone with Hal, Falstaff wishes it were bedtime, and in a soliloquy he decides that honour is only 'a word' and 'air'.","key":["Hal's generous praise of Hotspur and his offer of single combat show his new princely nature.","Worcester's cuckoo image explains the rebels' grievance.","Falstaff's catechism on honour challenges the values that drive Hotspur and Hal."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Hal offer?","o":["To surrender","To fight Hotspur in single combat","To pay the rebels","To go to Wales"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What bird does Worcester compare Henry to?","o":["An eagle","A cuckoo","A raven","A wren"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"concept"},{"q":"What does Falstaff conclude honour is?","o":["Worth dying for","A word, air","The King's gift","Everything"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Why does Hal offer to fight Hotspur alone?","o":["To win the crown from his father","To save lives on both sides","To prove Falstaff is a coward","To delay the battle until Wales"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"What is Falstaff questioning in his catechism on honour?","o":["Whether the King deserves the crown","Whether Hal will ever be a good king","Whether the rebels have a fair cause","Whether honour is worth dying for"],"a":3,"b":1,"p":"information"}]},"a5s2":{"sum":"Worcester decides not to tell Hotspur about the King's offer: the King will never trust the Percys, and Hotspur's youth would excuse him while the older men would pay. Vernon agrees. Worcester tells Hotspur the King will fight at once. Douglas sends back a defiance. Worcester mentions Hal's challenge, and Vernon describes how modestly and nobly Hal made it. Hotspur scorns Hal, makes a short speech, and the rebels embrace and go to battle.","key":["Worcester's lie makes the battle happen: the rebellion's leader betrays his own side to protect himself.","Vernon's account of Hal's 'blushing cital of himself' completes Hal's transformation in others' eyes.","Hotspur's 'the time of life is short' is his credo: a short life spent bravely."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Worcester hide from Hotspur?","o":["Northumberland's illness and his absence","The King's offer of pardon","Glendower's letter from Wales","Douglas's capture"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Why does Worcester hide it?","o":["He thinks the King will never trust them","He wants Hotspur dead","He forgot","Vernon told him to"],"a":0,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"What is the rebels' battle cry?","o":["Saint George!","Esperance! Percy!","For Wales!","Douglas!"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Worcester assume about the King's mercy?","o":["It would cover every rebel for life","It would be given only to Hotspur","It would not last for the older men","It would cost them all of their lands"],"a":2,"b":1,"p":"assumption"},{"q":"How does Worcester's silence change the outcome?","o":["It makes the King call off the fight","It lets Hotspur escape to Scotland","It makes Douglas leave the rebels","It makes the battle certain"],"a":3,"b":4,"p":"implication"}]},"a5s3":{"sum":"In the battle Douglas kills Sir Walter Blunt, who is dressed as the King, and is furious to learn Blunt was a decoy; the King has 'many marching in his coats'. Falstaff finds Blunt's body: 'there's honour for you!' He has led his soldiers to their deaths: only three of his hundred and fifty are left alive. Hal asks for his sword, and Falstaff offers his pistol case, which turns out to hold a bottle of sherry. Hal throws it at him and leaves. Falstaff prefers life to 'grinning honour'.","key":["The King's decoys show his cautious, politic way of fighting, and the cost to men like Blunt.","Blunt's 'grinning' corpse is Falstaff's proof that honour means death.","The sherry bottle in the pistol case is Falstaff's joke at the worst moment, and Hal has no patience left."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does Douglas kill Blunt?","o":["Blunt insulted him and his country before the battle","Blunt is dressed as the King","Blunt killed Hotspur","Blunt is guarding Hal"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"What is in Falstaff's pistol case?","o":["A pistol","A bottle of sherry","A letter","Nothing"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"question"},{"q":"How many of Falstaff's soldiers are left alive?","o":["None at all","Fewer than three","About half of them","All of them, unhurt"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Why does the King have many men in his coats?","o":["To show off the wealth of his army","To honour the knights who fought for him","To hide the fact that he is not there","To confuse enemies who hunt for him"],"a":3,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"What does 'there's honour for you!' mean over Blunt?","o":["Honour is the best reward","Blunt died a coward's death","Blunt will soon be made a lord","Honour brings only death"],"a":3,"b":2,"p":"concept"}]},"a5s4":{"sum":"The wounded Hal refuses to leave the field and praises his brother John's courage. Douglas attacks the King and nearly kills him; Hal drives Douglas off and his father admits he has 'redeem'd' his reputation. Hal and Hotspur meet: 'two stars keep not their motion in one sphere'. As they fight, Douglas attacks Falstaff, who falls as if dead. Hal kills Hotspur, finishes his last words ('food for worms'), covers his face and makes a eulogy. He sees Falstaff and mourns him. Falstaff gets up, stabs Hotspur's body in the thigh, and carries it off. He tells Hal and John that he killed Percy. Hal lets the lie stand.","key":["Hal saves his father's life, answering the fear that he wished him dead.","The duel between the two Harrys is the climax: Hotspur's death completes Hal's vow.","Falstaff's fake death and fake claim parody the heroic climax, and Hal's tolerance of the lie shows he no longer needs the glory."],"quiz":[{"q":"Whom does Hal rescue from Douglas?","o":["Falstaff","The King","Prince John","Blunt"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"How does Falstaff survive Douglas's attack?","o":["He runs away","He pretends to be dead","He surrenders","He kills Douglas"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Falstaff claim?","o":["That he saved the King","That he killed Hotspur","That Hal is dead","That Douglas surrendered"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"How does Hal's eulogy for Hotspur show his growth?","o":["He mocks Hotspur's body before the army","He honours his enemy with generosity","He claims that he never wanted to fight","He blames his father for Hotspur's death"],"a":1,"b":4,"p":"inference"},{"q":"Why does Hal let Falstaff's lie stand?","o":["He doesn't need public credit","He believes the lie to be true","He is too wounded to argue","He wants Falstaff knighted"],"a":0,"b":2,"p":"purpose"}]},"a5s5":{"sum":"The King has won. He sentences Worcester and Vernon to death for not passing on his offer. Hal reports that Douglas has been captured and asks to decide his fate; he gives John the honour of freeing him without ransom for his valour. The King divides his army: John and Westmoreland will march against Northumberland and the Archbishop at York, while the King and Hal go to Wales against Glendower and Mortimer.","key":["Worcester's execution punishes the lie that caused the battle.","Hal's generosity to Douglas shows royal magnanimity, and he gives the honour to John.","The ending is open: rebellion continues, setting up Henry IV, Part 2."],"quiz":[{"q":"What happens to Worcester?","o":["He is pardoned","He is executed","He escapes","He joins the King"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What happens to Douglas?","o":["He is executed at once with Worcester and Vernon","He is set free without ransom","He is imprisoned in the Tower","He becomes the King's ally"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Where will the King and Hal go next?","o":["York","Wales","France","Scotland"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Why does Hal free Douglas without ransom?","o":["To win the Scots over to the King","Because Douglas paid him in secret","Because John demanded it of him","To honour valour, even in an enemy"],"a":3,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"Why does the play end without full peace?","o":["Rebels in the north and Wales are still in arms","The King plans to invade France the next week","Hal and the King quarrel again over Falstaff","Hotspur's ghost is said to haunt the battlefield"],"a":0,"b":2,"p":"purpose"}]}},"notes":{"2":"Henry deposed Richard II in 1399 (the story of Richard II). The crusade to Jerusalem is partly penance for Richard's murder, which he ordered in that play. 'Civil butchery' is the war that put him on the throne.","3":"Mortimer led the King's men against Glendower and was captured. The historical Mortimer who was captured (Sir Edmund) was a different man from the Earl of March whom Richard named heir; Shakespeare, following Holinshed, merges them.","5":"Holy-rood day is 14 September, the feast of the Holy Cross. The battle of Holmedon (Humbleton Hill) took place in 1402.","8":"The King's wish that a fairy had swapped the babies plays on the folklore of changelings. 'Plantagenet' is the royal family name.","16":"Hal's first lines are prose and full of jokes about drink and food: the tavern world speaks prose, the court speaks verse.","17":"Falstaff puns on 'grace': the title 'your Grace', God's grace, and the grace said before a meal.","21":"Diana is the goddess of the moon and of hunting; thieves who work at night call themselves her foresters and 'minions of the moon'.","22":"'Lay by' was a highwayman's cry (hand over), 'Bring in' a drinker's call for more wine. The 'ridge of the gallows' is the crossbar where thieves were hanged.","24":"'My old lad of the castle' may be a joke on Sir John Oldcastle, the name Falstaff originally had; his descendants objected and the name was changed.","37":"The hangman kept the clothes of those he hanged, so 'suits' means both petitions at court and clothes.","42":"Hal quotes the Bible (Proverbs 1:20): 'Wisdom crieth without; she uttereth her voice in the streets.' Falstaff complains of his 'damnable iteration' of scripture.","47":"Falstaff twists the Puritan idea that every man should labour in his calling, a joke aimed at religious language.","55":"Gad's Hill, on the road between Rochester and London, was notorious for highway robbery. Pilgrims went to Canterbury to the shrine of Thomas Becket.","68":"'All-hallown summer' is a spell of warm weather around All Saints' Day (1 November): a summer that comes late, like an old man acting young.","80":"Hal's first soliloquy is the key to his character. He tells the audience that his wildness is a plan: he will let the 'base contagious clouds' hide him so that his reformation will look more glorious. The final couplet rhymes 'skill' and 'will'.","84":"The King threatens to be 'myself', the mighty king, rather than his naturally mild 'condition'. The scene opens in the middle of a quarrel.","92":"Hotspur's 'popinjay' speech mocks a perfumed courtier who disliked the smell of corpses and thought guns vile. It is a defence through comedy and shows Hotspur's contempt for court manners.","94":"Mortimer married Glendower's daughter. The King's reason for calling him a traitor is partly political: Mortimer has a claim to the throne.","95":"The Severn 'hid his crisp head' in fear: Hotspur turns the river into a frightened person, a heroic picture of Mortimer's single combat with Glendower.","105":"Richard II named Roger Mortimer, Earl of March, as heir presumptive in 1385. If Richard was rightful king, the Mortimer line had a better claim than Bolingbroke.","110":"Hotspur reminds his father and uncle that they helped depose Richard, 'that sweet lovely rose', and replaced him with 'this thorn, this canker, Bolingbroke'. A canker is a wild rose and a grub that eats flowers.","114":"Hotspur imagines honour as a drowned woman to be pulled from the sea, or a prize on the moon. He wants it without a 'corrival', a rival who shares it.","125":"Ravenspurgh, in Yorkshire, is where Bolingbroke landed in 1399 when he returned from exile. Berkeley Castle in Gloucestershire is where the Percys met him.","132":"Lord Scroop of Wiltshire was executed by Bolingbroke at Bristol in 1399. Shakespeare makes him the Archbishop's brother.","146":"Charles's Wain is the constellation of the Plough. The carriers' prose, full of complaint and detail, is Shakespeare's picture of everyday travellers.","171":"Saint Nicholas was the patron saint of travellers and also, jokingly, of thieves, who were called 'Saint Nicholas' clerks'.","173":"Gadshill claims his gang are protected by 'great oneyers' (great ones). He puns on 'pray' and 'prey': the great prey on the commonwealth.","175":"Fern-seed was believed to make the carrier invisible, because the seed itself is almost impossible to see.","198":"'Peach' means to turn informer. Ballads about criminals were sold in the streets.","209":"Buckram is a coarse, stiff cloth. Hal and Poins wear buckram suits over their clothes as a disguise.","228":"Falstaff calls the travellers 'gorbellied' (fat-bellied) and 'bacons' while being far fatter himself. 'Young men must live' is a joke from a man in his sixties.","240":"'Lards the lean earth': Falstaff's sweat drips like fat on the ground. Hal's sympathy is mixed with mockery.","245":"Hotspur reads a letter from an unnamed lord who refuses to join. His answers to each sentence are a comic dialogue with an absent man.","248":"Lady Percy's speech describes the symptoms of a man obsessed with war. Her list of military terms (basilisks, culverin, palisadoes) is the language Hotspur speaks even in his sleep.","257":"'Esperance' (hope) was the Percy family motto: 'Esperance ma comforte'.","268":"Hotspur trusts Kate 'so far' as a woman can be trusted: he will not tell her what he does not want repeated. His teasing is affectionate but excludes her.","278":"Hal has learnt the drawers' slang, which shows his ability to speak every kind of language, a skill a king needs. Some see it as condescending.","324":"Hal's parody of Hotspur ('Fie upon this quiet life! I want work') shows he knows exactly who his rival is.","345":"Falstaff's sword is 'hack'd like a hand-saw', and he says 'ecce signum', Latin for 'behold the sign', words used in the Mass.","357":"The number of buckram men grows from two to four, seven, nine and eleven. The audience knows there were only two.","380":"The strappado was a torture in which a victim was hoisted by the arms tied behind the back and dropped.","387":"Falstaff's escape, 'I knew ye as well as he that made ye', is so outrageous that it wins the argument by wit. The lion was believed not to attack a true prince.","431":"King Cambyses was the hero of an old-fashioned tragedy, mocked for its bombastic style. Falstaff plays the King like a ranting stage tyrant.","439":"Falstaff parodies the euphuistic style of John Lyly, full of balanced phrases and similes from natural history (camomile, pitch).","450":"Hal's insults (tun of man, bolting-hutch, bombard of sack, Manningtree ox) all describe Falstaff's body as a container of food and drink. 'Vice' and 'Iniquity' were characters in morality plays.","455":"Falstaff's plea for himself is comic and moving. Hal's reply, 'I do, I will', is the most famous line in the play: it looks ahead to Falstaff's rejection at the end of Part 2.","456":"Hal's reply is the turning point of the tavern play; the knocking that follows interrupts it at its most serious moment.","494":"The bill lists a halfpenny's worth of bread against two gallons of sherry. 'Ob.' is short for obolus, a halfpenny.","503":"Glendower's portents at his birth were reported in Holinshed's Chronicles. To Hotspur they are superstition.","512":"'I can call spirits from the vasty deep.' Hotspur's reply, 'But will they come when you do call for them?', is one of the play's best-known exchanges.","520":"The Archdeacon's map divides England into three: a 'tripartite' indenture. The rebels plan to carve up the kingdom before winning it.","540":"Merlin's prophecies, and the mole (moldwarp), dragon and griffin, belong to Welsh legend. Glendower read them as predictions of Henry's fall.","563":"Lady Mortimer's song is in Welsh; the original text does not give the words. Actors often sing a real Welsh song.","575":"Hotspur mocks Kate's mild oaths ('in good sooth') as the swearing of tradesmen's wives in Finsbury, where London citizens went on Sundays.","588":"The King's speech compares his own rare appearances, like a comet or a 'robe pontifical', with Richard's: he was 'the skipping King' who 'enfeoff'd himself to popularity'. The point is political: majesty depends on scarcity.","591":"Hal turns honour into commerce: Hotspur is his 'factor' (agent) who will be made to hand over every glory. The language of accounts and debts runs through Hal's speeches.","595":"'Lord Mortimer of Scotland' is George Dunbar, the Scottish Earl of March, who was on the King's side; he is not Edmund Mortimer, the rebel.","606":"Falstaff calls Bardolph's red face a 'memento mori' (a reminder of death) and compares it to the rich man Dives burning in hell.","620":"Falstaff claims he has lost bonds of forty pounds each and his grandfather's seal-ring. The Hostess says the Prince has called the ring copper.","659":"Falstaff's comic theology: if Adam fell when innocent, what can poor Jack do in the age of villainy? He has more flesh, so more frailty.","710":"Vernon describes Hal as Mercury, the winged messenger god, and his horse as Pegasus. The rebels' own messenger makes Hal a hero.","729":"The 'King's press' was the power to conscript soldiers. Captains could release men for money, and Falstaff takes bribes from those who can pay and fills their places with the poor.","738":"'Food for powder' (gunpowder): Falstaff's soldiers will die and fill a pit as well as better men would.","772":"Hotspur's speech retells the events of Richard II: Bolingbroke's landing at Ravenspurgh, his oath to claim only his dukedom, and the execution of Richard's favourites Bushy and Green.","804":"Worcester's cuckoo image: the cuckoo lays its egg in a sparrow's nest, and the chick grows so large it pushes the sparrow out.","817":"Falstaff's 'catechism' (a question-and-answer lesson in religion) on honour. A 'scutcheon' is a painted coat of arms hung at a funeral: honour is only a decoration for the dead.","823":"Worcester's reason for hiding the King's offer: the King will never trust them, and 'treason is but trusted like the fox'.","843":"Hotspur's short speech: life is short, and even if it lasted only an hour, it would be too long to spend basely.","853":"Historically several knights wore the King's coat of arms at Shrewsbury (1403) to protect him. Douglas is said to have killed several of them.","867":"Falstaff's soldiers were a hundred and fifty; fewer than three survive. 'There's honour for you!' looks at Blunt's corpse.","918":"Hotspur's last words are cut off: 'food for—'. Hal finishes the sentence, 'For worms', taking even his last line from him.","920":"Hal covers Hotspur's face with his 'favours' (plumes or scarves) and gives him an epitaph, honouring an enemy.","924":"'The better part of valour is discretion' is Falstaff's defence of faking death. He then stabs the dead Hotspur in the thigh.","934":"Hal agrees to support Falstaff's lie: 'if a lie may do thee grace, I'll gild it'. He no longer needs the credit.","949":"Hal gives the freedom of Douglas to John: generosity to a brave enemy, and to his brother.","950":"The play ends with the rebellion only half defeated. Northumberland, the Archbishop and Glendower remain, and their story continues in Henry IV, Part 2."},"gloss":{"wan":"pale","broils":"conflicts, fighting","strands":"shores","trenching":"cutting trenches into","intestine":"internal, civil","sepulchre":"tomb (here, Christ's tomb in Jerusalem)","bootless":"without success","expedience":"expedition; speed","athwart":"crosswise, unexpectedly","post":"messenger","Holy-rood":"Holy Cross","discomfited":"defeated","minion":"favourite, darling","coz":"cousin, kinsman","sack":"sweet white wine, like sherry","capons":"castrated cockerels, fattened for eating","wench":"girl, young woman","taffeta":"glossy silk","wag":"joker","Phoebus":"the sun god","durance":"long-lasting cloth; imprisonment","quiddities":"quibbles, clever arguments","reckoning":"bill","antic":"clown, buffoon","gib-cat":"tomcat","lugg'd":"led on a chain, baited","iteration":"repetition (of scripture)","visards":"masks","bespoke":"ordered","chops":"fat cheeks","madcap":"wild person","countenance":"support, approval; face","buckram":"coarse stiff linen","immask":"hide, mask","wards":"defensive positions in fencing","unyok'd":"unrestrained","foil":"a background that shows off a jewel","condition":"natural temperament","portly":"stately, imposing","peremptory":"overbearing","frontier":"defiance, hostile front","misprision":"misunderstanding","milliner":"seller of fancy goods","pouncet-box":"small perforated box for perfume","popinjay":"parrot; vain chatterer","parmaceti":"spermaceti, whale oil used as ointment","salt-petre":"gunpowder ingredient","indent":"make a bargain","sedgy":"reedy","hardiment":"bold deeds","crisp":"curly, rippling","belie":"tell lies about","choler":"anger","canker":"wild rose; grub that eats buds","subornation":"aiding a crime","gage":"pledge","corrival":"rival, partner","starling":"bird that can be taught to talk","politician":"schemer","candy":"sugary","cozeners":"cheats","prelate":"bishop or archbishop","ostler":"stable-boy at an inn","jade":"worn-out horse","withers":"ridge between a horse's shoulders","bots":"worms in horses","tench":"a spotted fish","pannier":"basket carried by a horse","franklin":"rich farmer, landowner","marks":"coins worth two-thirds of a pound","Trojans":"good fellows, roisterers","malt-worms":"drunkards","oneyers":"ones (great ones)","fern-seed":"seed believed to make the carrier invisible","setter":"robbers' scout","Zwounds":"by God's wounds (an oath)","Zounds":"by God's wounds (an oath)","'Sblood":"by God's blood (an oath)","colt":"trick, fool","peach":"turn informer","caterpillars":"parasites","gorbellied":"pot-bellied","chuffs":"misers","Esperance":"hope (the Percy motto)","paraquito":"little parrot","mammets":"dolls","loggerheads":"blockheads","hogsheads":"large barrels","drawers":"barmen who draw wine","corinthian":"lively fellow","under-skinker":"junior barman","bastard":"sweet Spanish wine","Anon":"coming, straight away","indenture":"contract of apprenticeship","brawn":"fattened pig","nether-stocks":"stockings","Titan":"the sun","shotten":"spawned out, thin","lath":"thin wood","ecce signum":"behold the proof (Latin)","target":"shield","Kendal green":"coarse green cloth worn by woodmen","tallow-keech":"lump of fat","strappado":"a torture","neat's-tongue":"ox tongue","stock-fish":"dried cod","tuck":"rapier","extempore":"improvised","arras":"tapestry hanging","bastinado":"beating with a stick","blue-caps":"Scots (from their bonnets)","joint-stool":"wooden stool","tickle-brain":"strong drink","micher":"truant","bolting-hutch":"bin for sifting flour","bombard":"large leather jug","Vice":"comic villain in morality plays","kine":"cows","major":"main premise of an argument","hue-and-cry":"public pursuit of a criminal","ob.":"halfpenny","induction":"beginning","nativity":"birth","cressets":"fire baskets, torches","beldam":"old woman, grandmother","vasty":"vast","indentures":"contracts","tripartite":"in three parts","moiety":"share","cantle":"slice, piece","canstick":"candlestick","cavil":"argue over trifles","moldwarp":"mole","cates":"delicacies","wilful-blunt":"stubbornly rude","harlotry":"silly hussy","division":"melodic variation","brach":"hound bitch","comfit-maker":"sweet-maker","sarcenet":"flimsy silk","pick-thanks":"flatterers","carded":"mixed, debased","bavin":"brushwood","enfeoff'd":"gave himself up","factor":"agent","engross":"buy up","apple-John":"apple eaten when shrivelled","memento mori":"reminder of death","ignis fatuus":"will-o'-the-wisp","links":"torches","chandler":"candle-maker","salamander":"lizard believed to live in fire","Dowlas":"coarse linen","bolters":"flour sieves","holland":"fine linen","ell":"measure of cloth (45 inches)","denier":"tiny coin","younker":"greenhorn, dupe","drawn fox":"fox driven from its hole","Maid Marian":"a disreputable figure of the May games","emboss'd":"swollen","exchequer":"treasury","charge":"command","Foot":"infantry","justling":"turbulent, jostling","advertisement":"news; advice","main":"stake in gambling","reversion":"future inheritance","estridges":"ostriches","beaver":"face-guard of a helmet","cuisses":"thigh armour","reprisal":"prize","gurnet":"fish","press":"conscription","caliver":"light musket","ancients":"standard-bearers","Lazarus":"the beggar in the Bible","gyves":"shackles","quilt":"padded bedcover (Falstaff)","parley":"talks between enemies","anointed":"consecrated with holy oil as king","sue his livery":"claim his inheritance","edicts":"decrees","busky":"bushy, wooded","distemperature":"sickness, disorder","chewet":"chattering jackdaw","articulate":"set out in articles","changelings":"turncoats","colossus":"giant statue","catechism":"question-and-answer lesson","scutcheon":"painted coat of arms","spleen":"hot temper","cital":"account, recital","carbonado":"meat slashed for grilling","Hydra":"many-headed monster","counterfeit":"fake, imitation","termagant":"violent, savage","embowel":"disembowel for embalming","powder":"salt (meat)","flesh'd":"blooded (for the first time)","bounty":"act of generosity","ransomless":"without ransom"}};
const PARTS={"2":[[0,3,"Shaken and pale with worry as we are, let us find a moment for frightened peace to catch her breath, and speak, still panting, of new wars to be started in distant lands."],[4,8,"No longer will this thirsty earth wet her lips with the blood of her own children. No longer will war carve trenches through her fields or crush her flowers under the hooves of enemy horses."],[9,17,"Those eyes that faced each other in hostility, which, like meteors in a stormy sky, all of one nature and one birth, recently clashed in the shock and savage close of civil war, will now march together in orderly ranks, all one way, no longer set against friends, relatives and allies. The edge of war, like a knife in a badly made sheath, will no longer cut its own master."],[18,25,"So, friends, we shall at once raise an English army and march as far as the tomb of Christ, whose soldier we now are, sworn to fight under his blessed cross, to chase the pagans out of those holy fields where his blessed feet walked, the feet that were nailed to the bitter cross for our sake fourteen hundred years ago."],[26,31,"But this plan of ours is already a year old, and there's no point telling you that we will go; that's not why we're meeting now. Instead let me hear from you, my noble cousin Westmoreland, what the Council decided last night about speeding up this urgent expedition."]],"3":[[0,3,"My lord, this business was being hotly debated, and many of the duties were assigned only last night, when suddenly a messenger arrived from Wales loaded with bad news."],[4,12,"The worst of it was that the noble Mortimer, leading the men of Herefordshire against the lawless and wild Glendower, was captured by that rough Welshman. A thousand of his men were slaughtered, and their dead bodies were so savagely and shamelessly mutilated by the Welshwomen that it can't be described or even mentioned without great shame."]],"5":[[0,6,"This, together with other news, did, my lord. More troubling and unwelcome news came from the North: on Holy Cross Day the brave Hotspur, young Harry Percy, met the bold Archibald, that ever-brave and tested Scot, at Holmedon."],[7,12,"They spent a grim and bloody hour there, as the messenger guessed from the sound of their guns and the way things looked. He rode off in the very heat of the battle, so he didn't know how it ended."]],"6":[[0,4,"Here is a dear and truly hard-working friend, Sir Walter Blunt, just off his horse and spattered with every kind of soil between Holmedon and here. He has brought us good and welcome news."],[5,11,"The Earl of Douglas is defeated. Sir Walter saw ten thousand bold Scots and twenty-two knights lying heaped in their own blood on the plains of Holmedon. Hotspur took as prisoners Mordake, Earl of Fife and eldest son of the beaten Douglas, and the Earls of Athol, Murray, Angus and Menteith."],[12,13,"Isn't that an honourable haul, a splendid prize? Well, cousin, isn't it?"]],"8":[[0,8,"Yes, and that's what makes me sad, and makes me sin by envying Lord Northumberland for being the father of such a blessed son, a son whom honour itself talks about, the straightest tree in the forest, Fortune's darling and pride. Meanwhile, when I look at the praise he earns, I see wild living and disgrace stain the face of my young Harry."],[9,12,"Oh, if only it could be proved that some fairy roaming at night had swapped our children in their cradles, and named mine Percy and his Plantagenet! Then I would have his Harry, and he would have mine."],[13,17,"But let me put my son out of my mind. What do you think, cousin, of young Percy's arrogance? He keeps for himself the prisoners he captured in this battle, and sends me word that I'll get none except Mordake, Earl of Fife."]],"80":[[0,8,"I know you all, and for a while I'll go along with your unrestrained, idle ways. But in this I'll copy the sun, which lets the low, polluting clouds hide its beauty from the world, so that when it chooses to be itself again, being missed, it may be wondered at all the more for breaking through the foul and ugly mists that seemed to strangle it."],[9,12,"If every day of the year were a holiday, playing would be as boring as working; but because holidays come rarely, they are longed for, and nothing pleases like the unusual."],[13,20,"So, when I throw off this loose behaviour and pay the debt I never promised to pay, the better I turn out than people expected, the more I will prove their expectations wrong. And like bright metal against a dull background, my reformation, shining over my faults, will look finer and attract more attention than goodness that has nothing to set it off."],[21,22,"I'll do wrong in such a way that I make wrongdoing into a clever tactic, making up for lost time when people least expect me to."]],"92":[[0,12,"My lord, I refused no prisoners. But I remember that when the fighting was over, when I was parched with rage and exhaustion, breathless and faint and leaning on my sword, a certain lord came along, neat and smartly dressed, as fresh as a bridegroom, his chin newly shaved so that it looked like a field of stubble after the harvest. He was perfumed like a dealer in fancy goods, and between his finger and thumb he held a little perfume box, which every so often he lifted to his nose and took away again, and the nose, offended, sniffed it up angrily when it came back. And all the time he smiled and chattered."],[13,19,"When the soldiers carried dead bodies past, he called them ignorant, ill-mannered rascals for bringing a dirty, ugly corpse between the wind and his noble self. He questioned me in fancy, ladylike language, and among other things demanded my prisoners on your Majesty's behalf."],[20,35,"Then I, smarting all over as my wounds cooled, in my pain and irritation at being pestered by this parrot, answered carelessly, I don't know what: he should have them, or he shouldn't. It made me mad to see him shine so smartly and smell so sweet, and talk like a lady's maid about guns and drums and wounds (God forgive the comparison!), telling me that the best thing on earth for an internal bruise was whale ointment, and that it was a great pity, so it was, that this wicked gunpowder should be dug out of the harmless earth, when it had killed many a fine brave fellow in such a cowardly way; and that, if it weren't for these awful guns, he'd have been a soldier himself."],[36,40,"I answered his feeble, disjointed chatter offhandedly, my lord, as I said. And I beg you, don't let his report be accepted as an accusation that comes between my loyalty and your Majesty."]],"94":[[0,8,"Why, he still refuses to hand over his prisoners, except on the condition that we pay the ransom at our own expense for his brother-in-law, that foolish Mortimer, who, I swear, deliberately threw away the lives of the men he led against that great magician, the damned Glendower, whose daughter, we hear, the Earl of March has recently married."],[9,15,"Should our treasury be emptied to bring a traitor home? Should we pay for treason, and strike bargains with cowards who have lost themselves and their rights? No, let him starve on the barren mountains, because I will never consider any man my friend who asks me to spend one penny to ransom the rebel Mortimer."]],"95":[[0,8,"Rebel Mortimer! He never turned against you, my sovereign lord, except by the fortune of war. To prove that, you need only one tongue for all those wounds, those gaping mouths of wounds, which he bravely received when, on the reedy bank of the gentle river Severn, in single combat, hand to hand, he spent the best part of an hour trading blows with the great Glendower."],[9,14,"Three times they paused for breath, and three times, by agreement, they drank from the swift Severn, which, frightened by their bloody looks, ran fearfully among the trembling reeds and hid its curly-waved head in the hollow bank, stained with the blood of these brave fighters."],[15,19,"Never did low and rotten scheming disguise itself with such deadly wounds, nor could the noble Mortimer have taken so many, and all willingly, if he were a traitor. So don't let him be slandered as a rebel."]],"96":[[0,4,"You're lying about him, Percy, you're lying about him. He never fought Glendower. I tell you, he would as soon have dared to face the Devil alone as have Owen Glendower for an enemy."],[5,11,"Aren't you ashamed? But, sir, from now on don't let me hear you talk about Mortimer. Send me your prisoners as fast as possible, or you'll hear from me in a way you won't like. My Lord Northumberland, you have permission to leave, with your son. Send us your prisoners, or you'll hear about it."]],"110":[[0,11,"Well then, I can't blame his cousin the King for wishing him to starve on the barren mountains. But is it to be that you, who put the crown on the head of this ungrateful man and for his sake bear the hateful stain of being accessories to murder, is it to be that you suffer a world of curses as his agents, his low instruments, the ropes, the ladder, or rather the hangman? Oh, forgive me for sinking so low to show the rank and position you hold under this cunning King."],[12,21,"Shall it be said in these times, to your shame, or written in history books to come, that men of your nobility and power pledged both to an unjust cause, as you both did, God forgive you, to put down Richard, that sweet lovely rose, and plant this thorn, this canker, Bolingbroke? And shall it be said, to your even greater shame, that you are fooled, thrown away and shaken off by the very man for whom you suffered these disgraces?"],[22,29,"No! There is still time for you to win back your banished honour and restore yourselves to the world's good opinion. Take revenge on the mocking, scornful contempt of this proud King, who thinks day and night about how to repay all he owes you with the bloody payment of your deaths. So, I say,"]],"130":[[0,5,"Then back to your Scottish prisoners. Hand them over at once without ransom, and use Douglas's son as your only go-between for raising forces in Scotland, which, for various reasons I'll send you in writing, I assure you will easily be granted."],[6,10,"You, my lord, while your son is busy in Scotland like this, will secretly worm your way into the confidence of that noble and well-loved churchman, the Archbishop."]],"173":[[0,9,"Why are you talking to me about the hangman? If I hang, I'll make a fine fat pair of gallows, because if I hang, old Sir John hangs with me, and you know he's no skinny wretch. Nonsense! There are other fine fellows you don't dream of, who for fun are happy to lend our trade some dignity, and who, if things were looked into, would put everything right to protect their own reputations. I don't keep company with footpads, with petty muggers armed with long staffs, or with those wild, moustached, purple-faced beer drinkers, but with nobles and quiet gentlemen, mayors and great ones, men who can keep their mouths shut, who will strike before they speak,"],[10,13,"and speak before they drink, and drink before they pray. And yet, by God's wounds, I'm lying, because they pray constantly to their saint, the Commonwealth; or rather, they don't pray to her but prey on her, because they ride up and down on her and use her as their boots."]],"192":[[0,8,"I'm cursed to be robbing in that thief's company. The rascal has moved my horse and tied him up I don't know where. If I walk even four more feet by the measuring rule, I'll run out of breath. Well, I'm sure I'll die a respectable death after all this, if I escape hanging for killing that rogue. I've sworn off his company every hour for the last twenty-two years, and yet I'm bewitched by the rascal. If he hasn't given me love potions to make me love him, I'll be hanged. It can't be anything else: I've drunk potions."],[9,15,"Poins! Hal! A plague on both of you! Bardolph! Peto! I'll starve before I rob a foot further. If turning honest and leaving these rogues isn't as good a deed as drinking, I'm the worst scoundrel that ever chewed with a tooth. Eight yards of rough ground is seventy miles on foot to me, and the stony-hearted villains know it well enough. A plague on it, when thieves can't be honest with one another!"],[16,17,"Whew! A plague on you all! Give me my horse, you rogues, give me my horse, and be hanged!"]],"245":[[0,10,"\"But for my own part, my lord, I would be glad to be there, because of the love I bear your family.\" He would be glad to; then why isn't he? \"Because of the love he bears our family!\" He shows by this that he loves his own barn better than our house. Let me see some more. \"The plan you are undertaking is dangerous.\" Well, that's certain: it's dangerous to catch a cold, to sleep, to drink. But I tell you, my lord fool, out of this nettle, danger, we pick this flower, safety. \"The plan you are undertaking is dangerous, the friends you have named unreliable, the timing itself badly chosen, and your whole plot too weak to balance against such a great opposition.\""],[11,19,"Is that what you say, is that what you say? I say to you again, you're a shallow, cowardly peasant, and you're lying. What a brainless fool this is! By God, our plot is as good a plot as was ever laid, our friends true and loyal: a good plot, good friends, and full of promise; an excellent plot, very good friends. What a cold-blooded rogue this is! Why, my lord of York approves of the plot and the whole course of action. By God's wounds, if I were next to this rascal now, I could brain him with his lady's fan. Aren't there my father, my uncle and myself? Lord Edmund Mortimer, my lord of York and Owen Glendower?"],[20,28,"And besides them, isn't there Douglas? Don't I have letters from all of them promising to meet me in arms by the ninth of next month? And haven't some of them set out already? What a godless rascal this is, an unbeliever! Ha! Now you'll see him, in pure fear and cowardice, go to the King and reveal everything we're doing. Oh, I could split myself in two and start punching myself, for trying to interest such a bowl of skimmed milk in such an honourable enterprise! Hang him! Let him tell the King, we're ready. I'll set out tonight."]],"248":[[0,9,"Oh, my good lord, why are you always alone like this? What have I done to be banished from my Harry's bed for this fortnight? Tell me, sweet lord, what is it that takes away your appetite, your pleasure and your precious sleep? Why do you keep your eyes fixed on the ground, and start so often when you sit alone? Why have you lost the fresh colour in your cheeks, and given away my treasures and my rights in you to dull-eyed brooding and bad-tempered gloom?"],[10,18,"As you slept fitfully, I've watched over you and heard you mutter stories of iron wars, give riding commands to your leaping horse, and shout \"Courage! To the battlefield!\" And you've talked of attacks and retreats, of trenches and tents, of fences of stakes, forts and parapets, of heavy guns, cannon and light cannon, of prisoners ransomed and soldiers killed, and all the events of a furious battle."],[19,27,"Your spirit inside you has been so much at war, and has so stirred you up in your sleep, that beads of sweat have stood on your forehead like bubbles in a stream that has just been disturbed, and strange expressions have crossed your face, like those we see when men hold their breath at some great and sudden command. Oh, what do these signs mean? My lord has some serious business in hand, and I must know it, or else he doesn't love me."]],"268":[[0,6,"Come, will you watch me ride? And when I'm on horseback, I'll swear I love you infinitely. But listen, Kate, from now on you mustn't question me about where I'm going or why. Where I must go, I must go. And, to sum up, I must leave you this evening, gentle Kate."],[7,12,"I know you're wise, but no wiser than Harry Percy's wife should be. You're faithful, but still a woman. And as for keeping secrets, no lady keeps them better, because I'm quite sure you won't reveal what you don't know. That's how far I'll trust you, gentle Kate."]],"278":[[0,8,"With three or four blockheads among sixty or eighty barrels of wine. I've plucked the lowest string of humility. My friend, I'm sworn brother to a trio of barmen and can call them all by their first names, Tom, Dick and Francis. They already swear on their salvation that though I'm only Prince of Wales, I'm the king of courtesy, and tell me straight out that I'm no stuck-up fellow like Falstaff, but a good-time lad, a lad of spirit, a good boy (by God, that's what they call me), and that when I'm King of England I'll have all the good lads in Eastcheap at my command."],[9,13,"They call heavy drinking \"dyeing scarlet\", and when you pause for breath while drinking, they cry \"Hem!\" and tell you to knock it back. In short, I learned so much in a quarter of an hour that I can drink with any tinker in his own slang for the rest of my life. I tell you, Ned, you lost a lot of honour by not being with me in this campaign."],[14,22,"But, sweet Ned, to sweeten the name of Ned I give you this pennyworth of sugar, which a junior barman pushed into my hand just now, one who has never spoken any other English in his life than \"Eight shillings and sixpence,\" and \"You are welcome,\" with the shrill addition, \"Coming, coming, sir! Put a pint of sweet wine on the Half-moon room's bill,\" or something like that. But, Ned, to pass the time till Falstaff comes, please stand in some side room while I ask my little barman why he gave me the sugar, and you keep calling \"Francis!\" so that all he can say to me is \"Coming.\" Step aside, and I'll show you an example."]],"324":[[0,8,"To think that this fellow should know fewer words than a parrot, and yet be born of a woman! His work is running upstairs and downstairs, his eloquence is reading out a bill. I'm not yet in the same mood as Percy, the Hotspur of the North, who kills six or seven dozen Scots before breakfast, washes his hands and says to his wife, \"Damn this quiet life! I want work.\" \"Oh my sweet Harry,\" she says, \"how many have you killed today?\" \"Give my roan horse a dose of medicine,\" he says, and answers, an hour later, \"About fourteen. A trifle, a trifle.\""],[9,11,"Please call in Falstaff. I'll play Percy, and that damned pig will play Lady Mortimer his wife. \"Cheers!\" says the drunkard. Call in the ribs, call in the lard."]],"385":[[0,5,"We two saw you four attack four men. You tied them up and took their money. Now see how a plain story will defeat you. Then we two attacked you four, and with one word we scared you away from your loot and have it, yes, and can show it to you here in the inn. And, Falstaff, you carried your guts away as nimbly and quickly, roaring for mercy and still running and roaring, as any bull-calf I ever heard."],[6,9,"What a wretch you are, to hack your sword the way you did and then say it was done in the fight! What trick, what device, what bolt-hole can you find now to hide you from this open and obvious shame?"]],"387":[[0,5,"By God, I knew you as well as the one who made you. Why, listen, gentlemen. Was it for me to kill the heir to the throne? Should I turn on the true Prince? Why, you know I'm as brave as Hercules, but beware of instinct: the lion won't touch the true prince. Instinct is a great thing; I was a coward on instinct."],[6,12,"I'll think better of myself and of you for the rest of my life: of myself as a brave lion, and of you as a true prince. But, by God, lads, I'm glad you have the money. Landlady, shut the doors! Stay up tonight, pray tomorrow. Gallants, lads, boys, hearts of gold, may every title of good fellowship be yours! What, shall we have some fun? Shall we have an improvised play?"]],"439":[[0,8,"Quiet, good pint-pot; quiet, good brain-tickler. Harry, I wonder not only where you spend your time but also what company you keep. For though camomile grows faster the more it is trodden on, youth wears out sooner the more it is wasted. That you are my son, I have partly your mother's word and partly my own opinion, but mainly a nasty habit of your eye and a silly drooping of your lower lip that convinces me. So if you are my son, here's the point: why, being my son, are you pointed at so much?"],[9,11,"Should the blessed sun of heaven play truant and eat blackberries? That's a question not to be asked. Should the son of England turn out a thief and steal purses? That's a question to be asked."],[12,18,"There is something, Harry, which you have often heard of, known to many in our land by the name of pitch. This pitch, as ancient writers tell us, makes you dirty; so does the company you keep. For, Harry, I'm not speaking to you now in drink but in tears, not in pleasure but in sorrow, not in words only but in griefs too. And yet there is a virtuous man whom I have often seen in your company, but I don't know his name."]],"450":[[0,6,"Are you swearing, you graceless boy? From now on never look at me. You've been dragged violently away from grace. A devil haunts you in the form of a fat old man; a barrel of a man is your companion. Why do you keep company with that trunk full of diseases, that sieve-bin of beastliness, that swollen bundle of dropsy, that huge leather jug of sherry, that roasted Manningtree ox with the stuffing in its belly, that respected Vice, that grey-haired Iniquity, that old ruffian, that aged emptiness?"],[7,10,"What is he good for except tasting sherry and drinking it? What is he neat and clean at except carving a chicken and eating it? What is he clever at except cunning? Cunning at what except villainy? Villainous in what? In everything. Worthy in what? In nothing."]],"455":[[0,4,"But to say I know more harm in him than in myself would be to say more than I know. That he's old, more's the pity, his white hairs prove. But if sherry and sugar are a crime, God help the wicked! If being old and merry is a sin, then many an old innkeeper I know is damned. If being fat means being hated, then Pharaoh's thin cows in the Bible are to be loved."],[5,9,"No, my good lord: banish Peto, banish Bardolph, banish Poins. But sweet Jack Falstaff, kind Jack Falstaff, loyal Jack Falstaff, brave Jack Falstaff, and all the braver for being old Jack Falstaff, don't banish him from your Harry's company, don't banish him from your Harry's company. Banish plump Jack, and you banish all the world."]],"508":[[0,10,"Oh, then the earth shook to see the heavens on fire, not out of fear of your birth. Sick nature often breaks out in strange eruptions. Often the pregnant earth is griped and troubled with a kind of colic, caused by unruly wind trapped inside her womb, which, struggling to get out, shakes the old grandmother earth and topples steeples and moss-covered towers. At your birth our grandmother earth, suffering from this disorder, shook in a fit."]],"509":[[0,8,"Cousin, there are not many men from whom I'll take such contradiction. Allow me to tell you once more that at my birth the face of the sky was full of fiery shapes, the goats ran down from the mountains and the herds made a strange noise in the frightened fields. These signs have marked me out as extraordinary, and the whole course of my life shows that I am not on the list of ordinary men."],[9,14,"Where is the man living, anywhere within the sea that beats against the shores of England, Scotland and Wales, who can call me his pupil or say he has taught me? And show me any son of a woman who can follow me along the hard paths of magic and keep up with me in deep experiments."]],"520":[[0,7,"The Archdeacon has divided it very equally into three parts. England, from the Trent and the Severn to here, to the south and east, is assigned to me. Everything westward, Wales beyond the Severn shore and all the fertile land within that boundary, goes to Owen Glendower. And to you, dear cousin, the rest to the north, lying beyond the Trent."],[8,14,"Our three-way contracts are drawn up, and once they are sealed by each of us, which can be done tonight, tomorrow, cousin Percy, you, I and my good lord of Worcester will set out to meet your father and the Scottish forces at Shrewsbury, as arranged."],[15,18,"My father-in-law Glendower isn't ready yet, and we won't need his help for a fortnight. In that time you can have gathered your tenants, friends and the gentlemen of your neighbourhood."]],"522":[[0,9,"It seems to me that my share, north from Burton here, isn't as big as either of yours. Look how this river comes winding in on me and cuts a huge half-moon, a monstrous chunk, out of the best of my land. I'll have the river dammed up at this point, and the smooth silver Trent will run here in a new channel, neatly and straight. It won't wind with such a deep curve to rob me of such a rich piece of valley here."]],"540":[[0,7,"I can't help it. Sometimes he makes me angry by telling me about the mole and the ant, about the dreamer Merlin and his prophecies, and about a dragon and a fish without fins, a griffin with clipped wings and a raven that's lost its feathers, a lion lying down and a cat rearing up, and such a load of rambling nonsense that it tries my patience."],[8,16,"I'll tell you what: last night he kept me for at least nine hours listing the names of the various devils that were his servants. I said \"hmm\" and \"very good\", but didn't take in a word. Oh, he's as tiresome as a tired horse or a nagging wife, worse than a smoky house. I'd much rather live on cheese and garlic in a windmill far away than eat delicacies and have him talk to me in any fine country house in Christendom."]],"541":[[0,4,"Honestly, he's a worthy gentleman, extremely well read and skilled in secret arts, as brave as a lion and wonderfully friendly, and as generous as the mines of India."],[5,11,"Shall I tell you something, cousin? He has a great respect for your temperament, and he holds himself back from his natural freedom when you cross him; he really does. I promise you, there's no man alive who could have provoked him as you have without feeling danger and rebuke. But please don't do it often, I beg you."]],"542":[[0,5,"Honestly, my lord, you're too stubborn and blunt, and since you came here you've done enough to drive him completely out of patience. You must learn to correct this fault, my lord. Although it sometimes shows greatness, courage and spirit, and that's the best credit it gives you,"],[6,12,"yet it often shows harsh anger, lack of manners and self-control, pride, arrogance, self-importance and scorn. Even the least of these, when it clings to a nobleman, loses him people's affection and leaves a stain on all his other good qualities, cheating them of praise."]],"586":[[0,7,"I don't know whether God wills it, for some offence I have committed, that in his secret judgement he will breed revenge and a punishment for me out of my own blood. But by the way you live your life you make me believe that you are marked out only as the burning vengeance and rod of heaven to punish my wrongdoings."],[8,13,"Otherwise, tell me, how could such unruly and low desires, such poor, base, coarse and mean activities, such empty pleasures and rough company as you are joined and bound to, go along with the greatness of your blood and stand level with your princely heart?"]],"587":[[0,3,"If it please your Majesty, I wish I could clear myself of all offences as plainly as I am sure I can clear myself of many I am accused of."],[4,10,"Yet let me beg this much allowance: that, once I have disproved many stories invented by smiling flatterers and low gossips, which great men must often listen to, I may be pardoned, on my sincere submission, for the things that are true, in which my youth has wandered into faults and disorder."]],"588":[[0,9,"God pardon you! But let me wonder, Harry, at your tastes, which fly in a completely different direction from all your ancestors. You have rudely lost your place in the Council, which your younger brother has filled, and you are almost a stranger to the hearts of the whole Court and the princes of my blood. The hope and expectation of your future is ruined, and every man's soul is predicting your downfall."],[10,16,"If I had been so free with my presence, so worn out in men's eyes like a hired horse, so stale and cheap in common company, public opinion, which helped me to the crown, would have stayed loyal to the king in possession and left me in disgraced exile, a fellow of no importance or prospects."],[17,30,"By being seldom seen, I couldn't move without being wondered at like a comet, so that men would tell their children, \"That's him,\" and others would say, \"Where? Which one is Bolingbroke?\" And then I stole every grace from heaven and dressed myself in such humility that I plucked loyalty from men's hearts and loud cheers and greetings from their mouths, even in the presence of the crowned King. In this way I kept myself fresh and new; my presence, like a bishop's robe, was never seen without being wondered at. And so my public appearances, rare but splendid, were like a feast and gained great dignity by being rare."],[31,44,"The frivolous King Richard ambled up and down with shallow jesters and quick, brushwood wits, soon lit and soon burnt out. He watered down his majesty and mixed his royalty with capering fools, let his great name be soiled by their mockery, and, against the dignity of his title, lent his presence to laughing at jeering boys and putting up with the challenges of every beardless, empty mocker. He became a companion of the common streets and gave himself away to popularity, so that, being swallowed daily by men's eyes, they were sick of honey and began to hate the taste of sweetness, of which a little more than a little is far too much."],[45,55,"So when he had to appear in public, he was like the cuckoo in June, heard but not noticed; seen, but with eyes that, tired and dulled by familiarity, gave him no special attention, such as is given to sun-like majesty when it shines rarely on admiring eyes. Instead they were drowsy, let their eyelids droop, slept in his face, and gave him the sort of look that sullen men give their enemies, being glutted, gorged and full of his presence."],[56,62,"And you are in exactly that position, Harry, because you've lost your princely privilege by mixing with low company. There isn't an eye that isn't tired of the common sight of you, except mine, which has wanted to see more of you, and which now does what I don't want it to do: blinds itself with foolish, loving tears."]],"590":[[0,12,"For all the world, what you are at this moment, Richard was then, when I landed from France at Ravenspurgh; and just as I was then, Percy is now. Now, by my sceptre and my soul as well, he has a worthier claim to the state than you, the mere shadow of an heir. With no right, or anything that looks like right, he fills the fields of the kingdom with armour, turns to face the armed jaws of the lion, and, though he's no older than you, leads old lords and reverend bishops into bloody battles and bruising combat."],[13,24,"What everlasting honour he has won against the famous Douglas, whose great deeds, fierce raids and great name in arms give him the highest rank and military title among all soldiers in every Christian kingdom! Three times this Hotspur, Mars in nappies, this infant warrior, has defeated great Douglas in his campaigns. He captured him once, set him free and made a friend of him, to swell the cry of deep defiance and shake the peace and security of our throne."],[25,35,"And what do you say to this? Percy, Northumberland, his Grace the Archbishop of York, Douglas and Mortimer have made a pact against us and are in arms. But why do I tell you this news? Why, Harry, do I tell you about my enemies, when you are my closest and dearest enemy? You're quite likely, out of cowardly fear, low tendencies and a fit of bad temper, to fight against me in Percy's pay, to follow at his heels and bow when he frowns, to show how degenerate you are."]],"591":[[0,8,"Don't think so; you won't find it so. And God forgive those who have turned your Majesty's good opinion so far away from me! I will make up for all this on Percy's head, and at the end of some glorious day I'll be bold enough to tell you that I am your son, when I'll wear a garment all of blood and stain my face with a bloody mask, which, when it is washed away, will scour my shame away with it."],[9,17,"And that will be the day, whenever it comes, when this same child of honour and fame, this gallant Hotspur, this much-praised knight, and your disregarded Harry happen to meet. I wish every honour that sits on his helmet were multiplied many times, and my shames on my head doubled! Because the time will come when I'll make this northern youth exchange his glorious deeds for my disgraces."],[18,23,"Percy is only my agent, my good lord, buying up glorious deeds on my behalf; and I'll call him to such a strict account that he'll hand over every glory, yes, even the smallest honour he has ever won, or I'll tear the account from his heart."],[24,30,"This, in the name of God, I promise here. If I carry it out and survive, I beg your Majesty to let it heal the long-standing wounds of my wild behaviour. If not, the end of life cancels all debts, and I'll die a hundred thousand deaths before I break the smallest part of this vow."]],"596":[[0,8,"The Earl of Westmoreland set out today, and with him my son, Lord John of Lancaster, because this news is five days old. Next Wednesday, Harry, you'll set out; on Thursday we will march ourselves. Our meeting place is Bridgnorth. And, Harry, you will march through Gloucestershire; so, by my reckoning of what needs to be done, our whole army will meet at Bridgnorth in about twelve days."],[9,10,"We have our hands full of business. Let's go. Opportunity feeds the enemy fat while men delay."]],"606":[[0,6,"No, I swear it doesn't. I make as good use of it as many men do of a skull or a reminder of death. I never see your face without thinking of hell-fire and the rich man in the Bible who lived in purple, because there he is in his robes, burning, burning. If you had any leaning towards virtue, I'd swear by your face; my oath would be \"By this fire, which is God's angel.\" But you're completely lost to sin, and if it weren't for the light in your face, you'd be the son of utter darkness."],[7,14,"When you ran up Gad's Hill in the night to catch my horse, if I didn't think you were a will-o'-the-wisp or a ball of fireworks, then money is worth nothing. Oh, you're a permanent torchlight parade, an everlasting bonfire! You've saved me a thousand marks in torches, walking with you at night from tavern to tavern. But the sherry you've drunk at my expense would have bought me lights just as cheaply at the dearest candle-maker's in Europe. I've kept that fire-eating salamander of yours supplied with fire for the last thirty-two years. God reward me for it!"]],"696":[[0,7,"Sick now! Failing now! This sickness infects the very lifeblood of our enterprise; it's spreading even here to our camp. He writes to me here that he has an internal illness, and that his allies could not be gathered so quickly by a deputy, nor did he think it right to put such a dangerous and precious trust in anyone but himself."],[8,13,"Still, he boldly advises that we should go ahead with our small combined forces to see how fortune is inclined towards us, because, as he writes, there's no backing out now, since the King certainly knows all our plans. What do you say to that?"]],"698":[[0,9,"A dangerous gash, a limb cut right off. And yet, honestly, it's not. His absence now seems worse than it will turn out to be. Would it be wise to stake all our resources on one throw of the dice, to bet so much on the risky chance of one uncertain hour? It wouldn't, because then we'd be reaching the very bottom of our hope, the very edge, the utmost limit of all our fortunes."]],"701":[[0,8,"But still I wish your father had been here. The nature of our enterprise can't stand division. Some people who don't know why he's away will think that wisdom, loyalty and simple disapproval of what we're doing kept the Earl away. And think how such a belief could turn the tide among our nervous supporters and raise doubts about our cause."],[9,15,"You know well that we who are the attackers must keep clear of close examination and block every peephole and every loophole through which the eye of reason might pry into us. This absence of your father's draws back a curtain that shows the ignorant a cause for fear they never dreamed of before."]],"710":[[0,6,"All equipped, all in armour, all plumed like ostriches flapping their wings in the wind, like eagles that have just bathed, glittering in golden coats like statues of saints, as full of spirit as the month of May and as splendid as the sun at midsummer, as playful as young goats and as wild as young bulls."],[7,13,"I saw young Harry, with his visor up and his thigh armour on, splendidly armed, rise from the ground like winged Mercury and vault into the saddle so easily that it was as if an angel had dropped from the clouds to turn and wheel a fiery Pegasus and enchant the world with noble horsemanship."]],"711":[[0,7,"No more, no more! Worse than the sun in March, this praise breeds fevers. Let them come. They come like sacrificial animals in their finery, and we will offer them, all hot and bleeding, to the fiery-eyed goddess of smoky war. Armoured Mars will sit on his altar up to his ears in blood. I'm on fire to hear that this rich prize is so near and not yet ours."],[8,13,"Come, let me try out my horse, which is to carry me like a thunderbolt against the chest of the Prince of Wales. Harry and Harry will meet, hot horse against horse, and never part till one drops down a corpse. Oh, if only Glendower had come!"]],"729":[[0,9,"If I'm not ashamed of my soldiers, I'm a pickled fish. I've abused the King's power to conscript men damnably. In exchange for a hundred and fifty soldiers, I've got three hundred and something pounds. I conscripted nobody but well-off householders and farmers' sons; I sought out engaged bachelors whose wedding banns had been read twice, a whole load of comfortable cowards who would as soon hear the Devil as a drum, men who fear the bang of a musket more than a wounded bird or a hurt wild duck. I conscripted nobody but such milksops, with hearts no bigger than pinheads, and they've all bought themselves out of service."],[10,22,"And now my whole company consists of standard-bearers, corporals, lieutenants and gentlemen of the ranks, wretches as ragged as Lazarus in the painted wall-hanging, where the glutton's dogs licked his sores; and men who were never really soldiers at all, but dishonest servants who've been sacked, younger sons of younger brothers, runaway barmen and stable-boys out of work, the cankers of a calm world and a long peace, ten times more dishonourably ragged than an old patched flag. And I've got such men to fill the places of those who bought themselves out that you'd think I had a hundred and fifty tattered Prodigal Sons just back from keeping pigs and eating swill and husks. A madman met me on the way and told me I'd emptied all the gallows and conscripted the dead bodies."],[23,31,"No eye has ever seen such scarecrows. I won't march through Coventry with them, that's certain. And the villains march with their legs wide apart, as if they still had shackles on, because I got most of them out of prison. There's only a shirt and a half in my whole company, and the half-shirt is two napkins tacked together and thrown over the shoulders like a herald's sleeveless coat, and the shirt, to tell the truth, was stolen from my landlord at Saint Albans or the red-nosed innkeeper of Daventry. But that doesn't matter; they'll find plenty of linen drying on every hedge."]],"771":[[0,7,"And God forbid that I should stand anywhere else, as long as you stand against the anointed King, outside the law and true allegiance! But to my message. The King has sent to find out the nature of your grievances, and why you summon up such bold hostility out of the heart of peace at home, teaching his dutiful country reckless cruelty."],[8,13,"If the King has in any way forgotten your services, which he admits are many, he invites you to name your grievances, and you will quickly have what you want with interest, and a full pardon for yourself and those you have misled into this."]],"772":[[0,13,"The King is kind, and we know well that the King knows when to promise and when to pay. My father, my uncle and I gave him the very crown he wears. And when he had fewer than twenty-six followers, out of favour with the world, wretched and low, a poor, ignored outlaw sneaking home, my father welcomed him ashore. And when he heard him swear and vow to God that he had come only to be Duke of Lancaster, to claim his inheritance and beg for peace, with innocent tears and passionate words, my father, moved by kindness and pity, swore to help him, and did so too."],[14,25,"Now, when the lords and barons of the kingdom saw that Northumberland supported him, great and small came to him with caps off and knees bent, met him in towns, cities and villages, waited for him on bridges, stood in lanes, laid gifts before him, offered him their oaths, gave him their heirs as pages, and followed at his heels in glittering crowds. Immediately, as greatness becomes aware of itself, he went a step higher than the vow he made to my father, when he was weak, on the bare shore at Ravenspurgh."],[26,36,"And now, if you please, he takes it on himself to reform certain laws and harsh decrees that weigh too heavily on the country, cries out against abuses, seems to weep over his country's wrongs; and with this face, this pretended look of justice, he won the hearts of everyone he was fishing for. He went further: cut off the heads of all the favourites whom the absent King had left behind here as his deputies when he was in person at the Irish war."]],"774":[[0,9,"Then to the point. Shortly afterwards he deposed the King, soon after that took his life, and straight after that taxed the whole country. To make it worse, he allowed his kinsman March (who, if everyone had his rightful place, is really his king) to be held hostage in Wales, left there without ransom. He belittled me in my lucky victories and tried to trap me with spies;"],[10,16,"scolded my uncle and drove him from the Council table, angrily dismissed my father from Court, broke oath after oath, committed wrong after wrong, and, in short, drove us to raise this army for our safety, and at the same time to look into his claim to the throne, which we now find too crooked to last long."]],"785":[[0,6,"You very likely can. Tomorrow, good Sir Michael, is a day when the fortune of ten thousand men must be put to the test. For, sir, at Shrewsbury, as I am reliably told, the King, with a mighty army quickly raised, meets Lord Harry Percy."],[7,13,"And I fear, Sir Michael, what with the illness of Northumberland, whose army was the largest, and what with the absence of Owen Glendower, who was also counted on as a main strength and isn't coming, held back by prophecies, I fear Percy's army is too weak for an immediate trial of strength with the King."]],"799":[[0,5,"Well, my lord of Worcester! It isn't right that you and I should meet on such terms as we meet on now. You have betrayed our trust and made us take off our comfortable robes of peace to squeeze our old limbs into harsh steel armour. This isn't right, my lord, this isn't right."],[6,12,"What do you say to it? Will you untie this brutal knot of hated war again and move once more in that obedient orbit where you gave out a fair and natural light, and no longer be a meteor sucked up from the earth, a fearful omen, a sign of evil let loose on generations not yet born?"]],"804":[[0,8,"Your Majesty chose to turn your favourable looks away from me and our whole family. And yet I must remind you, my lord, that we were the first and dearest of your friends. For your sake I broke my staff of office in Richard's time and rode day and night to meet you on the way and kiss your hand, when you were nowhere near as strong and fortunate in position and reputation as I was."],[9,16,"It was I, my brother and his son who brought you home and boldly faced the dangers of the time. You swore to us, and you swore that oath at Doncaster, that you intended nothing against the state and claimed no more than your newly inherited right, the seat of Gaunt, the dukedom of Lancaster. To this we swore our help."],[17,28,"But in a short time fortune rained down in showers on your head, and such a flood of greatness fell on you, what with our help, the King's absence, the wrongs of a disorderly time, the injuries you seemed to have suffered, and the contrary winds that kept the King so long in his unlucky Irish wars that everyone in England thought he was dead, that out of this swarm of advantages you let yourself be quickly persuaded to seize total power into your own hands, and forgot your oath to us at Doncaster."],[29,41,"And after being fed by us, you treated us as that unkind gull, the cuckoo, treats the sparrow: you crushed our nest and grew so huge on our feeding that even our love didn't dare come near you for fear of being swallowed. So we were forced, for safety's sake, to fly out of your sight on quick wings and raise this army. And so we stand against you by means you yourself have forged against yourself, through unkind treatment, threatening looks, and the breaking of all the faith and promises you swore to us in your earlier enterprise."]],"805":[[0,10,"These things, indeed, you have listed, proclaimed at market crosses and read out in churches, to trim the garment of rebellion with some fine colour that may please the eyes of fickle turncoats and poor malcontents, who gape and rub their elbows at the news of riotous change. And rebellion has never lacked such watercolours to paint over its cause, nor sullen beggars hungry for a time of chaos and confusion."]],"806":[[0,9,"In both our armies there are many souls who will pay very dearly for this encounter, once they meet in battle. Tell your nephew that the Prince of Wales joins with all the world in praising Henry Percy. By my hopes of heaven, if this present rebellion is not counted against him, I don't think there is a braver gentleman alive, more actively brave or more bravely young, more daring or more bold, to grace this modern age with noble deeds."],[10,17,"For my part, I say it to my shame, I have been a truant from chivalry, and I hear that's how he regards me too. Yet I say this before my father's Majesty: I am willing to let him have the advantage of his great name and reputation, and, to save blood on both sides, I will try my fortune with him in single combat."]],"807":[[0,7,"And, Prince of Wales, we would dare to risk you in it, though countless considerations count against it. No, good Worcester, no, we love our people well; we love even those who are misled on your nephew's side. And if they will accept our offer of mercy, he, they and you, yes, every man, shall be my friend again, and I'll be his."],[8,13,"So tell your nephew, and then bring me word what he will do. But if he won't give in, rebuke and fearful punishment wait at our command, and they will do their job. So go. We won't be troubled with a reply now. Our offer is fair; consider it wisely."]],"817":[[0,4,"It isn't due yet; I'd hate to pay him before the day it's due. Why should I be so eager to meet someone who isn't calling on me? Well, it doesn't matter; honour spurs me on. Yes, but what if honour marks me off the list of the living when I come on? What then? Can honour set a broken leg? No. Or an arm? No. Or take away the pain of a wound? No."],[5,10,"Honour has no skill in surgery, then? No. What is honour? A word. What is in that word \"honour\"? Air. A fine sum! Who has it? The man who died on Wednesday. Does he feel it? No. Does he hear it? No. So it can't be sensed? Yes, by the dead. But won't it live with the living? No. Why? Slander won't allow it. So I'll have none of it. Honour is just a painted coat of arms at a funeral. And so my catechism ends."]],"823":[[0,12,"Then we are all ruined. It isn't possible, it can't be, that the King will keep his word to love us. He'll always suspect us and find a time to punish this offence through other faults. All our lives suspicion will be watching us with a hundred eyes, because treason is trusted only like a fox, which, however tame, well cared for and locked up, will still have some wild trick of its ancestors. However we look, sad or merry, people will misread our looks, and we'll feed like oxen in a stall: the better looked after, the nearer to death."],[13,22,"My nephew's offence may well be forgotten. It has the excuse of youth and hot blood, and a nickname that gives him licence, a hare-brained Hotspur, ruled by his temper. All his offences rest on my head and on his father's. We led him on, and since he caught his corruption from us, we, as the source of it all, will pay for all. So, good cousin, don't let Harry know about the King's offer in any circumstances."]],"839":[[0,8,"No, by my soul. I never in my life heard a challenge made more modestly, unless a brother were challenging a brother to a friendly bout of arms. He gave you every honour due to a man, decorated your praises with a princely tongue, listed your merits like a history book, always making you better than his praise by dismissing praise itself as inadequate compared with you."],[9,17,"And, what really suited him like a true prince, he gave a blushing account of himself and scolded his truant youth so gracefully that it was as if he had a double gift, teaching and learning at the same moment. There he stopped. But let me tell the world, if he survives the hostility of this day, England never had so sweet a hope, so much misjudged in his wildness."]],"840":[[0,5,"Cousin, I think you've fallen in love with his follies. I never heard of any prince so wildly free. But let him be what he likes, before night I'll embrace him with a soldier's arm so that he'll shrink under my courtesy."],[6,9,"To arms, to arms, quickly! And, comrades, soldiers, friends, you'd better think for yourselves about what you have to do, because I don't really have the gift of words to stir up your blood with persuasion."]],"846":[[0,6,"I thank him for cutting short my speech, because talking isn't my trade. Only this: let each man do his best. And here I draw a sword whose steel I mean to stain with the best blood I can meet in the adventure of this dangerous day. Now, Esperance! Percy! And charge!"],[7,10,"Sound all the lofty instruments of war, and to that music let us all embrace, because, heaven to earth, some of us will never do such a kindness a second time."]],"918":[[0,9,"Oh Harry, you've robbed me of my youth! I can bear the loss of fragile life better than the proud titles you've won from me; they wound my thoughts worse than your sword wounds my flesh. But thoughts are the slaves of life, and life is time's fool, and time, which surveys the whole world, must have an end. Oh, I could prophesy, except that the earthy, cold hand of death lies on my tongue. No, Percy, you are dust, and food for..."]],"920":[[0,6,"For worms, brave Percy. Farewell, great heart! Badly woven ambition, how much you have shrunk! When this body held a spirit, a kingdom was too small a boundary for it; but now two paces of the lowest earth is room enough. This earth that carries you dead does not carry so brave a gentleman alive."],[7,14,"If you could feel courtesy, I wouldn't show my admiration so openly. But let my plumes cover your mangled face, and on your behalf I'll thank myself for doing these kind last rites. Goodbye, and take your praise with you to heaven! Let your disgrace sleep with you in the grave and not be remembered in your epitaph!"]],"924":[[0,8,"Disembowelled! If you disembowel me today, I'll give you permission to salt me and eat me too tomorrow. By God's blood, it was time to fake it, or that hot-tempered savage Scot would have paid me off in full. Fake! I'm lying; I'm no fake. To die is to be a fake, because a man without a man's life is only a fake of a man. But to fake dying, when a man stays alive by it, is not to be a fake but the true and perfect image of life. The better part of courage is caution, and by that better part I have saved my life."],[9,14,"By God's wounds, I'm afraid of this gunpowder Percy, even though he's dead. What if he's faking too, and gets up? By my faith, I'm afraid he'd turn out the better faker. So I'll make sure of him; yes, and I'll swear I killed him. Why couldn't he get up as well as I did? Nothing can disprove me but eyes, and nobody sees me. So, my man, with a new wound in your thigh, come along with me."]],"942":[[0,9,"This is how rebellion always met its punishment. Evil-minded Worcester! Didn't we send mercy, pardon and terms of friendship to all of you? And would you twist our offers into their opposite, and abuse your nephew's trust? Three knights on our side killed today, a noble earl and many others would be alive at this hour if you had, like a Christian, carried honest messages between our armies."]],"950":[[0,6,"Then all that remains is to divide our army. You, my son John, and my cousin Westmoreland, head towards York with all possible speed to meet Northumberland and the churchman Scroop, who, we hear, are busy raising arms. I, and you, son Harry, will go towards Wales to fight Glendower and the Earl of March."],[7,10,"Rebellion in this country will lose its power when it meets the check of another day like this. And since this business has gone so well, let's not stop until all that is ours is won."]]};
const QUOTES={"themes":{"honour":["Honour","Hotspur's glory, Hal's redemption, Falstaff's 'word' and 'air', and who really owns honour."],"kingship":["Kingship and power","How a king wins, keeps and shows his power, and whether Henry's crown is rightful."],"fathers":["Fathers and sons","The King and Hal, Northumberland and Hotspur, Falstaff as a false father."],"rebellion":["Rebellion and order","Civil war, broken oaths, plots and the division of the kingdom."],"misrule":["Tavern and misrule","Eastcheap, sack, robbery, play-acting and holiday against the world of duty."],"counterfeit":["Lies and counterfeits","Falstaff's lies, the King's decoys, Hal's disguise and faked death."],"war":["War and its cost","Battle, wounds, soldiers as 'food for powder' and the price paid by ordinary men."]},"quotes":[{"id":"2","who":"KING","text":"So shaken as we are, so wan with care,","themes":["kingship","rebellion"],"note":"The King's first words: a ruler exhausted by the civil war that made him king."},{"id":"2","who":"KING","text":"The edge of war, like an ill-sheathed knife, No more shall cut his master.","themes":["war","rebellion"],"note":"Civil war is a knife that wounds its own owner."},{"id":"8","who":"KING","text":"A son who is the theme of honour's tongue;","themes":["honour","fathers"],"note":"The King praises Hotspur, the son he wishes he had."},{"id":"8","who":"KING","text":"See riot and dishonour stain the brow Of my young Harry.","themes":["fathers","honour"],"note":"Hal's reputation as the King sees it."},{"id":"8","who":"KING","text":"And call'd mine Percy, his Plantagenet!","themes":["fathers"],"note":"The changeling wish that sets up the Hal/Hotspur comparison."},{"id":"16","who":"HAL","text":"What a devil hast thou to do with the time of the day?","themes":["misrule"],"note":"Falstaff lives outside time; Hal will 'redeem' time."},{"id":"21","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"let us be Diana's foresters, gentlemen of the shade, minions of the Moon;","themes":["misrule","counterfeit"],"note":"Falstaff dresses up thieving in poetic titles."},{"id":"22","who":"HAL","text":"now ill as low an ebb as the foot of the ladder, and by-and-by in as high a flow as the ridge of the gallows.","themes":["misrule"],"note":"Hal reminds Falstaff that thieves end on the gallows."},{"id":"31","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"Do not thou, when thou art king, hang a thief.","themes":["misrule","kingship"],"note":"Falstaff hopes Hal's reign will be a reign of misrule."},{"id":"43","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"Before I knew thee, Hal, I knew nothing; and now am I, if a man should speak truly, little better than one of the wicked.","themes":["misrule","counterfeit"],"note":"Falstaff comically blames Hal for corrupting him."},{"id":"43","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"I must give over this life, and I will give it over;","themes":["misrule"],"note":"A repentance that lasts one line."},{"id":"47","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"'tis no sin for a man to labour in his vocation.","themes":["misrule"],"note":"Robbery as a respectable trade."},{"id":"52","who":"HAL","text":"he will give the Devil his due.","themes":["misrule"],"note":"Hal turns a proverb into a joke about Falstaff's soul."},{"id":"61","who":"HAL","text":"Well, then, once in my days I'll be a madcap.","themes":["misrule"],"note":"Hal agrees to the robbery, briefly."},{"id":"76","who":"POINS","text":"the incomprehensible lies that this same fat rogue will tell us when we meet at supper:","themes":["counterfeit","misrule"],"note":"Poins predicts the buckram story."},{"id":"80","who":"HAL","text":"I know you all, and will awhile uphold The unyok'd humour of your idleness:","themes":["misrule","counterfeit"],"note":"Hal's first soliloquy reveals his wildness is a plan."},{"id":"80","who":"HAL","text":"Yet herein will I imitate the Sun,","themes":["kingship"],"note":"The sun is a traditional symbol of kingship."},{"id":"80","who":"HAL","text":"If all the year were playing holidays, To sport would be as tedious as to work;","themes":["misrule"],"note":"Holiday only works because it is rare."},{"id":"80","who":"HAL","text":"And, like bright metal on a sullen ground, My reformation, glittering o'er my fault, Shall show more goodly and attract more eyes","themes":["kingship","counterfeit"],"note":"Hal plans his image like a jeweller setting a gem."},{"id":"80","who":"HAL","text":"Redeeming time, when men think least I will.","themes":["honour","kingship"],"note":"The couplet that defines Hal's whole journey."},{"id":"84","who":"KING","text":"I will from henceforth rather be myself, Mighty and to be fear'd,","themes":["kingship"],"note":"The King decides to rule by fear."},{"id":"87","who":"KING","text":"Worcester, get thee gone; for I do see Danger and disobedience in thine eye:","themes":["kingship","rebellion"],"note":"The King dismisses Worcester and sows the rebellion."},{"id":"92","who":"HOTSPUR","text":"Came there a certain lord, neat, trimly dress'd, Fresh as a bridegroom;","themes":["honour","war"],"note":"Hotspur's mockery of the courtier."},{"id":"92","who":"HOTSPUR","text":"To be so pester'd with a popinjay,","themes":["honour"],"note":"Hotspur despises fine talk."},{"id":"92","who":"HOTSPUR","text":"and, but for these vile guns, He would himself have been a soldier.","themes":["war"],"note":"The courtier's excuse for cowardice."},{"id":"94","who":"KING","text":"Shall we buy treason? and indent with fears When they have lost and forfeited themselves?","themes":["rebellion","kingship"],"note":"The King refuses to ransom Mortimer."},{"id":"95","who":"HOTSPUR","text":"Three times they breathed, and three times did they drink,","themes":["honour","war"],"note":"Hotspur's heroic picture of Mortimer's fight."},{"id":"101","who":"HOTSPUR","text":"As this ingrate and canker'd Bolingbroke.","themes":["rebellion","kingship"],"note":"Hotspur refuses to call Henry king."},{"id":"107","who":"WORCESTER","text":"And for whose death we in the world's wide mouth Live scandalized and foully spoken of.","themes":["rebellion","kingship"],"note":"The Percys share the guilt of Richard's death."},{"id":"110","who":"HOTSPUR","text":"To put down Richard, that sweet lovely rose, And plant this thorn, this canker, Bolingbroke?","themes":["kingship","rebellion"],"note":"Richard as a rose, Henry as a weed."},{"id":"110","who":"HOTSPUR","text":"That you are fool'd, discarded, and shook off By him for whom these shames ye underwent?","themes":["rebellion","honour"],"note":"Hotspur's case for rebellion: ingratitude."},{"id":"110","who":"HOTSPUR","text":"wherein you may redeem Your banish'd honours,","themes":["honour"],"note":"Hotspur also talks of redeeming honour."},{"id":"112","who":"HOTSPUR","text":"O, the blood more stirs To rouse a lion than to start a hare!","themes":["honour","war"],"note":"Danger excites Hotspur."},{"id":"114","who":"HOTSPUR","text":"By Heaven, methinks it were an easy leap, To pluck bright honour from the pale-faced Moon;","themes":["honour"],"note":"Honour as an impossible, glorious prize."},{"id":"114","who":"HOTSPUR","text":"And pluck up drowned honour by the locks;","themes":["honour"],"note":"Honour as a drowned woman to rescue."},{"id":"114","who":"HOTSPUR","text":"But out upon this half-faced fellowship!","themes":["honour"],"note":"Hotspur will not share honour."},{"id":"115","who":"WORCESTER","text":"He apprehends a world of figures here, But not the form of what he should attend.","themes":["honour","rebellion"],"note":"Worcester sees Hotspur's imagination outrun his sense."},{"id":"120","who":"HOTSPUR","text":"I'll have a starling shall be taught to speak Nothing but Mortimer,","themes":["rebellion"],"note":"Hotspur's comic, childish fury."},{"id":"122","who":"HOTSPUR","text":"And that same sword-and-buckler Prince of Wales,","themes":["honour","fathers"],"note":"Hotspur scorns Hal as a brawler."},{"id":"125","who":"HOTSPUR","text":"Of this vile politician, Bolingbroke.","themes":["kingship","counterfeit"],"note":"Henry as a schemer."},{"id":"127","who":"HOTSPUR","text":"Why, what a candy deal of courtesy This fawning greyhound then did proffer me!","themes":["kingship","counterfeit"],"note":"Henry's past flattery remembered."},{"id":"138","who":"WORCESTER","text":"To save our heads by raising of a head;","themes":["rebellion"],"note":"A pun: raise an army to keep their heads."},{"id":"138","who":"WORCESTER","text":"The King will always think him in our debt,","themes":["rebellion","kingship"],"note":"Debt that can never be repaid drives the rebellion."},{"id":"142","who":"HOTSPUR","text":"O, let the hours be short, Till fields and blows and groans applaud our sport!","themes":["war","honour"],"note":"War as sport."},{"id":"173","who":"GADSHILL","text":"they pray continually to their saint, the Commonwealth; or, rather, not pray to her, but prey on her,","themes":["misrule","rebellion"],"note":"The powerful prey on the country."},{"id":"175","who":"GADSHILL","text":"we have the receipt of fernseed,—we walk invisible.","themes":["misrule","counterfeit"],"note":"Thieves believe they are untouchable."},{"id":"192","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"If the rascal have not given me medicines to make me love him, I'll be hang'd;","themes":["misrule","fathers"],"note":"Falstaff's love for Hal is real."},{"id":"192","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"a plague upon't, when thieves cannot be true one to another!","themes":["misrule","counterfeit"],"note":"Comic complaint about honour among thieves."},{"id":"228","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"young men must live.","themes":["misrule"],"note":"Said by a fat old man."},{"id":"240","who":"HAL","text":"Fat Falstaff sweats to death, And lards the lean earth as he walks along:","themes":["misrule"],"note":"Hal's comic picture of Falstaff running."},{"id":"245","who":"HOTSPUR","text":"out of this nettle, danger, we pluck this flower, safety.","themes":["honour","rebellion"],"note":"Hotspur's answer to caution."},{"id":"245","who":"HOTSPUR","text":"What a frosty-spirited rogue is this!","themes":["honour"],"note":"Hotspur despises the cautious."},{"id":"248","who":"LADY PERCY","text":"In thy faint slumbers I by thee have watch'd, And heard thee murmur tales of iron wars;","themes":["war"],"note":"War has taken over Hotspur's sleep."},{"id":"248","who":"LADY PERCY","text":"Some heavy business hath my lord in hand, And I must know it, else he loves me not.","themes":["rebellion"],"note":"Kate demands a share in his secret."},{"id":"257","who":"HOTSPUR","text":"That roan shall be my throne.","themes":["honour","war"],"note":"Hotspur's 'throne' is his warhorse."},{"id":"263","who":"LADY PERCY","text":"Out, you mad-headed ape!","themes":["honour"],"note":"Kate's affectionate abuse."},{"id":"266","who":"HOTSPUR","text":"this is no world To play with mammets and to tilt with lips:","themes":["war","honour"],"note":"Hotspur sets love aside for war."},{"id":"268","who":"HOTSPUR","text":"for I well believe Thou wilt not utter what thou dost not know;","themes":["counterfeit"],"note":"Hotspur's teasing, cautious trust."},{"id":"278","who":"HAL","text":"I am sworn brother to a leash of drawers;","themes":["misrule","kingship"],"note":"Hal learns the language of the common people."},{"id":"278","who":"HAL","text":"when I am King of England, I shall command all the good lads in Eastcheap.","themes":["kingship","misrule"],"note":"Hal's popularity with the people."},{"id":"322","who":"HAL","text":"I am now of all humours that have showed themselves humours since the old days of goodman Adam","themes":["misrule"],"note":"Hal in a holiday mood."},{"id":"324","who":"HAL","text":"he that kills me some six or seven dozen of Scots at a breakfast, washes his hands, and says to his wife, Fie upon this quiet life! I want work.","themes":["honour","war"],"note":"Hal parodies Hotspur's love of fighting."},{"id":"327","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"A plague of all cowards, I say, and a vengeance too!","themes":["counterfeit","misrule"],"note":"The biggest coward attacks cowardice."},{"id":"330","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"There live not three good men unhang'd in England; and one of them is fat, and grows old:","themes":["misrule","counterfeit"],"note":"Falstaff's self-praise."},{"id":"345","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"I have 'scaped by miracle.","themes":["counterfeit"],"note":"The lie begins."},{"id":"355","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"if I fought not with fifty of them, I am a bunch of radish:","themes":["counterfeit"],"note":"The numbers grow."},{"id":"357","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"Four rogues in buckram let drive at me,—","themes":["counterfeit"],"note":"Two become four."},{"id":"374","who":"HAL","text":"O monstrous! eleven buckram men grown out of two!","themes":["counterfeit"],"note":"Hal sums up the lie."},{"id":"376","who":"HAL","text":"These lies are like the father that begets them, gross as a mountain, open, palpable.","themes":["counterfeit","fathers"],"note":"Falstaff as father of lies."},{"id":"380","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"if reasons were as plentiful as blackberries, I would give no man a reason upon compulsion, I.","themes":["counterfeit","misrule"],"note":"Falstaff dodges with a pun on 'reasons' and 'raisins'."},{"id":"381","who":"HAL","text":"this bed-presser, this horse-back-breaker, this huge hill of flesh,—","themes":["misrule"],"note":"Insults as friendship."},{"id":"385","who":"HAL","text":"What trick, what device, what starting-hole canst thou now find out to hide thee from this open and apparent shame?","themes":["counterfeit"],"note":"Hal springs the trap."},{"id":"387","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"By the Lord, I knew ye as well as he that made ye.","themes":["counterfeit"],"note":"Falstaff's outrageous escape."},{"id":"387","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"the lion will not touch the true Prince.","themes":["kingship","counterfeit"],"note":"Falstaff flatters Hal's royalty."},{"id":"387","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"What, shall we be merry? shall we have a play extempore?","themes":["misrule"],"note":"The tavern play begins."},{"id":"439","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"Shall the son of England prove a thief, and take purses? a question to be ask'd.","themes":["fathers","kingship","misrule"],"note":"Falstaff, as the King, puns on sun and son."},{"id":"441","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"there is virtue in that Falstaff: him keep with, the rest banish.","themes":["misrule","fathers"],"note":"Falstaff pleads his own case."},{"id":"442","who":"HAL","text":"Do thou stand for me, and I'll play my father.","themes":["fathers","kingship"],"note":"Hal takes the King's role."},{"id":"450","who":"HAL","text":"there is a devil haunts thee, in the likeness of an old fat man,—a tun of man is thy companion.","themes":["fathers","misrule"],"note":"Hal, as the King, condemns Falstaff."},{"id":"452","who":"HAL","text":"That villainous abominable misleader of youth, Falstaff, that old white-bearded Satan.","themes":["misrule","fathers"],"note":"The morality-play view of Falstaff."},{"id":"455","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"If sack and sugar be a fault, God help the wicked!","themes":["misrule"],"note":"Falstaff's defence."},{"id":"455","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"banish not him thy Harry's company, banish not him thy Harry's company: banish plump Jack, and banish all the world.","themes":["misrule","fathers"],"note":"Falstaff's great plea."},{"id":"456","who":"HAL","text":"I do, I will.","themes":["misrule","kingship"],"note":"Hal's reply looks ahead to Falstaff's rejection."},{"id":"466","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"never call a true piece of gold a counterfeit:","themes":["counterfeit"],"note":"True and false coin: a key image."},{"id":"469","who":"HAL","text":"Now, my masters, for a true face and good conscience.","themes":["counterfeit"],"note":"Hal is about to lie to the sheriff."},{"id":"479","who":"HAL","text":"The man, I do assure you, is not here;","themes":["counterfeit","kingship"],"note":"Hal protects Falstaff with a lie."},{"id":"494","who":"HAL","text":"O monstrous! but one half-pennyworth of bread to this intolerable deal of sack!","themes":["misrule"],"note":"The tavern bill sums up Falstaff."},{"id":"494","who":"HAL","text":"The money shall be paid back again with advantage.","themes":["honour","kingship"],"note":"Hal begins to repay his debts."},{"id":"503","who":"GLENDOWER","text":"The frame and huge foundation of the Earth Shaked like a coward.","themes":["rebellion"],"note":"Glendower's portents."},{"id":"504","who":"HOTSPUR","text":"if your mother's cat had but kitten'd, though yourself had never been born.","themes":["rebellion"],"note":"Hotspur deflates Glendower."},{"id":"512","who":"GLENDOWER","text":"I can call spirits from the vasty deep.","themes":["rebellion"],"note":"Glendower's claim to magic."},{"id":"513","who":"HOTSPUR","text":"But will they come when you do call for them?","themes":["rebellion","counterfeit"],"note":"Hotspur's famous retort."},{"id":"515","who":"HOTSPUR","text":"tell truth, and shame the Devil.","themes":["counterfeit","honour"],"note":"Hotspur's plain honesty."},{"id":"520","who":"MORTIMER","text":"Th' archdeacon hath divided it Into three limits very equally.","themes":["rebellion"],"note":"The rebels divide the kingdom."},{"id":"522","who":"HOTSPUR","text":"See how this river comes me cranking in,","themes":["rebellion"],"note":"Hotspur fights over the map."},{"id":"534","who":"HOTSPUR","text":"I had rather be a kitten, and cry mew, Than one of these same metre ballet-mongers;","themes":["honour"],"note":"Hotspur despises poetry."},{"id":"536","who":"HOTSPUR","text":"I'll cavil on the ninth part of a hair.","themes":["rebellion","honour"],"note":"Generous in gifts, stubborn in bargains."},{"id":"540","who":"HOTSPUR","text":"O, he's as tedious As a tired horse, a railing wife;","themes":["rebellion"],"note":"Hotspur on Glendower."},{"id":"542","who":"WORCESTER","text":"In faith, my lord, you are too wilful-blunt;","themes":["honour","rebellion"],"note":"Worcester's warning about Hotspur's temper."},{"id":"542","who":"WORCESTER","text":"Loseth men's hearts, and leaves behind a stain","themes":["honour"],"note":"A nobleman's faults lose him support."},{"id":"545","who":"MORTIMER","text":"My wife can speak no English, I no Welsh.","themes":["rebellion","war"],"note":"A divided alliance, and a tender marriage."},{"id":"553","who":"MORTIMER","text":"I understand thy kisses, and thou mine, And that's a feeling disputation:","themes":["war"],"note":"Love without a shared language."},{"id":"565","who":"HOTSPUR","text":"I had rather hear Lady, my brach, howl in Irish.","themes":["honour"],"note":"Hotspur mocks the Welsh song."},{"id":"575","who":"HOTSPUR","text":"Swear me, Kate, like a lady as thou art, A good mouth-filling oath;","themes":["honour"],"note":"Hotspur's love of bold speech."},{"id":"586","who":"KING","text":"But thou dost, in thy passages of life, Make me believe that thou art only mark'd For the hot vengeance and the rod of Heaven To punish my mistreadings.","themes":["fathers","kingship"],"note":"The King sees Hal as God's punishment."},{"id":"587","who":"HAL","text":"Find pardon on my true submission.","themes":["fathers"],"note":"Hal submits to his father."},{"id":"588","who":"KING","text":"Opinion, that did help me to the crown,","themes":["kingship"],"note":"Henry knows power rests on public opinion."},{"id":"588","who":"KING","text":"By being seldom seen, I could not stir But, like a comet, I was wonder'd at;","themes":["kingship"],"note":"Scarcity makes majesty."},{"id":"588","who":"KING","text":"And then I stole all courtesy from Heaven,","themes":["kingship","counterfeit"],"note":"Henry admits his humility was a performance."},{"id":"588","who":"KING","text":"The skipping King, he ambled up and down","themes":["kingship"],"note":"Henry's contempt for Richard."},{"id":"588","who":"KING","text":"He was but as the cuckoo is in June, Heard, not regarded;","themes":["kingship"],"note":"Too common to be noticed."},{"id":"588","who":"KING","text":"And in that very line, Harry, stand'st thou;","themes":["fathers","kingship"],"note":"Hal is another Richard."},{"id":"589","who":"HAL","text":"I shall hereafter, my thrice-gracious lord, Be more myself.","themes":["fathers","kingship"],"note":"Hal's brief promise."},{"id":"590","who":"KING","text":"And even as I was then is Percy now.","themes":["fathers","rebellion"],"note":"Hotspur is the young Bolingbroke."},{"id":"590","who":"KING","text":"this Hotspur, Mars in swathing-clothes,","themes":["honour","war"],"note":"Hotspur as an infant war god."},{"id":"590","who":"KING","text":"Why, Harry, do I tell thee of my foes, Which art my near'st and dearest enemy?","themes":["fathers"],"note":"The King's bitterest line to his son."},{"id":"591","who":"HAL","text":"I will redeem all this on Percy's head,","themes":["honour","fathers"],"note":"Hal's vow."},{"id":"591","who":"HAL","text":"When I will wear a garment all of blood,","themes":["war","honour"],"note":"Redemption through blood."},{"id":"591","who":"HAL","text":"Percy is but my factor, good my lord, T' engross up glorious deeds on my behalf;","themes":["honour"],"note":"Honour as a commodity to be collected."},{"id":"591","who":"HAL","text":"Or I will tear the reckoning from his heart.","themes":["honour","war"],"note":"The account will be settled in blood."},{"id":"592","who":"KING","text":"A hundred thousand rebels die in this.","themes":["fathers","kingship"],"note":"The King is won over."},{"id":"600","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"Company, villainous company, hath been the spoil of me.","themes":["misrule"],"note":"Falstaff blames his friends."},{"id":"606","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"I never see thy face but I think upon hell-fire,","themes":["misrule"],"note":"Bardolph's face as a reminder of death."},{"id":"659","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"Thou see'st I have more flesh than another man; and therefore more frailty.","themes":["misrule","counterfeit"],"note":"Comic self-defence."},{"id":"677","who":"HAL","text":"The land is burning; Percy stands on high; And either they or we must lower lie.","themes":["war","honour"],"note":"Hal leaves the tavern for the war."},{"id":"679","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"O, I could wish this tavern were my drum!","themes":["misrule","war"],"note":"Falstaff would rather fight in the tavern."},{"id":"684","who":"DOUGLAS","text":"Thou art the king of honour:","themes":["honour"],"note":"Douglas's praise of Hotspur."},{"id":"696","who":"HOTSPUR","text":"Sick now! droop now! this sickness doth infect The very life-blood of our enterprise;","themes":["rebellion"],"note":"Northumberland's absence."},{"id":"701","who":"WORCESTER","text":"This absence of your father's draws a curtain,","themes":["rebellion"],"note":"Worcester fears the effect of the absence."},{"id":"702","who":"HOTSPUR","text":"If we, without his help, can make a head To push against the kingdom, with his help We shall o'erturn it topsy-turvy down.","themes":["rebellion","honour"],"note":"Hotspur turns bad news into good."},{"id":"709","who":"HOTSPUR","text":"The nimble-footed madcap Prince of Wales,","themes":["honour","misrule"],"note":"Hotspur still sees Hal as a madcap."},{"id":"710","who":"VERNON","text":"I saw young Harry—with his beaver on,","themes":["honour","kingship"],"note":"Vernon's vision of Hal transformed."},{"id":"710","who":"VERNON","text":"Rise from the ground like feather'd Mercury,","themes":["honour"],"note":"Hal as a god."},{"id":"711","who":"HOTSPUR","text":"Harry and Harry shall, hot horse to horse, Meet, and ne'er part till one drop down a corse.","themes":["honour","war"],"note":"The two Harrys must meet."},{"id":"717","who":"HOTSPUR","text":"Doomsday is near; die all, die merrily.","themes":["war","honour"],"note":"Hotspur's reckless courage."},{"id":"729","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"I have misused the King's press damnably.","themes":["war","misrule"],"note":"Falstaff's corruption as a recruiter."},{"id":"729","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"No eye hath seen such scarecrows.","themes":["war"],"note":"The poor men he leads."},{"id":"738","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"food for powder, food for powder; they'll fill a pit as well as better:","themes":["war"],"note":"Common soldiers as cannon fodder."},{"id":"746","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"To the latter end of a fray and the beginning of a feast Fits a dull fighter and a keen guest.","themes":["war","misrule"],"note":"Falstaff's priorities."},{"id":"772","who":"HOTSPUR","text":"The King is kind; and well we know the King Knows at what time to promise, when to pay.","themes":["kingship","rebellion"],"note":"Hotspur's sarcasm about Henry's promises."},{"id":"772","who":"HOTSPUR","text":"A poor unminded outlaw sneaking home—","themes":["kingship","rebellion"],"note":"Henry's humble return."},{"id":"774","who":"HOTSPUR","text":"Into his title, the which now we find Too indirect for long continuance.","themes":["kingship","rebellion"],"note":"The rebels attack Henry's right to the crown."},{"id":"795","who":"KING","text":"How bloodily the Sun begins to peer Above yon busky hill!","themes":["war"],"note":"An ominous dawn."},{"id":"797","who":"KING","text":"For nothing can seem foul to those that win.","themes":["war","kingship"],"note":"Victory justifies itself."},{"id":"802","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"Rebellion lay in his way, and he found it.","themes":["rebellion","misrule"],"note":"Falstaff's quip at Worcester."},{"id":"804","who":"WORCESTER","text":"As that ungentle gull, the cuckoo-bird, Useth the sparrow;","themes":["rebellion","kingship"],"note":"Henry as an ungrateful cuckoo."},{"id":"805","who":"KING","text":"And never yet did insurrection want Such water-colours to impaint his cause;","themes":["rebellion","counterfeit"],"note":"Rebellion always finds excuses."},{"id":"806","who":"HAL","text":"I do not think a braver gentleman, More active-valiant or more valiant-young,","themes":["honour"],"note":"Hal's generous praise of Hotspur."},{"id":"806","who":"HAL","text":"I have a truant been to chivalry;","themes":["honour"],"note":"Hal admits his past."},{"id":"806","who":"HAL","text":"Try fortune with him in a single fight.","themes":["honour","war"],"note":"Hal offers single combat."},{"id":"812","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"Hal, if thou see me down in the battle, and bestride me, so; 'tis a point of friendship.","themes":["war","misrule"],"note":"Falstaff hopes Hal will protect him."},{"id":"815","who":"HAL","text":"Why, thou owest God a death.","themes":["war"],"note":"Hal's blunt reply."},{"id":"817","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"honour pricks me on. Yea, but how if honour prick me off when I come on?","themes":["honour","war"],"note":"Falstaff questions honour."},{"id":"817","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"What is honour? a word. What is that word, honour? air.","themes":["honour"],"note":"The heart of the catechism."},{"id":"817","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"Who hath it? he that died o' Wednesday.","themes":["honour","war"],"note":"Honour belongs to the dead."},{"id":"817","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"honour is a mere scutcheon:—and so ends my catechism.","themes":["honour"],"note":"Honour as a funeral decoration."},{"id":"823","who":"WORCESTER","text":"For treason is but trusted like the fox,","themes":["rebellion","counterfeit"],"note":"Worcester's reason for deceit."},{"id":"823","who":"WORCESTER","text":"A hare-brain'd Hotspur, govern'd by a spleen:","themes":["honour","rebellion"],"note":"Worcester's view of his nephew."},{"id":"839","who":"VERNON","text":"I never in my life Did hear a challenge urged more modestly,","themes":["honour"],"note":"Hal's modesty."},{"id":"839","who":"VERNON","text":"England did never owe so sweet a hope, So much misconstrued in his wantonness.","themes":["kingship","honour"],"note":"Vernon sees through Hal's reputation."},{"id":"843","who":"HOTSPUR","text":"O gentlemen, the time of life is short! To spend that shortness basely were too long,","themes":["honour","war"],"note":"Hotspur's creed."},{"id":"843","who":"HOTSPUR","text":"An if we live, we live to tread on kings;","themes":["rebellion","honour"],"note":"Rebellion's glory."},{"id":"846","who":"HOTSPUR","text":"Now, Esperance! Percy! and set on.","themes":["war","honour"],"note":"The battle cry."},{"id":"862","who":"HOTSPUR","text":"The King hath many marching in his coats.","themes":["counterfeit","kingship"],"note":"The King's decoys."},{"id":"867","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"Sir Walter Blunt: there's honour for you! here's no vanity!","themes":["honour","war"],"note":"Falstaff looks at the corpse."},{"id":"867","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"I have led my ragamuffins where they are peppered:","themes":["war"],"note":"Falstaff's men are dead."},{"id":"876","who":"HAL","text":"What, is't a time to jest and dally now?","themes":["war","misrule"],"note":"Hal has no time for Falstaff's jokes."},{"id":"878","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"I like not such grinning honour as Sir Walter hath: give me life;","themes":["honour","war"],"note":"Falstaff chooses life."},{"id":"887","who":"HAL","text":"And God forbid, a shallow scratch should drive The Prince of Wales from such a field as this,","themes":["honour","war"],"note":"Hal refuses to leave the battle."},{"id":"899","who":"HAL","text":"It is the Prince of Wales that threatens thee; Who never promiseth but he means to pay.","themes":["honour","kingship"],"note":"Hal keeps his promises."},{"id":"902","who":"KING","text":"Thou hast redeem'd thy lost opinion;","themes":["fathers","honour"],"note":"The King's approval at last."},{"id":"903","who":"HAL","text":"O God, they did me too much injury That ever said I hearken'd for your death!","themes":["fathers"],"note":"Hal answers the rumours."},{"id":"910","who":"HAL","text":"Two stars keep not their motion in one sphere;","themes":["honour","kingship"],"note":"England cannot hold both Harrys."},{"id":"912","who":"HAL","text":"And all the budding honours on thy crest I'll crop, to make a garland for my head.","themes":["honour"],"note":"Hal will take Hotspur's honour."},{"id":"918","who":"HOTSPUR","text":"O Harry, thou hast robb'd me of my youth!","themes":["honour"],"note":"Hotspur's dying lament."},{"id":"918","who":"HOTSPUR","text":"I better brook the loss of brittle life Than those proud titles thou hast won of me;","themes":["honour"],"note":"Honour matters more than life to Hotspur."},{"id":"918","who":"HOTSPUR","text":"But thoughts the slave of life, and life Time's fool,","themes":["honour"],"note":"A philosophical last thought."},{"id":"920","who":"HAL","text":"For worms, brave Percy: fare thee well, great heart!","themes":["honour","war"],"note":"Hal completes Hotspur's last sentence."},{"id":"920","who":"HAL","text":"When that this body did contain a spirit, A kingdom for it was too small a bound; But now two paces of the vilest earth Is room enough.","themes":["honour","war"],"note":"Death reduces greatness."},{"id":"922","who":"HAL","text":"I could have better spared a better man:","themes":["misrule","fathers"],"note":"Hal's mixed epitaph for Falstaff."},{"id":"924","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"to die, is to be a counterfeit;","themes":["counterfeit"],"note":"Falstaff's logic of faking death."},{"id":"924","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"The better part of valour is discretion;","themes":["honour","counterfeit"],"note":"Falstaff's famous excuse."},{"id":"930","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"if I be not Jack Falstaff, then am I a Jack.","themes":["counterfeit"],"note":"Falstaff returns from the dead."},{"id":"932","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"Lord, Lord, how this world is given to lying!","themes":["counterfeit"],"note":"Falstaff complains of liars."},{"id":"932","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"fought a long hour by Shrewsbury clock.","themes":["counterfeit"],"note":"Falstaff's precise lie."},{"id":"934","who":"HAL","text":"For my part, if a lie may do thee grace, I'll gild it with the happiest terms I have.","themes":["counterfeit","honour"],"note":"Hal lets Falstaff take the credit."},{"id":"938","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"If I do grow great, I'll grow less;","themes":["misrule"],"note":"Another promise to reform."},{"id":"942","who":"KING","text":"Thus ever did rebellion find rebuke.","themes":["rebellion","kingship"],"note":"The King's victory."},{"id":"949","who":"HAL","text":"Up to his pleasure, ransomless and free:","themes":["honour","kingship"],"note":"Hal's generosity to Douglas."},{"id":"950","who":"KING","text":"Rebellion in this land shall lose his sway,","themes":["rebellion","kingship"],"note":"The work is not finished."}]};
const EX={"intro":["King Henry IV took the crown from Richard II with the help of the Percy family, and his reign has been troubled ever since. He longs to lead a crusade to Jerusalem, but war in Wales and the North keeps him at home, and his son and heir, Prince Hal, spends his days in the taverns of Eastcheap with the fat old knight Sir John Falstaff and a gang of thieves. The King wishes he had a son like Harry Percy, called Hotspur, the brave young lord who has just defeated the Scots.","When the King demands Hotspur's prisoners and refuses to ransom his brother-in-law Mortimer, the Percys rebel. Hotspur's uncle Worcester builds an alliance with the Scots under Douglas, the Archbishop of York, and the Welsh leader Glendower. Meanwhile Hal and Poins trick Falstaff into robbing travellers and then rob him in turn, enjoying his outrageous lies. In a tavern play Hal rehearses his interview with his father, and when the King tells him he is as worthless as Richard, Hal vows to redeem himself by defeating Hotspur.","At Shrewsbury the rebels fight without Northumberland or Glendower, and Worcester hides the King's offer of pardon from Hotspur. Hal saves his father's life, kills Hotspur in single combat and generously lets Falstaff, who has faked his own death, claim the kill. The battle is won, but the rebellion is not over: the play ends with the King dividing his army to face the rest of his enemies, and the story continues in Henry IV, Part 2."],"keyfacts":[["Full title","The First Part of King Henry the Fourth, with the Life and Death of Henry Surnamed Hotspur (title of the first quarto, 1598); usually called Henry IV, Part 1"],["Written","About 1596 to 1597; printed in quarto in 1598 and many times after, then in the First Folio of 1623 among the Histories"],["Genre","History (placed among the First Folio's Histories, after Richard II); it mixes political history with comic scenes and is the second play of the second tetralogy (Richard II, Henry IV Parts 1 and 2, Henry V)"],["Setting","England and Wales, 1402 to 1403: the royal court in London, the Boar's Head tavern in Eastcheap, Gad's Hill, Warkworth, Bangor, York and the battlefield at Shrewsbury"],["Protagonist","Prince Hal, with King Henry"],["Antagonists","Hotspur and the Percy rebels; in a different way Falstaff, who pulls Hal away from duty"],["Inciting incident","The quarrel over Hotspur's prisoners and the King's refusal to ransom Mortimer (1.3)"],["Turning point","Hal's interview with his father and his vow to redeem himself on Percy's head (3.2)"],["Climax","Hal kills Hotspur in single combat at Shrewsbury (5.4)"],["Resolution","The King's victory, Worcester's execution and the freeing of Douglas, with the rest of the rebellion still to be fought (5.5)"],["Time span","About ten months historically (Holmedon, September 1402, to Shrewsbury, July 1403), compressed into a few weeks on stage"],["Major conflicts","The King against the Percys; Hal against Hotspur; Hal's duty against his tavern life; honour against survival"],["Themes","Honour, kingship and legitimacy, fathers and sons, rebellion and order, tavern and misrule, lies and counterfeits, war and its cost"]],"timeline":[{"s":"a1s1","t":"The King postpones his crusade because of war in Wales and the North","id":2},{"s":"a1s1","t":"The King wishes Hotspur were his son","id":8},{"s":"a1s2","t":"Poins plans the Gad's Hill robbery","id":55},{"s":"a1s2","t":"Hal reveals his plan to reform: 'I know you all'","id":80},{"s":"a1s3","t":"Hotspur refuses to hand over his prisoners","id":92},{"s":"a1s3","t":"The King refuses to ransom Mortimer","id":94},{"s":"a1s3","t":"Worcester reveals the plot to rebel","id":130},{"s":"a2s2","t":"Falstaff and the gang rob the travellers","id":226},{"s":"a2s2","t":"Hal and Poins rob the robbers","id":237},{"s":"a2s3","t":"Hotspur leaves Kate for the war","id":268},{"s":"a2s4","t":"Falstaff's men in buckram multiply","id":357},{"s":"a2s4","t":"Hal exposes Falstaff's lies","id":385},{"s":"a2s4","t":"The tavern play: 'banish plump Jack'","id":455},{"s":"a2s4","t":"Hal: 'I do, I will'","id":456},{"s":"a3s1","t":"The rebels divide England on a map","id":520},{"s":"a3s1","t":"Hotspur mocks Glendower","id":513},{"s":"a3s2","t":"The King compares Hal to Richard II","id":588},{"s":"a3s2","t":"Hal vows to redeem himself on Percy's head","id":591},{"s":"a3s3","t":"Hal gets Falstaff a command","id":669},{"s":"a4s1","t":"Northumberland is sick and will not come","id":696},{"s":"a4s1","t":"Vernon describes Hal in armour","id":710},{"s":"a4s2","t":"Falstaff's ragged soldiers: food for powder","id":738},{"s":"a4s3","t":"Blunt offers the rebels pardon","id":771},{"s":"a5s1","t":"Hal offers to fight Hotspur in single combat","id":806},{"s":"a5s1","t":"Falstaff's catechism: honour is air","id":817},{"s":"a5s2","t":"Worcester hides the King's offer","id":823},{"s":"a5s3","t":"Douglas kills Blunt, dressed as the King","id":854},{"s":"a5s4","t":"Hal saves his father from Douglas","id":899},{"s":"a5s4","t":"Hal kills Hotspur","id":920},{"s":"a5s4","t":"Falstaff claims he killed Percy","id":930}],"irony":{"8":"The King wishes he could swap Hal for Hotspur; by the end Hal will kill the son he envies.","59":"Hal refuses to rob, then agrees, then reveals his whole tavern life is a performance.","80":"Hal tells only the audience his plan. Everyone on stage, including his father, believes he is a wastrel.","114":"Hotspur wants honour 'without corrival'; Hal will take all of it from him.","122":"Hotspur thinks the King does not love Hal and would be glad if he died; Hal will save the King's life.","198":"Falstaff threatens to 'peach' on the others; he will be the one exposed.","245":"Hotspur is sure 'out of this nettle, danger, we pluck this flower, safety'; the rebellion will end in his death.","324":"Hal mocks Hotspur for killing 'some fourteen' Scots before breakfast; Hal will soon kill Hotspur himself.","387":"Falstaff says the lion will not touch the true prince, inventing a royal instinct to excuse his cowardice.","456":"'I do, I will' sounds like a joke inside the play, but it is a promise Hal keeps at the end of Part 2.","588":"The King compares Hal to Richard while Hal has already told the audience he plans exactly the 'seldom seen' effect his father recommends.","590":"The King believes Hal might fight for Percy; within the play Hal will save his father's life.","696":"Northumberland's 'inward sickness' leaves his son to die without him.","710":"The rebels' own man gives the most glowing picture of the prince they mean to defeat.","817":"Falstaff says honour cannot set a leg; in the battle he will steal the honour of killing Hotspur.","823":"Worcester hides the offer to save himself; it leads to his execution.","863":"Douglas is fooled by the King's decoys, the kind of 'counterfeit' the King learnt from his own political career."},"devices":{"sun":"Sun, clouds and stars","money":"Money, debt and coins","body":"Bodies, food and fat","beasts":"Beasts and birds","play":"Playing and theatre","time":"Time and clocks"},"dev":{"2":[["body","England as a mother whose lips are wet with her children's blood.","Shall daub her lips with her own children's blood;"]],"16":[["time","Hal turns clock time into food and drink: hours as cups of sack, minutes as capons.","unless hours were cups of sack, and minutes capons"]],"21":[["sun","Thieves as 'minions of the Moon', who work by night rather than by the sun.","minions of the Moon"]],"80":[["sun","Hal as the sun hidden by 'base contagious clouds', waiting to break through.","Yet herein will I imitate the Sun,"],["money","Reformation as paying 'the debt I never promised'.","And pay the debt I never promised,"]],"92":[["beasts","The courtier as a 'popinjay', a chattering parrot.","To be so pester'd with a popinjay,"]],"95":[["body","The river Severn hides its 'crisp head' in fear at the fighters.","And hid his crisp head in the hollow bank"]],"110":[["beasts","Richard as a rose, Bolingbroke as a thorn and a canker.","To put down Richard, that sweet lovely rose,"]],"112":[["beasts","Hunting a lion against startling a hare.","To rouse a lion than to start a hare!"]],"114":[["sun","Honour hangs on the pale-faced moon or lies drowned in the deep.","To pluck bright honour from the pale-faced Moon;"]],"127":[["beasts","Bolingbroke as a 'fawning greyhound'.","This fawning greyhound then did proffer me!"]],"240":[["body","Falstaff's sweat as fat basting the earth.","And lards the lean earth as he walks along:"]],"245":[["sun","Danger as a nettle from which safety is plucked as a flower.","out of this nettle, danger, we pluck this flower, safety."]],"329":[["sun","Falstaff as butter melting in the sun.","Didst thou never see Titan kiss a dish of butter?"]],"381":[["body","Falstaff as a 'huge hill of flesh'.","this huge hill of flesh"]],"429":[["play","Chair, dagger and cushion become throne, sceptre and crown.","this chair shall be my state, this dagger my sceptre, and this cushion my crown."]],"439":[["sun","The 'son of England' and the 'Sun of heaven': a pun on royalty.","Shall the blessed Sun of heaven prove a micher, and eat blackberries?"]],"450":[["body","Falstaff as a container: 'trunk of humours', 'bombard of sack', 'Manningtree ox'.","that huge bombard of sack"]],"466":[["money","True gold and counterfeit coin: is Hal genuine?","never call a true piece of gold a counterfeit:"]],"588":[["sun","The King as a comet, wondered at because rarely seen.","But, like a comet, I was wonder'd at;"],["beasts","Richard as the cuckoo in June, heard but ignored.","He was but as the cuckoo is in June,"]],"591":[["money","Percy as Hal's 'factor', buying up honour that Hal will claim.","Percy is but my factor, good my lord,"]],"606":[["body","Bardolph's red face as a memento mori and a bonfire.","O, thou art a perpetual triumph, an everlasting bonfire-light!"]],"710":[["beasts","Hal's men plumed like ostriches and eagles.","All plumed like estridges that with the wind"]],"711":[["beasts","The enemy as sacrifices dressed for the altar.","They come like sacrifices in their trim,"]],"804":[["beasts","Henry as the cuckoo who takes over the sparrow's nest.","As that ungentle gull, the cuckoo-bird,"]],"805":[["play","Rebels paint their cause with 'water-colours'.","Such water-colours to impaint his cause;"]],"817":[["money","Honour as a 'trim reckoning', an account that comes to nothing.","A trim reckoning!"]],"843":[["time","Life measured on a 'dial's point', a clock hand.","If life did ride upon a dial's point,"]],"910":[["sun","Two stars cannot share one sphere.","Two stars keep not their motion in one sphere;"]],"918":[["time","Life as 'Time's fool'.","and life Time's fool,"]],"932":[["time","Falstaff's lie measured by 'Shrewsbury clock'.","fought a long hour by Shrewsbury clock."]]},"stage":{"palace":"The King's palace in London","room":"The Prince's lodgings in London","inn":"An inn-yard in Rochester before dawn","road":"The open road, at Gad's Hill and near Coventry","castle":"Warkworth Castle, the Percys' home in the North","tavern":"The Boar's Head tavern in Eastcheap","house":"The Archdeacon's house at Bangor in Wales","camp":"The rebel camp near Shrewsbury","tent":"The King's camp near Shrewsbury","church":"The Archbishop's palace in York","battle":"The battlefield at Shrewsbury"},"stagerules":[["archbishop","church"],["palace","palace"],["prince's","room"],["inn-yard","inn"],["road","road"],["castle","castle"],["tavern","tavern"],["archdeacon","house"],["king's camp","tent"],["plain|field","battle"],["camp","camp"]],"place":{"a1s1":"The palace, London","a1s2":"The Prince's lodgings, London","a1s3":"The palace, London","a2s1":"An inn-yard at Rochester","a2s2":"The road at Gad's Hill","a2s3":"Warkworth Castle","a2s4":"The Boar's Head, Eastcheap","a3s1":"The Archdeacon's house, Bangor","a3s2":"The palace, London","a3s3":"The Boar's Head, Eastcheap","a4s1":"The rebel camp near Shrewsbury","a4s2":"A road near Coventry","a4s3":"The rebel camp near Shrewsbury","a4s4":"The Archbishop's palace, York","a5s1":"The King's camp near Shrewsbury","a5s2":"The rebel camp","a5s3":"The battlefield","a5s4":"Another part of the battlefield","a5s5":"The battlefield, after the victory"},"arc":{"Hal's honour":[1,1,1,1,1,2,2,2,5,4,6,4,4,5,7,7,7,10,10],"Hotspur's honour":[8,8,9,9,9,8,8,8,7,7,7,7,7,6,6,6,6,0,0]},"arcnote":"Hal's honour starts at almost nothing: his father thinks him a disgrace and he spends his time robbing travellers in jest. It barely moves through Acts 1 and 2, though the audience knows from 'I know you all' that he is waiting. It jumps in 3.2 when he vows to redeem himself, rises as Vernon describes him in arms and he offers single combat, and peaks in 5.4 when he saves his father and kills Hotspur. Hotspur's honour begins high with Holmedon and peaks as he plans the rebellion, then slips as his temper, his quarrel with Glendower and his father's absence weaken the cause. It falls to nothing when Hal takes his 'proud titles' and Falstaff carries off his body.","iago":[["a1s2","Hal agrees to Poins's plan to rob the robbers at Gad's Hill."],["a1s2","Alone, Hal promises to throw off his loose behaviour like the sun breaking through clouds."],["a2s2","Hal and Poins rob Falstaff's gang, who run away."],["a2s4","Hal exposes Falstaff's buckram lies."],["a2s4","In the tavern play Hal plays his father and condemns Falstaff: 'I do, I will.'"],["a2s4","Hal protects Falstaff from the sheriff and promises to repay the stolen money."],["a3s2","The King compares Hal to Richard II and fears he will fight for Percy."],["a3s2","Hal vows to redeem himself on Percy's head and is given a command."],["a3s3","Hal repays the robbery money and gets Falstaff a company of foot."],["a4s1","Vernon reports Hal armed and vaulting onto his horse like Mercury."],["a4s2","Hal rebukes Falstaff and hurries on to Shrewsbury."],["a5s1","Hal praises Hotspur and offers to fight him in single combat."],["a5s3","Hal throws the sack bottle at Falstaff: no time to jest."],["a5s4","Wounded, Hal refuses to leave the field."],["a5s4","Hal saves his father from Douglas and is told he has redeemed himself."],["a5s4","Hal kills Hotspur and honours him with his favours."],["a5s4","Hal lets Falstaff claim the kill."],["a5s5","Hal frees Douglas without ransom."]],"rel":{"1":[["KING","HAL","hate"],["KING","HOTSPUR","trust"],["HOTSPUR","KING","hate"],["WORCESTER","KING","deceive"],["HAL","FALSTAFF","love"],["FALSTAFF","HAL","love"],["POINS","FALSTAFF","deceive"],["HAL","POINS","trust"],["HOTSPUR","MORTIMER","love"]],"2":[["HAL","FALSTAFF","deceive"],["HOTSPUR","LADY PERCY","love"],["LADY PERCY","HOTSPUR","love"],["HOTSPUR","HAL","hate"],["FALSTAFF","HAL","love"],["HAL","HOSTESS","trust"]],"3":[["HOTSPUR","GLENDOWER","hate"],["MORTIMER","GLENDOWER","trust"],["WORCESTER","HOTSPUR","trust"],["KING","HAL","trust"],["HAL","KING","love"],["FALSTAFF","HOSTESS","deceive"]],"4":[["HOTSPUR","DOUGLAS","love"],["VERNON","HAL","trust"],["HOTSPUR","NORTHUMBERLAND","trust"],["BLUNT","HOTSPUR","trust"],["HOTSPUR","KING","hate"]],"5":[["WORCESTER","HOTSPUR","deceive"],["HAL","HOTSPUR","hate"],["HAL","KING","love"],["KING","HAL","love"],["FALSTAFF","HAL","deceive"],["DOUGLAS","KING","hate"],["HAL","DOUGLAS","trust"]]},"context":[["Date","Written about 1596 to 1597, straight after Richard II. It was a huge success: the first quarto of 1598 was read to pieces, and it was reprinted more often than any other Shakespeare play in his lifetime."],["Sources","Shakespeare's main source was Raphael Holinshed's Chronicles (1587), with Samuel Daniel's poem on the civil wars and an anonymous old play, The Famous Victories of Henry V, which had a wild prince and his tavern friends."],["The real history","The battle of Holmedon was fought in September 1402 and Shrewsbury in July 1403. Shakespeare makes Hotspur Hal's age; in fact he was older than the King. He also merges two Edmund Mortimers into one."],["Richard II","The play continues the story of Richard II, in which Bolingbroke, helped by Northumberland, deposed Richard and had him murdered. Henry's guilt, and the Percys' sense that he owes them, come from that play."],["The Henriad","1 Henry IV is the second of four plays, with Richard II, 2 Henry IV and Henry V, that follow the crown from Richard to Hal's triumph as Henry V. Critics call them the Henriad or second tetralogy."],["Falstaff and Oldcastle","Falstaff was first called Sir John Oldcastle, after a real Lollard martyr. Oldcastle's descendant Lord Cobham objected, and the name was changed. Traces survive in the text, such as 'my old lad of the castle'."],["Holiday and misrule","Elizabethan holidays had a Lord of Misrule who turned order upside down for a day. Falstaff plays this role, and Hal's 'If all the year were playing holidays' sets work against play."],["The morality play","Hal's tavern insults call Falstaff 'that reverend Vice' and 'that grey Iniquity', characters who tempted the young hero in older morality plays. Hal is cast as Youth choosing between vice and virtue."],["Wales and the North","Glendower's Wales and the Percys' North were frontier regions that resisted London. Lady Mortimer's Welsh song is one of the few moments in Shakespeare where another language is heard on stage."],["Soldiers and the press","Captains conscripted soldiers under the 'King's press' and many took bribes to release those who could pay. Falstaff's ragged company reflects real abuses in the 1590s, when England was raising troops for Ireland."],["Staging","Falstaff has been played by many great comic actors, and the tavern play, the buckram story and the honour catechism are famous set pieces. Productions often stage Parts 1 and 2 together."]],"critics":{"honour":[["Harold Bloom, 'Shakespeare: The Invention of the Human' (1998)","Paraphrased: Bloom treated Falstaff as the play's genius, whose wit exposes honour and power as hollow, and argued that his vitality makes him greater than the history around him."],["William Empson, 'Falstaff' in 'Essays on Shakespeare' (1986)","Paraphrased: Empson took Falstaff seriously as a man of courage and intelligence who sees through the code of honour that sends men like Blunt to their deaths."]],"kingship":[["Stephen Greenblatt, 'Invisible Bullets' (1985)","Paraphrased: Greenblatt argued that Hal is a calculating politician who studies the people he will rule; the play lets us see power's tricks, yet in the end confirms that power."],["E. M. W. Tillyard, 'Shakespeare's History Plays' (1944)","Paraphrased: Tillyard saw the play as part of a larger story of order restored, with Hal as the ideal prince who learns to balance the extremes of Hotspur and Falstaff."]],"fathers":[["Ernst Kris, 'Prince Hal's Conflict' (1948)","Paraphrased: Kris read the play psychologically: Hal is torn between his real father, a usurper, and Falstaff as a substitute father, and his rejection of Falstaff resolves his guilt."],["Valerie Traub, 'Prince Hal's Falstaff' (1989)","Paraphrased: Traub linked Falstaff's huge, feminised body to the world Hal must leave behind to become a man and a king."]],"misrule":[["C. L. Barber, 'Shakespeare's Festive Comedy' (1959)","Paraphrased: Barber saw Falstaff as a Lord of Misrule whose holiday licence lets the play mock authority, until Hal ends the holiday and returns to rule."],["Mikhail Bakhtin's ideas of carnival, applied by many critics","Paraphrased: critics using Bakhtin see the tavern scenes as carnival, where the body, food and laughter turn the official world upside down."]],"counterfeit":[["Jonathan Bate and Eric Rasmussen, introduction to the RSC edition (2007)","Paraphrased: they noted how often the play deals in counterfeits, from the King's decoys to Falstaff's fake death, and how Hal's own reformation is a kind of performance."],["James L. Calderwood, 'Metadrama in Shakespeare's Henriad' (1979)","Paraphrased: Calderwood argued that the plays are about language and acting, and that Hal's skill at playing roles is what makes him a successful king."]],"rebellion":[["Phyllis Rackin, 'Stages of History' (1990)","Paraphrased: Rackin argued that the history plays show the past as a contest between versions of history, and that Henry's lack of a clear right to the crown keeps rebellion alive."],["Lily B. Campbell, 'Shakespeare's Histories' (1947)","Paraphrased: Campbell read the play against Elizabethan fears of rebellion, and saw it as warning that rebels, however brave, bring ruin."]]},"symbols":{"Honour":"\\bhonou?rs?\\b|\\bhonou?rable\\b","Sack":"\\bsack\\b","Crown":"\\bcrown\\w*|\\bking\\b","Time":"\\btime\\b|\\bhours?\\b|\\bclock\\b","Debt":"\\bdebt\\w*|\\bpay\\w*|\\breckoning\\b","Percy":"\\bpercy\\b|\\bhotspur\\b"},"symbols2":[["The sun","The image of kingship. Hal promises to 'imitate the Sun' and break through the clouds; Falstaff puns on the 'son of England'; the battle opens with a bloody sunrise."],["Sack","Falstaff's sherry stands for appetite, pleasure and misrule: a halfpenny of bread to two gallons of sack. At Shrewsbury he carries a bottle in his pistol case."],["The crown","Henry's crown is stolen, and the play keeps asking who deserves it. In the tavern it becomes a cushion; on the field the King's coats are worn by decoys."],["Time and debt","Hal will 'redeem' time and pay 'the debt I never promised'. Falstaff owes everyone money, and the King thinks he is always in the Percys' debt."],["The map","The rebels divide England into three before winning it, a picture of a kingdom torn apart."],["Counterfeit coins","'Never call a true piece of gold a counterfeit': the question of what is genuine runs from Hal's reformation to the King's decoys and Falstaff's fake death."],["Buckram","The cheap cloth of Hal's disguise, multiplied in Falstaff's lie into eleven imaginary men."],["Hotspur's body","Fought over and honoured, then carried off on Falstaff's back as a prize: the end of Hotspur's honour."]],"solo":{"80":1,"192":1,"245":1,"729":1,"817":1,"867":1,"878":1,"924":1,"938":1},"why":["Opens the play with a king worn out by the war that made him king.","Introduces civil war as self-wounding, a theme of the whole play.","Shows how much the King admires Hotspur.","Sets up the King's poor opinion of Hal.","The changeling wish creates the play's central pairing.","Hal's question shows Falstaff lives outside time; Hal will later 'redeem' it.","Falstaff disguises crime in poetry.","Hal reminds Falstaff that thieves end on the gallows.","Falstaff hopes Hal's reign will favour him.","Falstaff's comic blame of Hal reverses who corrupts whom.","Shows Falstaff's endless promises to reform.","Falstaff borrows religious language to excuse theft.","A proverb that hints at Falstaff's damnation.","Hal plays at being wild.","Poins sets up the buckram comedy.","The opening of Hal's crucial soliloquy.","The sun image links Hal to kingship.","Holiday is only valuable because it is rare.","Hal plans his reformation as a display.","The couplet sums up Hal's journey.","The King decides to rule by fear.","The King's dismissal of Worcester starts the rebellion.","Hotspur's contempt for the court.","The 'popinjay' shows Hotspur's sharp tongue.","Hotspur mocks a man who fears war.","The King's political reason for refusing the ransom.","Hotspur's heroic picture of Mortimer.","Hotspur refuses to call Henry king.","The Percys share the guilt of Richard's death.","The rose and the canker: Richard and Henry.","The rebels' grievance: ingratitude.","Hotspur too talks of redeeming honour.","Danger excites Hotspur.","Hotspur's most famous lines on honour.","Honour as a drowned woman to be rescued.","Hotspur will not share honour.","Worcester sees Hotspur's imagination running away with him.","Hotspur's comic, childish fury.","Hotspur's contempt for Hal.","Henry as a schemer.","Henry's past flattery remembered.","Worcester's pun on 'head'.","Unpayable debt drives the Percys.","War as sport for Hotspur.","The great prey on the commonwealth.","Thieves think themselves invisible.","Falstaff's real love for Hal.","Honour among thieves, comically.","Comic hypocrisy from a fat old man.","Hal's comic picture of Falstaff.","Hotspur's optimism about danger.","Hotspur despises caution.","War has taken over Hotspur's sleep.","Kate asks for trust.","Hotspur loves his horse more than his bed.","Kate's affectionate insult.","Hotspur sets love aside for war.","Hotspur's limited trust in his wife.","Hal learns the language of the people.","Hal's popularity, useful to a king.","Hal in holiday mood.","Hal parodies Hotspur.","The biggest coward attacks cowardice.","Falstaff's self-praise.","The lie begins.","The numbers grow.","Two become four.","Hal sums up the lie.","Falstaff as father of lies.","Falstaff dodges the question.","Hal's insults are part of the game.","Hal springs the trap.","Falstaff's outrageous escape.","Falstaff flatters Hal's royalty.","The tavern play begins.","Falstaff's pun on son and sun.","Falstaff pleads his own case.","Hal takes his father's role.","Hal condemns Falstaff, as the King.","The morality-play view of Falstaff.","Falstaff's defence of pleasure.","Falstaff's great plea.","Hal's reply looks to the future.","Is Hal true gold or counterfeit?","Hal is about to lie.","Hal protects Falstaff.","The bill sums up Falstaff.","Hal begins to repay debts.","Glendower's portents.","Hotspur deflates Glendower.","Glendower's magic.","Hotspur's famous retort.","Hotspur's blunt honesty.","The rebels divide the kingdom.","Hotspur fights over the map.","Hotspur despises poetry.","Generous in gifts, stubborn in bargains.","Hotspur's impatience with Glendower.","Worcester names Hotspur's fault.","A nobleman's faults lose him support.","A divided alliance and a tender marriage.","Love without words.","Hotspur mocks the Welsh song.","Hotspur loves bold speech.","The King's guilt.","Hal submits.","Henry knows power rests on opinion.","Scarcity makes majesty.","Henry admits his humility was acted.","Henry's contempt for Richard.","Too common to be noticed.","Hal is another Richard.","Hal's short promise.","Hotspur is the young Bolingbroke.","Hotspur as an infant war god.","The King's bitterest line.","Hal's vow.","Redemption through blood.","Honour as a commodity.","The account settled in blood.","The King is won over.","Falstaff blames his company.","Bardolph's face as memento mori.","Comic self-defence.","Hal leaves for war.","Falstaff prefers the tavern.","Douglas's praise of Hotspur.","Northumberland's absence.","Worcester's fear.","Hotspur turns bad news into good.","Hotspur still sees Hal as a madcap.","Hal transformed.","Hal as a god.","The two Harrys must meet.","Hotspur's reckless courage.","Falstaff's corruption.","The poor men he leads.","Common soldiers as cannon fodder.","Falstaff's priorities.","Hotspur's sarcasm.","Henry's humble return.","The attack on Henry's title.","An ominous dawn.","Victory justifies itself.","Falstaff's quip.","Henry as a cuckoo.","Rebels always find excuses.","Hal's generosity.","Hal admits his past.","Hal offers single combat.","Falstaff asks for protection.","Hal's blunt reply.","Falstaff questions honour.","The heart of the catechism.","Honour belongs to the dead.","Honour as decoration.","Worcester's reason for deceit.","Worcester's view of Hotspur.","Hal's modesty.","Vernon sees through Hal's reputation.","Hotspur's creed.","Rebellion's glory.","The battle cry.","The King's decoys.","Falstaff and the corpse.","Falstaff's men are dead.","Hal has no time for jokes.","Falstaff chooses life.","Hal refuses to leave the field.","Hal keeps his promises.","The King's approval.","Hal answers the rumours.","England cannot hold both Harrys.","Hal will take Hotspur's honour.","Hotspur's dying lament.","Honour above life.","A philosophical end.","Hal completes Hotspur's sentence.","Death reduces greatness.","Hal's mixed epitaph for Falstaff.","Falstaff's logic.","Falstaff's famous excuse.","Falstaff rises.","Falstaff complains of liars.","Falstaff's precise lie.","Hal lets Falstaff take the credit.","Another promise to reform.","The King's victory.","Hal's generosity to Douglas.","The work is not finished."],"insults":[{"id":"16","f":"Thou art so fat-witted","who":"Hal","n":"Hal's first words to Falstaff are a joke about his fat."},{"id":"57","f":"You will, chops?","who":"Poins","n":"Poins mocks Falstaff's fat cheeks."},{"id":"92","f":"popinjay","who":"Hotspur","n":"Hotspur calls the courtier a parrot."},{"id":"101","f":"ingrate and canker'd Bolingbroke","who":"Hotspur","n":"Hotspur insults the King by his old name."},{"id":"124","f":"wasp-stung and impatient fool","who":"Northumberland","n":"A father loses patience with his son."},{"id":"125","f":"vile politician, Bolingbroke","who":"Hotspur","n":"To Hotspur, Henry is a schemer."},{"id":"245","f":"frosty-spirited rogue","who":"Hotspur","n":"Hotspur despises a cautious ally."},{"id":"263","f":"Out, you mad-headed ape!","who":"Lady Percy","n":"Kate's loving abuse of her husband."},{"id":"265","f":"you paraquito","who":"Lady Percy","n":"Kate calls Hotspur a little parrot."},{"id":"331","f":"How now, wool-sack?","who":"Hal","n":"Hal greets Falstaff with a fat joke."},{"id":"376","f":"thou whoreson, obscene greasy tallow-keech","who":"Hal","n":"The tavern's most inventive insults."},{"id":"382","f":"you starveling, you eel-skin, you dried neat's-tongue","who":"Falstaff","n":"Falstaff answers with thin jokes."},{"id":"450","f":"that bolting-hutch of beastliness","who":"Hal","n":"Hal, as the King, piles up insults."},{"id":"452","f":"that old white-bearded Satan","who":"Hal","n":"Hal names Falstaff the devil."},{"id":"604","f":"the Knight of the Burning Lamp","who":"Falstaff","n":"Falstaff mocks Bardolph's red face."},{"id":"638","f":"no more faith in thee than in a stew'd prune","who":"Falstaff","n":"Falstaff insults the Hostess."},{"id":"658","f":"thou whoreson, impudent, emboss'd rascal","who":"Hal","n":"Hal exposes Falstaff's false accusation."},{"id":"731","f":"how now, quilt!","who":"Hal","n":"Hal teases Falstaff on the road."},{"id":"759","f":"You speak it out of fear and cold heart.","who":"Douglas","n":"Douglas accuses Vernon of cowardice."},{"id":"803","f":"Peace, chewet, peace!","who":"Hal","n":"Hal silences Falstaff in front of the enemy."},{"id":"899","f":"Hold up thy head, vile Scot","who":"Hal","n":"Hal challenges Douglas."},{"id":"861","f":"A fool go with thy soul","who":"Douglas","n":"Douglas curses Blunt's ghost."}],"pronouns":[{"id":"16","f":"Thou art so fat-witted","n":"Hal and Falstaff use 'thou', the language of intimacy and jokes."},{"id":"87","f":"your presence is too bold","n":"The King switches to cold 'you' as he dismisses Worcester."},{"id":"263","f":"I'll know your business, Harry","n":"Kate uses 'you' when she is serious."},{"id":"266","f":"I love thee not","n":"Hotspur uses 'thee' even as he teases her."},{"id":"586","f":"But thou dost, in thy passages of life","n":"The King uses 'thou' to his son, as a father."},{"id":"587","f":"So please your Majesty","n":"Hal uses 'your Majesty' to his father: formal and submissive."},{"id":"910","f":"and think not, Percy,","n":"Hal addresses Hotspur directly, as an equal and a rival."},{"id":"896","f":"I will assay thee; so, defend thyself.","n":"The King uses 'thee' to an enemy in combat."}],"predict":{"a1s1":["The King sets out for Jerusalem","The crusade is postponed by rebellion"],"a1s2":["Hal refuses to join the robbery","Hal agrees to rob the robbers"],"a1s3":["Hotspur hands over the prisoners","The Percys plan a rebellion"],"a2s1":["The carriers catch the thieves","Gadshill learns where the money is"],"a2s2":["Falstaff fights off the attackers","Falstaff runs away"],"a2s3":["Hotspur tells Kate his plans","He teases her and leaves"],"a2s4":["Falstaff admits his cowardice","He invents a new excuse"],"a3s1":["The rebels agree peacefully","Hotspur quarrels with Glendower"],"a3s2":["The King disowns Hal","Hal vows to defeat Hotspur"],"a3s3":["Falstaff pays his debts","Falstaff gets a command"],"a4s1":["Northumberland arrives with his army","Northumberland is sick"],"a4s2":["Falstaff raises a fine company","He recruits scarecrows"],"a4s3":["Hotspur accepts the King's offer","Worcester will answer in the morning"],"a4s4":["The Archbishop joins Hotspur at Shrewsbury","He prepares to defend York"],"a5s1":["The rebels accept pardon","Hal offers single combat"],"a5s2":["Worcester tells Hotspur of the offer","He hides it"],"a5s3":["Douglas kills the King","Douglas kills Blunt in the King's coat"],"a5s4":["Hotspur kills Hal","Hal kills Hotspur; Falstaff claims the kill"]},"contrast":[{"t":"Hal and Hotspur","pts":["Both are called Harry and are the same age in the play.","Hotspur seeks honour openly; Hal hides his worth to reveal it later.","Hotspur cannot share honour; Hal praises his rival and gives away the credit for killing him."],"ids":[114,591]},{"t":"Hotspur and Falstaff","pts":["Hotspur would die for honour; Falstaff says honour is air.","Both are brilliant talkers, one in verse, one in prose.","Hotspur dies; Falstaff fakes death and survives."],"ids":[114,817]},{"t":"The King and Falstaff","pts":["Both act as fathers to Hal.","The King lectures Hal on being seldom seen; Falstaff wants him always in the tavern.","Hal plays the King and condemns Falstaff in the tavern play."],"ids":[588,455]},{"t":"Lady Percy and Lady Mortimer","pts":["Both wives are left behind by the war.","Kate argues with Hotspur in quick English; Lady Mortimer cannot speak to her husband at all.","Lady Mortimer sings; Kate refuses."],"ids":[248,553]},{"t":"Hotspur and Glendower","pts":["Both are proud and quick to take offence.","Glendower believes in portents and magic; Hotspur mocks them.","Both fail the rebellion: Hotspur by rashness, Glendower by absence."],"ids":[503,515]}],"analysis":{"HAL":["Hal is the play's centre and its puzzle. To his father he is a disgrace, spending his days with thieves and drunkards; to Falstaff he is 'sweet wag'. But in his first soliloquy he tells the audience that he knows exactly what he is doing: 'I know you all', and he will 'imitate the Sun', letting the clouds hide him so that his reformation will 'attract more eyes'. From then on we watch him with double vision, enjoying the tavern while knowing it will end.","In the tavern Hal is witty, quick and sometimes cold. He teases Francis for fun, exposes Falstaff's lies with relish, and in the play extempore takes his father's part to condemn 'that old white-bearded Satan'. His answer to 'banish plump Jack, and banish all the world' is 'I do, I will', a joke that sounds like a promise. He learns the language of the people, which will make him a king who can speak to everyone.","Facing his father in 3.2, Hal moves from apology to a vow: 'I will redeem all this on Percy's head.' He thinks of honour as an account: Hotspur is 'but my factor', collecting glory for Hal to claim. At Shrewsbury he keeps every promise: he refuses to leave the field though wounded, saves his father from Douglas, praises Hotspur generously and kills him in single combat.","After the duel Hal shows a new largeness. He covers Hotspur's face and gives him an epitaph, mourns Falstaff with mixed feelings ('I could have better spared a better man'), lets Falstaff take the credit for killing Percy, and frees Douglas without ransom. He has become a prince who can be generous because he no longer needs to prove himself. Whether his transformation is sincere growth or brilliant calculation is the play's great debate."],"FALSTAFF":["Sir John Falstaff is one of Shakespeare's most famous creations: fat, old, witty, cowardly, lying and irresistible. He lives for sack, food and jokes, robs travellers and borrows money he never repays. Yet he is no simple villain. His wit is so quick that he escapes every trap, and his love for Hal seems real: 'If the rascal have not given me medicines to make me love him, I'll be hang'd.'","Falstaff's great scenes are performances. He invents eleven men in buckram, then claims he knew it was Hal all along and would not hurt 'the true prince'. In the tavern play he plays the King and defends himself, and his plea, 'banish plump Jack, and banish all the world', makes him the voice of pleasure and life against duty.","In the war he becomes darker. He abuses the King's press, taking bribes and leading 'food for powder' to their deaths, and admits that hardly three of his hundred and fifty are left alive. His catechism on honour, 'What is honour? a word', is both funny and serious: he sees that honour is what dead men like Blunt are left with.","At Shrewsbury Falstaff fakes death, stabs the dead Hotspur and claims he killed him. It is a parody of the heroic climax and a final triumph of survival. Critics divide between those who see him as a corrupting Vice whom Hal must reject and those who see him as the play's truest voice, exposing the violence and hypocrisy of the political world."],"HOTSPUR":["Harry Percy, called Hotspur, is the son the King wishes he had: brave, famous, 'the theme of honour's tongue'. He has just beaten the Scots at Holmedon, and he talks of plucking 'bright honour from the pale-faced Moon'. He is impatient, hot-tempered and plain-spoken, despising courtiers, poetry and anything that looks like flattery.","Hotspur's virtues are also his flaws. He cannot listen: his uncle and father struggle to get a word in, and he flies into comic rages about starlings and Bolingbroke. At Bangor he mocks Glendower to his face, nearly breaking the alliance, and argues over a river while saying he would give away three times as much land to a friend.","With his wife Kate Hotspur is teasing and affectionate but shuts her out of his plans: war is his real love. His speech before Shrewsbury, 'the time of life is short', shows his creed: a short life spent nobly is better than a long one spent basely. He insists on fighting even when his father and Glendower fail him.","Hotspur dies at Hal's hand, mourning the loss of his 'proud titles' more than his life, and his last sentence is finished by his killer. He is a figure of chivalry out of place in a world of politicians, and Shakespeare makes him attractive as well as reckless. His death completes Hal's transformation."],"KING":["King Henry IV, once Bolingbroke, took the crown from Richard II and has not had a peaceful day since. The play opens with him 'shaken' and 'wan with care', planning a crusade that is partly penance for Richard's death, and it never happens. He is a skilled politician who knows that power depends on 'opinion' and that a king should be 'seldom seen'.","He is also a troubled father. He wishes Hal had been swapped in the cradle for Hotspur, and he half believes that Hal's wildness is God's punishment for his own sins. In 3.2 he compares Hal to Richard and Hotspur to himself as a young man, a bitter speech that reveals his fears and his methods.","As king he can be hard and cunning. He dismisses Worcester, refuses to ransom Mortimer because Mortimer has a better claim to the throne, and fights Shrewsbury with decoys wearing his coat. But he also offers the rebels a genuine pardon. His victory at Shrewsbury and his recognition that Hal 'hast redeem'd thy lost opinion' bring father and son together, though the rebellion goes on."],"WORCESTER":["Thomas Percy, Earl of Worcester, Hotspur's uncle, is the real organiser of the rebellion. Dismissed from court in 1.3, he lays out the plot to unite the Scots, the Archbishop, Mortimer and Glendower. He is cool and calculating, the opposite of his nephew, and he warns Hotspur that his temper 'loseth men's hearts'.","His key act is a lie. In 5.2 he decides not to tell Hotspur about the King's generous offer, believing that the King will never trust the older Percys: 'treason is but trusted like the fox'. His fear for himself causes the battle. Captured, he is sent to execution, and the King blames him for every death on the field."],"GLENDOWER":["Owen Glendower, the Welsh leader, is proud, learned and mysterious. He claims that the earth shook and the sky burned at his birth, and that he can 'call spirits from the vasty deep'. He was brought up at the English court and is a poet and musician as well as a warrior who has beaten Henry three times.","Hotspur mocks him relentlessly, and Glendower, surprisingly, controls his temper. He loves his daughter and translates for her and Mortimer. But he does not arrive at Shrewsbury, 'o'er-rul'd by prophecies', and his absence helps doom the rebellion."],"LADY PERCY":["Kate, Lady Percy, is one of Shakespeare's liveliest wives. In 2.3 she gives a vivid account of her husband talking of war in his sleep and demands to know his business: 'else he loves me not'. She is witty and fearless, calling him a 'mad-headed ape' and threatening to break his little finger.","Hotspur loves her but will not trust her with his secret, teasing that she cannot tell what she does not know. At Bangor she banters with him while Lady Mortimer sings. Her role shows the private cost of Hotspur's obsession with honour, a cost that becomes tragic in Part 2, where she mourns him."]},"motifs":[["The sun and the son","Hal will 'imitate the Sun'; Falstaff puns on 'the son of England'; the King was a 'comet'; two stars cannot share one sphere. Royal light depends on being seen at the right moment.",[16,18,75,107,172]],["Time and debt","Hal will 'redeem' time and pay a debt he never promised; Falstaff lives outside time and owes everyone. Honour too is an account to be settled.",[5,19,87,118,119,153]],["Counterfeits","Lies, decoys and disguises: buckram suits, the King's coats, Falstaff's fake death and Hal's own performance of wildness.",[67,83,163,180,183,185]],["Honour","Hotspur's bright honour, Falstaff's 'word' and 'air', Blunt's grinning corpse, and Hal's harvest of Hotspur's 'budding honours'.",[33,152,153,164,173,175]],["The body","Falstaff's huge body, full of sack and food, set against thin Hal and the wounded and dead of Shrewsbury.",[49,70,78,123,137,178]],["Playing","The tavern play, Hal's role as truant, the King's acted humility: power in this play is a performance.",[15,74,77,81,108,185]]],"bigq":[["Is Hal's reformation sincere?",["His soliloquy shows it was planned from the start","He seems to enjoy the tavern genuinely","His father's speech moves him to a real vow","At Shrewsbury his actions match his words"],[15,18,19,116,169,185]],["Is Falstaff a villain or a hero?",["He robs, lies and sends soldiers to die","His wit and love of life are irresistible","His honour speech tells hard truths","Hal both loves and rejects him"],[46,81,137,153,179,181]],["What is honour?",["To Hotspur it is glory worth more than life","To Falstaff it is air","To Hal it can be collected like a debt","Blunt's death asks what it is worth"],[33,118,153,164,175,173]],["Is Henry a rightful king?",["He deposed and killed Richard","Hotspur calls him a 'vile politician'","He rules well and wins the battle","His guilt shapes his reign"],[0,29,39,106,108,141]],["Who is Hal's real father?",["The King gives him duty and the crown","Falstaff gives him pleasure and laughter","The tavern play tests both roles","Hal chooses his father at Shrewsbury"],[3,77,78,111,115,170]],["Is the rebellion justified?",["The Percys made Henry king and were cast off","Mortimer had a better claim","The rebels are divided and self-seeking","Worcester's lie causes the battle"],[29,30,42,93,145,156]]],"examq":[["How does Shakespeare present Hal's development in the play?",[15,19,82,116,169,185]],["How does Shakespeare present honour in 1 Henry IV?",[33,35,118,153,164,175]],["How does Shakespeare present Falstaff?",[9,67,72,81,137,181]],["How does Shakespeare present the relationship between fathers and sons?",[3,4,104,111,115,170]],["How does Shakespeare present kingship and power?",[0,20,106,107,108,172]],["How does Shakespeare present rebellion?",[29,41,93,128,145,156]]],"check":["Answered the exact question in the first sentence","Made a clear argument (a thesis) and kept to it","Used short quotations, built into my own sentences","Explained what the quotation suggests, not just what it says","Zoomed in on a single word or image","Named a method (soliloquy, imagery, prose and verse, parallel plots, irony, structure) and its effect","Linked to context where it helps the argument","Considered more than one interpretation, especially of Hal and Falstaff","Referred to the beginning, middle and end of the play","Ended with a conclusion that answers the question"],"models":[[15,"Shakespeare uses Hal's first soliloquy to change how the audience sees him.","Until now he has seemed a witty idler; alone, he reveals a plan.","'I know you all' sounds detached, even cold, about friends he has just joked with.","The sun image ties his future to kingship and tells us the tavern is only a cloud."],[153,"Falstaff's catechism questions the value of honour.","The question-and-answer form, borrowed from religious teaching, makes his doubts sound logical.","Reducing honour to 'a word' and 'air' strips it of glory.","It is placed just before a battle where Blunt will die for honour, giving Falstaff's view grim force."],[33,"Hotspur's honour speech shows his heroic but reckless nature.","He imagines plucking honour from the moon or the bottom of the sea, impossible places.","The extravagant images show a man ruled by imagination.","Worcester's comment that he sees 'a world of figures' makes the point: Hotspur cannot see the real situation."],[107,"The King's speech about being seldom seen reveals his political method.","He compares himself to a comet, wondered at because rare.","His view of majesty is theatrical: it depends on how others see him.","He uses it to attack Hal, whose 'vile participation' makes him common like Richard."],[81,"Falstaff's plea 'banish plump Jack' is the comic and emotional peak of the tavern play.","The repetition of 'banish not him thy Harry's company' sounds like real pleading under the joke.","'Banish all the world' makes Falstaff stand for life itself.","Hal's reply, 'I do, I will', turns the game serious and points to the future."],[175,"Hotspur's dying speech reveals what he valued most.","He minds the loss of 'proud titles' more than his 'brittle life'.","His last thought turns philosophical, 'life Time's fool'.","Hal finishing his sentence ('For worms') shows that even his last words are taken from him."]],"lang":{"un":[["80","Redeeming time, when men think least I will.","Making up for wasted time when people least expect it."],["588","Opinion, that did help me to the crown,","Public opinion, which helped me get the crown."],["591","Percy is but my factor, good my lord,","Percy is only my agent, my lord."],["817","What is honour? a word.","Honour is just a word."]],"pun":[["138","To save our heads by raising of a head;","head","double meaning","Head as the part of the body and as an army."],["439","Shall the blessed Sun of heaven prove a micher","Sun","pun","Sun and son: the King's heir."],["173","not pray to her, but prey on her","prey","pun","Pray and prey: worship and rob."],["37","Yea, for obtaining of suits","suits","double meaning","Petitions at court and the clothes of hanged men."],["380","if reasons were as plentiful as blackberries","reasons","pun","Reasons sounded like 'raisins'."],["930","then am I a Jack","Jack","double meaning","His name and a rogue."]],"st":[["80","Redeeming time, when men think least I will.",[["Redeeming","Buying back, saving."],["time","Wasted time."]]],["114","To pluck bright honour from the pale-faced Moon;",[["pluck","A violent snatch."],["honour","The key word."]]],["918","O Harry, thou hast robb'd me of my youth!",[["robb'd","Theft: Hal takes what was Hotspur's."],["youth","The future he loses."]]]]},"dir":[["80","I know you all","How should Hal speak the start of his soliloquy?",[["Coldly, looking after his friends","He is detached and calculating."],["Directly to the audience, sharing a secret","He makes us his accomplices."],["Uncertainly, persuading himself","He is not sure he can change."]]],["456","I do, I will.","How should Hal say 'I do, I will'?",[["Lightly, as part of the game","The tavern play goes on."],["Seriously, after a pause","He means it, and everyone feels it."],["Sadly, looking at Falstaff","He knows what he must do one day."]]],["387","By the Lord, I knew ye as well as he that made ye.","How should Falstaff deliver his escape?",[["Triumphantly, as if he had planned it","He enjoys his own cleverness."],["After a desperate pause","He is thinking fast."],["Sincerely, believing it","He has convinced himself."]]],["817","What is honour? a word.","How should Falstaff speak the honour catechism?",[["As a comic routine to the audience","He wins laughs."],["Quietly and seriously","He is afraid of dying."],["Bitterly, looking at the battlefield","He knows what honour costs."]]],["920","For worms, brave Percy: fare thee well, great heart!","How should Hal speak over Hotspur's body?",[["With real grief","He has lost a worthy rival."],["Coolly, as a victor","He has taken Hotspur's honour."],["Exhausted, kneeling","The fight has cost him too."]]],["924","The better part of valour is discretion;","How should Falstaff rise from the dead?",[["Slowly, checking no one is looking","The comedy of caution."],["Leaping up, delighted","Life itself triumphs."],["Shaken, with a look at the dead","He has seen what honour means."]]]],"ev":{"ctx":[["80","110","456"],["61","709","912"],["856","912","806"],["107","110","125"],["80","456","125"],["24","450","452"],["80","322","456"],["450","452","456"],["512","553","563"],["729"],["374","442","456"]],"cr":{"honour":[["867","878","456"],["867","878","924"]],"kingship":[["80","456","589"],["80","589","912"]],"fathers":[["442","450","589"],["376","450","456"]],"misrule":[["80","442","456"],["374","456","679"]],"counterfeit":[["862","924","466"],["80","442","374"]],"rebellion":[["110","125","942"],["942","802","138"]]}}};
